ADDART({id:"machupicchu",t:"Machu Picchu",c:"Countries and Places",i:"⛰️",w:"Machu Picchu",r:["perucountry","andes","aztec","maya"],
p:[["City in the clouds","Machu Picchu is a 15th-century Inca citadel built on a mountain ridge about 2,430 meters above sea level in the Andes of Peru."],["Masterful stonework","Its walls are made of stones cut so precisely that they fit together without mortar."],["Rediscovered","Little known outside the region for centuries, it became world famous after a visit by Hiram Bingham in 1911."]],
l:"Machu Picchu is an Inca site high in the Andes Mountains of southern Peru, above the Urubamba River valley. It was probably built around 1450 as a royal estate for the Inca emperor Pachacuti and was abandoned about a century later, around the time of the Spanish conquest. The Spanish never found it, so it survived largely intact. It is a UNESCO World Heritage Site and one of the most famous archaeological sites in the world.",
s:[["The Inca Empire","The Inca built the largest empire in the Americas, stretching along the Andes from present-day Ecuador to Chile, with its capital at Cusco. They had no writing system as we know it, but they kept records with knotted cords called quipu and built roads of more than 30,000 kilometers to connect their lands."],["A royal estate","Most archaeologists think Machu Picchu was a retreat built for Pachacuti, the emperor who expanded the Inca state in the 1400s. Up to about 750 people may have lived there, including nobles, priests and servants. The site has temples, palaces, houses, plazas, fountains and storehouses."],["Building on a mountain","The Inca shaped the steep ridge with stone terraces for farming, which also stopped the soil from sliding. Underground drainage channels carried rainwater away. The finest buildings use ashlar masonry, stones cut so exactly that a knife blade cannot fit between them, which helps them survive earthquakes."],["Abandoned and forgotten","Machu Picchu was abandoned around the 1500s, perhaps because of the Spanish conquest, civil war or disease such as smallpox. Local farmers still knew about it, but the outside world did not. In 1911 the American explorer Hiram Bingham was guided there by local people and published photographs that made it famous."]],
k:[["Location","Cusco Region, Peru"],["Altitude","About 2,430 m"],["Built","c. 1450"],["Built by","The Inca, probably for Emperor Pachacuti"],["World Heritage Site","Since 1983"]],
d:[["c. 1450","Machu Picchu is built"],["1532","Spanish conquistadors arrive in the Inca Empire"],["c. 1570s","The site is abandoned by about this time"],["1911","Hiram Bingham publicizes the site"],["1983","Named a UNESCO World Heritage Site"],["2007","Voted one of the New Seven Wonders of the World"]],
s2:[["The Temple of the Sun","The curved Temple of the Sun, or Torreón, is built over a rock cave. Its windows line up with the rising Sun at the June solstice. Nearby, the Intihuatana stone, a carved rock pillar, may have been used to track the Sun’s movement through the year."],["The Inca Trail","Many visitors hike the Inca Trail, a stone path built by the Inca, for four days through mountains and cloud forest, passing other ruins along the way. They arrive at the Sun Gate, Inti Punku, with a view over the whole site. Others take a train to the town below and a bus up the mountain."],["Huayna Picchu","The steep peak rising behind the ruins is Huayna Picchu, Young Mountain. The Inca built terraces and a temple even on its narrow summit. Climbing its stone steps is popular but dizzying, and only a limited number of hikers are allowed each day."],["Return of the objects","Bingham’s expeditions took thousands of objects, such as pottery and bones, to Yale University in the United States. After years of requests from Peru, Yale returned them between 2011 and 2012, and they are now displayed in Cusco."],["Protecting the site","Machu Picchu receives more than a million visitors in many years. To protect it from wear, Peru limits the number of tickets each day and requires visitors to follow set routes with guides. Landslides and heavy rains also threaten the site and the railway that serves it."],["Living Inca heritage","Quechua, the language of the Inca, is still spoken by millions of people in the Andes. Farming terraces, weaving and festivals such as Inti Raymi, the Inca Festival of the Sun held in Cusco every June, keep traditions of the Inca world alive."]],
sp:["The New Seven Wonders","In 2007 a worldwide public vote organized by a Swiss foundation chose the New Seven Wonders of the World: Machu Picchu, the Great Wall of China, Petra in Jordan, the Colosseum in Rome, Chichén Itzá in Mexico, the Taj Mahal in India and the statue of Christ the Redeemer in Rio de Janeiro. The Pyramids of Giza were given an honorary place."],
g:[["Inca","The people and empire of the Andes in the 15th and 16th centuries"],["Citadel","A fortress or stronghold on high ground"],["Terrace","A flat step cut into a hillside for farming"],["Quipu","Knotted cords used by the Inca to keep records"]],
f:["Machu Picchu stands about 2,430 meters above sea level.","Its finest walls were built without mortar.","The Spanish never found it."],
fun:"Some walls at Machu Picchu fit so tightly that a knife blade cannot slip between the stones.",
tl:"Ang Machu Picchu ay sinaunang lungsod ng mga Inca sa kabundukan ng Andes sa Peru, na itinayo noong mga 1450. Kilala ito sa mga pader na batong walang semento at isa sa New Seven Wonders of the World."});

ADDART({id:"angkor",t:"Angkor",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🛕",w:"Angkor",r:["hindu","buddh","mekong","vietnamcountry"],
p:[["Capital of an empire","Angkor was the capital of the Khmer Empire, which ruled much of mainland Southeast Asia from the 9th to the 15th centuries."],["The greatest temple","Its great temple, Angkor Wat, is the largest religious monument in the world."],["A city of water","The city was surrounded by huge reservoirs and canals that watered rice fields."]],
l:"Angkor, in northwestern Cambodia near the city of Siem Reap, was the capital of the Khmer Empire from the 9th to the 15th centuries. Its ruins spread over about 400 square kilometers and include hundreds of temples, among them Angkor Wat, built in the 12th century, and the temple-city of Angkor Thom with its smiling stone faces. Studies suggest that at its height Greater Angkor was one of the largest urban areas in the pre-industrial world.",
s:[["The Khmer Empire","The Khmer Empire was founded in 802, when King Jayavarman II declared himself a universal king. Over the next centuries, Khmer kings controlled lands across present-day Cambodia, Thailand, Laos and southern Vietnam. They built roads, rest houses and hospitals as well as temples."],["Angkor Wat","Angkor Wat was built in the early 1100s by King Suryavarman II as a temple to the Hindu god Vishnu and probably his own tomb. Its five towers, shaped like lotus buds, represent Mount Meru, the home of the gods. It is surrounded by a moat about 190 meters wide and is so important to Cambodia that it appears on the national flag."],["Angkor Thom and the Bayon","Around 1200, King Jayavarman VII, a Buddhist, built the walled city of Angkor Thom. At its center stands the Bayon temple, with more than 200 huge stone faces smiling calmly in every direction. Its walls are carved with scenes of battles, markets, fishing and daily life."],["A hydraulic city","The Khmer built enormous reservoirs called barays to store water from the monsoon rains. The West Baray is about 8 kilometers long. Canals and channels carried water to rice fields and around the city, supporting a large population. Laser surveys from aircraft have revealed traces of the city hidden under the forest."]],
k:[["Location","Near Siem Reap, Cambodia"],["Capital of","The Khmer Empire, 9th–15th centuries"],["Area","About 400 km² of archaeological park"],["Angkor Wat built","Early 12th century"],["World Heritage Site","Since 1992"]],
d:[["802","Jayavarman II founds the Khmer Empire"],["c. 1113–1150","Angkor Wat built under Suryavarman II"],["c. 1181–1218","Jayavarman VII builds Angkor Thom and the Bayon"],["1431","Ayutthaya armies sack Angkor; the capital later moves south"],["1992","Named a UNESCO World Heritage Site"],["2012","Lidar survey reveals hidden city plan"]],
s2:[["Carvings in stone","Angkor Wat has more than 1,000 square meters of bas-relief carvings. One famous panel shows the Churning of the Ocean of Milk, a Hindu story in which gods and demons pull on a giant serpent to stir up the elixir of immortality. Thousands of carved apsaras, heavenly dancers, decorate the walls."],["Hindu and Buddhist","Angkor’s kings followed Hinduism or Buddhism, and the temples reflect both. Angkor Wat was built for Vishnu, but over time it became a Buddhist temple and has been used by Buddhist monks ever since. Cambodia today is mostly Theravada Buddhist."],["Why Angkor declined","After the 1300s, Angkor declined. Wars with the kingdom of Ayutthaya, in present-day Thailand, changes in trade toward the coast and long droughts followed by floods that damaged the water system probably all played a part. The capital moved south toward Phnom Penh, though Angkor Wat was never fully abandoned."],["Ta Prohm","At the temple of Ta Prohm, giant silk-cotton and strangler fig trees grow over the walls and roofs, their roots wrapping around the stones. It was deliberately left much as explorers found it, to show how the jungle reclaimed Angkor. It appeared in the 2001 film Lara Croft: Tomb Raider."],["Restoration","During Cambodia’s civil war and the rule of the Khmer Rouge in the 1970s, Angkor was neglected and some sculptures were looted. Since the 1990s, teams from Cambodia and many other countries have worked together to restore the temples, and stolen statues have been returned from museums abroad."],["Sunrise at Angkor Wat","Angkor is Cambodia’s most visited site. Many visitors gather before dawn to watch the Sun rise behind the towers of Angkor Wat, reflected in the pools in front of it. The temples are also used for festivals, Buddhist ceremonies and performances of classical Khmer dance."]],
sp:["Angkor on the flag","Cambodia’s flag shows a white outline of Angkor Wat on a red band between two blue bands. Cambodia is one of very few countries to show a building on its national flag. The temple is a deep source of national pride, and its image also appears on banknotes, beer labels and the badges of schools and sports teams."],
g:[["Khmer","The main people and language of Cambodia"],["Baray","A large reservoir built by the Khmer"],["Bas-relief","A carving that stands out slightly from a flat surface"],["Lidar","A way of mapping the ground using laser pulses"]],
f:["Angkor Wat is the largest religious monument in the world.","The Bayon has more than 200 carved stone faces.","Angkor Wat appears on Cambodia’s national flag."],
fun:"Angkor Wat appears on the flag of Cambodia.",
tl:"Ang Angkor sa Cambodia ang kabisera ng Imperyong Khmer mula ika-9 hanggang ika-15 siglo. Naroon ang Angkor Wat, ang pinakamalaking monumentong panrelihiyon sa mundo."});

ADDART({id:"paris",t:"Paris",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🗼",w:"Paris",r:["francecountry","frrev","impress","gothic","davinci"],
p:[["The capital of France","Paris lies on the River Seine in northern France and is the country’s political, economic and cultural center."],["City of art","It is home to the Louvre, the world’s most visited art museum, and was a world center of painting for centuries."],["Famous landmarks","The Eiffel Tower, Notre-Dame Cathedral and the Arc de Triomphe are known around the world."]],
l:"Paris is the capital and largest city of France, with about 2.1 million people in the city itself and more than 12 million in its metropolitan region. Built on both banks of the River Seine, it began as a settlement on an island over 2,000 years ago. Paris has been a world center of art, fashion, food, science and ideas, and it hosted the Summer Olympic Games in 1900, 1924 and 2024.",
s:[["From Lutetia to Paris","Around the 3rd century BCE, a Celtic people called the Parisii settled on an island in the Seine, now the Île de la Cité. The Romans conquered it in 52 BCE and called it Lutetia. By the 6th century it had become the capital of the Frankish kings, and in the Middle Ages it grew into one of Europe’s largest cities."],["Kings and revolution","French kings built palaces such as the Louvre and, outside the city, Versailles. In 1789 the people of Paris stormed the Bastille prison, beginning the French Revolution, which ended the monarchy and spread ideas of liberty and equality. Paris saw more revolutions in 1830, 1848 and 1871."],["Haussmann’s Paris","In the 1850s and 1860s, under Emperor Napoleon III, the official Georges-Eugène Haussmann rebuilt much of Paris. Narrow medieval streets were replaced with wide, tree-lined boulevards, parks, sewers and cream-colored stone apartment buildings, creating the look the city still has today."],["The Eiffel Tower","Built by Gustave Eiffel’s company for the World’s Fair of 1889, the iron Eiffel Tower is about 330 meters tall. Many artists hated it at first, and it was meant to stand for only 20 years, but it proved useful for radio and became the symbol of Paris. Millions of people climb it every year."]],
k:[["Country","France"],["Population","About 2.1 million (city), over 12 million (metropolitan area)"],["River","Seine"],["Famous landmarks","Eiffel Tower, Louvre, Notre-Dame, Arc de Triomphe"],["Olympic Games","1900, 1924, 2024"]],
d:[["52 BCE","Romans conquer the settlement of Lutetia"],["1163","Building of Notre-Dame Cathedral begins"],["1789","Storming of the Bastille begins the French Revolution"],["1889","Eiffel Tower opens for the World’s Fair"],["2019","Fire badly damages Notre-Dame"],["2024","Paris hosts the Summer Olympics; Notre-Dame reopens in December"]],
s2:[["The Louvre","The Louvre began as a fortress around 1200, became a royal palace and opened as a public museum in 1793, during the Revolution. Its collection includes Leonardo da Vinci’s Mona Lisa, the ancient Greek statue Venus de Milo and the Winged Victory of Samothrace. A glass pyramid by the architect I. M. Pei became its entrance in 1989."],["Notre-Dame","Notre-Dame de Paris, begun in 1163, is a masterpiece of Gothic architecture, with flying buttresses, rose windows and gargoyles. In April 2019 a fire destroyed its roof and spire. After five years of work by hundreds of craftspeople, it reopened in December 2024."],["Artists’ city","In the 1800s and early 1900s, artists from around the world came to Paris. The Impressionists, such as Claude Monet, painted its streets and parks, and later Pablo Picasso, Henri Matisse and many others worked in districts such as Montmartre and Montparnasse. Writers, musicians and filmmakers followed."],["Food and fashion","Paris is famous for cafés, bakeries selling baguettes and croissants, and restaurants of every kind. It is also a world capital of fashion, home to fashion houses such as Chanel and Dior, and hosts Paris Fashion Week twice a year."],["Getting around","The Paris Métro, opened in 1900, is one of the world’s oldest and busiest subway systems, famous for its Art Nouveau entrances. In recent years the city has added many bike lanes and cleaned the Seine so that people could swim in it again, a goal reached in 2025 for the first time in about a century."],["A diverse city","Paris is home to people from across France and the world, including large communities with roots in North and West Africa, the Caribbean, Asia and the rest of Europe. Its suburbs, called the banlieues, are home to millions, and the Grand Paris Express project is building new metro lines to link them."]],
sp:["Paris 2024","In 2024 Paris hosted the Summer Olympics and Paralympics, exactly 100 years after it last held them. The opening ceremony was held along the Seine instead of in a stadium, with athletes sailing past landmarks on boats. Events took place beneath the Eiffel Tower, at the Grand Palais and at the Palace of Versailles."],
g:[["Boulevard","A wide city street, often lined with trees"],["Gothic","A style of medieval architecture with pointed arches"],["Metropolitan area","A city together with its surrounding suburbs"],["World’s Fair","A large international exhibition of inventions and culture"]],
f:["The Eiffel Tower was meant to stand for only 20 years.","The Louvre is the world’s most visited art museum.","Paris has hosted the Summer Olympics three times."],
fun:"The Eiffel Tower was supposed to be taken down after 20 years.",
tl:"Ang Paris ang kabisera ng France, nasa pampang ng Ilog Seine. Kilala ito sa Eiffel Tower, sa museong Louvre at sa sining, pagkain at moda. Dito ginanap ang Olimpiyada ng 2024."});

;
/* data/places3.js */
// Countries and Places: world cities and islands, part 3: New York City, Sydney and Greenland.
// Same format as data/countries1.js (see data/animals-load.js).
addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded",()=>{Object.assign(window.LOC=window.LOC||{},{newyork:[-74.0,40.71,"United States of America",36,"New York City"],sydney:[151.21,-33.87,"Australia",36,"Sydney"],greenland:[-41,72,"Greenland",14,"Greenland"]})});

ADDART({id:"newyork",t:"New York City",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🗽",w:"New York City",r:["usacountry","trade","un","atlantic"],
p:[["America’s biggest city","New York City is the most populous city in the United States, with about 8.5 million people."],["Five boroughs","It is made up of five boroughs: Manhattan, Brooklyn, Queens, the Bronx and Staten Island."],["A world city","It is home to the United Nations headquarters, Wall Street, Broadway and the Statue of Liberty."]],
l:"New York City lies at the mouth of the Hudson River on the Atlantic coast of the northeastern United States. With about 8.5 million residents and around 20 million in its metropolitan area, it is the largest city in the country and one of the world’s great centers of finance, culture, media and trade. Founded as the Dutch trading post of New Amsterdam in the 1620s, it grew into a gateway for millions of immigrants.",
s:[["New Amsterdam","The land was home to the Lenape people when Europeans arrived. In 1609 Henry Hudson, sailing for the Dutch, explored the river now named after him, and in the 1620s the Dutch founded New Amsterdam on the southern tip of Manhattan. In 1664 the English seized the colony and renamed it New York, after the Duke of York."],["Gateway for immigrants","From the 1800s, New York became the main port of entry to the United States. Between 1892 and 1954, about 12 million immigrants passed through the inspection station on Ellis Island. Irish, Germans, Italians, Jews from Eastern Europe, Chinese and many others built neighborhoods, and later waves came from the Caribbean, Latin America, Asia and Africa."],["The five boroughs","In 1898 the city of New York joined with Brooklyn and other areas to form the five boroughs. Manhattan, a narrow island, is the dense center of business and culture. Brooklyn and Queens are the most populous boroughs; the Bronx is the birthplace of hip hop; and Staten Island is reached by a free ferry."],["City of skyscrapers","New York helped invent the skyscraper. The Woolworth Building, the Chrysler Building and the Empire State Building, completed in 1931, each became the world’s tallest building in turn. Today One World Trade Center, about 541 meters tall including its spire, is the tallest building in the Western Hemisphere."]],
k:[["Country","United States"],["Population","About 8.5 million (city), about 20 million (metropolitan area)"],["Boroughs","Manhattan, Brooklyn, Queens, the Bronx, Staten Island"],["Tallest building","One World Trade Center, about 541 m"],["Languages spoken","Possibly more than 600"]],
d:[["1624","Dutch settlers found New Amsterdam"],["1664","The English take control and rename it New York"],["1886","Statue of Liberty dedicated"],["1898","Five boroughs unite into one city"],["1931","Empire State Building completed"],["2001","September 11 attacks destroy the World Trade Center"]],
s2:[["The Statue of Liberty","The Statue of Liberty, a gift from France designed by Frédéric Auguste Bartholdi, was dedicated in 1886 on an island in New York Harbor. Holding a torch and a tablet, the copper statue turned green over time. For immigrants arriving by ship, it was the first sight of their new country."],["Central Park","Central Park, designed by Frederick Law Olmsted and Calvert Vaux and opened in the 1850s and 1860s, covers about 340 hectares in the middle of Manhattan. Its lakes, meadows, woods and paths are visited by tens of millions of people every year and inspired city parks around the world."],["Money and the world","Wall Street, in lower Manhattan, is home to the New York Stock Exchange and has become a name for the whole American financial industry. The headquarters of the United Nations stands beside the East River, where leaders from nearly every country meet each year for the General Assembly."],["Arts and entertainment","Broadway theaters stage musicals and plays seen by millions of visitors. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, the Museum of Modern Art and the American Museum of Natural History are among the world’s great museums. Jazz, hip hop, salsa and punk all grew in New York’s neighborhoods."],["September 11","On 11 September 2001, terrorists flew hijacked planes into the twin towers of the World Trade Center, which collapsed, killing nearly 3,000 people in New York, Washington and Pennsylvania. A memorial with two reflecting pools now marks where the towers stood, beside the new One World Trade Center."],["A city of many languages","New York is one of the most linguistically diverse cities on Earth. Researchers estimate that more than 600 languages are spoken there, and over a third of its residents were born outside the United States. Its subway, one of the largest in the world, runs 24 hours a day."]],
sp:["The subway","The New York City Subway opened in 1904 and now has 472 stations, more than any other metro system in the world. It carries several million riders on a typical weekday and runs around the clock, which few metros do. Many lines have express and local tracks side by side, so some trains skip stations to travel faster."],
g:[["Borough","One of the five main divisions of New York City"],["Immigrant","A person who moves to live in another country"],["Skyscraper","A very tall building with many floors"],["Stock exchange","A market where shares in companies are bought and sold"]],
f:["About 12 million immigrants passed through Ellis Island.","The subway has 472 stations, more than any other system.","More than 600 languages may be spoken in New York."],
fun:"The New York City Subway has more stations than any other metro system in the world.",
tl:"Ang New York City ang pinakamalaking lungsod sa Estados Unidos, may limang borough kabilang ang Manhattan. Kilala ito sa Statue of Liberty, sa mga skyscraper, sa Broadway at sa punong-tanggapan ng United Nations."});

ADDART({id:"sydney",t:"Sydney",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🎭",w:"Sydney",r:["australiacountry","nzcountry","pacific","reef"],
p:[["Australia’s largest city","Sydney, on the southeast coast, is Australia’s largest and oldest city, with more than 5 million people."],["A great harbor","It is built around Sydney Harbour, one of the world’s finest natural harbors."],["Famous icons","The Sydney Opera House and the Sydney Harbour Bridge are among the most recognized structures on Earth."]],
l:"Sydney is the capital of the state of New South Wales and the largest city in Australia, with a population of about 5.5 million. It lies around Port Jackson, better known as Sydney Harbour, on the Pacific coast. The land has been home to Aboriginal peoples, including the Gadigal of the Eora Nation, for tens of thousands of years. Sydney began as a British penal colony in 1788 and has grown into a multicultural global city.",
s:[["First peoples","Aboriginal people have lived in the Sydney region for at least 30,000 years, and perhaps much longer. The Eora peoples fished in the harbor in bark canoes and left rock engravings and paintings around the coast. Many places in Sydney keep Aboriginal names, such as Parramatta, Woolloomooloo and Bondi."],["The First Fleet","In January 1788, eleven British ships known as the First Fleet arrived in Sydney Cove with about 1,500 people, around half of them convicts. Their landing on 26 January is marked as Australia Day, though many Aboriginal Australians call it Invasion Day or Survival Day, because it began the loss of their lands. Disease and conflict devastated local communities."],["From colony to city","The convict colony slowly grew into a trading port. Gold rushes in the 1850s brought new wealth and settlers. In 1901, when Australia’s colonies joined into one nation, Sydney and Melbourne were rivals to be capital, so a new capital, Canberra, was built between them."],["Opera House","The Sydney Opera House, designed by the Danish architect Jørn Utzon, opened in 1973. Its white roofs look like sails or shells and are covered in more than a million tiles. Building it was so difficult and costly that Utzon left the project in 1966. It was named a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2007."]],
k:[["Country","Australia"],["State","New South Wales (capital)"],["Population","About 5.5 million"],["Founded","1788 as a British colony"],["Famous landmarks","Opera House, Harbour Bridge, Bondi Beach"]],
d:[["1770","James Cook lands at Botany Bay, south of the harbor"],["1788","The First Fleet arrives in Sydney Cove"],["1932","Sydney Harbour Bridge opens"],["1973","Sydney Opera House opens"],["2000","Sydney hosts the Summer Olympics"],["2007","Opera House named a World Heritage Site"]],
s2:[["The Harbour Bridge","The steel arch of the Sydney Harbour Bridge, opened in 1932, is nicknamed the Coathanger. It carries trains, cars, bicycles and people across the harbor. Visitors can climb to the top of the arch, about 134 meters above the water, on guided climbs."],["Beaches","Sydney has dozens of ocean beaches, from Bondi and Manly to Coogee and Cronulla. Surf lifesaving clubs, whose volunteers patrol the beaches, began here in the early 1900s. Ocean pools cut into rocks along the shore let swimmers enjoy the sea in safety."],["New Year’s Eve","Sydney is one of the first major cities in the world to welcome the New Year, and its fireworks over the harbor, launched from the Harbour Bridge and from barges, are watched by more than a million people on the shore and by many more on television."],["Multicultural Sydney","More than a third of Sydney’s people were born overseas. After World War II, immigrants came from Britain, Italy, Greece and Lebanon, and later from Vietnam, China, India, the Philippines and many other countries. Suburbs such as Cabramatta, Harris Park and Leichhardt are known for the food of these communities."],["Nature in the city","Sydney is surrounded by national parks. The Royal National Park, created in 1879 to the south, is the world’s second-oldest national park. To the west, the Blue Mountains are named for the blue haze given off by their eucalyptus forests."],["The 2000 Olympics","Sydney hosted the Summer Olympics in 2000. The Aboriginal athlete Cathy Freeman lit the cauldron at the opening ceremony and later won gold in the 400 meters, a moment many Australians remember. The games were praised for their organization and friendly atmosphere."]],
sp:["Building the Opera House","In 1957 Jørn Utzon won an international design competition with sketches of sail-like roofs, but no one knew how to build them. After years of trials, Utzon found a solution: every roof shell is a piece cut from the same imaginary sphere, so they could all be made from standard parts. The building cost about 14 times its first estimate, yet it became one of the most admired buildings of the 20th century."],
g:[["Penal colony","A settlement where convicts were sent as punishment"],["Harbor","A sheltered stretch of water where ships can anchor"],["Eora","Aboriginal peoples of the Sydney coastal area"],["Multicultural","Made up of many cultures"]],
f:["The Opera House roofs are covered in more than a million tiles.","The Harbour Bridge is nicknamed the Coathanger.","Canberra was built as a compromise between Sydney and Melbourne."],
fun:"The Sydney Harbour Bridge is nicknamed the Coathanger.",
tl:"Ang Sydney ang pinakamalaking lungsod ng Australia at nasa paligid ng Sydney Harbour. Kilala ito sa Sydney Opera House at Harbour Bridge. Matagal na itong tahanan ng mga Aboriginal na tao bago dumating ang mga Briton noong 1788."});

ADDART({id:"greenland",t:"Greenland",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🧊",w:"Greenland",r:["arctic","climchange","iceland","viking"],
p:[["The largest island","Greenland is the world’s largest island that is not a continent, about 2.2 million square kilometers in area."],["Under the ice","About four-fifths of it is covered by an ice sheet up to about 3 kilometers thick."],["Inuit homeland","Most of its 57,000 people are Inuit, and it is a self-governing territory within the Kingdom of Denmark."]],
l:"Greenland lies between the Arctic and Atlantic Oceans, northeast of Canada. It is the largest island in the world, yet one of the least populated places on Earth, with about 57,000 people, most of them Kalaallit, or Greenlandic Inuit. Its huge ice sheet holds about a tenth of the world’s fresh water. Greenland is an autonomous territory of the Kingdom of Denmark, with its own government in the capital, Nuuk.",
s:[["Land of ice","The Greenland ice sheet covers about 1.7 million square kilometers and in places is more than 3 kilometers thick. Its weight presses the rock beneath below sea level. At the edges, glaciers flow down to the sea and break off into icebergs. If all of it melted, global sea levels would rise by about 7 meters."],["First peoples","People reached Greenland from North America about 4,500 years ago. Several cultures came and went, and the ancestors of today’s Inuit, the Thule people, arrived around the 1200s. They hunted seals, whales and walruses from kayaks and traveled by dog sled across the ice."],["The Norse","Around 985, Erik the Red led Norse settlers from Iceland to southwestern Greenland, giving the island its inviting name to attract farmers. The Norse colonies lasted about 450 years before disappearing in the 1400s, perhaps because of a colder climate, falling trade and isolation."],["Danish rule and self-government","A Danish-Norwegian missionary, Hans Egede, arrived in 1721, beginning colonial rule. Greenland became part of Denmark in 1953. It gained home rule in 1979 and wider self-government in 2009, which recognized Greenlanders as a people with the right to self-determination. Denmark still handles defense and foreign policy."]],
k:[["Status","Autonomous territory within the Kingdom of Denmark"],["Capital","Nuuk"],["Area","About 2.2 million km²"],["Population","About 57,000"],["Languages","Greenlandic (Kalaallisut), Danish"]],
d:[["c. 2500 BCE","First people arrive from North America"],["c. 985","Erik the Red founds Norse settlements"],["c. 1200s","Thule ancestors of the Inuit spread across Greenland"],["1721","Hans Egede’s mission begins Danish colonial rule"],["1979","Home rule established"],["2009","Self-government expanded; Greenlandic becomes the official language"]],
s2:[["Life in Greenland","No roads link Greenland’s towns, so people travel by boat, plane, helicopter, snowmobile or dog sled. Most live along the southwestern coast, where the sea stays open longer. Fishing, especially for shrimp and halibut, provides most of the country’s exports."],["Northern lights and midnight sun","North of the Arctic Circle, the Sun does not set for weeks in summer and does not rise for weeks in winter. On dark winter nights, the aurora borealis, or northern lights, ripples across the sky in green and purple curtains."],["Melting ice","Greenland’s ice sheet has been losing ice since the 1990s as temperatures rise, and its melting is now one of the biggest causes of rising seas. Scientists drill ice cores, which hold air bubbles trapped for more than 100,000 years, to learn about past climates."],["Ilulissat Icefjord","The Ilulissat Icefjord on the west coast, a UNESCO World Heritage Site, is fed by Sermeq Kujalleq, one of the fastest-moving glaciers in the world. Huge icebergs drift out of the fjord into Disko Bay. An iceberg from this region may have been the one that sank the Titanic in 1912."],["Greenlandic culture","Greenlanders keep traditions such as drum dancing, mask dancing and kayak building, and the national dress is decorated with tiny glass beads. Their language, Kalaallisut, is related to Inuit languages in Canada. The national day, 21 June, is the longest day of the year."],["A place in world affairs","Greenland’s location between North America and Europe makes it important for defense, and the United States has operated a military base there since World War II. Its minerals and the opening of Arctic sea routes as ice melts have drawn growing international interest, and Greenlanders debate their future, including possible full independence."]],
sp:["Why green?","According to old Icelandic sagas, Erik the Red named the land Greenland because people would be more eager to go there if it had a good name. His settlements were in sheltered southern fjords that are green in summer, but most of the island is white with ice. In Greenlandic, its name is Kalaallit Nunaat, Land of the Greenlanders."],
g:[["Ice sheet","A huge, thick layer of ice covering land"],["Inuit","Indigenous peoples of the Arctic"],["Fjord","A long, narrow inlet of the sea between cliffs"],["Autonomous","Self-governing"]],
f:["Greenland is the largest island in the world.","If its ice sheet melted, seas would rise by about 7 meters.","No roads connect its towns."],
fun:"There are no roads between towns in Greenland.",
tl:"Ang Greenland ang pinakamalaking pulo sa mundo, karamihang natatakpan ng makapal na yelo. Karamihan sa mga 57,000 naninirahan dito ay Inuit, at ito ay may sariling pamahalaan sa ilalim ng Kaharian ng Denmark."});

;
