// Space articles, part 2.1: Earth as a planet, the Kuiper Belt and Oort Cloud, and space weather.
ADDART({id:"earthplanet",t:"Earth as a Planet",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🌍",w:"Earth",r:["solar","moon","sun","tect","climate"],
p:[["Our home planet","Earth is the third planet from the Sun and the only place in the universe known to have life."],["A water world","About 71 percent of Earth’s surface is covered by oceans of liquid water."],["Protected by layers","Its atmosphere and magnetic field shield life from harmful radiation and small space rocks."]],
l:"Earth is the third planet from the Sun and the largest of the four rocky planets. It formed about 4.54 billion years ago from a disk of gas and dust around the young Sun. Earth has one natural satellite, the Moon, and is the only known world with liquid water on its surface and with life. It orbits the Sun once every 365.25 days at an average distance of about 150 million kilometers, while spinning on its axis once every 24 hours.",
s:[["Shape and size","Earth is nearly a sphere, but its spin makes it bulge slightly at the equator, where it is about 12,756 kilometers across, about 43 kilometers wider than from pole to pole. Its highest point, the top of Mount Everest, is about 8.8 kilometers above sea level, and its deepest, the Challenger Deep in the Pacific, about 11 kilometers below."],["Inside Earth","Earth has layers like an onion. The thin rocky crust floats on a hot, slowly flowing mantle. At the center is a core made mostly of iron and nickel: a liquid outer core and a solid inner core about as hot as the surface of the Sun. Movements in the liquid outer core create Earth’s magnetic field."],["Day, night and seasons","Earth spins once every 24 hours, giving day and night. Its axis is tilted by about 23.4 degrees, so as it orbits the Sun, first one hemisphere and then the other leans toward the Sun, causing the seasons. When it is summer in the Northern Hemisphere, it is winter in the Southern Hemisphere."],["The atmosphere","Earth’s atmosphere is about 78 percent nitrogen and 21 percent oxygen, with small amounts of argon, carbon dioxide and water vapor. It keeps the planet warm through the greenhouse effect, gives us air to breathe, and burns up most meteors before they reach the ground. The ozone layer within it blocks much of the Sun’s harmful ultraviolet light."]],
k:[["Distance from the Sun","About 150 million km (1 astronomical unit)"],["Diameter","About 12,756 km at the equator"],["Year","365.25 days"],["Day","About 24 hours"],["Age","About 4.54 billion years"],["Moons","1"]],
d:[["c. 4.54 billion years ago","Earth forms"],["c. 4.5 billion years ago","The Moon forms after a giant collision"],["c. 3.7 billion years ago","Earliest evidence of life"],["c. 2.4 billion years ago","Oxygen begins to build up in the atmosphere"],["240 BCE","Eratosthenes estimates Earth’s circumference"],["1968","Apollo 8 astronauts photograph Earthrise"]],
s2:[["The Goldilocks zone","Earth orbits in the Sun’s habitable zone, the region where a planet is neither too hot nor too cold for liquid water. Venus, closer in, is scorching under a thick carbon dioxide atmosphere, while Mars, farther out, is cold and dry. Earth’s size also matters: its gravity is strong enough to hold on to a thick atmosphere."],["Measuring Earth","Around 240 BCE, the Greek scholar Eratosthenes, working in Alexandria, measured the angle of the Sun’s shadow in two Egyptian cities and calculated Earth’s circumference. His answer was remarkably close to the true value of about 40,000 kilometers. Today satellites measure Earth’s shape to within centimeters."],["The magnetic shield","Earth’s magnetic field reaches tens of thousands of kilometers into space, forming a bubble called the magnetosphere. It deflects most of the solar wind, the stream of charged particles from the Sun. Without it, the solar wind could slowly strip away the atmosphere, as scientists think happened on Mars."],["Earth and the Moon","The Moon is unusually large compared with its planet. It steadies Earth’s tilt, which keeps the seasons fairly stable, and its gravity raises the ocean tides. The Moon is slowly moving away from Earth, about 3.8 centimeters a year, and Earth’s day is very gradually getting longer."],["Seen from space","Photographs of Earth from space changed how people see their planet. Earthrise, taken by the Apollo 8 crew in 1968, and The Blue Marble, taken by Apollo 17 in 1972, showed Earth as a small, fragile world. In 1990 the Voyager 1 probe photographed Earth from about 6 billion kilometers away as a tiny dot, the Pale Blue Dot."],["A changing planet","Earth is always changing. Plates of its crust move a few centimeters a year, building mountains and opening oceans. Over millions of years its climate has swung between ice ages and warm periods. Today, human activities, such as burning fossil fuels, are warming the climate faster than at any time in human history."]],
sp:["The Pale Blue Dot","On 14 February 1990, Voyager 1 turned its camera back toward home and photographed Earth from about 6 billion kilometers away. Earth appears as a tiny speck, less than a single pixel, in a ray of scattered sunlight. The astronomer Carl Sagan, who suggested the photograph, wrote that everyone who has ever lived, lived out their life on that mote of dust suspended in a sunbeam."],
g:[["Axis","An imaginary line through a planet’s poles around which it spins"],["Habitable zone","The region around a star where liquid water could exist on a planet"],["Magnetosphere","The region around a planet controlled by its magnetic field"],["Astronomical unit","The average distance from Earth to the Sun, about 150 million km"]],
f:["Earth is the densest planet in the solar system.","About 71 percent of Earth’s surface is covered by water.","The Moon is moving away from Earth by about 3.8 centimeters a year."],
fun:"Earth is the densest planet in the solar system.",
tl:"Ang Daigdig ang ikatlong planeta mula sa Araw at ang tanging lugar na alam nating may buhay. Mga 71 porsiyento ng ibabaw nito ay tubig, at pinoprotektahan ito ng atmospera at magnetic field."});

ADDART({id:"kuiper",t:"The Kuiper Belt and Oort Cloud",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🧊",w:"Kuiper belt",r:["pluto","comet","solar","uranep"],
p:[["Beyond Neptune","The Kuiper Belt is a vast ring of icy bodies beyond the orbit of Neptune."],["Home of dwarf planets","Pluto, Eris, Haumea and Makemake are among the dwarf planets found in this distant region."],["A distant shell","Much farther out, a huge shell of icy objects called the Oort Cloud may surround the whole solar system."]],
l:"The Kuiper Belt is a doughnut-shaped region of icy objects that orbits the Sun beyond Neptune, roughly 30 to 50 times farther from the Sun than Earth. It contains dwarf planets such as Pluto and Makemake and probably hundreds of thousands of objects more than 100 kilometers across. Far beyond it, the Oort Cloud is thought to be a giant spherical shell of trillions of icy bodies, stretching perhaps a quarter of the way to the nearest star. These regions are leftovers from the formation of the solar system and the source of many comets.",
s:[["Leftovers of creation","When the planets formed about 4.6 billion years ago, not all the material clumped into large worlds. Far from the Sun, where it was cold, countless small bodies of ice and rock were left behind. Gravity from the giant planets pushed many of them outward, forming the Kuiper Belt and the Oort Cloud."],["Discovering the belt","In the mid-1900s astronomers including Kenneth Edgeworth and Gerard Kuiper suggested that there might be many small bodies beyond Neptune. Pluto, discovered in 1930, was the first known. In 1992 David Jewitt and Jane Luu found a second, and since then astronomers have found thousands of Kuiper Belt objects."],["Dwarf planets","Several Kuiper Belt objects are large enough to be round. In 2005 the discovery of Eris, almost the same size as Pluto, led the International Astronomical Union in 2006 to create a new category, dwarf planets. Pluto, Eris, Haumea and Makemake are classed as dwarf planets, and others may join them."],["The Oort Cloud","The Oort Cloud is named after the Dutch astronomer Jan Oort, who proposed it in 1950 to explain where long-period comets come from. It may extend from about 2,000 to 100,000 or more astronomical units from the Sun. No object in the main Oort Cloud has ever been seen directly, because they are so small and far away."]],
k:[["Kuiper Belt distance","About 30–50 AU from the Sun"],["Oort Cloud distance","Perhaps 2,000–100,000 AU"],["Dwarf planets","Pluto, Eris, Haumea, Makemake"],["First object found","Pluto (1930)"],["Visited by","New Horizons spacecraft"]],
d:[["1930","Clyde Tombaugh discovers Pluto"],["1950","Jan Oort proposes the Oort Cloud"],["1992","First Kuiper Belt object after Pluto and Charon is found"],["2005","Eris is discovered"],["2015","New Horizons flies past Pluto"],["2019","New Horizons flies past Arrokoth"]],
s2:[["New Horizons","NASA’s New Horizons spacecraft, launched in 2006, flew past Pluto in July 2015, revealing mountains of water ice, a heart-shaped plain of frozen nitrogen and a hazy atmosphere. On 1 January 2019, it flew past Arrokoth, a small object shaped like a snowman, the most distant object ever explored up close."],["Short and long comets","Comets that return every 200 years or less, such as Halley’s Comet, mostly come from the Kuiper Belt and a related region called the scattered disk. Comets with very long orbits, taking thousands or millions of years, are thought to fall in from the Oort Cloud when passing stars or the Milky Way’s gravity nudge them toward the Sun."],["Pluto’s neighbors","Pluto has five known moons; the largest, Charon, is half its size, so the two orbit a point in space between them. Haumea spins so fast that it is stretched into an egg shape and has rings. Makemake has a small dark moon, and Eris has a moon named Dysnomia."],["Is there a Planet Nine?","Some astronomers have noticed that several distant objects have oddly lined-up orbits. They suggest that an undiscovered planet, perhaps several times Earth’s mass, might be shaping them from far beyond the Kuiper Belt. Others think the pattern is due to chance or to how telescopes search the sky. No such planet has been found."],["Frozen time capsules","Because Kuiper Belt and Oort Cloud objects have stayed cold and far from the Sun since the solar system formed, they preserve its original ingredients. Studying them, and the comets that come from them, helps scientists understand how planets formed and where Earth’s water may have come from."],["How far is it?","The Oort Cloud is so distant that light from the Sun takes more than a year to reach its outer parts. The Voyager probes, the fastest objects leaving the solar system, will take around 300 years to reach its inner edge and perhaps 30,000 years to pass through it."]],
sp:["Arrokoth","Arrokoth, about 36 kilometers long, is made of two flattened lobes gently stuck together, like a snowman. It probably formed when two smaller bodies slowly drifted together soon after the solar system was born. Its name comes from the Powhatan and Algonquian languages and means sky. It is the most distant and most primitive object ever visited by a spacecraft."],
g:[["Dwarf planet","A round body orbiting the Sun that has not cleared its orbit of other objects"],["Comet","An icy body that grows a glowing tail when it nears the Sun"],["Astronomical unit (AU)","The average distance from Earth to the Sun"],["Long-period comet","A comet that takes more than 200 years to orbit the Sun"]],
f:["Pluto was the first Kuiper Belt object discovered.","No Oort Cloud object has ever been seen directly.","Arrokoth is the most distant object explored up close by a spacecraft."],
fun:"The Voyager probes will take about 300 years just to reach the inner edge of the Oort Cloud.",
tl:"Ang Kuiper Belt ay malawak na singsing ng mga nagyeyelong bagay lampas sa Neptune, kung saan naroon si Pluto. Mas malayo pa ang Oort Cloud, na pinagmumulan ng maraming kometa."});

ADDART({id:"spaceweather",t:"Space Weather and Auroras",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🌌",w:"Space weather",r:["sun","earthplanet","magnet","sat","aurora"],
p:[["Storms from the Sun","Space weather is the changing conditions in space caused by the Sun, such as solar flares and blasts of charged particles."],["Lights in the sky","When charged particles reach Earth, they can make the sky glow with auroras near the poles."],["Effects on technology","Strong solar storms can disturb satellites, radio, GPS and power grids."]],
l:"Space weather describes the conditions in space around Earth, driven mainly by the Sun. The Sun constantly sends out a stream of charged particles called the solar wind, and sometimes erupts with solar flares and huge clouds of magnetized gas called coronal mass ejections. When these reach Earth, they can trigger geomagnetic storms, produce beautiful auroras and disrupt satellites, communications and electrical power systems. Space agencies monitor the Sun to forecast space weather, much as meteorologists forecast weather on Earth.",
s:[["The solar wind","The Sun’s outer atmosphere, the corona, is so hot, over a million degrees Celsius, that particles escape into space. This flow, the solar wind, streams past Earth at around 400 kilometers per second. Earth’s magnetic field deflects most of it, but some particles are channeled toward the poles."],["Flares and eruptions","Sunspots are cooler, darker regions where the Sun’s magnetic field is tangled. Here, sudden releases of energy cause solar flares, bursts of light and radiation. Coronal mass ejections hurl billions of tonnes of charged gas into space and can reach Earth in one to three days."],["Auroras","Auroras, called the northern lights or aurora borealis in the north and the southern lights or aurora australis in the south, happen when charged particles strike gases high in the atmosphere, making them glow. Oxygen gives green and red light, and nitrogen gives blue and purple. Auroras usually appear in rings around the magnetic poles."],["The solar cycle","The Sun’s activity rises and falls over a cycle of about 11 years. At solar maximum there are many sunspots, flares and eruptions; at solar minimum, few. The current cycle, Solar Cycle 25, reached its maximum around 2024 and 2025, bringing auroras seen much farther from the poles than usual."]],
k:[["Main source","The Sun"],["Solar wind speed","Around 400 km/s, faster in storms"],["Solar cycle","About 11 years"],["Aurora colors","Green and red (oxygen), blue and purple (nitrogen)"],["Strongest recorded storm","The Carrington Event, 1859"]],
d:[["1610s","Galileo and others observe sunspots with telescopes"],["1859","The Carrington Event, the largest known solar storm"],["1989","Solar storm causes a blackout in Quebec, Canada"],["1995","SOHO solar observatory is launched"],["2018","Parker Solar Probe is launched"],["May 2024","Strongest geomagnetic storm in two decades brings auroras to low latitudes"]],
s2:[["The Carrington Event","On 1 September 1859, the astronomer Richard Carrington saw a brilliant flare on the Sun. Within a day, a huge geomagnetic storm hit Earth. Auroras were seen as far south as the Caribbean, and telegraph systems sparked and failed. A storm that strong today could damage satellites and power grids worldwide."],["Blackouts and satellites","In March 1989 a geomagnetic storm caused electrical currents in the ground that overloaded the power grid in Quebec, Canada, leaving millions of people without electricity for hours. Space weather can also damage satellite electronics, increase drag on low satellites, disrupt GPS and radio signals and expose airline crews and astronauts to more radiation."],["Watching the Sun","Spacecraft such as SOHO, the Solar Dynamics Observatory and the Parker Solar Probe, which flies closer to the Sun than any spacecraft before, study the Sun constantly. Satellites between Earth and the Sun measure the solar wind before it arrives, giving warnings of up to about an hour."],["Forecasts","Space weather centers, such as the US Space Weather Prediction Center and similar services in Europe, Asia and elsewhere, issue alerts and forecasts. Power companies, airlines and satellite operators use them to protect equipment, for example by changing flight routes over the poles during storms."],["The May 2024 storm","In May 2024, a series of coronal mass ejections caused the strongest geomagnetic storm in more than 20 years. Auroras were photographed from places where they are almost never seen, including parts of Mexico, northern India and southern Europe. Phone cameras often captured the colors better than the human eye."],["Auroras on other worlds","Auroras also occur on other planets with atmospheres and magnetic fields. Jupiter has the most powerful auroras in the solar system, bigger than Earth itself, and Saturn, Uranus and Neptune have them too. Telescopes have even seen auroral glows on Mars, which has only patches of magnetism in its crust."]],
sp:["Where to see the northern lights","The best places to see auroras are under the auroral ovals, which circle the magnetic poles. In the north, these include northern Norway, Sweden, Finland, Iceland, Greenland, Alaska and northern Canada; in the south, Antarctica, and sometimes southern New Zealand and Tasmania. Auroras are best seen on clear, dark winter nights far from city lights."],
g:[["Solar wind","A stream of charged particles flowing from the Sun"],["Coronal mass ejection","A huge burst of magnetized gas from the Sun’s corona"],["Geomagnetic storm","A disturbance of Earth’s magnetic field caused by the Sun"],["Aurora","Natural colored light in the sky near the poles"]],
f:["The northern lights are called the aurora borealis.","The Sun’s activity follows a cycle of about 11 years.","Jupiter has the strongest auroras in the solar system."],
fun:"Jupiter’s auroras are bigger than the whole Earth.",
tl:"Ang space weather ay mga pagbabago sa kalawakan na dulot ng Araw, gaya ng solar flare. Kapag umabot sa Daigdig ang mga partikulong ito, maaaring lumitaw ang aurora malapit sa mga polo at maapektuhan ang mga satellite at kuryente."});

;
/* data/space2-2.js */
// Space articles, part 2.2: constellations, nebulae, supernovae and galaxies.
ADDART({id:"constellations",t:"Constellations and the Night Sky",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"✨",w:"Constellation",r:["stars","milky","gmyth","explor"],
p:[["Pictures in the stars","Constellations are patterns of stars that people have named and used to tell stories for thousands of years."],["88 official areas","Astronomers divide the whole sky into 88 official constellations."],["Finding the way","Sailors and travelers have used stars and constellations to find directions and tell the seasons."]],
l:"A constellation is a group of stars that forms a recognizable pattern in the night sky, such as Orion the hunter or the Southern Cross. Cultures around the world have seen different pictures in the stars and linked them to myths, farming seasons and navigation. Since 1922, the International Astronomical Union has recognized 88 official constellations, which together cover the entire sky like the pieces of a map. The stars in a constellation usually only look close together; they may lie at very different distances from Earth.",
s:[["Patterns, not groups","From Earth, stars seem to lie on a great dome, but in reality they are spread through space at vastly different distances. The stars of Orion’s Belt, for example, are hundreds of light-years apart. Constellations are like drawings made by connecting dots that happen to line up from our point of view."],["Ancient sky maps","Babylonian astronomers named many constellations over 3,000 years ago, and Greek writers such as Ptolemy, in the 2nd century CE, listed 48 of them. Chinese astronomers divided the sky into 28 lunar mansions and hundreds of small star groups, and many other cultures created their own sky maps."],["The 88 constellations","As European explorers sailed south from the 1500s, they mapped southern stars never seen from Europe and added new constellations, such as Crux, the Southern Cross. In 1922 the International Astronomical Union fixed the list at 88, and in 1930 drew official boundaries, so that every point in the sky belongs to one constellation."],["The zodiac","As Earth orbits the Sun, the Sun appears to move against the background stars through a band of constellations called the zodiac, such as Taurus, Leo and Scorpius. Astronomy, the science of the universe, is different from astrology, the belief that the positions of stars and planets influence people’s lives, which is not supported by scientific evidence."]],
k:[["Official constellations","88"],["Largest","Hydra"],["Smallest","Crux (the Southern Cross)"],["Brightest star","Sirius, in Canis Major"],["Fixed by","International Astronomical Union, 1922 and 1930"]],
d:[["c. 1000 BCE","Babylonian star catalogs name many constellations"],["c. 150 CE","Ptolemy lists 48 constellations"],["964","Al-Sufi publishes the Book of Fixed Stars"],["1500s–1700s","European navigators chart southern constellations"],["1922","IAU adopts 88 official constellations"],["1930","Official constellation boundaries are set"]],
s2:[["Stars of many cultures","The Pleiades star cluster is called Matariki by the Māori of New Zealand, whose New Year begins when it rises before dawn in midwinter. It is Subaru in Japan, Krittika in India and the Seven Sisters in Greek myth. Aboriginal Australians see an emu in the sky, formed by the dark dust lanes of the Milky Way."],["Navigating by the stars","In the Northern Hemisphere, Polaris, the North Star, sits almost directly above the North Pole, so it shows which way is north. In the south, sailors use the Southern Cross to find south. Polynesian navigators crossed thousands of kilometers of ocean by memorizing where stars rise and set on the horizon."],["Star names","Many bright stars have names from Arabic, given by astronomers of the Islamic world, such as Aldebaran, Betelgeuse, Rigel and Vega. In his Book of Fixed Stars of 964, the Persian astronomer Abd al-Rahman al-Sufi described the constellations and recorded the earliest known observation of the Andromeda Galaxy, as a small cloud."],["Orion","Orion, the hunter, is one of the easiest constellations to recognize, seen from almost everywhere on Earth. Its belt of three stars points to Sirius, the brightest star in the night sky. The red giant Betelgeuse marks his shoulder, and below the belt glows the Orion Nebula, a nursery where new stars are forming."],["Light pollution","Artificial light from cities makes the sky glow, hiding all but the brightest stars. Studies suggest that more than a third of humanity can no longer see the Milky Way from home. Dark-sky parks and reserves around the world protect places where people can still see thousands of stars."],["Asterisms","Some famous star patterns are not official constellations but parts of them, called asterisms. The Big Dipper, or Plough, is part of Ursa Major, the Great Bear, and its two end stars point to Polaris. The Summer Triangle of Vega, Deneb and Altair spans three constellations."]],
sp:["Matariki","Matariki, the Māori name for the Pleiades star cluster, marks the Māori New Year in midwinter, when the cluster reappears in the dawn sky in June or July. It is a time for remembering those who have died, celebrating the present and planning for the year ahead. Since 2022 Matariki has been a public holiday in New Zealand, the first official holiday based on Indigenous knowledge of the stars."],
g:[["Constellation","A named pattern or area of stars in the sky"],["Asterism","A star pattern that is not an official constellation"],["Zodiac","The band of constellations the Sun appears to pass through in a year"],["Light-year","The distance light travels in one year, about 9.46 trillion km"]],
f:["There are 88 official constellations.","Hydra is the largest constellation and Crux the smallest.","Many star names come from Arabic."],
fun:"The smallest constellation, Crux (the Southern Cross), appears on the flags of Australia, New Zealand, Brazil, Papua New Guinea and Samoa.",
tl:"Ang mga konstelasyon ay mga hugis o pangkat ng mga bituin na binigyan ng pangalan, gaya ng Orion at Southern Cross. May 88 opisyal na konstelasyon, at ginamit ang mga ito ng mga mandaragat upang malaman ang direksiyon."});

ADDART({id:"nebula",t:"Nebulae and the Birth of Stars",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"☁️",w:"Nebula",r:["stars","supernova","milky","jwst"],
p:[["Clouds in space","A nebula is a giant cloud of gas and dust in space."],["Star nurseries","New stars and planets are born when parts of these clouds collapse under gravity."],["Glowing colors","Some nebulae glow with light from hot young stars, painting space in red, green and blue."]],
l:"A nebula is an enormous cloud of gas, mostly hydrogen and helium, and dust floating in space. Some nebulae are regions where new stars are being born; others are the glowing remains of dying stars. Nebulae can be hundreds of light-years across. Telescopes such as Hubble and the James Webb Space Telescope have photographed them in stunning detail, revealing pillars, bubbles and shells of glowing gas.",
s:[["How stars are born","Inside cold, dense clouds of gas and dust, gravity pulls clumps of material together. As a clump collapses, it spins faster and heats up, forming a protostar surrounded by a flat disk. When its core becomes hot enough, around 10 million degrees Celsius, hydrogen begins to fuse into helium, and a new star shines. Leftover material in the disk can form planets."],["Kinds of nebulae","Emission nebulae glow because nearby hot stars energize their gas, often making it shine red from hydrogen. Reflection nebulae shine blue by reflecting starlight off dust. Dark nebulae are dense clouds of dust that block the light behind them, appearing as dark shapes against brighter regions."],["Nebulae from dying stars","When a star like the Sun runs out of fuel, it puffs off its outer layers, forming a glowing shell called a planetary nebula, despite having nothing to do with planets. The name came from early astronomers whose telescopes showed these round shapes looking like planets. Much more massive stars explode as supernovae, leaving expanding clouds of debris."],["The Orion Nebula","The Orion Nebula, about 1,350 light-years away, is the closest large region of star formation to Earth. It can be seen with the naked eye as a fuzzy patch in Orion’s sword. Inside, telescopes have found hundreds of young stars, many surrounded by disks where planets may be forming."]],
k:[["Made of","Mostly hydrogen and helium gas, with dust"],["Size","Up to hundreds of light-years across"],["Nearest large star nursery","Orion Nebula, about 1,350 light-years away"],["Kinds","Emission, reflection, dark, planetary, supernova remnant"],["Famous image","Pillars of Creation in the Eagle Nebula"]],
d:[["964","Al-Sufi records the Andromeda Galaxy as a small cloud"],["1610","Nicolas-Claude Fabri de Peiresc discovers the Orion Nebula with a telescope"],["1774","Charles Messier begins publishing his catalog of nebulae"],["1864","William Huggins shows that some nebulae are made of glowing gas"],["1995","Hubble photographs the Pillars of Creation"],["2022","Webb photographs the Carina Nebula and the Pillars again"]],
s2:[["The Pillars of Creation","In 1995 the Hubble Space Telescope photographed three towering columns of gas and dust in the Eagle Nebula, about 6,500 light-years away. Called the Pillars of Creation, they are several light-years tall, and new stars are forming inside them. In 2022 the James Webb Space Telescope photographed them again in infrared light, revealing young stars hidden in the dust."],["Seeing through dust","Dust blocks visible light, hiding newborn stars. Infrared light, however, passes through dust more easily. That is why infrared telescopes, such as Spitzer and Webb, can peer inside star-forming clouds and see stars and planetary disks as they form."],["The Messier catalog","In the 1700s the French astronomer Charles Messier, who hunted comets, made a list of fuzzy objects that could be confused with comets. His catalog of 110 objects includes many famous nebulae, star clusters and galaxies: M1 is the Crab Nebula, M42 the Orion Nebula and M31 the Andromeda Galaxy."],["The Crab Nebula","The Crab Nebula is the remains of a star that exploded in 1054. Astronomers in China and elsewhere recorded a new star bright enough to see in daylight for weeks. At its center spins a pulsar, a neutron star rotating about 30 times a second."],["Stars are born in groups","Stars usually form in clusters from the same cloud. Over time the clusters drift apart. The Sun was probably born in such a cluster about 4.6 billion years ago, and its siblings are now scattered across the galaxy. The Pleiades is a young cluster about 100 million years old, still with some leftover dust."],["Recycling the universe","Stars make heavier elements, such as carbon, oxygen and iron, in their cores and in explosions. When they die, they return these elements to the clouds between stars, where new stars and planets form. The atoms in our bodies were made in earlier generations of stars."]],
sp:["We are made of star stuff","Almost all of the hydrogen in the universe was made in the Big Bang, but most heavier elements were made in stars. The calcium in our bones, the iron in our blood and the oxygen we breathe were forged in stars that lived and died before the Sun was born. As the astronomer Carl Sagan put it, we are made of star stuff."],
g:[["Nebula","A cloud of gas and dust in space (plural: nebulae)"],["Protostar","A young star that is still forming"],["Planetary nebula","A glowing shell of gas thrown off by a dying Sun-like star"],["Infrared","Light with longer wavelengths than red light, invisible to our eyes"]],
f:["The Orion Nebula can be seen with the naked eye.","Planetary nebulae have nothing to do with planets.","The Crab Nebula comes from a star that exploded in 1054."],
fun:"The iron in your blood was made inside stars that died before the Sun was born.",
tl:"Ang nebula ay malaking ulap ng gas at alikabok sa kalawakan. Dito isinisilang ang mga bagong bituin at planeta. May mga nebula ring nabubuo mula sa mga namamatay na bituin."});

ADDART({id:"supernova",t:"Supernovae",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"💥",w:"Supernova",r:["stars","bh","pulsar","nebula"],
p:[["Exploding stars","A supernova is the explosion of a star, one of the most powerful events in the universe."],["Brighter than a galaxy","For a few weeks, a single supernova can outshine an entire galaxy of billions of stars."],["Makers of elements","Supernovae scatter heavy elements into space, which later become part of new stars, planets and living things."]],
l:"A supernova is a gigantic explosion that occurs at the end of some stars’ lives. It can release as much energy in a few weeks as the Sun will give off in its entire lifetime of about 10 billion years. Supernovae happen in two main ways: when a massive star’s core collapses, or when a white dwarf star gains too much material and blows apart. They create and spread heavy elements, trigger new star formation, and can leave behind neutron stars or black holes.",
s:[["Massive stars collapse","Stars more than about eight times the Sun’s mass fuse heavier and heavier elements in their cores, until they build up iron. Iron cannot release energy by fusion, so the core suddenly collapses under its own gravity in less than a second. The outer layers rebound in a huge explosion, leaving a neutron star or a black hole."],["White dwarfs explode","A white dwarf is the dense, Earth-sized remnant of a Sun-like star. If it orbits a companion star and pulls gas from it, or merges with another white dwarf, it can reach a critical mass and explode completely. These Type Ia supernovae shine with a similar peak brightness, making them useful for measuring distances in space."],["Historic supernovae","Astronomers have recorded supernovae in our galaxy for about 2,000 years. Chinese records describe a guest star in 185 CE. The supernova of 1006 was the brightest ever recorded, and the 1054 explosion created the Crab Nebula. Tycho Brahe observed one in 1572 and Johannes Kepler in 1604, the last seen in the Milky Way with the naked eye."],["SN 1987A","In February 1987 a star exploded in the Large Magellanic Cloud, a small galaxy near the Milky Way. SN 1987A was the closest supernova seen in nearly 400 years and was visible to the naked eye from the Southern Hemisphere. Detectors in Japan and the United States caught a burst of neutrinos from it, confirming theories of how stars collapse."]],
k:[["Energy","Can outshine a whole galaxy for weeks"],["Main types","Core-collapse and white dwarf (Type Ia)"],["Last naked-eye supernova in the Milky Way","1604 (Kepler’s Supernova)"],["Closest recent","SN 1987A, Large Magellanic Cloud"],["Leaves behind","Neutron star, black hole, or nothing"]],
d:[["185 CE","Chinese astronomers record a guest star"],["1006","Brightest supernova ever recorded"],["1054","Supernova that created the Crab Nebula"],["1572","Tycho Brahe observes a supernova"],["1604","Kepler observes the last naked-eye supernova in the Milky Way"],["1987","SN 1987A seen in the Large Magellanic Cloud"]],
s2:[["Measuring the universe","Because Type Ia supernovae reach a similar peak brightness, astronomers use them as standard candles: comparing how bright they look with how bright they really are reveals their distance. In 1998 two teams used them to discover that the expansion of the universe is speeding up, work that won the 2011 Nobel Prize in Physics."],["Forging elements","Elements heavier than iron, such as some gold, silver and uranium, are made in supernovae and in collisions of neutron stars. The explosions blast these elements into space, enriching clouds of gas. The Sun and Earth formed from such enriched material."],["Shock waves and new stars","The shock waves from supernovae race outward through space, compressing nearby clouds of gas and dust. This can trigger the birth of new stars. Some scientists think a nearby supernova may have helped spark the formation of the solar system."],["Is Earth in danger?","A supernova within about 30 to 50 light-years could damage Earth’s ozone layer, but no star that close is expected to explode for millions of years. Betelgeuse, a red supergiant in Orion about 550 light-years away, will explode someday, perhaps within the next 100,000 years. It would be bright enough to see in daylight, but too far away to harm us."],["Finding supernovae","Supernovae in our own galaxy are rare, perhaps a couple per century, and many are hidden by dust. But astronomers find thousands each year in other galaxies using automated sky surveys. The Vera C. Rubin Observatory in Chile, which began operating in 2025, is expected to find millions over its ten-year survey of the sky."],["Hypernovae and gamma rays","The most powerful explosions, sometimes called hypernovae, come from very massive, fast-spinning stars. They can produce gamma-ray bursts, narrow beams of intense radiation that are the brightest explosions known, detectable across billions of light-years."]],
sp:["The guest star of 1054","On 4 July 1054, Chinese astronomers recorded a guest star in the constellation Taurus, bright enough to be seen in daylight for 23 days and at night for nearly two years. Today, at that spot, telescopes see the Crab Nebula, an expanding cloud of debris about 11 light-years across, with a rapidly spinning neutron star at its heart."],
g:[["Supernova","The explosion of a star"],["White dwarf","The small, dense remains of a Sun-like star"],["Neutron star","The extremely dense collapsed core of a massive star"],["Standard candle","An object of known brightness used to measure distances"]],
f:["A supernova can outshine a whole galaxy.","The last naked-eye supernova in the Milky Way was seen in 1604.","Some gold on Earth was made in exploding stars and colliding neutron stars."],
fun:"The supernova of 1006 was bright enough to cast shadows at night.",
tl:"Ang supernova ay pagsabog ng isang bituin, isa sa pinakamalakas na pangyayari sa sansinukob. Maaari nitong lampasan ang liwanag ng buong galaxy, at nagkakalat ito ng mabibigat na elemento sa kalawakan."});

ADDART({id:"galaxies",t:"Galaxies",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🌀",w:"Galaxy",r:["milky","stars","bh","darkmatter","hubble"],
p:[["Cities of stars","A galaxy is an enormous collection of stars, gas, dust and dark matter held together by gravity."],["Billions of galaxies","The observable universe contains hundreds of billions, perhaps trillions, of galaxies."],["Our neighbor","The Andromeda Galaxy, about 2.5 million light-years away, is the nearest large galaxy to the Milky Way."]],
l:"A galaxy is a vast system of stars, gas, dust and dark matter bound together by gravity. Galaxies range from dwarfs with a few million stars to giants with over a trillion. Our own galaxy, the Milky Way, contains perhaps 100 to 400 billion stars. Until the 1920s, many astronomers thought the Milky Way was the whole universe; Edwin Hubble showed that it is just one of countless galaxies scattered across space.",
s:[["Spirals, ellipticals and irregulars","Edwin Hubble sorted galaxies by their shapes. Spiral galaxies, such as the Milky Way and Andromeda, have flat, rotating disks with curving arms of young stars and a central bulge. Elliptical galaxies are smooth, rounded collections of mostly old stars. Irregular galaxies have no clear shape."],["Island universes","In the 1700s, some thinkers, including the philosopher Immanuel Kant, suggested that fuzzy spiral nebulae might be distant island universes. In 1923–1924, Edwin Hubble used the Mount Wilson telescope to measure stars in the Andromeda nebula and showed it was far beyond the Milky Way, proving that other galaxies exist."],["Black holes at the center","Most large galaxies have a supermassive black hole at their center, millions or billions of times the Sun’s mass. The Milky Way’s, called Sagittarius A*, has about 4 million solar masses. In some galaxies, matter falling into the central black hole shines brilliantly as a quasar, outshining the whole galaxy."],["Groups and clusters","Galaxies gather in groups and clusters. The Milky Way, Andromeda and more than 50 smaller galaxies form the Local Group. Clusters can contain thousands of galaxies, and clusters link into superclusters and great filaments, forming a cosmic web with huge empty voids between them."]],
k:[["Galaxies in the observable universe","Hundreds of billions to trillions"],["Nearest large galaxy","Andromeda, about 2.5 million light-years"],["Main types","Spiral, elliptical, irregular"],["Our group","The Local Group, more than 50 galaxies"],["Most distant known","Seen as they were less than 300 million years after the Big Bang"]],
d:[["964","Al-Sufi records the Andromeda Galaxy"],["1755","Kant proposes island universes"],["1924","Hubble proves Andromeda is a separate galaxy"],["1929","Hubble shows galaxies are moving apart"],["1995","Hubble Deep Field image reveals thousands of galaxies"],["2022–2024","Webb finds galaxies from the early universe"]],
s2:[["The Hubble Deep Field","In 1995 astronomers pointed the Hubble Space Telescope at a tiny, seemingly empty patch of sky for about ten days. The resulting image, the Hubble Deep Field, revealed around 3,000 galaxies, some seen as they were billions of years ago. Later deep fields by Hubble and Webb have revealed many thousands more."],["Andromeda is coming","The Andromeda Galaxy is moving toward the Milky Way at about 110 kilometers per second. In roughly 4 to 5 billion years, the two galaxies may collide and merge into a giant elliptical galaxy. Because stars are so far apart, they would almost never crash into each other."],["Colliding galaxies","Galaxies often collide and merge. Gravity pulls them into strange shapes, with long tails of stars, and squeezes their gas, sparking bursts of star formation. Large galaxies such as the Milky Way grew partly by swallowing smaller ones; streams of stars from these eaten galaxies can still be traced."],["Looking back in time","Because light takes time to travel, we see distant galaxies as they were long ago. The James Webb Space Telescope has observed galaxies whose light left them less than 300 million years after the Big Bang, about 13.5 billion years ago, helping astronomers learn how the first galaxies formed."],["The Magellanic Clouds","The Large and Small Magellanic Clouds are two dwarf galaxies orbiting the Milky Way, visible to the naked eye from the Southern Hemisphere as faint patches of light. They are named after Ferdinand Magellan, whose crew described them during their voyage around the world, though peoples of the Southern Hemisphere had known them for thousands of years."],["Dark matter holds them together","Galaxies spin so fast that the gravity of their visible stars and gas could not hold them together. Astronomers think each galaxy sits within a much larger halo of invisible dark matter, whose gravity keeps it from flying apart. Studying galaxy rotation was key to discovering dark matter."]],
sp:["How many galaxies?","Counting galaxies in deep images and allowing for those too faint to see, astronomers estimate that the observable universe contains hundreds of billions to perhaps two trillion galaxies. If each has on average tens to hundreds of billions of stars, the universe may hold more stars than there are grains of sand on all of Earth’s beaches."],
g:[["Galaxy","A huge system of stars, gas, dust and dark matter held together by gravity"],["Spiral galaxy","A galaxy with a flat disk and curving arms"],["Quasar","An extremely bright galaxy center powered by a supermassive black hole"],["Local Group","The group of galaxies that includes the Milky Way and Andromeda"]],
f:["The Andromeda Galaxy can be seen with the naked eye on dark nights.","Most large galaxies have a supermassive black hole at their center.","The Milky Way and Andromeda may merge in about 4 to 5 billion years."],
fun:"The Andromeda Galaxy is the most distant object most people can see with the naked eye.",
tl:"Ang galaxy ay napakalaking kumpol ng mga bituin, gas, alikabok at dark matter na pinagbubuklod ng grabidad. Bilyon-bilyon ang galaxy sa sansinukob, at ang Andromeda ang pinakamalapit na malaking kapitbahay ng Milky Way."});

;
/* data/space2-3.js */
// Space articles, part 2.3: dark matter and dark energy, the cosmic microwave background, and gravitational waves.
ADDART({id:"darkmatter",t:"Dark Matter and Dark Energy",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🕳️",w:"Dark matter",r:["galaxies","bigbang","grav","relat"],
p:[["The invisible universe","Ordinary matter, everything made of atoms, makes up only about 5 percent of the universe."],["Dark matter","About 27 percent is dark matter, an invisible substance known only from its gravity."],["Dark energy","About 68 percent is dark energy, a mysterious effect that is making the universe expand faster and faster."]],
l:"Dark matter and dark energy are two of the biggest mysteries in science. Dark matter is an unseen form of matter that does not give off, absorb or reflect light, but whose gravity holds galaxies and galaxy clusters together. Dark energy is the name given to whatever is causing the expansion of the universe to speed up. Together they appear to make up about 95 percent of the universe, yet no one knows exactly what either of them is.",
s:[["Clues from galaxies","In the 1930s the Swiss astronomer Fritz Zwicky found that galaxies in the Coma Cluster moved so fast that the cluster should fly apart unless it contained far more mass than could be seen. In the 1970s Vera Rubin and Kent Ford showed that stars at the edges of spiral galaxies orbit as fast as those near the center, another sign of hidden mass."],["What could dark matter be?","Dark matter is not ordinary dark objects such as dust or dead stars; measurements show there is not enough ordinary matter to explain it. Most physicists think it is a new kind of particle that barely interacts with ordinary matter. Experiments deep underground, in space and at particle colliders are searching for it, so far without success."],["Bending light","Gravity bends light, an effect predicted by Einstein. Massive galaxy clusters act like giant lenses, distorting the images of galaxies behind them. By mapping this gravitational lensing, astronomers can see where mass is, including dark matter. The Bullet Cluster, two colliding clusters, shows the dark matter separated from the hot gas, strong evidence that it is real."],["The accelerating universe","Since the Big Bang, the universe has been expanding. In 1998 two teams studying distant exploding stars discovered that the expansion is speeding up rather than slowing down. Saul Perlmutter, Brian Schmidt and Adam Riess won the 2011 Nobel Prize in Physics for the discovery. The cause was named dark energy."]],
k:[["Ordinary matter","About 5% of the universe"],["Dark matter","About 27%"],["Dark energy","About 68%"],["Dark matter first proposed","1933, Fritz Zwicky"],["Accelerating expansion discovered","1998"]],
d:[["1933","Zwicky finds hidden mass in the Coma Cluster"],["1970s","Vera Rubin and Kent Ford measure galaxy rotation"],["1998","Expansion of the universe found to be accelerating"],["2006","Bullet Cluster provides evidence for dark matter"],["2011","Nobel Prize for the discovery of accelerating expansion"],["2023","Euclid space telescope launched to map dark matter and dark energy"]],
s2:[["Vera Rubin","Vera Rubin, an American astronomer, measured how fast stars orbit in dozens of spiral galaxies and found that the outer stars move far too fast to be held by visible matter alone. Her work convinced many scientists that dark matter exists. The Vera C. Rubin Observatory in Chile, which began operating in 2025, is named in her honor."],["The cosmic web","Computer simulations show that, after the Big Bang, dark matter clumped together first, forming a vast web of filaments. Ordinary gas then fell into these filaments, and galaxies formed where they crossed. Maps of millions of galaxies match this picture closely."],["What is dark energy?","One idea is that dark energy is a property of empty space itself, which Einstein included in his equations as the cosmological constant. Others suggest it could change over time. Results from the Dark Energy Spectroscopic Instrument in 2024 and 2025 hinted that dark energy might be weakening, but more data are needed to be sure."],["Other explanations","A minority of scientists think the evidence for dark matter might instead mean that gravity behaves differently on very large scales than our current theories say. Ideas such as Modified Newtonian Dynamics explain some galaxy motions but struggle to explain observations of galaxy clusters and the early universe."],["Space telescopes","The European Space Agency’s Euclid telescope, launched in 2023, and other new survey telescopes are mapping billions of galaxies to measure how dark matter is distributed and how dark energy has affected the expansion of the universe over time."],["The fate of the universe","If dark energy keeps pushing the universe apart, galaxies will drift farther and farther away from one another. In the far future, astronomers in the Milky Way might see no other galaxies at all beyond their local group, as the rest would have moved out of sight."]],
sp:["A universe we cannot see","Everything we have ever seen with telescopes, all the stars, planets, gas and galaxies, makes up only about one-twentieth of the universe. The rest is dark matter and dark energy, which we know about only because of their effects on what we can see. Understanding them may require new laws of physics."],
g:[["Dark matter","Invisible matter detected only by its gravity"],["Dark energy","The unknown cause of the speeding-up expansion of the universe"],["Gravitational lensing","The bending of light by the gravity of massive objects"],["Cosmological constant","A term in Einstein’s equations representing the energy of empty space"]],
f:["Ordinary matter makes up only about 5 percent of the universe.","Vera Rubin’s measurements of galaxy rotation supported dark matter.","The universe’s expansion is speeding up."],
fun:"About 95 percent of the universe is made of something no one has ever seen directly.",
tl:"Ang dark matter at dark energy ay malalaking misteryo sa agham. Hindi ito nakikita, ngunit tinatayang bumubuo ng mga 95 porsiyento ng sansinukob. Pinagbubuklod ng dark matter ang mga galaxy, habang pinabibilis ng dark energy ang paglawak ng sansinukob."});

ADDART({id:"cmb",t:"The Cosmic Microwave Background",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"📡",w:"Cosmic microwave background",r:["bigbang","darkmatter","galaxies","light"],
p:[["The oldest light","The cosmic microwave background is light left over from the early universe, about 380,000 years after the Big Bang."],["Everywhere in the sky","It comes from every direction and fills the whole universe."],["A baby picture","Maps of it show tiny ripples that grew into galaxies and clusters of galaxies."]],
l:"The cosmic microwave background, or CMB, is faint radiation that fills the entire universe and is the oldest light we can see. It was released about 380,000 years after the Big Bang, when the universe had cooled enough for atoms to form and light to travel freely. Stretched by the expansion of space over 13.8 billion years, that light now arrives as microwaves, with a temperature just 2.7 degrees above absolute zero. Its discovery in 1965 provided strong evidence for the Big Bang.",
s:[["When light set free","In the early universe, matter was a hot, dense fog of charged particles that scattered light, like fog scatters car headlights. About 380,000 years after the Big Bang, the temperature dropped to around 3,000 degrees, electrons joined nuclei to form atoms, and the fog cleared. Light that was released then has been traveling ever since."],["An accidental discovery","In 1964 and 1965, Arno Penzias and Robert Wilson, working with a large horn-shaped antenna at Bell Labs in New Jersey, found a faint hiss of microwave noise coming from every direction. After ruling out pigeons nesting in the antenna, they realized it came from space. Physicists at nearby Princeton recognized it as the afterglow of the Big Bang, and Penzias and Wilson won the 1978 Nobel Prize in Physics."],["Cooled by expansion","When it was released, the CMB glowed like hot metal. As the universe expanded, the light was stretched to longer and longer wavelengths. Today its temperature is about 2.725 kelvin, or minus 270.4 degrees Celsius, and it peaks in the microwave part of the spectrum."],["Ripples in the glow","The CMB is almost exactly the same temperature everywhere, but satellite maps show tiny variations of about 1 part in 100,000. These ripples mark slightly denser regions of the early universe, which gravity later pulled together to form galaxies and clusters. Their pattern reveals the age, shape and ingredients of the universe."]],
k:[["Released","About 380,000 years after the Big Bang"],["Temperature today","About 2.725 K (−270.4 °C)"],["Discovered","1965 by Arno Penzias and Robert Wilson"],["Variations","About 1 part in 100,000"],["Mapped by","COBE, WMAP and Planck satellites"]],
d:[["1948","Ralph Alpher and Robert Herman predict leftover radiation from the Big Bang"],["1965","Penzias and Wilson detect the CMB"],["1989","COBE satellite launched"],["2001","WMAP satellite launched"],["2009","Planck satellite launched"],["2013","Planck releases detailed maps of the CMB"]],
s2:[["COBE","NASA’s Cosmic Background Explorer, launched in 1989, showed that the CMB has exactly the spectrum expected from the Big Bang and detected its tiny ripples for the first time. John Mather and George Smoot won the 2006 Nobel Prize in Physics for this work. Smoot said that seeing the map was like seeing the face of God, a remark that became famous."],["WMAP and Planck","NASA’s WMAP satellite, launched in 2001, and the European Space Agency’s Planck, launched in 2009, mapped the CMB in ever greater detail. Planck’s measurements helped fix the age of the universe at about 13.8 billion years and showed that it is about 5 percent ordinary matter, 27 percent dark matter and 68 percent dark energy."],["Seeing it at home","Before digital broadcasting, a small part of the static or snow on an untuned analog television was caused by the CMB. Some of the flickering dots people saw were photons that had been traveling for more than 13 billion years."],["A flat universe","The size of the ripples in the CMB shows that space on the largest scales is very nearly flat, meaning that parallel lines stay parallel. This matches the predictions of the theory of cosmic inflation, which proposes that the universe expanded incredibly fast in a tiny fraction of a second after the Big Bang."],["The cold spot","Maps of the CMB show a few puzzling features, such as an unusually large cold spot in the southern sky. Most scientists think these are chance fluctuations, but some have suggested more exotic explanations. Studying such details helps test theories of the early universe."],["Ground telescopes","Telescopes in very dry, high places, such as the South Pole and the Atacama Desert in Chile, study the CMB from the ground. They search for faint swirling patterns in its polarization that could be signs of gravitational waves from the earliest moments after the Big Bang."]],
sp:["The pigeons of Holmdel","When Penzias and Wilson first heard the mysterious hiss in their antenna, they suspected the pigeons nesting inside it, and the white droppings the birds left behind, which they called a white dielectric material. They removed the pigeons and cleaned the antenna, but the hiss remained. It was not caused by birds at all, but by the afterglow of the birth of the universe."],
g:[["Microwave","A type of radio wave with a short wavelength"],["Kelvin","A temperature scale starting at absolute zero"],["Absolute zero","The lowest possible temperature, about −273.15 °C"],["Cosmic inflation","A proposed burst of extremely rapid expansion just after the Big Bang"]],
f:["The CMB is the oldest light in the universe.","It was discovered by accident in 1965.","Its temperature is less than 3 degrees above absolute zero."],
fun:"Part of the snow on old analog televisions came from the afterglow of the Big Bang.",
tl:"Ang cosmic microwave background ay pinakalumang liwanag sa sansinukob, inilabas mga 380,000 taon matapos ang Big Bang. Nasa lahat ng direksiyon ito at malakas na ebidensiya ng Big Bang."});

ADDART({id:"gravwaves",t:"Gravitational Waves",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"〰️",w:"Gravitational wave",r:["relat","bh","pulsar","grav"],
p:[["Ripples in spacetime","Gravitational waves are ripples in the fabric of space and time caused by massive objects accelerating."],["Einstein’s prediction","Albert Einstein predicted them in 1916 as part of his general theory of relativity."],["First detected in 2015","Scientists first detected them directly in 2015, from two black holes colliding over a billion light-years away."]],
l:"Gravitational waves are ripples in spacetime that travel at the speed of light, produced when massive objects such as black holes or neutron stars move violently, for example when they orbit and collide. Albert Einstein predicted them in 1916, but they are so faint that he doubted they could ever be measured. In September 2015 the LIGO observatories in the United States detected waves from two merging black holes, opening a new way to observe the universe.",
s:[["Stretching space","According to general relativity, mass and energy curve spacetime, and accelerating masses send out waves in it, like ripples spreading on a pond. As a gravitational wave passes, it stretches space in one direction and squeezes it in another, by an incredibly tiny amount."],["How LIGO works","LIGO, the Laser Interferometer Gravitational-Wave Observatory, has two detectors, in Washington and Louisiana. Each has two arms 4 kilometers long at right angles. Laser light bounces back and forth along the arms; a passing wave changes their lengths by less than a thousandth of the width of a proton, which shows up in how the light beams combine."],["The first detection","On 14 September 2015, both LIGO detectors recorded a signal lasting a fraction of a second, a rising chirp. It came from two black holes, about 36 and 29 times the Sun’s mass, merging about 1.3 billion light-years away. In that instant, energy equal to about three Suns was released as gravitational waves."],["A Nobel Prize","Rainer Weiss, Kip Thorne and Barry Barish, who led the development of LIGO, won the 2017 Nobel Prize in Physics. Thousands of scientists from many countries worked on the discovery. Since then, LIGO, the Virgo detector in Italy and the KAGRA detector in Japan have detected hundreds of mergers."]],
k:[["Predicted","1916 by Albert Einstein"],["First direct detection","14 September 2015"],["Speed","The speed of light"],["Detectors","LIGO (USA), Virgo (Italy), KAGRA (Japan)"],["Nobel Prize","2017 (Weiss, Thorne, Barish)"]],
d:[["1916","Einstein predicts gravitational waves"],["1974","Hulse and Taylor discover a binary pulsar"],["2015","LIGO makes the first direct detection"],["2017","Waves from colliding neutron stars detected along with light"],["2017","Nobel Prize in Physics awarded for the detection"],["2023","Pulsar timing arrays find evidence of a gravitational-wave background"]],
s2:[["Indirect evidence first","In 1974 Russell Hulse and Joseph Taylor discovered a pair of neutron stars orbiting each other. Over years, the orbit shrank exactly as expected if the stars were losing energy by giving off gravitational waves. This indirect evidence won them the 1993 Nobel Prize in Physics."],["Colliding neutron stars","On 17 August 2017, detectors caught gravitational waves from two neutron stars merging about 130 million light-years away. Seconds later, telescopes detected a burst of gamma rays, and over the next days, observatories around the world watched the glow of the explosion. It showed that such collisions create heavy elements, such as gold and platinum."],["Listening to the universe","Because gravitational wave signals can be converted into sound, scientists often describe detectors as listening to the universe. Merging black holes produce a rising chirp. Each signal reveals the masses and spins of the objects and how far away they were."],["Pulsar timing arrays","Pulsars are spinning neutron stars that send out radio pulses with clocklike regularity. In 2023, teams that had timed dozens of pulsars for years in North America, Europe, China, India and Australia reported evidence of a gentle background of very long gravitational waves, probably from pairs of supermassive black holes across the universe."],["Future detectors","The European Space Agency is planning LISA, the Laser Interferometer Space Antenna, three spacecraft flying millions of kilometers apart, due to launch in the 2030s. It will detect waves from supermassive black holes. New ground observatories, such as the Einstein Telescope in Europe and Cosmic Explorer in the United States, are also being planned."],["Why it matters","Gravitational waves let astronomers observe events that give off little or no light, such as black holes colliding. Combining gravitational waves with light, a field called multi-messenger astronomy, gives a fuller picture of the universe’s most violent events and tests Einstein’s theory in extreme conditions."]],
sp:["A tiny wobble","The waves detected in 2015 had traveled for 1.3 billion years, since before complex life appeared on Earth. By the time they reached LIGO, they changed the 4-kilometer arms by less than one ten-thousandth of the width of a proton. Measuring such a tiny change required shielding the detectors from vibrations, including passing trucks, ocean waves and even earthquakes on the other side of the world."],
g:[["Spacetime","Space and time combined into a single fabric in Einstein’s theory"],["Interferometer","An instrument that combines light waves to measure tiny distances"],["Binary","A pair of objects orbiting each other"],["Multi-messenger astronomy","Studying cosmic events using different signals, such as light and gravitational waves"]],
f:["Einstein predicted gravitational waves in 1916.","The first direct detection was in September 2015.","Gravitational waves travel at the speed of light."],
fun:"The gravitational waves first detected in 2015 had been traveling for 1.3 billion years.",
tl:"Ang gravitational wave ay alon sa espasyo at panahon na nililikha ng malalaking bagay gaya ng nagbabanggaang black hole. Hinulaan ito ni Einstein noong 1916 at unang natuklasan noong 2015."});

;
/* data/space2-4.js */
// Space articles, part 2.4: the Voyager probes, living in space, and the search for life beyond Earth.
ADDART({id:"voyager",t:"The Voyager Probes",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🛰️",w:"Voyager program",r:["jupiter","saturn","uranep","space","kuiper"],
p:[["Grand tour","Voyager 1 and Voyager 2, launched in 1977, flew past the giant planets and sent back the first close-up views of many of them."],["Into interstellar space","Both probes have left the Sun’s bubble of solar wind and are now in interstellar space."],["A message for the stars","Each carries a Golden Record of sounds and images from Earth."]],
l:"The Voyager probes are two NASA spacecraft launched in 1977 to study the outer planets. Taking advantage of a rare alignment of Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus and Neptune, Voyager 2 became the only spacecraft to visit all four giant planets, while Voyager 1 explored Jupiter and Saturn and its large moon Titan. Nearly 50 years later, both are still sending data from beyond the edge of the Sun’s influence, making them the most distant human-made objects.",
s:[["A rare alignment","In the late 1970s, the outer planets lined up in a way that happens only about once every 175 years. A spacecraft could use each planet’s gravity to swing on to the next, a technique called a gravity assist, saving years of travel. NASA built two identical probes to take advantage of it."],["Jupiter and Saturn","The Voyagers reached Jupiter in 1979. They discovered active volcanoes on its moon Io, the first found beyond Earth, and faint rings around Jupiter. At Saturn, in 1980 and 1981, they revealed intricate detail in its rings and studied Titan’s thick, hazy atmosphere."],["Uranus and Neptune","Voyager 2 flew past Uranus in 1986 and Neptune in 1989, the only spacecraft ever to visit them. It discovered new moons and rings at both, found Neptune’s Great Dark Spot, a giant storm, and spotted geysers on Neptune’s moon Triton. Its images are still our best close-up views of these ice giants."],["Leaving the solar system","The Sun’s solar wind blows a huge bubble around the solar system called the heliosphere. Voyager 1 crossed its edge, the heliopause, in August 2012, becoming the first spacecraft to reach interstellar space. Voyager 2 followed in November 2018. They are still within the Sun’s gravitational reach, and will be for tens of thousands of years."]],
k:[["Launched","1977 (Voyager 2 on 20 August, Voyager 1 on 5 September)"],["Planets visited","Voyager 1: Jupiter, Saturn; Voyager 2: Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune"],["Entered interstellar space","Voyager 1 in 2012, Voyager 2 in 2018"],["Power","Radioisotope generators using plutonium"],["Distance of Voyager 1","Over 25 billion km from the Sun"]],
d:[["1977","Both Voyagers are launched"],["1979","Voyagers fly past Jupiter"],["1980–1981","Voyagers fly past Saturn"],["1986","Voyager 2 flies past Uranus"],["1989","Voyager 2 flies past Neptune"],["2012","Voyager 1 enters interstellar space"]],
s2:[["The Golden Record","Each Voyager carries a gold-plated copper record, a message to any intelligent beings who might find it. It contains greetings in 55 languages, music from many cultures, from Bach to Javanese gamelan and Peruvian panpipes, natural sounds such as rain and whales, and 115 images of life on Earth. A committee led by Carl Sagan chose the contents."],["Powered by plutonium","Sunlight is too weak in the outer solar system for solar panels, so each Voyager is powered by radioisotope thermoelectric generators, which turn heat from decaying plutonium into electricity. Their power falls by about 4 watts a year, so engineers have gradually switched off heaters and instruments to keep them running."],["Talking across billions of kilometers","Voyager 1’s radio signal, about as powerful as a refrigerator light bulb, takes more than 22 hours to reach Earth. NASA’s Deep Space Network of giant antennas in California, Spain and Australia listens for it. In 2023 and 2024, engineers fixed computer and thruster problems from Earth, using programming techniques from the 1970s."],["The Pale Blue Dot","On 14 February 1990, before its cameras were switched off, Voyager 1 took a family portrait of the solar system, including a picture of Earth as a tiny speck from about 6 billion kilometers away. Carl Sagan called it the Pale Blue Dot, and it became one of the most famous images in history."],["The fastest and farthest","Voyager 1 travels at about 17 kilometers per second relative to the Sun, covering roughly 540 million kilometers a year. In about 40,000 years, it will pass within about 1.6 light-years of a star called Gliese 445. Voyager 2 is slightly slower and heading in a different direction."],["The end of the mission","As their power runs down, the Voyagers will be able to operate fewer and fewer instruments. NASA expects that sometime in the late 2020s or 2030s, they will no longer have enough power to send data. They will then continue silently through the galaxy, carrying their Golden Records, for billions of years."]],
sp:["Sounds of Earth","The Golden Record’s greetings range from ancient Sumerian and Akkadian to Amoy, Bengali, Swahili and Zulu. Its music includes a Navajo night chant, a Senegalese percussion piece, Chuck Berry’s Johnny B. Goode, Beethoven and a song from Azerbaijan. On the cover, diagrams explain how to play the record and where Earth is, using the positions of pulsars as a map."],
g:[["Gravity assist","Using a planet’s gravity to change a spacecraft’s speed and direction"],["Heliosphere","The bubble around the Sun formed by the solar wind"],["Interstellar space","The space between the stars, beyond the heliosphere"],["Radioisotope generator","A power source that makes electricity from the heat of radioactive decay"]],
f:["Voyager 2 is the only spacecraft to have visited Uranus and Neptune.","Voyager 1 is the most distant human-made object.","Each Voyager carries a Golden Record with greetings in 55 languages."],
fun:"A radio signal from Voyager 1 takes more than 22 hours to reach Earth.",
tl:"Ang Voyager 1 at 2 ay mga sasakyang pangkalawakan ng NASA na inilunsad noong 1977. Binisita nila ang malalaking planeta at ngayon ay nasa interstellar space na, dala ang Golden Record na may mga tunog at larawan mula sa Daigdig."});

ADDART({id:"astronauts",t:"Living in Space",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"👩‍🚀",w:"Astronaut",r:["iss","space","apollo","tereshkova","armstrong"],
p:[["Astronauts","Astronauts, also called cosmonauts in Russia and taikonauts in China, are people trained to travel and work in space."],["Life without weight","In orbit, astronauts float in microgravity, which changes how they eat, sleep and even how their bodies work."],["Hundreds have flown","Since Yuri Gagarin’s flight in 1961, more than 600 people from over 40 countries have been to space."]],
l:"Living in space means surviving in an environment with no air, extreme temperatures, harmful radiation and almost no felt gravity. Since Yuri Gagarin became the first human in space in 1961, astronauts have orbited Earth, walked on the Moon and lived for months aboard space stations. The International Space Station has been continuously occupied since November 2000, and China’s Tiangong station since 2021. Spaceflight is changing too, as private companies now fly crews into orbit.",
s:[["Becoming an astronaut","Astronauts are usually scientists, engineers, doctors or pilots. Training takes years and includes learning spacecraft systems, robotics, foreign languages and survival skills. They practice spacewalks underwater in giant pools, which mimic weightlessness, and fly in aircraft that dive to create about 20 seconds of free fall at a time."],["Why astronauts float","Astronauts on the space station are not beyond Earth’s gravity; it is still about 90 percent as strong there. They float because the station and everyone in it are falling around Earth together, at about 28,000 kilometers per hour, in a constant free fall called orbit. This condition is called microgravity."],["Daily life","Astronauts eat packaged and dried food, often with sauces to help it stick to spoons. Water is recycled from sweat, breath and urine. They sleep in sleeping bags tied to walls, and use toilets that work with suction. Because there is no up or down, they can work on the ceiling as easily as the floor."],["Effects on the body","Without gravity pulling on them, muscles and bones weaken: astronauts can lose about 1 to 2 percent of bone mass in some bones each month. Fluids shift toward the head, faces look puffy, and some astronauts’ eyesight changes. To stay healthy they exercise about two hours a day on treadmills, bikes and resistance machines."]],
k:[["First human in space","Yuri Gagarin, 12 April 1961"],["First woman in space","Valentina Tereshkova, 1963"],["Longest single flight","Valeri Polyakov, about 437 days (1994–1995)"],["People who have flown to space","More than 600"],["Space stations in use","International Space Station, Tiangong"]],
d:[["1961","Yuri Gagarin orbits Earth"],["1963","Valentina Tereshkova becomes the first woman in space"],["1965","Alexei Leonov makes the first spacewalk"],["1969","Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin walk on the Moon"],["2000","The ISS becomes permanently occupied"],["2021","China begins crewed operations on Tiangong"]],
s2:[["Spacewalks","Outside the spacecraft, astronauts wear spacesuits that are like small personal spacecraft, supplying oxygen, controlling temperature and protecting against tiny meteoroids. Alexei Leonov made the first spacewalk in 1965, and his suit swelled so much that he struggled to get back inside. Today spacewalks to repair and upgrade stations can last more than seven hours."],["Radiation","Earth’s atmosphere and magnetic field shield us from much of the radiation in space. Astronauts on the space station receive in a day roughly what people on the ground receive in several months to a year from natural sources. On trips to the Moon or Mars, radiation from the Sun and from deep space would be one of the greatest dangers."],["Space stations","The Soviet Union launched the first space station, Salyut 1, in 1971, followed by the long-lasting Mir. The International Space Station, built by the United States, Russia, Europe, Japan and Canada, has hosted astronauts from more than 20 countries. China’s Tiangong station is built and operated by China, which plans to host astronauts from other countries."],["Private spaceflight","In 2020, SpaceX’s Crew Dragon became the first privately built spacecraft to carry astronauts to the space station. Companies such as Blue Origin and Virgin Galactic now fly tourists on short trips to the edge of space, and private crews have flown missions in orbit, including the first spacewalk by non-professional astronauts in 2024."],["Growing plants","Astronauts have grown lettuce, radishes, chili peppers and flowers in space. Experiments show how plants sense direction without gravity, using light instead. Growing food will be important for long journeys, such as missions to Mars, which could take two to three years there and back."],["Twins in space","In 2015 and 2016, NASA astronaut Scott Kelly spent nearly a year on the space station, while his identical twin brother, Mark, a retired astronaut, stayed on Earth. Comparing them helped scientists learn how long spaceflight affects the body, from genes to the immune system. Most changes returned to normal after Scott came home."]],
sp:["Overview effect","Many astronauts describe a powerful feeling when they see Earth from space: the planet looks small, beautiful and fragile, with no borders visible between countries. This change in outlook is called the overview effect. Some astronauts say it made them feel a deep sense of connection to all people and a strong wish to protect the planet."],
g:[["Microgravity","The condition of near weightlessness experienced in orbit"],["Orbit","The curved path of an object around a planet or star"],["Spacewalk","Activity by an astronaut outside a spacecraft"],["Cosmonaut","A Russian astronaut"]],
f:["Astronauts on the space station see about 16 sunrises a day.","The ISS has been continuously occupied since November 2000.","Astronauts can grow a few centimeters taller in space because their spines stretch."],
fun:"Astronauts on the space station see about 16 sunrises and sunsets every day.",
tl:"Ang mga astronaut ay sinanay upang maglakbay at magtrabaho sa kalawakan. Sa orbit, lumulutang sila dahil sa microgravity, at kailangan nilang mag-ehersisyo araw-araw upang mapanatiling malakas ang buto at kalamnan."});

ADDART({id:"astrobio",t:"The Search for Life Beyond Earth",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"👽",w:"Astrobiology",r:["exoplanet","mars","jupiter","saturn","jwst"],
p:[["Are we alone?","Astrobiology is the science that asks whether life exists anywhere else in the universe."],["Where to look","Scientists search on Mars, on icy moons with hidden oceans and on planets around other stars."],["No life found yet","So far, no life has been found beyond Earth, but the search has never been more active."]],
l:"The search for life beyond Earth is one of the oldest questions people have asked: are we alone in the universe? Astrobiology combines astronomy, biology, chemistry and geology to study how life began on Earth and where else it might exist. Scientists look for signs of past or present microbes on Mars, explore moons such as Europa and Enceladus that hide oceans beneath their ice, study the atmospheres of planets around other stars and listen for radio signals from possible civilizations. No evidence of extraterrestrial life has yet been confirmed.",
s:[["What life needs","As far as we know, life needs liquid water, a source of energy and chemical building blocks such as carbon, hydrogen, nitrogen and oxygen. On Earth, microbes called extremophiles thrive in boiling hot springs, acidic lakes, deep ice and rocks kilometers underground, showing that life can survive in harsh places."],["Mars","Mars once had rivers, lakes and perhaps an ocean. Rovers such as Curiosity and Perseverance have found ancient lakebeds and organic molecules, the carbon-based chemicals of life. In 2024 Perseverance found a rock nicknamed Cheyava Falls with patterns that could have been made by ancient microbes, though they could also have formed without life."],["Ocean moons","Jupiter’s moon Europa and Saturn’s moon Enceladus have salty oceans of liquid water beneath icy crusts. The Cassini spacecraft flew through plumes of water spraying from Enceladus and found salts and organic molecules. NASA’s Europa Clipper and Europe’s JUICE are on their way to study Jupiter’s icy moons in the 2030s."],["Planets around other stars","Astronomers have found more than 6,000 exoplanets, some rocky and in the habitable zone where liquid water could exist. Telescopes like the James Webb Space Telescope can study their atmospheres, looking for gases such as oxygen, methane or water vapor that might hint at life, called biosignatures."]],
k:[["Life found beyond Earth","None confirmed"],["Main targets","Mars, Europa, Enceladus, Titan, exoplanets"],["Known exoplanets","More than 6,000"],["Famous equation","Drake equation (1961)"],["Radio search","SETI, since 1960"]],
d:[["1960","Frank Drake begins the first radio search, Project Ozma"],["1976","Viking landers test Martian soil for life"],["1977","The Wow! signal is detected"],["1995","First exoplanet around a Sun-like star is found"],["2005","Cassini discovers water plumes on Enceladus"],["2024","Europa Clipper is launched"]],
s2:[["The Drake equation","In 1961 the astronomer Frank Drake wrote an equation to estimate how many civilizations in our galaxy might be able to communicate with us. It multiplies factors such as the number of stars, how many have planets, how many planets could support life and how long civilizations last. Many factors are unknown, so estimates range from almost none to millions."],["Listening for signals","The Search for Extraterrestrial Intelligence, or SETI, uses radio telescopes to listen for signals that could only come from technology. In 1977 a telescope in Ohio recorded a strong, brief signal, and the astronomer Jerry Ehman wrote Wow! beside it. It was never detected again, and its origin remains unknown."],["The Fermi paradox","The physicist Enrico Fermi is said to have asked, Where is everybody? If the galaxy is billions of years old and has many planets, why have we seen no sign of other civilizations? Possible answers include that life is rare, that civilizations are far apart or short-lived, or that we have not looked carefully enough."],["Titan","Saturn’s largest moon, Titan, has a thick atmosphere and lakes and seas of liquid methane and ethane. It may also hide a water ocean deep underground. NASA’s Dragonfly mission, a nuclear-powered drone, is planned to launch in 2028 to fly between sites on Titan studying its chemistry."],["Life as we don’t know it","Some scientists wonder whether life elsewhere could be very different, perhaps using liquids other than water, such as methane on Titan. Recognizing such life would be hard, so astrobiologists try to think about what signs any living system might leave behind, such as complex chemistry out of balance with its surroundings."],["Protecting other worlds","Spacecraft sent to places where life might exist are carefully cleaned, so that microbes from Earth do not contaminate them and confuse the search. Cassini and Galileo were deliberately crashed into Saturn and Jupiter at the ends of their missions, so they could never hit and contaminate Enceladus or Europa."]],
sp:["Extremophiles","Some of Earth’s toughest creatures are tardigrades, or water bears, microscopic animals about half a millimeter long. They can survive drying out, freezing, extreme heat and pressure, and even, for short periods, the vacuum and radiation of space, as experiments in orbit in 2007 showed. Extremophiles like these give hope that life could exist in harsh places beyond Earth."],
g:[["Astrobiology","The study of life in the universe"],["Extremophile","A living thing that thrives in extreme conditions"],["Biosignature","A sign, such as a gas, that could indicate life"],["SETI","The Search for Extraterrestrial Intelligence"]],
f:["No life beyond Earth has yet been confirmed.","Europa and Enceladus have oceans beneath their ice.","Tardigrades have survived exposure to space."],
fun:"Tiny animals called tardigrades have survived being exposed to outer space.",
tl:"Ang astrobiology ay agham na nagtatanong kung may buhay sa ibang bahagi ng sansinukob. Naghahanap ang mga siyentipiko sa Mars, sa mga buwang may karagatan sa ilalim ng yelo, at sa mga planeta sa ibang bituin, ngunit wala pang natatagpuang buhay."});

;
/* data/physics2-1.js */
// Physics articles, part 2.1: energy, heat and thermodynamics, and waves.
ADDART({id:"energyphys",t:"Energy",c:"Science",i:"⚡",w:"Energy",r:["newton","grav","elec","thermo","relat"],
p:[["The ability to do work","In physics, energy is the capacity to do work, such as moving an object, heating it or making light."],["Never created or destroyed","Energy can change from one form to another, but the total amount always stays the same."],["Measured in joules","Scientists measure energy in joules, named after the English physicist James Prescott Joule."]],
l:"Energy is one of the most important ideas in physics. It is the ability to cause change: to move things, warm them, light them up or make them grow. Energy comes in many forms, including kinetic energy of motion, potential energy stored by position, chemical energy in food and fuel, thermal energy, electrical energy, light and nuclear energy. According to the law of conservation of energy, energy can be transformed from one form into another but never created or destroyed.",
s:[["Kinetic and potential","A moving object has kinetic energy: the faster and heavier it is, the more it has. Potential energy is stored energy, such as that of a book on a high shelf, a stretched spring or water behind a dam. When the book falls, its potential energy turns into kinetic energy."],["Conservation of energy","In the 1840s scientists including Julius Robert von Mayer, James Joule and Hermann von Helmholtz showed that energy is conserved. Joule measured how much work was needed to warm water by stirring it, proving that mechanical work and heat are forms of the same thing. This law is one of the most thoroughly tested principles in science."],["Energy changes","Every day we see energy changing form. A car engine turns chemical energy in fuel into motion and heat. A solar panel turns light into electricity. Plants turn sunlight into chemical energy through photosynthesis, and our bodies turn the chemical energy in food into movement and warmth."],["Measuring energy","The unit of energy is the joule. Lifting an apple about one meter takes roughly one joule. Food energy is often given in kilocalories, where one kilocalorie is about 4,184 joules. Electricity is usually sold in kilowatt-hours; one kilowatt-hour is 3.6 million joules, enough to run a 100-watt lamp for 10 hours."]],
k:[["Unit","Joule (J)"],["Main law","Conservation of energy"],["Common forms","Kinetic, potential, thermal, chemical, electrical, nuclear, light"],["Power","Energy per second, measured in watts"],["Famous equation","E = mc² (mass–energy equivalence)"]],
d:[["1687","Newton’s laws of motion lay the foundations of mechanics"],["1807","Thomas Young uses the word energy in its modern sense"],["1840s","Joule, Mayer and Helmholtz establish conservation of energy"],["1850","Clausius states the laws of thermodynamics"],["1905","Einstein shows that mass and energy are equivalent"],["1938","Nuclear fission is discovered"]],
s2:[["E = mc²","In 1905 Albert Einstein showed that mass is a form of energy. His equation E = mc², where c is the speed of light, means a tiny amount of mass contains an enormous amount of energy. This is the source of the energy released in nuclear reactors, nuclear weapons and the Sun, where a small fraction of mass turns into energy during nuclear reactions."],["Power","Power is how fast energy is used or transferred, measured in watts, where one watt is one joule per second. A light-emitting diode bulb may use about 10 watts, a microwave oven around 1,000 watts, and a large power station several billion watts."],["Efficiency","No machine turns all the energy it uses into the useful form we want; some is always lost, usually as heat. Efficiency measures the useful share. LED bulbs turn a much larger share of electricity into light than old incandescent bulbs, which mostly made heat."],["Energy in living things","Almost all energy for life on Earth comes from the Sun, captured by plants and other organisms through photosynthesis. Animals get energy by eating plants or other animals. Inside cells, energy from food is stored in a molecule called ATP, which powers activities from muscle movement to thinking."],["Energy for society","Humans use energy for heating, cooling, transport, industry and electricity. Most of the world’s energy still comes from fossil fuels: coal, oil and natural gas. Burning them releases carbon dioxide, which warms the climate, so many countries are expanding renewable sources such as solar, wind and hydropower."],["Work and energy","In physics, work is done when a force moves something. Pushing a heavy box across a floor does work and uses energy; holding it still, though tiring, does no work in the physics sense. The work done equals the force multiplied by the distance moved in the direction of the force."]],
sp:["A joule of energy","A joule is a small amount of energy. A person climbing one flight of stairs uses about 2,000 joules of energy against gravity. A banana contains about 400,000 joules of food energy. The Sun gives off about 380 trillion trillion joules every second, of which Earth receives only a tiny fraction, still far more than all of humanity uses."],
g:[["Energy","The ability to do work or cause change"],["Kinetic energy","The energy of motion"],["Potential energy","Stored energy due to position or condition"],["Joule","The unit of energy"]],
f:["Energy can change form but cannot be created or destroyed.","One kilowatt-hour equals 3.6 million joules.","Almost all of the energy for life on Earth comes from the Sun."],
fun:"Lifting an apple about one meter takes roughly one joule of energy.",
tl:"Ang enerhiya ay kakayahang gumawa ng trabaho o magdulot ng pagbabago. Maaari itong magbago ng anyo ngunit hindi nalilikha o nawawala. Sinusukat ito sa joule."});

ADDART({id:"thermo",t:"Heat and Thermodynamics",c:"Science",i:"🌡️",w:"Thermodynamics",r:["energyphys","states","steam","atom"],
p:[["Heat on the move","Heat is energy that flows from hotter things to colder things."],["Temperature","Temperature measures how fast the particles in a substance are moving."],["The laws of thermodynamics","Four laws describe how heat and energy behave, from engines to stars."]],
l:"Thermodynamics is the branch of physics that studies heat, temperature and energy and how they change. It explains why ice melts, how engines and refrigerators work and why heat always flows from warm objects to cool ones. It grew in the 1800s from efforts to improve steam engines, and its laws are among the most universal in science, applying to everything from living cells to black holes.",
s:[["Heat and temperature","All matter is made of particles that are always moving. Temperature measures their average energy of motion: in hot things they move faster. Heat is energy transferred because of a temperature difference. A bathtub of warm water holds more heat energy than a cup of boiling water, even though its temperature is lower."],["How heat travels","Heat moves in three ways. In conduction, it passes through materials as particles bump into each other, as when a metal spoon warms in hot soup. In convection, warm fluids rise and cool ones sink, carrying heat, as in boiling water or the atmosphere. In radiation, heat travels as infrared light, even through empty space, as from the Sun."],["Temperature scales","The Celsius scale sets water’s freezing point at 0 degrees and boiling point at 100 degrees at sea level. The Fahrenheit scale, used mainly in the United States, puts them at 32 and 212. Scientists use the kelvin scale, which starts at absolute zero, minus 273.15 degrees Celsius, the coldest possible temperature."],["The laws","The first law says energy is conserved: heat added to a system goes into raising its energy or doing work. The second law says that heat flows naturally from hot to cold and that disorder, called entropy, tends to increase. The third law says absolute zero can never quite be reached. A zeroth law states that objects at the same temperature are in balance."]],
k:[["Absolute zero","0 kelvin, −273.15 °C"],["Water boils","100 °C at sea level"],["Ways heat travels","Conduction, convection, radiation"],["Key concept","Entropy"],["Founders","Carnot, Clausius, Kelvin, Boltzmann"]],
d:[["1712","Thomas Newcomen builds a working steam engine"],["1824","Sadi Carnot publishes his study of heat engines"],["1848","Lord Kelvin proposes an absolute temperature scale"],["1850","Rudolf Clausius states the second law"],["1877","Ludwig Boltzmann links entropy to probability"],["1995","Scientists create a Bose–Einstein condensate near absolute zero"]],
s2:[["Entropy and the arrow of time","Entropy is a measure of disorder, or of how many ways the particles in a system can be arranged. The second law says that in an isolated system entropy never decreases. That is why a broken egg never reassembles itself and why time seems to flow in one direction. Ludwig Boltzmann connected entropy to the statistics of particles."],["Engines","A heat engine, such as a car engine or power station, turns heat into work by letting heat flow from a hot source to a cold one. Sadi Carnot showed in 1824 that no engine can be perfectly efficient: some heat must always be released to the cold side. The bigger the temperature difference, the more efficient the engine can be."],["Refrigerators and heat pumps","A refrigerator moves heat from its cold inside to the warmer room, which needs energy, supplied by electricity. It works by compressing and expanding a special fluid that absorbs heat when it evaporates. Heat pumps use the same idea to warm homes by drawing heat from outdoor air or the ground, even in cold weather."],["Expansion","Most materials expand when heated and contract when cooled, because their particles move more and take up more space. Bridges and railway tracks have gaps to allow for this. Water is unusual: it expands when it freezes, which is why ice floats and pipes can burst in winter."],["Near absolute zero","At very low temperatures, matter behaves strangely. Helium becomes a superfluid that can flow uphill and through tiny cracks. In 1995 physicists cooled atoms to billionths of a degree above absolute zero, creating a Bose–Einstein condensate, a new state of matter in which atoms act together as one."],["Heat and climate","Thermodynamics helps explain Earth’s climate. The Sun warms the surface, which gives off infrared radiation. Greenhouse gases, such as carbon dioxide and water vapor, absorb some of it and keep the planet warm. Adding more greenhouse gases traps more heat, raising global temperatures."]],
sp:["Why a metal bench feels colder","On a cold morning, a metal bench feels much colder than a wooden one, even though both are the same temperature. Metal is a good conductor, so it draws heat away from your hand quickly, while wood is a poor conductor and does not. Our skin senses how fast heat leaves it, not the actual temperature of the object."],
g:[["Temperature","A measure of how hot or cold something is"],["Entropy","A measure of disorder in a system"],["Conduction","Heat transfer through direct contact"],["Absolute zero","The lowest possible temperature, 0 kelvin"]],
f:["Absolute zero is −273.15 °C.","Water expands when it freezes.","Heat always flows naturally from hot to cold."],
fun:"Near absolute zero, liquid helium can creep up the walls of its container.",
tl:"Ang thermodynamics ay sangay ng pisika na nag-aaral ng init, temperatura at enerhiya. Ipinaliliwanag nito kung bakit dumadaloy ang init mula sa mainit patungo sa malamig at kung paano gumagana ang mga makina at refrigerator."});

ADDART({id:"waves",t:"Waves",c:"Science",i:"🌊",w:"Wave",r:["sound","light","tsunami","quantum","gravwaves"],
p:[["Energy on the move","A wave is a disturbance that carries energy from one place to another without carrying matter along with it."],["Many kinds","Sound, light, ocean waves, earthquake waves and radio are all waves."],["Measured by wavelength","Waves are described by their wavelength, frequency and amplitude."]],
l:"A wave is a repeating disturbance that transfers energy through a material or through space. When a wave passes, the material it travels through moves back and forth or up and down, but does not travel along with the wave. Waves are everywhere: ripples on a pond, sound in the air, light from the Sun, radio signals, the shaking of earthquakes. Understanding waves is key to music, communication, medicine and much of modern technology.",
s:[["Parts of a wave","The wavelength is the distance between two crests, or high points. The frequency is how many waves pass a point each second, measured in hertz. The amplitude is the height of the wave, which tells how much energy it carries. The speed of a wave equals its wavelength multiplied by its frequency."],["Transverse and longitudinal","In transverse waves, such as waves on a string or light, the disturbance moves at right angles to the direction the wave travels. In longitudinal waves, such as sound, particles move back and forth in the same direction as the wave, forming compressions and stretched-out regions."],["Mechanical waves","Mechanical waves need a material to travel through. Sound travels through air, water and solids, but not through empty space. Earthquakes send seismic waves through the rock of the Earth; by studying how they travel, scientists have learned about Earth’s layers deep inside."],["Electromagnetic waves","Light, radio waves, microwaves, infrared, ultraviolet, X-rays and gamma rays are electromagnetic waves, made of changing electric and magnetic fields. They need no material and can travel through space at the speed of light, about 300,000 kilometers per second. They differ only in wavelength."]],
k:[["Wavelength","Distance between two crests"],["Frequency","Waves per second, in hertz (Hz)"],["Speed of sound in air","About 343 m/s"],["Speed of light","About 300,000 km/s"],["Main types","Mechanical and electromagnetic"]],
d:[["c. 500 BCE","Pythagoras studies the vibrations of strings"],["1678","Christiaan Huygens proposes the wave theory of light"],["1801","Thomas Young’s double-slit experiment shows light behaves as a wave"],["1865","James Clerk Maxwell predicts electromagnetic waves"],["1887","Heinrich Hertz produces radio waves"],["1924","Louis de Broglie proposes that particles behave like waves"]],
s2:[["Interference","When two waves meet, they combine. If crests line up with crests, the waves add up and grow larger; if crests meet troughs, they cancel out. In 1801 Thomas Young shone light through two narrow slits and saw stripes of light and dark, proving that light behaves as a wave. Noise-canceling headphones use interference to cancel out unwanted sound."],["Reflection, refraction and diffraction","Waves bounce off surfaces, which is reflection, as in an echo or a mirror. They bend when they pass from one material to another at an angle, which is refraction, which is why a straw looks bent in water. They spread out around obstacles and through gaps, which is diffraction, which lets us hear sounds around corners."],["The Doppler effect","When a source of waves moves toward you, the waves bunch up and their frequency rises; when it moves away, they spread out and the frequency falls. That is why an ambulance siren sounds higher as it approaches and lower as it passes. Astronomers use the same effect with light to measure how fast stars and galaxies move."],["Resonance","Every object has natural frequencies at which it vibrates easily. If you push it at that frequency, the vibrations grow large; this is resonance. It lets a singer shatter a glass and makes musical instruments ring. Engineers design bridges and buildings to avoid dangerous resonance with wind or earthquakes."],["Waves in technology","Radio, television, mobile phones and Wi-Fi send information using electromagnetic waves. Ultrasound scanners use high-frequency sound to make images inside the body. Microwave ovens use microwaves to heat food, and sonar uses sound waves to find objects underwater."],["Waves and particles","In the early 1900s, physicists discovered that light also behaves like a stream of particles called photons, and that particles such as electrons also behave like waves. This wave–particle duality is a central idea of quantum mechanics and is used in electron microscopes."]],
sp:["Ocean waves","Most ocean waves are made by wind blowing over the water. Although the waves travel across oceans, the water itself mostly moves in circles, rising and falling as the wave passes. Near the shore, the bottom slows the wave, it grows taller and topples over as a breaker. Tsunamis are different: they are caused by earthquakes or landslides and move the whole depth of the ocean."],
g:[["Wavelength","The distance between two wave crests"],["Frequency","The number of waves passing a point each second"],["Amplitude","The height of a wave, related to its energy"],["Hertz","The unit of frequency: one wave per second"]],
f:["Sound cannot travel through empty space.","Light travels about a million times faster than sound in air.","The Doppler effect makes a siren’s pitch change as it passes."],
fun:"Sound travels about four times faster in water than in air.",
tl:"Ang alon ay paggalaw na nagdadala ng enerhiya mula sa isang lugar patungo sa iba. Alon ang tunog, liwanag, radyo at alon sa dagat. Sinusukat ito sa wavelength, frequency at amplitude."});

;
/* data/physics2-2.js */
// Physics articles, part 2.2: fluids and pressure, and time and clocks.
ADDART({id:"fluids",t:"Pressure and Fluids",c:"Science",i:"💧",w:"Fluid mechanics",r:["states","flight","ships","grav"],
p:[["Liquids and gases","A fluid is any substance that can flow, including liquids such as water and gases such as air."],["Pressure","Pressure is force spread over an area, and fluids push in every direction."],["Floating and flying","Fluids explain why ships float, why balloons rise and how airplanes stay in the air."]],
l:"Fluid mechanics is the study of how liquids and gases behave when they are still and when they move. Fluids push on everything they touch, creating pressure that increases with depth. Their behavior explains why ships float, how hydraulic machines lift heavy loads, how airplanes fly, how blood flows through our bodies and how weather systems move across the planet. Key ideas come from scientists such as Archimedes, Blaise Pascal and Daniel Bernoulli.",
s:[["What is pressure?","Pressure is the force pushing on a surface divided by the area of that surface. A sharp knife cuts well because its force is concentrated on a tiny area, while snowshoes spread a person’s weight so they do not sink. Pressure is measured in pascals; air pressure at sea level is about 101,000 pascals."],["Air pressure","The air above us has weight and presses down on everything, about 10 tonnes on every square meter at sea level. We do not feel it because the pressure inside our bodies pushes outward equally. Air pressure falls with height, which is why ears pop in airplanes and climbers on high mountains need extra oxygen."],["Pressure in liquids","In a liquid, pressure increases with depth because of the weight of liquid above. At the bottom of the deepest ocean trench, the pressure is more than 1,000 times that at the surface. Divers must rise slowly from deep water to let their bodies adjust, and deep-sea submersibles need very thick walls."],["Archimedes and buoyancy","According to legend, the Greek scientist Archimedes realized while stepping into a bath that an object in a fluid is pushed up by a force equal to the weight of fluid it displaces. This upward push is called buoyancy. An object floats if it displaces its own weight of fluid before it is fully submerged."]],
k:[["Unit of pressure","Pascal (Pa)"],["Sea-level air pressure","About 101,000 Pa (1 atmosphere)"],["Key principles","Archimedes, Pascal, Bernoulli"],["Fluids include","Liquids and gases"],["Deepest ocean pressure","Over 1,000 times surface pressure"]],
d:[["c. 250 BCE","Archimedes describes buoyancy"],["1643","Evangelista Torricelli invents the barometer"],["1648","Pascal shows air pressure falls with height"],["1738","Daniel Bernoulli publishes Hydrodynamica"],["1783","First hot-air balloon flights with people"],["1903","The Wright brothers make the first powered airplane flight"]],
s2:[["How ships float","A steel ship floats even though steel is denser than water, because its hull encloses a large volume of air and displaces a great deal of water. If enough water rushes into the hull, the ship becomes heavier than the water it displaces and sinks. Submarines dive and rise by filling tanks with water or air."],["Pascal’s principle","Blaise Pascal discovered that pressure applied to a fluid in a closed container is passed equally to every part of it. Hydraulic machines, such as car brakes, car lifts and excavators, use this: a small push on a narrow piston creates a large force on a wide one, letting a person lift a car with ease."],["Bernoulli’s principle","Daniel Bernoulli showed in 1738 that when a fluid speeds up, its pressure drops. This helps explain how airplane wings produce lift, why a shower curtain is pulled inward when the water runs and how spray bottles work. Lift on a wing comes from it turning the airflow downward and from the pressure differences this creates."],["Measuring air pressure","In 1643 Evangelista Torricelli, a student of Galileo, filled a glass tube with mercury and turned it upside down in a dish. The mercury fell to a height of about 76 centimeters, held up by the pressure of the air. This was the first barometer. Falling pressure often warns of storms, and very low pressure marks the center of typhoons and hurricanes."],["Viscosity","Viscosity is a fluid’s resistance to flowing. Honey and lava are very viscous, while water and air are not. Engine oil is designed to stay slippery enough when cold and thick enough when hot to protect engine parts. Some fluids, such as a mix of cornstarch and water, become thicker when squeezed quickly."],["Fluids in the body","The heart pumps blood through blood vessels under pressure. Blood pressure is measured with two numbers: the higher pressure when the heart beats and the lower pressure between beats. Our lungs work by changing air pressure inside the chest, so air flows in and out."]],
sp:["Eureka!","According to a story told by the Roman writer Vitruvius, King Hiero II of Syracuse asked Archimedes to find out whether his new crown was pure gold. While bathing, Archimedes noticed the water level rise and realized he could measure the crown’s volume by the water it displaced, and so compare its density with gold. He supposedly ran through the streets shouting Eureka, I have found it."],
g:[["Fluid","A substance that can flow, such as a liquid or gas"],["Pressure","Force divided by the area it acts on"],["Buoyancy","The upward force a fluid exerts on an object in it"],["Viscosity","A fluid’s resistance to flowing"]],
f:["Air presses down with about 10 tonnes on every square meter at sea level.","Ships float because they displace their own weight of water.","Hydraulic machines can lift cars with a small push."],
fun:"Air presses on every square meter of the ground with a force about equal to the weight of 10 tonnes.",
tl:"Ang fluid ay anumang bagay na dumadaloy, gaya ng tubig at hangin. Ang presyon ay puwersang nakakalat sa isang lugar. Ipinaliliwanag ng fluid mechanics kung bakit lumulutang ang barko at lumilipad ang eroplano."});

ADDART({id:"timephys",t:"Time and Clocks",c:"Science",i:"⏱️",w:"Time",r:["relat","earthplanet","gps","atom","clocks"],
p:[["Measuring time","People have measured time for thousands of years using the Sun, the Moon, water, sand, pendulums and atoms."],["The second","Today the second is defined using vibrations of cesium atoms."],["Time is relative","Einstein showed that time passes at different rates depending on speed and gravity."]],
l:"Time is the ongoing sequence of events from the past through the present to the future. Measuring it accurately has shaped farming, navigation, science and modern life. Early people tracked days, months and years using the Sun, Moon and stars; later came sundials, water clocks, mechanical clocks and pendulums. Today atomic clocks keep time so precisely that they would lose less than a second in millions or even billions of years, and they make technologies such as GPS possible.",
s:[["Natural clocks","The day comes from Earth’s spin, the month roughly from the Moon’s cycle of phases, about 29.5 days, and the year from Earth’s orbit around the Sun. Ancient Egyptians divided day and night into 12 hours each, and Babylonian astronomers used a counting system based on 60, which gives us 60 minutes in an hour and 60 seconds in a minute."],["Early clocks","Sundials told time by the shadow of a stick or blade. Water clocks, used in Egypt, Babylon, China and Greece, measured time by water flowing steadily into or out of a vessel. In 1088 Su Song in China built a huge astronomical clock tower driven by water. Sandglasses and candles marked with lines were also used."],["Mechanical clocks","Mechanical clocks with weights and gears appeared in European towers in the late 1200s. In 1656 Christiaan Huygens built the first pendulum clock, which was far more accurate. Spring-driven watches followed. In the 1700s John Harrison built marine chronometers accurate enough at sea for sailors to work out their longitude."],["Atomic clocks","Atoms of each element absorb and give off light at exact frequencies. Since 1967, the second has been defined as 9,192,631,770 vibrations of radiation from cesium-133 atoms. Atomic clocks based on this idea are the world’s timekeepers, and newer optical clocks using other atoms are even more precise."]],
k:[["Unit","The second (s)"],["Definition","9,192,631,770 oscillations of cesium-133 radiation"],["Most accurate clocks","Optical atomic clocks"],["Time standard","Coordinated Universal Time (UTC)"],["Time zones","Based on 24 zones, with many local variations"]],
d:[["c. 1500 BCE","Egyptians use sundials and water clocks"],["1088","Su Song builds his astronomical clock tower in China"],["1656","Christiaan Huygens builds the first pendulum clock"],["1761","John Harrison’s chronometer proves accurate at sea"],["1884","Greenwich is chosen as the prime meridian"],["1967","The second is defined using cesium atoms"]],
s2:[["Time zones","When railways and telegraphs linked distant cities in the 1800s, local times based on the Sun caused confusion. In 1884 an international conference chose Greenwich, in London, as the prime meridian, the starting line for longitude and world time. The world was later divided into time zones, though many countries set their own, and some use half-hour or quarter-hour offsets."],["Time and relativity","Einstein’s theory of relativity showed that time is not the same for everyone. A moving clock ticks more slowly than one at rest, and a clock in stronger gravity ticks more slowly than one in weaker gravity. These effects are tiny in daily life but have been measured with atomic clocks on airplanes and even on clocks a few centimeters higher than others."],["GPS needs relativity","GPS satellites carry atomic clocks. Because they move fast, their clocks run slow by about 7 microseconds a day; because they are in weaker gravity, they run fast by about 45 microseconds a day. Without correcting for the combined difference of about 38 microseconds a day, GPS positions would drift by kilometers each day."],["Leap seconds","Earth’s spin is slowly and irregularly changing, so atomic time and time based on Earth’s rotation drift apart. Since 1972, leap seconds have been added from time to time to keep them together, which can disrupt computers. In 2022 international scientists voted to stop adding leap seconds by 2035."],["Calendars","Calendars organize days into years. The Julian calendar, introduced by Julius Caesar in 46 BCE, had a leap year every four years. The Gregorian calendar of 1582 fixed a small error by skipping some leap years. Many cultures also keep other calendars, such as the Islamic lunar calendar and the Chinese lunisolar calendar."],["What is time?","Physicists and philosophers still debate what time really is. Why does it seem to flow only forward? Many link the arrow of time to the increase of entropy, or disorder, described by thermodynamics. In relativity, time and space are joined into a single four-dimensional spacetime."]],
sp:["The longitude prize","For centuries, sailors could find their latitude from the Sun and stars, but not their longitude, causing many shipwrecks. In 1714 the British government offered a prize for a solution. The clockmaker John Harrison spent decades building marine clocks; his H4, a large pocket watch, kept time so well on a voyage to Jamaica in 1761–1762 that it allowed longitude to be found within a few kilometers."],
g:[["Second","The basic unit of time"],["Atomic clock","A clock that keeps time using the vibrations of atoms"],["Time zone","A region that uses the same standard time"],["Leap second","An extra second added to keep clocks in step with Earth’s rotation"]],
f:["The second is defined by the vibrations of cesium atoms.","GPS satellites must correct for relativity.","Greenwich was chosen as the prime meridian in 1884."],
fun:"A clock on a high shelf ticks very slightly faster than one on the floor, because gravity is a little weaker there.",
tl:"Ang panahon ay sinusukat ng tao gamit ang Araw, Buwan, tubig, pendulum at ngayon ay atomo. Ang segundo ay batay sa pag-vibrate ng mga atomo ng cesium, at ipinakita ni Einstein na ang takbo ng panahon ay nag-iiba ayon sa bilis at grabidad."});

;
/* data/physics2-3.js */
// Physics articles, part 2.3: particle physics, antimatter and radioactivity.
ADDART({id:"particles",t:"Particle Physics",c:"Science",i:"⚛️",w:"Particle physics",r:["atom","quantum","antimatter","relat","radioact"],
p:[["The smallest pieces","Particle physics studies the tiniest known building blocks of matter and the forces between them."],["The Standard Model","A theory called the Standard Model describes 17 fundamental particles and three of the four forces of nature."],["Giant machines","Scientists study particles by smashing them together in huge machines called particle accelerators."]],
l:"Particle physics is the branch of physics that studies the fundamental particles that make up matter and radiation, and the forces through which they interact. Everything around us is made of atoms, which are made of protons, neutrons and electrons; protons and neutrons are themselves made of smaller particles called quarks. The Standard Model of particle physics, developed in the 1960s and 1970s, describes these particles with remarkable accuracy, and its last missing piece, the Higgs boson, was discovered in 2012.",
s:[["Inside the atom","In 1897 J. J. Thomson discovered the electron. In 1911 Ernest Rutherford showed that atoms have a tiny, dense nucleus, and he later identified the proton; James Chadwick discovered the neutron in 1932. In the 1960s, experiments revealed that protons and neutrons are made of three quarks each, held together by particles called gluons."],["Matter particles","The Standard Model has 12 matter particles. Six are quarks, with names such as up, down and strange; two up quarks and a down quark make a proton. Six are leptons, including the electron and three kinds of ghostly neutrinos, which pass through almost everything; trillions of them stream through your body every second."],["Force particles","Forces are carried by particles called bosons. Photons carry the electromagnetic force, which holds atoms together and produces light. Gluons carry the strong force, which binds quarks. W and Z bosons carry the weak force, responsible for some kinds of radioactivity. Gravity is not included in the Standard Model."],["The Higgs boson","In 1964 Peter Higgs and others proposed an invisible field filling all of space that gives some particles their mass. Its sign would be a particle, the Higgs boson. On 4 July 2012, scientists at CERN announced they had found it at the Large Hadron Collider. Peter Higgs and François Englert won the 2013 Nobel Prize in Physics."]],
k:[["Fundamental particles","17 in the Standard Model"],["Matter particles","6 quarks and 6 leptons"],["Forces described","Electromagnetic, strong, weak (not gravity)"],["Largest accelerator","Large Hadron Collider, 27 km ring"],["Higgs boson found","2012"]],
d:[["1897","J. J. Thomson discovers the electron"],["1911","Rutherford discovers the atomic nucleus"],["1932","Chadwick discovers the neutron"],["1964","Quarks are proposed; the Higgs mechanism is proposed"],["1995","The top quark is discovered"],["2012","The Higgs boson is discovered at CERN"]],
s2:[["The Large Hadron Collider","The Large Hadron Collider at CERN, near Geneva on the border of Switzerland and France, is the world’s largest and most powerful particle accelerator. Protons race in opposite directions around a 27-kilometer underground ring at nearly the speed of light and collide up to a billion times a second. Giant detectors record the particles that fly out."],["CERN","CERN, the European Organization for Nuclear Research, was founded in 1954 to rebuild European science after World War II and to bring countries together. Today it has more than 20 member states and scientists from over 100 countries work there. The World Wide Web was invented at CERN by Tim Berners-Lee in 1989 to help physicists share information."],["Neutrinos","Neutrinos are among the most common particles in the universe but are extremely hard to detect, since they rarely interact with anything. Huge detectors, such as Super-Kamiokande in Japan, a tank holding 50,000 tonnes of ultra-pure water deep underground, and IceCube, buried in the ice at the South Pole, catch a few of them. Experiments showed that neutrinos have a tiny mass, winning the 2015 Nobel Prize."],["Particles from space","Cosmic rays, high-energy particles from space, constantly hit Earth’s atmosphere and create showers of new particles. Before accelerators, scientists discovered new particles such as the positron and the muon in cosmic rays, using detectors on mountaintops and in balloons."],["What the Standard Model cannot explain","The Standard Model leaves big questions unanswered. It does not include gravity, cannot explain dark matter or dark energy, and does not say why there is more matter than antimatter in the universe. Physicists are searching for new particles and theories, such as supersymmetry or string theory, that might go beyond it."],["Uses of particle physics","Particle physics has led to many practical tools. Accelerators are used to treat cancer with beams of protons or X-rays, to make medical isotopes and to study materials. PET scanners in hospitals use antimatter. Detector technology has improved medical imaging, airport security and computing."]],
sp:["Up, down, charm and strange","The physicist Murray Gell-Mann named quarks after a word in James Joyce’s novel Finnegans Wake: Three quarks for Muster Mark! The six kinds, called flavors, have playful names: up, down, charm, strange, top and bottom. Quarks also have a property called color, unrelated to real colors, which describes how they interact through the strong force."],
g:[["Quark","A fundamental particle that makes up protons and neutrons"],["Lepton","A family of fundamental particles that includes the electron and neutrinos"],["Boson","A particle that carries a force"],["Particle accelerator","A machine that speeds up particles to high energies"]],
f:["Protons are made of three quarks.","The Large Hadron Collider has a 27-kilometer ring.","The World Wide Web was invented at CERN."],
fun:"Trillions of neutrinos from the Sun pass through your body every second without you noticing.",
tl:"Ang particle physics ay nag-aaral ng pinakamaliliit na bahagi ng bagay, gaya ng quark at electron, at ng mga puwersa sa pagitan nila. Natuklasan ang Higgs boson noong 2012 sa Large Hadron Collider."});

ADDART({id:"antimatter",t:"Antimatter",c:"Science",i:"🔄",w:"Antimatter",r:["particles","bigbang","quantum","energyphys"],
p:[["A mirror of matter","Antimatter is made of particles that have the same mass as ordinary particles but opposite charge."],["Mutual destruction","When matter and antimatter meet, they destroy each other in a flash of energy."],["A cosmic puzzle","The Big Bang should have made equal amounts of both, yet our universe is made almost entirely of matter."]],
l:"Antimatter is matter made of antiparticles, partners of ordinary particles with the same mass but opposite electric charge and other properties. The antiparticle of the electron is the positron, and of the proton, the antiproton. When a particle meets its antiparticle, both are destroyed, or annihilated, and their mass is turned completely into energy. Antimatter is made naturally in cosmic rays and radioactive decay, and in tiny amounts in laboratories, and it is used in medical PET scanners.",
s:[["A prediction from equations","In 1928 the British physicist Paul Dirac wrote an equation combining quantum mechanics and relativity to describe the electron. It had two solutions, one of which seemed to describe an electron with positive charge. Dirac boldly suggested that such antiparticles really exist."],["The positron","In 1932 Carl Anderson, studying cosmic rays in the United States, photographed the track of a particle with the mass of an electron but curving the opposite way in a magnetic field. It was the positron, the first antiparticle discovered. Dirac and Anderson both won Nobel Prizes for this work."],["Annihilation","When matter and antimatter meet, their entire mass turns into energy, according to Einstein’s E = mc². An electron and positron usually turn into two gamma rays flying in opposite directions. Because all the mass is converted, annihilation releases far more energy per kilogram than any chemical or nuclear reaction."],["Making antimatter","Physicists make antiprotons by smashing protons into targets at high energy. In 1995 scientists at CERN created the first atoms of antihydrogen, made of an antiproton and a positron. Since 2010, experiments have trapped antihydrogen atoms for minutes using magnetic fields, and in 2023 showed that antimatter falls downward under gravity, like ordinary matter."]],
k:[["First predicted","1928 by Paul Dirac"],["First discovered","Positron, 1932, by Carl Anderson"],["First anti-atoms","Antihydrogen, 1995, at CERN"],["Annihilation","Turns all mass into energy"],["Medical use","PET scanners"]],
d:[["1928","Dirac predicts antimatter"],["1932","Anderson discovers the positron"],["1955","The antiproton is discovered"],["1995","First antihydrogen atoms are made"],["2010","Antihydrogen atoms are trapped"],["2023","Experiment shows antimatter falls under gravity"]],
s2:[["PET scans","Positron emission tomography, or PET, is a medical imaging method that uses antimatter. Patients receive a substance containing a radioactive isotope that gives off positrons. When the positrons meet electrons in the body, they annihilate, producing gamma rays that a scanner detects, revealing activity in organs such as the brain and the growth of cancers."],["Where is all the antimatter?","Physicists believe the Big Bang should have created matter and antimatter in equal amounts, which would then have annihilated, leaving only light. Yet the universe is full of matter. Something must have favored matter slightly, perhaps by about one extra particle in a billion. Explaining this imbalance is one of the greatest unsolved problems in physics."],["Antimatter in nature","Antimatter is produced in tiny amounts all the time. Cosmic rays striking the atmosphere create it, and some radioactive substances, such as potassium-40 found in bananas and in our bodies, occasionally give off positrons. Thunderstorms have been found to produce bursts of positrons, detected by satellites."],["Is antimatter fuel possible?","Antimatter would be the most powerful fuel imaginable: a gram annihilating with a gram of matter would release about as much energy as a large nuclear bomb. But making it is extremely difficult and costly. All the antimatter ever made at CERN amounts to only billionths of a gram, far too little to power anything."],["Storing antimatter","Because antimatter is destroyed when it touches ordinary matter, it cannot be kept in a normal container. Scientists hold charged antiparticles in a vacuum using electric and magnetic fields in devices called Penning traps. In 2024 CERN researchers tested a portable trap by driving it around the laboratory site on a truck, carrying ordinary protons, as a step toward moving antiprotons to other laboratories."],["Testing nature’s symmetry","Physicists compare hydrogen and antihydrogen atoms with extreme precision, measuring the light they absorb and how they fall. Any tiny difference could reveal new physics and help explain why the universe contains more matter than antimatter. So far, they behave identically."]],
sp:["Star Trek and science","Science fiction often uses antimatter to power starships, as in Star Trek’s warp drive. While the physics of annihilation is real, the engineering is far beyond us. To fuel even a short trip, a spacecraft would need tonnes of antimatter, but humanity has made less than a millionth of a gram in total."],
g:[["Antiparticle","A particle with the same mass as another but opposite charge"],["Positron","The antiparticle of the electron"],["Annihilation","The destruction of a particle and its antiparticle, releasing energy"],["Antihydrogen","An atom made of an antiproton and a positron"]],
f:["The positron was the first antiparticle discovered, in 1932.","PET scanners in hospitals use antimatter.","Antimatter falls downward under gravity, as an experiment showed in 2023."],
fun:"Bananas give off a tiny amount of antimatter, because of the radioactive potassium they contain.",
tl:"Ang antimatter ay binubuo ng mga partikulong may parehong masa ngunit kabaligtarang karga ng karaniwang partikulo. Kapag nagtagpo ang matter at antimatter, naglalaho sila at nagiging enerhiya. Ginagamit ito sa PET scan."});

ADDART({id:"radioact",t:"Radioactivity",c:"Science",i:"☢️",w:"Radioactive decay",r:["curie","atom","nuclear","particles","fusion"],
p:[["Unstable atoms","Radioactivity happens when the nucleus of an unstable atom breaks down and gives off energy as radiation."],["Discovered in 1896","Henri Becquerel discovered radioactivity, and Marie and Pierre Curie studied it and found new radioactive elements."],["Useful and dangerous","Radiation is used in medicine, energy and dating ancient objects, but large doses are harmful to living things."]],
l:"Radioactivity, or radioactive decay, is the process by which an unstable atomic nucleus loses energy by giving off radiation. Some elements, such as uranium and radium, are naturally radioactive, and radioactive forms of common elements exist too. Radiation can be alpha particles, beta particles or gamma rays. Discovered in 1896, radioactivity revealed that atoms have an inner structure and led to nuclear power, cancer treatments, radiocarbon dating and nuclear weapons.",
s:[["Discovery","In 1896 the French physicist Henri Becquerel found that uranium salts darkened a photographic plate wrapped in black paper, even without sunlight. Marie Curie named the effect radioactivity. With Pierre Curie, she discovered two new radioactive elements, polonium and radium, in 1898. The three shared the 1903 Nobel Prize in Physics."],["Kinds of radiation","Alpha particles, made of two protons and two neutrons, are stopped by a sheet of paper or skin. Beta particles are fast electrons or positrons and can be stopped by a few millimeters of aluminum. Gamma rays are high-energy light that needs thick lead or concrete to block. Each kind affects living tissue differently."],["Half-life","Each radioactive substance decays at its own rate, measured by its half-life: the time it takes for half of the atoms in a sample to decay. Some half-lives are fractions of a second; carbon-14’s is about 5,730 years; uranium-238’s is about 4.5 billion years, roughly the age of Earth."],["Background radiation","We are all exposed to low levels of natural radiation every day, from rocks, soil, the air, cosmic rays and even our own bodies, which contain radioactive potassium and carbon. A radioactive gas called radon, which seeps from the ground in some areas, is the largest natural source for most people."]],
k:[["Discovered","1896 by Henri Becquerel"],["Main types","Alpha, beta, gamma"],["Carbon-14 half-life","About 5,730 years"],["Unit of dose","Sievert (Sv)"],["Warning symbol","The trefoil, introduced in 1946"]],
d:[["1895","Wilhelm Röntgen discovers X-rays"],["1896","Becquerel discovers radioactivity"],["1898","The Curies discover polonium and radium"],["1902","Rutherford and Soddy explain radioactive decay"],["1949","Willard Libby develops radiocarbon dating"],["1986","Chernobyl nuclear disaster"]],
s2:[["Radiocarbon dating","Living things take in carbon, including a small amount of radioactive carbon-14. When they die, they stop taking it in, and the carbon-14 slowly decays. By measuring how much remains, scientists can tell how long ago a plant or animal died, up to about 50,000 years. Willard Libby developed the method in 1949 and won the 1960 Nobel Prize in Chemistry."],["Medicine","Radiation is used to diagnose and treat disease. Radioactive tracers help doctors see how organs work, and radiotherapy uses beams of radiation to destroy cancer cells. Radiation is also used to sterilize medical equipment and to kill bacteria in some foods."],["Dangers","High doses of radiation damage living cells and DNA, causing burns, radiation sickness and an increased risk of cancer. Marie Curie died in 1934 of a blood disorder probably caused by years of exposure; her notebooks are still radioactive and are kept in lead-lined boxes. Today workers wear badges to measure their exposure."],["Nuclear accidents","In 1986 an explosion at the Chernobyl nuclear power plant in Soviet Ukraine released large amounts of radioactive material over Europe, and the area around it is still restricted. In 2011 a tsunami caused meltdowns at the Fukushima Daiichi plant in Japan. These disasters led to stricter safety rules worldwide."],["Energy from decay","Heat from radioactive decay deep inside Earth helps keep its core hot and drives volcanoes and plate movements. Spacecraft such as the Voyager probes and the Curiosity and Perseverance rovers on Mars use generators that turn the heat of decaying plutonium into electricity."],["Measuring radiation","Geiger counters detect radiation by the clicks it causes in a gas-filled tube. The effect of radiation on the body is measured in sieverts. A typical person receives about 2 to 3 millisieverts a year from natural sources; a chest X-ray gives about 0.1 millisieverts."]],
sp:["The radium craze","After radium was discovered, people were fascinated by its glow. In the early 1900s, it was added to drinks, toothpaste, cosmetics and watch dials that glowed in the dark. Many young women who painted radium watch dials, licking their brushes to make a fine point, later suffered terrible illnesses. Their court cases in the 1920s led to new safety laws for workers."],
g:[["Radioactive decay","The breakdown of an unstable atomic nucleus"],["Half-life","The time for half of a radioactive sample to decay"],["Isotope","A form of an element with a different number of neutrons"],["Sievert","A unit measuring the effect of radiation on the body"]],
f:["Radioactivity was discovered in 1896.","Carbon-14 dating works for objects up to about 50,000 years old.","Marie Curie’s notebooks are still radioactive."],
fun:"Marie Curie’s notebooks are still so radioactive that they are kept in lead-lined boxes.",
tl:"Ang radioactivity ay nangyayari kapag ang di-matatag na nukleyo ng atomo ay nagbibigay ng radiation. Natuklasan ito noong 1896, at ginagamit ito sa medisina, enerhiya at pagtukoy sa edad ng mga sinaunang bagay, ngunit mapanganib ito sa malaking dosis."});

;
/* data/physics2-4.js */
// Physics articles, part 2.4: nuclear fusion and superconductivity.
ADDART({id:"fusion",t:"Nuclear Fusion",c:"Science",i:"☀️",w:"Nuclear fusion",r:["sun","stars","nuclear","particles","energyphys"],
p:[["The power of the stars","Nuclear fusion is the process that powers the Sun and other stars, joining light atoms into heavier ones."],["Enormous energy","Fusion releases huge amounts of energy from small amounts of fuel, without producing greenhouse gases."],["A goal on Earth","Scientists around the world are working to build fusion power plants, but none yet produces electricity for the grid."]],
l:"Nuclear fusion is a reaction in which two light atomic nuclei combine to form a heavier nucleus, releasing a great deal of energy. Fusion powers the Sun and all stars, where hydrogen is fused into helium at temperatures of millions of degrees. On Earth, scientists are trying to harness fusion to generate clean electricity, using heavy forms of hydrogen as fuel. It is extremely difficult, because the fuel must be heated to over 100 million degrees and held together long enough for fusion to happen.",
s:[["How fusion works","Atomic nuclei are positively charged, so they repel each other. To fuse, they must collide at very high speeds, which requires extremely high temperatures. When light nuclei fuse, the new nucleus weighs slightly less than the original ones; the missing mass becomes energy, following Einstein’s E = mc²."],["Fusion in stars","In the Sun’s core, at about 15 million degrees Celsius and enormous pressure, hydrogen nuclei fuse into helium. Every second, the Sun turns about 600 million tonnes of hydrogen into helium, converting around 4 million tonnes of mass into energy. Larger stars fuse heavier elements, up to iron, in their cores."],["Fusion versus fission","Today’s nuclear power stations use fission, the splitting of heavy atoms such as uranium. Fusion joins light atoms instead. Fusion fuel is abundant, it produces no long-lasting high-level radioactive waste, and a fusion reactor cannot run out of control the way a fission reactor can, since the reaction stops if conditions are disturbed."],["Holding a star in a box","No material can touch fuel at 100 million degrees. Tokamaks, doughnut-shaped machines first developed in the Soviet Union in the 1950s and 1960s, hold the hot, electrically charged gas, called plasma, with powerful magnetic fields. Another method, inertial confinement, uses lasers to crush tiny fuel pellets."]],
k:[["Fuel","Deuterium and tritium (heavy hydrogen)"],["Temperature needed on Earth","Over 100 million °C"],["Main machine types","Tokamaks, stellarators, laser fusion"],["Largest project","ITER, in France"],["Ignition first achieved","December 2022, National Ignition Facility, USA"]],
d:[["1920","Arthur Eddington suggests stars are powered by fusing hydrogen"],["1939","Hans Bethe explains the fusion reactions in stars"],["1952","First hydrogen bomb tested"],["1968","Soviet tokamak achieves record results"],["1997","JET in the United Kingdom sets a fusion power record"],["2022","National Ignition Facility achieves ignition"]],
s2:[["Ignition","On 5 December 2022, the National Ignition Facility in California used 192 lasers to compress a tiny capsule of fuel, producing more fusion energy than the laser energy delivered to it, for the first time. This was called ignition. However, the lasers themselves used far more electricity than was produced, so practical power is still far off."],["ITER","ITER, being built in southern France, is a huge international tokamak backed by the European Union, China, India, Japan, South Korea, Russia and the United States. It aims to produce ten times more fusion power than the heating power put into the plasma. Its schedule has slipped; research operations are now planned to begin in the 2030s."],["Stellarators and private companies","Stellarators are twisted cousins of tokamaks; Germany’s Wendelstein 7-X has set records for long plasma pulses. Dozens of private companies, in the United States, the United Kingdom, China, Japan and elsewhere, are racing to build smaller, cheaper fusion machines, some aiming to deliver electricity in the 2030s."],["Records in Asia and Europe","China’s EAST tokamak and South Korea’s KSTAR have held hot plasmas for record lengths of time, and in 2025 EAST sustained a plasma for over 1,000 seconds. In 2023, the Joint European Torus, JET, in the United Kingdom, set a record for total fusion energy in its final experiments before closing."],["Fuel from the sea","Deuterium, a heavy form of hydrogen, can be extracted from seawater; about one in every 6,400 hydrogen atoms in water is deuterium. Tritium is rare but can be made inside a reactor from lithium. The deuterium in a liter of seawater could, in principle, provide as much energy through fusion as burning hundreds of liters of gasoline."],["Fusion bombs","Uncontrolled fusion is the source of the enormous power of hydrogen bombs, first tested in 1952. They use a fission explosion to trigger fusion. The goal of fusion energy research is the opposite: a slow, controlled, safe release of energy for peaceful use."]],
sp:["Always 30 years away","Fusion researchers sometimes joke that practical fusion power has been 30 years away for the last 70 years. The challenges are huge, but progress has been real: experiments have reached the temperatures of stars, held plasmas for minutes and, in 2022, released more fusion energy than the lasers delivered. Whether fusion will power homes by the 2040s or later remains an open question."],
g:[["Fusion","Joining light atomic nuclei to form a heavier one"],["Fission","Splitting a heavy atomic nucleus"],["Plasma","A hot gas of charged particles, the fourth state of matter"],["Tokamak","A doughnut-shaped machine that holds plasma with magnetic fields"]],
f:["The Sun converts about 4 million tonnes of mass into energy every second.","Fusion ignition was first achieved in 2022.","Deuterium for fusion can be extracted from seawater."],
fun:"Every second, the Sun converts about 4 million tonnes of matter into energy.",
tl:"Ang nuclear fusion ay pagsasama ng magagaang na nukleyo ng atomo upang bumuo ng mas mabigat, na naglalabas ng napakalaking enerhiya. Ito ang nagpapaliwanag sa Araw. Sinisikap ng mga siyentipiko na gamitin ito para sa malinis na kuryente."});

ADDART({id:"superconduct",t:"Superconductivity",c:"Science",i:"🧲",w:"Superconductivity",r:["magnet","elec","quantum","thermo"],
p:[["Zero resistance","Superconductors are materials that conduct electricity with no resistance at all when cooled below a certain temperature."],["Floating magnets","They push away magnetic fields, which can make magnets float above them."],["Used every day","Superconducting magnets are used in hospital MRI scanners, particle accelerators and some high-speed trains."]],
l:"Superconductivity is a state in which certain materials, when cooled below a critical temperature, carry electric current with absolutely no resistance and expel magnetic fields. Discovered in 1911, it allows electricity to flow forever in a loop without losing energy. Superconductors are used to make the powerful magnets in MRI scanners, particle accelerators and fusion experiments, and in some maglev trains. Scientists are searching for materials that superconduct at room temperature, which could transform technology.",
s:[["Discovery","In 1911 the Dutch physicist Heike Kamerlingh Onnes, who had learned to liquefy helium, cooled mercury to about 4 degrees above absolute zero and found that its electrical resistance suddenly vanished. He won the 1913 Nobel Prize in Physics. Many other metals were later found to superconduct at very low temperatures."],["Why resistance matters","In ordinary wires, electrons bump into atoms as they flow, losing energy as heat; this is resistance. In a superconductor, electrons pair up and move together without scattering. A current set flowing in a superconducting loop can keep circulating for years without a power source."],["The Meissner effect","In 1933 Walther Meissner and Robert Ochsenfeld found that superconductors push magnetic fields out of their interior. Because of this, a small magnet placed above a cooled superconductor floats in midair, a striking demonstration often shown in science classes."],["How it works","In 1957 John Bardeen, Leon Cooper and Robert Schrieffer explained superconductivity in metals with the BCS theory: vibrations in the material bind electrons into pairs, called Cooper pairs, which move through the metal without resistance. The theory won the 1972 Nobel Prize in Physics, making Bardeen the only person to win the physics prize twice."]],
k:[["Discovered","1911 by Heike Kamerlingh Onnes"],["Key properties","Zero resistance, expulsion of magnetic fields"],["Classic theory","BCS theory (1957)"],["High-temperature superconductors","Discovered 1986"],["Main uses","MRI scanners, particle accelerators, maglev trains"]],
d:[["1911","Kamerlingh Onnes discovers superconductivity"],["1933","The Meissner effect is discovered"],["1957","BCS theory explains superconductivity"],["1986","Bednorz and Müller discover high-temperature superconductors"],["1987","Superconductors working above the boiling point of liquid nitrogen are found"],["2023","Claims of a room-temperature superconductor, LK-99, are not confirmed"]],
s2:[["High-temperature superconductors","In 1986 Georg Bednorz and Alex Müller in Switzerland discovered that certain ceramic materials made of copper oxides superconduct at much higher temperatures. Within a year, scientists found materials that work above minus 196 degrees Celsius, the temperature of liquid nitrogen, which is far cheaper than liquid helium. Bednorz and Müller won the 1987 Nobel Prize."],["MRI scanners","Magnetic resonance imaging scanners in hospitals use superconducting coils cooled by liquid helium to create very strong, steady magnetic fields. These let doctors see detailed pictures of soft tissues such as the brain, muscles and organs without using harmful radiation. MRI is the most common everyday use of superconductivity."],["Big science","The Large Hadron Collider uses more than 1,200 superconducting magnets, cooled to 1.9 degrees above absolute zero, to steer protons around its ring. The ITER fusion experiment uses giant superconducting magnets to hold its plasma. Superconducting circuits are also at the heart of many quantum computers."],["Maglev trains","Some magnetic levitation trains use superconducting magnets to float above the track and travel without wheels touching the rails. Japan’s superconducting maglev set a world speed record of 603 kilometers per hour in 2015, and a line between Tokyo and Nagoya is under construction."],["The search for room temperature","A superconductor that worked at room temperature and normal pressure could make power lines without losses, cheaper MRI scanners and better electronics. Some hydrogen-rich materials superconduct near room temperature, but only when squeezed at enormous pressures. In 2023 claims about a material called LK-99 went viral online, but other labs found it was not a superconductor."],["Power cables","Superconducting cables can carry several times more electricity than copper cables of the same size, with almost no loss. They are being tested in cities, including in Germany, South Korea, Japan, China and the United States, to deliver power in crowded areas where there is little room for new lines."]],
sp:["A floating magnet","One of the most famous physics demonstrations is a small magnet hovering above a dark disk of high-temperature superconductor cooled with liquid nitrogen, wisps of cold vapor drifting around it. Because the superconductor expels and pins magnetic fields, the magnet stays locked in place, and it can even be spun or pushed along a track like a tiny maglev train."],
g:[["Superconductor","A material that conducts electricity with zero resistance when cold enough"],["Resistance","Opposition to the flow of electric current"],["Critical temperature","The temperature below which a material becomes superconducting"],["Meissner effect","The expulsion of magnetic fields from a superconductor"]],
f:["Superconductivity was discovered in 1911.","MRI scanners use superconducting magnets.","John Bardeen is the only person to win the Nobel Prize in Physics twice."],
fun:"A current in a superconducting loop can flow for years without any power source.",
tl:"Ang superconductivity ay kalagayan kung saan dumadaloy ang kuryente nang walang resistensya kapag pinalamig ang materyal. Ginagamit ito sa MRI scanner, particle accelerator at maglev train."});

;
/* data/ai-1.js */
// Artificial intelligence articles, part 1: the history of AI, algorithms and machine learning.
ADDART({id:"aihistory",t:"The History of Artificial Intelligence",c:"Technology",i:"🧠",w:"History of artificial intelligence",r:["ai","comp","mlearn","neural","llm"],
p:[["Can machines think?","In 1950 Alan Turing asked whether machines could think and proposed a test to find out."],["Booms and winters","AI research has gone through periods of excitement and funding, followed by disappointment, called AI winters."],["The deep learning era","Since the 2010s, neural networks trained on huge amounts of data have transformed AI."]],
l:"The history of artificial intelligence stretches from ancient myths of mechanical servants to today’s systems that can write, draw and hold conversations. As a field of science, AI began in the 1950s, when researchers set out to build machines that could reason, learn and solve problems. Progress came in waves, with bursts of optimism followed by AI winters when results fell short. Since about 2012, advances in neural networks, computing power and data have produced dramatic breakthroughs.",
s:[["Early dreams","Ancient Greek myths told of Talos, a giant bronze guardian, and many cultures imagined artificial beings. In the 1800s Ada Lovelace wrote that Charles Babbage’s Analytical Engine could work on things other than numbers, such as music, though she doubted it could originate ideas. The word robot came from Karel Čapek’s 1920 play R.U.R."],["Alan Turing","The British mathematician Alan Turing laid the foundations of computer science in 1936 with his idea of a universal machine. In 1950 he published Computing Machinery and Intelligence, proposing the imitation game, now called the Turing test: if a person chatting by text cannot tell a machine from a human, the machine might be said to think."],["The birth of AI","The term artificial intelligence was coined by John McCarthy for a summer workshop at Dartmouth College in the United States in 1956, organized with Marvin Minsky, Claude Shannon and Nathaniel Rochester. Early programs proved math theorems, played checkers and solved puzzles, and researchers predicted that human-level AI was only decades away."],["AI winters","Early AI struggled with real-world problems such as understanding language and recognizing objects, which turned out to be far harder than playing games. Funding was cut in the 1970s and again in the late 1980s, periods known as AI winters. In between, expert systems, programs encoding the rules of human specialists, were briefly popular in business."]],
k:[["Term coined","1956, by John McCarthy"],["Turing test proposed","1950"],["AI winters","Mid-1970s and late 1980s to early 1990s"],["Deep learning breakthrough","2012 (AlexNet)"],["Key pioneers","Turing, McCarthy, Minsky, Hinton, LeCun, Bengio, Fei-Fei Li"]],
d:[["1950","Turing proposes the imitation game"],["1956","Dartmouth workshop names artificial intelligence"],["1966","ELIZA chatbot is created"],["1997","IBM’s Deep Blue defeats chess champion Garry Kasparov"],["2016","DeepMind’s AlphaGo defeats Go champion Lee Sedol"],["2022","ChatGPT is released to the public"]],
s2:[["ELIZA","In 1966 Joseph Weizenbaum at MIT created ELIZA, a simple program that imitated a therapist by turning a person’s sentences back into questions. Although it understood nothing, many users felt it understood them. Weizenbaum was troubled by this and became a critic of trusting computers too much."],["Machines beat champions","In 1997 IBM’s Deep Blue defeated the world chess champion Garry Kasparov in a six-game match. In 2011 IBM’s Watson won the television quiz show Jeopardy!. In 2016 DeepMind’s AlphaGo beat Lee Sedol, one of the world’s best players of Go, a game long thought too complex for computers."],["The deep learning revolution","In 2012 a neural network called AlexNet, built by Alex Krizhevsky, Ilya Sutskever and Geoffrey Hinton, won an image-recognition contest by a huge margin, using graphics chips to train on the ImageNet database of over a million labeled images created by Fei-Fei Li’s team. This sparked a boom in deep learning across speech, vision and language."],["Language models","In 2017 Google researchers introduced the transformer, a new neural network design. It led to large language models, trained on vast amounts of text. OpenAI’s ChatGPT, released in November 2022, gained 100 million users within about two months, and similar chatbots from many companies and countries followed."],["Science and medicine","AI has begun to change science. In 2020 DeepMind’s AlphaFold predicted the 3D shapes of proteins with remarkable accuracy, solving a problem biologists had worked on for 50 years. In 2024 Demis Hassabis and John Jumper shared the Nobel Prize in Chemistry for it, and Geoffrey Hinton and John Hopfield won the Nobel Prize in Physics for foundational work on neural networks."],["Debates about the future","As AI grows more capable, people debate its effects on jobs, creativity, privacy, fairness and safety. Some researchers believe AI could one day match or exceed human abilities in most tasks; others are skeptical. Governments have begun to create laws and safety institutes to guide its development."]],
sp:["The Turing test","Turing imagined a judge exchanging typed messages with two hidden players, one human and one machine. If the judge could not reliably tell which was which, the machine would pass. Turing predicted that by about the year 2000, computers would fool judges at least some of the time. Modern chatbots can often do so, but many scientists argue that passing the test does not prove a machine truly understands."],
g:[["Artificial intelligence","Computer systems that perform tasks normally requiring human intelligence"],["AI winter","A period of reduced funding and interest in AI research"],["Expert system","A program that uses rules from human experts to solve problems"],["Deep learning","Machine learning using neural networks with many layers"]],
f:["The term artificial intelligence was coined in 1956.","Deep Blue beat the world chess champion in 1997.","Two 2024 Nobel Prizes recognized work related to AI."],
fun:"ELIZA, a chatbot from 1966, understood nothing, yet many users believed it understood them.",
tl:"Nagsimula ang larangan ng artificial intelligence noong dekada 1950, nang itanong ni Alan Turing kung kayang mag-isip ng makina. Dumaan ito sa mga panahon ng pag-asa at pagkabigo, at mula 2010s ay mabilis na umunlad dahil sa mga neural network."});

ADDART({id:"algorithm",t:"Algorithms",c:"Technology",i:"🧮",w:"Algorithm",r:["comp","alg","mlearn","internet","goldage"],
p:[["Step-by-step instructions","An algorithm is a precise set of steps for solving a problem or completing a task."],["Everywhere in computing","Every computer program is built from algorithms, from searching the web to recommending videos."],["An ancient idea","The word comes from the name of al-Khwarizmi, a mathematician in 9th-century Baghdad."]],
l:"An algorithm is a finite list of clear, step-by-step instructions for solving a problem or carrying out a task. A cooking recipe, the method for long division and directions to a friend’s house are all algorithms. In computing, algorithms tell computers exactly what to do, and they power everything from calculators and maps to search engines, social media feeds and artificial intelligence. Designing fast, correct algorithms is a central part of computer science.",
s:[["Origins","The word algorithm comes from the Latinized name of Muhammad ibn Musa al-Khwarizmi, a Persian mathematician who worked at the House of Wisdom in Baghdad around 820. His books explained how to calculate with Hindu-Arabic numerals and solve equations. Much earlier, around 300 BCE, Euclid described an algorithm for finding the greatest common divisor of two numbers that is still used today."],["What makes an algorithm","A good algorithm has clear inputs and outputs, each step is precise and unambiguous, and it finishes after a finite number of steps. It must also be correct, giving the right answer for every valid input. Algorithms can be written in everyday language, in diagrams called flowcharts or in programming languages."],["Searching and sorting","Two of the most common jobs are searching and sorting. Linear search checks items one by one. Binary search, which works on sorted lists, checks the middle item and throws away half the list each time, so it can find one name among a million in about 20 steps. Sorting algorithms, such as merge sort and quicksort, put data in order."],["Measuring speed","Computer scientists compare algorithms by how their running time grows as the input gets bigger. An algorithm that checks every pair of items in a list becomes slow very quickly as the list grows, while a smarter one may barely slow down. Choosing the right algorithm can make the difference between an answer in a second and one that would take years."]],
k:[["Named after","Al-Khwarizmi (c. 780–850)"],["Oldest known in use","Euclid’s algorithm, c. 300 BCE"],["Key tasks","Searching, sorting, finding routes, compressing data"],["Famous search engine method","PageRank (1998)"],["Important question","P versus NP, unsolved"]],
d:[["c. 300 BCE","Euclid describes his algorithm for common divisors"],["c. 820","Al-Khwarizmi writes on calculation and algebra"],["1843","Ada Lovelace publishes an algorithm for Babbage’s engine"],["1936","Alan Turing defines computation with the Turing machine"],["1959","Edsger Dijkstra publishes his shortest-path algorithm"],["1998","Google’s PageRank algorithm is described"]],
s2:[["Ada Lovelace","In 1843 Ada Lovelace published notes on Charles Babbage’s planned Analytical Engine, including a step-by-step method for calculating a series of numbers called Bernoulli numbers. It is often described as the first published computer program, written for a machine that was never completed."],["Finding the shortest route","In 1956 the Dutch computer scientist Edsger Dijkstra designed, reportedly in about 20 minutes at a café, an algorithm for finding the shortest path between two points in a network, published in 1959. Variants of it help map apps find routes and help data travel across the internet."],["Ranking the web","In 1998 Larry Page and Sergey Brin described PageRank, an algorithm that ranked web pages by how many other important pages linked to them. It helped make Google’s search engine more useful than its rivals. Modern search engines use many algorithms together."],["Recommendation algorithms","Social media apps, video sites and online shops use algorithms that learn from what people click, watch and buy to recommend more content. They can help people find things they enjoy, but critics worry they can spread misinformation, encourage endless scrolling or show people only views they already agree with."],["Fairness","Algorithms are written and trained by people and can reflect human biases. If an algorithm used for hiring, lending or policing learns from biased past data, it may treat some groups unfairly. Researchers and governments are working on ways to test algorithms for fairness and make their decisions explainable."],["Unsolved problems","Some problems seem to have no fast algorithm. The famous P versus NP question asks whether every problem whose answer can be checked quickly can also be solved quickly. It is one of the seven Millennium Prize Problems, each with a reward of one million US dollars for a solution."]],
sp:["An algorithm for every day","People follow algorithms all the time without calling them that. Tying shoelaces, brushing teeth, folding a paper airplane and solving a Rubik’s Cube all follow step-by-step methods. Teaching computers to do things means breaking tasks down into steps so precise that a machine, which cannot guess what we mean, can follow them exactly."],
g:[["Algorithm","A precise, step-by-step method for solving a problem"],["Binary search","A fast search that repeatedly halves a sorted list"],["Flowchart","A diagram showing the steps of a process"],["Program","Instructions written in a language a computer can run"]],
f:["The word algorithm comes from the name of al-Khwarizmi.","Binary search can find one item among a million in about 20 steps.","Euclid’s algorithm is more than 2,000 years old."],
fun:"Edsger Dijkstra is said to have invented his famous shortest-route algorithm in about 20 minutes at a café.",
tl:"Ang algorithm ay malinaw na serye ng mga hakbang upang malutas ang isang problema. Lahat ng computer program ay binubuo ng mga algorithm. Hinango ang salita sa pangalan ni al-Khwarizmi, isang matematiko sa Baghdad."});

ADDART({id:"mlearn",t:"Machine Learning",c:"Technology",i:"📈",w:"Machine learning",r:["ai","neural","algorithm","stats","llm"],
p:[["Learning from data","Machine learning is a kind of AI in which computers learn patterns from examples instead of being given exact rules."],["Everyday uses","It powers spam filters, voice assistants, translation apps, product recommendations and fraud detection."],["Three main types","The main kinds are supervised learning, unsupervised learning and reinforcement learning."]],
l:"Machine learning is a branch of artificial intelligence in which computer systems improve at a task by learning from data, rather than following instructions written step by step by a programmer. A machine learning model is trained on many examples, such as photos labeled cat or dog, and adjusts itself until it can make accurate predictions on new examples it has never seen. Most modern AI, from face recognition to chatbots, is built using machine learning.",
s:[["Rules versus learning","Writing exact rules for some tasks is almost impossible. How would you describe in rules every way a handwritten 7 can look? Machine learning instead shows the computer thousands of examples and lets it discover the patterns itself. The term was popularized by Arthur Samuel of IBM, whose checkers program learned to play better than he did in the 1950s."],["Supervised learning","In supervised learning, the model learns from examples that come with the right answers, called labels. Shown thousands of emails marked spam or not spam, it learns to sort new emails. It is used to recognize speech, detect diseases in scans and predict house prices."],["Unsupervised learning","In unsupervised learning, the data has no labels, and the model looks for structure on its own, such as grouping customers with similar shopping habits or finding unusual patterns that might reveal fraud. It can reveal patterns people had not noticed."],["Reinforcement learning","In reinforcement learning, a program learns by trial and error, receiving rewards for good actions and penalties for bad ones. It has learned to play video games and board games at superhuman levels and to control robots. AlphaGo used it to master the game of Go by playing millions of games against itself."]],
k:[["Main types","Supervised, unsupervised, reinforcement"],["Term popularized","1959, by Arthur Samuel"],["Needs","Data, algorithms and computing power"],["Common tools","Neural networks, decision trees, regression"],["Key risk","Learning bias from biased data"]],
d:[["1952","Arthur Samuel begins his self-improving checkers program"],["1958","Frank Rosenblatt builds the perceptron"],["1986","Backpropagation for training neural networks is popularized"],["2009","ImageNet database is released"],["2012","Deep learning wins the ImageNet contest"],["2016","AlphaGo defeats Lee Sedol"]],
s2:[["Training and testing","To check that a model really works, data is split into a training set, used to learn, and a test set, kept aside. If a model does very well on training data but poorly on new data, it has overfitted, memorizing the examples instead of learning general patterns, like a student who memorizes answers without understanding."],["Data is key","Machine learning needs large amounts of good data. If the data is too small, unrepresentative or biased, the model will be too. A face-recognition system trained mainly on photos of one group of people, for example, may work worse for others. Collecting and checking data is often the hardest part of a project."],["Everyday machine learning","Machine learning recommends songs and videos, suggests the next word as you type, translates languages, filters spam, recognizes faces to unlock phones, detects credit card fraud and predicts traffic. In science, it helps find new medicines, forecast weather and analyze telescope images."],["Weather forecasting","In the 2020s, machine learning weather models trained on decades of past weather began to rival or beat traditional forecasting systems on many measures, producing forecasts in minutes rather than hours on supercomputers. Weather services now use them alongside physics-based models."],["Explainability","Many powerful models, especially large neural networks, are hard to interpret: they give an answer but not a clear reason. In areas such as medicine, law and lending, people need to understand decisions. The field of explainable AI develops ways to show which features led a model to its output."],["Learning in medicine","Machine learning systems can analyze medical images such as X-rays, eye scans and skin photos, sometimes as accurately as specialists for specific tasks. They are used as aids to doctors, not replacements, and must be carefully tested on patients from many backgrounds before use."]],
sp:["Teaching a computer to see cats","In 2012 researchers at Google trained a huge neural network on 10 million unlabeled frames from YouTube videos. Without being told what a cat was, the network developed a detector that responded strongly to cat faces, simply because cats appeared so often. The experiment became famous as an example of a machine learning to recognize things on its own."],
g:[["Machine learning","AI that learns patterns from data"],["Model","The program produced by training, which makes predictions"],["Training data","Examples used to teach a model"],["Overfitting","When a model memorizes examples instead of learning general patterns"]],
f:["Arthur Samuel’s checkers program learned to beat him.","Most modern AI is built using machine learning.","Biased data can produce biased models."],
fun:"A Google neural network learned to recognize cats in 2012 just by watching YouTube videos.",
tl:"Ang machine learning ay uri ng AI kung saan natututo ang kompyuter mula sa mga halimbawa sa halip na sundin ang eksaktong patakaran. Ginagamit ito sa spam filter, pagsasalin ng wika at rekomendasyon ng mga video."});

;
/* data/ai-2.js */
// Artificial intelligence articles, part 2: neural networks, large language models and computer vision.
ADDART({id:"neural",t:"Neural Networks",c:"Technology",i:"🕸️",w:"Neural network (machine learning)",r:["mlearn","brain","ai","llm","compvision"],
p:[["Inspired by the brain","Artificial neural networks are computer systems loosely inspired by the networks of nerve cells in the brain."],["Layers of neurons","They are made of layers of simple units that pass numbers to one another and adjust their connections as they learn."],["Deep learning","Networks with many layers, called deep neural networks, power most of today’s advanced AI."]],
l:"An artificial neural network is a computing system made of many simple connected units, called artificial neurons, arranged in layers. Each connection has a strength, or weight, that is adjusted during training so the network learns to turn inputs, such as the pixels of an image, into useful outputs, such as the word cat. Neural networks were first proposed in the 1940s and 1950s, but only became powerful in the 2010s, with large datasets and fast computer chips. They now drive image recognition, speech recognition, translation and chatbots.",
s:[["Artificial neurons","An artificial neuron receives numbers from other neurons, multiplies each by a weight, adds them up and passes the result through a simple function that decides how strongly to fire. A single neuron is very simple, but millions or billions connected together can represent extremely complex patterns."],["Layers","A network has an input layer, which receives data, one or more hidden layers that transform it, and an output layer, which gives the answer. In an image network, early layers might detect edges, middle layers shapes such as eyes or wheels, and later layers whole objects. Deep networks may have dozens or hundreds of layers."],["How networks learn","Training starts with random weights. The network makes a guess, its error is measured, and a method called backpropagation works backward through the layers, nudging every weight slightly in the direction that reduces the error. Repeated over millions of examples, these small adjustments add up to a network that performs well."],["A long history","In 1943 Warren McCulloch and Walter Pitts described a mathematical model of neurons. In 1958 Frank Rosenblatt built the perceptron, a machine that could learn to recognize simple shapes. Interest faded when its limits were shown, but in the 1980s researchers including Geoffrey Hinton, David Rumelhart and Ronald Williams popularized backpropagation, reviving the field."]],
k:[["First model","McCulloch and Pitts, 1943"],["Training method","Backpropagation and gradient descent"],["Hardware","Graphics processing units (GPUs) and AI chips"],["Famous types","Convolutional networks, recurrent networks, transformers"],["Turing Award","2018: Hinton, LeCun and Bengio"]],
d:[["1943","McCulloch and Pitts model artificial neurons"],["1958","Rosenblatt builds the perceptron"],["1986","Backpropagation paper by Rumelhart, Hinton and Williams"],["1989","Yann LeCun trains networks to read handwritten digits"],["2012","AlexNet triggers the deep learning boom"],["2017","The transformer design is introduced"]],
s2:[["Convolutional networks","Convolutional neural networks, developed by Yann LeCun and others, scan an image with small filters to detect patterns wherever they appear. In the 1990s LeCun’s networks read handwritten numbers on bank checks. Today convolutional networks help recognize faces, read medical scans and guide self-driving cars."],["Recurrent networks and transformers","Language and speech come in sequences. Recurrent neural networks process sequences one step at a time, remembering what came before. In 2017 the transformer design replaced this with a mechanism called attention, which lets the network look at all words in a sentence at once and weigh how they relate. Transformers now power most language and many image models."],["Why GPUs matter","Training a neural network means doing an enormous number of multiplications. Graphics processing units, first designed for video games, can do thousands of these at once. Using GPUs, and later specialized AI chips, made it possible to train much bigger networks, and AI training now takes place in huge data centers."],["The godfathers of deep learning","Geoffrey Hinton, Yann LeCun and Yoshua Bengio kept working on neural networks when many others had given up. In 2018 they shared the Turing Award, often called the Nobel Prize of computing. In 2024 Hinton shared the Nobel Prize in Physics with John Hopfield for foundational discoveries that enabled machine learning with artificial neural networks."],["Brains and networks","Artificial neural networks are only loosely like the brain. The human brain has about 86 billion neurons and trillions of connections, uses about 20 watts of power and learns from relatively few examples. Artificial networks need vast amounts of data and energy. Neuroscientists and AI researchers continue to learn from each other."],["Black boxes","It is hard to know exactly why a neural network makes a particular decision, because its knowledge is spread across millions or billions of weights. Researchers in the field of interpretability are developing tools to look inside networks and understand the features and concepts they have learned."]],
sp:["Reading the mail","One of the first successful uses of neural networks was reading handwritten numbers. In the late 1980s and 1990s, Yann LeCun and colleagues at Bell Labs trained a convolutional network called LeNet to recognize digits. By the late 1990s, systems based on it were reading a large share of the handwritten checks processed in the United States, years before the deep learning boom."],
g:[["Artificial neuron","A simple unit that combines inputs and passes on a signal"],["Weight","A number that sets the strength of a connection"],["Backpropagation","A method for adjusting weights to reduce errors"],["GPU","A graphics processing unit, a chip that does many calculations at once"]],
f:["The first mathematical model of a neuron was published in 1943.","Graphics chips made deep learning practical.","The human brain uses about 20 watts of power."],
fun:"The human brain runs on about 20 watts, roughly the power of a dim light bulb.",
tl:"Ang neural network ay sistemang pang-kompyuter na hango nang bahagya sa mga nerve cell ng utak. Binubuo ito ng mga layer ng simpleng yunit na natututo mula sa datos, at ito ang nagpapaandar sa karamihan ng makabagong AI."});

ADDART({id:"llm",t:"Large Language Models",c:"Technology",i:"💬",w:"Large language model",r:["ai","neural","mlearn","aisafety","langevo"],
p:[["Models of language","Large language models are AI systems trained on enormous amounts of text to understand and generate language."],["Chatbots","They power chatbots and assistants that can answer questions, write, translate, summarize and help with computer code."],["Powerful but imperfect","They can be very useful, but they can also make mistakes and state false information confidently."]],
l:"A large language model, or LLM, is a type of artificial intelligence trained on huge collections of text, such as books, articles and websites, to predict and generate language. Built on the transformer neural network design, LLMs learn patterns in grammar, facts and reasoning from their training data. Since the release of ChatGPT in 2022, chatbots based on LLMs, made by companies and research groups in many countries, have been used by hundreds of millions of people for writing, learning, research and programming.",
s:[["Predicting the next word","At its core, a language model is trained to predict the next piece of text, called a token, given the words before it. By practicing this on trillions of words, the model learns a great deal about language and the world. When it answers a question, it generates its reply one token at a time."],["Transformers","LLMs are based on the transformer, introduced by researchers at Google in a 2017 paper titled Attention Is All You Need. Its attention mechanism lets the model consider how every word in a passage relates to every other, capturing meaning across long stretches of text. Models have grown from millions to hundreds of billions of adjustable weights."],["From model to assistant","After the first stage of training on text, models are fine-tuned to follow instructions and be helpful. A common method, reinforcement learning from human feedback, has people rate different answers so the model learns which are better. Developers also train models to refuse harmful requests and to follow guidelines."],["What they can do","LLMs can answer questions, explain ideas, draft essays and emails, summarize documents, translate between many languages, solve math problems and write computer code. Some can also understand images and audio. Newer systems, sometimes called agents, can use tools such as web search and carry out multi-step tasks."]],
k:[["Based on","The transformer (2017)"],["Trained on","Very large collections of text"],["Basic task","Predicting the next token"],["Widespread public use","Since late 2022"],["Known problem","Hallucination, stating false information"]],
d:[["2017","Transformer paper is published"],["2018","Google’s BERT and OpenAI’s first GPT are released"],["2020","GPT-3 shows strong abilities from scale"],["2022","ChatGPT is released"],["2023","Many companies release rival chatbots"],["2024","EU adopts the AI Act, including rules for general-purpose models"]],
s2:[["Hallucinations","Because LLMs generate text that sounds likely, they sometimes produce statements that are false, invent quotations or cite sources that do not exist. These errors are called hallucinations. Users are advised to check important facts, and developers are working on ways to make models more accurate and to have them admit uncertainty."],["Many models","Large language models are built by companies and research groups around the world, including in the United States, China, Europe and elsewhere. Some, such as OpenAI’s ChatGPT, Google’s Gemini and Anthropic’s Claude, are offered as services; others are released openly so anyone can download and adapt them."],["Learning and schools","LLMs can act as tutors, explaining topics step by step and answering follow-up questions. They also raise concerns about cheating and over-reliance. Schools and universities are developing rules on when AI tools may be used and teaching students how to use them responsibly and check their output."],["Energy and computing","Training the largest models requires thousands of specialized chips running for weeks or months in data centers that use large amounts of electricity and water for cooling. Researchers are working on more efficient models and chips, and companies are investing in new power sources."],["Copyright and data","LLMs learn from text written by people, raising questions about copyright, credit and payment for authors, artists and news organizations whose work was used in training. Several court cases and new laws are addressing these issues, and the debate continues."],["Many languages","Most training text has been in English and a few other widely used languages, so models often work less well in languages with less online text. Projects around the world are gathering text and building models for languages such as Swahili, Tagalog, Bengali, Yoruba and Indigenous languages."]],
sp:["What is a token?","Language models do not read whole words but tokens, pieces of text that may be a word, part of a word or a punctuation mark. A common English word might be one token, while a rare or long word may be split into several. On average, an English word is about one and a third tokens. Models have a limit, called a context window, on how many tokens they can consider at once."],
g:[["Large language model","An AI system trained on huge amounts of text to understand and generate language"],["Token","A small piece of text processed by a language model"],["Hallucination","A false statement produced confidently by an AI model"],["Fine-tuning","Further training of a model for a particular purpose"]],
f:["Large language models generate text one token at a time.","The transformer was introduced in 2017.","LLMs can state false information confidently, so facts should be checked."],
fun:"A language model reads text in pieces called tokens; an average English word is about one and a third tokens.",
tl:"Ang large language model ay uri ng AI na sinanay sa napakaraming teksto upang umunawa at bumuo ng wika. Ito ang nagpapaandar sa mga chatbot. Kapaki-pakinabang ito, ngunit maaari ring magkamali, kaya dapat suriin ang mahahalagang impormasyon."});

ADDART({id:"compvision",t:"Computer Vision",c:"Technology",i:"👁️",w:"Computer vision",r:["neural","mlearn","camera","eye","robot"],
p:[["Teaching computers to see","Computer vision is the field of AI that helps computers understand images and videos."],["All around us","It unlocks phones by face, reads text in photos, sorts packages and helps cars detect pedestrians."],["From pixels to meaning","A computer sees an image as a grid of numbers and must learn to find objects, faces and actions in them."]],
l:"Computer vision is a field of artificial intelligence that enables computers to interpret and understand visual information from the world, such as photographs, videos and medical scans. To a computer, an image is just a grid of numbers representing the color of each pixel. Computer vision systems learn to turn these numbers into meaning: recognizing objects, reading text, tracking movement and measuring shapes. Since the 2010s, deep neural networks have made computer vision far more accurate.",
s:[["Seeing is hard","Humans recognize a friend’s face in an instant, but this is extremely hard for computers. The same object can look very different depending on light, angle, distance and what is hiding part of it. Early researchers in the 1960s thought teaching computers to see might be a summer project; it took decades."],["Main tasks","Image classification decides what is in a picture. Object detection finds and outlines several objects, such as cars, people and traffic lights. Segmentation labels every pixel, separating sky from road. Other tasks include recognizing faces, reading text, estimating depth and tracking moving objects in video."],["How it works today","Modern systems use convolutional neural networks and, increasingly, vision transformers, trained on millions of labeled images. Early layers detect edges and colors, later layers detect shapes and parts, and the final layers recognize whole objects. ImageNet, a database of over 14 million labeled images, was key to their development."],["Everyday uses","Computer vision unlocks smartphones by face, suggests tags in photo albums, reads car license plates, scans barcodes and checks products on factory lines for defects. Farmers use drones with cameras to monitor crops, and translation apps read signs and menus in other languages through a phone camera."]],
k:[["Key database","ImageNet, over 14 million images"],["Main tasks","Classification, detection, segmentation, tracking"],["Main tools","Convolutional networks, vision transformers"],["Breakthrough","2012, AlexNet"],["Concerns","Privacy and surveillance, bias"]],
d:[["1966","MIT’s Summer Vision Project begins"],["1989","Neural networks read handwritten digits"],["2001","Viola–Jones method enables fast face detection"],["2009","ImageNet is released"],["2012","AlexNet wins the ImageNet contest"],["2015","Computers surpass human error rates on the ImageNet test"]],
s2:[["Medical imaging","Computer vision systems help doctors analyze X-rays, CT scans, MRI images, eye scans and skin photos, spotting signs of disease such as tumors, fractures and diabetic eye disease. In some tests they match specialists on narrow tasks. They are used to assist doctors and must be checked carefully in real clinics."],["Self-driving cars","Autonomous vehicles use cameras, often together with radar and laser sensors called lidar, to see lanes, signs, other vehicles and pedestrians. Robotaxi services now operate in some cities in the United States and China, but handling every situation safely, such as bad weather and unusual events, remains challenging."],["Faces and privacy","Face recognition can find a person in photos or video by comparing their face with stored images. It is used to unlock phones and at some airport gates, but its use for public surveillance raises serious privacy concerns. Studies have found some systems less accurate for certain groups, and some cities and countries have restricted its use."],["Seeing the planet","Satellites take images of the whole Earth every day. Computer vision helps analyze them to track deforestation, monitor floods and wildfires, count buildings after disasters, map crops and even find illegal fishing boats. It helps scientists and aid groups respond quickly."],["Images that fool machines","Researchers have shown that tiny changes to an image, invisible to people, can make a vision system misidentify it completely, for example seeing a panda as a gibbon. Stickers placed on road signs have fooled some systems. Making computer vision robust against such tricks is important for safety."],["Generating images","Computer vision now works in reverse too: AI image generators create pictures from text descriptions. They are trained on huge collections of images and captions. They raise questions about copyright, about artists’ livelihoods and about fake images, sometimes called deepfakes, that could mislead people."]],
sp:["The summer project","In 1966, at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology, Seymour Papert launched the Summer Vision Project, hoping students could build a significant part of a visual system in a single summer. The goal turned out to be far harder than expected. Researchers often tell the story to show how difficult it is to teach machines what humans do effortlessly."],
g:[["Pixel","A tiny dot that is the smallest part of a digital image"],["Object detection","Finding and locating objects in an image"],["Segmentation","Labeling each pixel of an image by what it shows"],["Lidar","A sensor that measures distance using laser light"]],
f:["To a computer, an image is a grid of numbers.","ImageNet contains over 14 million labeled images.","Tiny invisible changes can fool computer vision systems."],
fun:"In 1966 researchers thought teaching a computer to see could be done in a single summer.",
tl:"Ang computer vision ay larangan ng AI na tumutulong sa kompyuter na maunawaan ang mga larawan at video. Ginagamit ito sa pagbubukas ng telepono gamit ang mukha, pagbasa ng teksto sa larawan at pagtulong sa mga sasakyan na makakita ng tao."});

;
/* data/ai-3.js */
// Artificial intelligence and computing articles, part 3: AI safety and ethics, and quantum computing.
ADDART({id:"aisafety",t:"AI Safety and Ethics",c:"Technology",i:"⚖️",w:"AI safety",r:["ai","llm","hrights","law","algorithm"],
p:[["Using AI responsibly","AI ethics asks how artificial intelligence should be designed and used fairly, honestly and safely."],["Real risks today","Concerns include bias, privacy, misinformation, job changes and misuse of AI tools."],["Looking ahead","AI safety researchers also study how to keep increasingly powerful AI systems reliable and under human control."]],
l:"AI safety and ethics is the study of how to make sure artificial intelligence benefits people and avoids causing harm. Ethics covers questions of fairness, privacy, transparency, responsibility and the effects of AI on work and society. Safety research focuses on making AI systems behave reliably and as intended, resisting misuse and remaining under meaningful human control as they become more capable. Governments, companies, researchers and international bodies are all developing rules and practices for responsible AI.",
s:[["Bias and fairness","AI systems learn from data, and data can reflect past discrimination. Systems used to screen job applicants, assess loans or predict crime have sometimes treated people unfairly based on race, gender or other characteristics. Researchers test systems for bias, use more representative data and design methods to reduce unfair outcomes."],["Privacy and surveillance","AI can analyze huge amounts of personal data, from photos and location to shopping habits. Face recognition can identify people in crowds. This raises concerns about privacy and surveillance by companies and governments. Laws such as the European Union’s General Data Protection Regulation give people some control over their data."],["Misinformation and deepfakes","AI can generate realistic text, images, audio and video, including deepfakes that show people saying or doing things they never did. These can be used to spread false information, commit fraud or harass people. Tools to detect AI-generated content and to label it, such as digital watermarks, are being developed."],["Alignment","Alignment research aims to make AI systems pursue the goals their designers and users actually intend, rather than finding shortcuts or behaving in unexpected ways. As AI systems become more capable and act more independently, researchers study how to test them, understand their inner workings and make sure they follow human values and instructions."]],
k:[["Main concerns","Bias, privacy, misinformation, safety, jobs, misuse"],["Key EU law","The AI Act (adopted 2024)"],["International summits","Bletchley Park 2023, Seoul 2024, Paris 2025"],["Research areas","Alignment, interpretability, robustness, evaluation"],["UNESCO guidance","Recommendation on the Ethics of AI (2021)"]],
d:[["1942","Isaac Asimov publishes his Three Laws of Robotics in a story"],["2018","EU’s data protection law (GDPR) takes effect"],["2019","OECD adopts principles on AI"],["2021","UNESCO adopts its Recommendation on the Ethics of AI"],["2023","First international AI Safety Summit at Bletchley Park"],["2024","EU adopts the AI Act"]],
s2:[["Asimov’s laws","In 1942 the science fiction writer Isaac Asimov introduced the Three Laws of Robotics: a robot must not harm a human, must obey humans unless that causes harm, and must protect itself unless that conflicts with the first two laws. His stories showed how such simple rules could lead to unexpected problems, a theme that still shapes discussions of AI safety."],["Laws and rules","The European Union’s AI Act, adopted in 2024, sorts AI uses by risk, banning some, such as social scoring by governments, and setting strict rules for high-risk uses such as in hiring and medicine. China, the United States, the United Kingdom, Canada, Brazil, India and many other countries have issued their own rules or guidelines, and approaches differ."],["International cooperation","The first international AI Safety Summit was held at Bletchley Park in the United Kingdom in 2023, where the codebreakers of World War II worked. Further summits followed in Seoul in 2024 and Paris in 2025. Several countries have set up AI safety institutes to test advanced models, and the United Nations has created an advisory body on AI."],["Jobs and the economy","AI can automate tasks in many jobs, from customer service and translation to programming and design, while creating new jobs and raising productivity. Economists disagree about how quickly and how much work will change. Many experts call for training and education to help workers adapt."],["Debates about long-term risk","Some prominent researchers warn that very advanced AI could pose serious risks if it becomes difficult to control, and have called for such risks to be taken as seriously as pandemics. Others argue that these concerns are exaggerated or distract from present harms such as bias and misinformation. The debate is active among scientists, companies and governments."],["Responsible development","AI developers publish safety policies, test models for dangerous capabilities before release, invite outside researchers to probe for weaknesses, a practice called red-teaming, and build in safeguards against misuse. Critics say voluntary measures are not enough and that independent oversight is needed."]],
sp:["The Three Laws of Robotics","Isaac Asimov’s laws first appeared in his 1942 short story Runaround. First: a robot may not injure a human being or, through inaction, allow a human being to come to harm. Second: a robot must obey orders given by humans, except where they conflict with the First Law. Third: a robot must protect its own existence, as long as this does not conflict with the first two laws. Real AI cannot simply be programmed with such rules, but they started a lasting conversation."],
g:[["AI ethics","The study of right and wrong in designing and using AI"],["Bias","Unfair favoring of some people or outcomes over others"],["Deepfake","A realistic fake image, video or audio made with AI"],["Alignment","Making AI systems pursue the goals humans intend"]],
f:["Isaac Asimov wrote the Three Laws of Robotics in 1942.","The EU adopted the AI Act in 2024.","The first AI Safety Summit was held at Bletchley Park in 2023."],
fun:"The first international AI Safety Summit was held at Bletchley Park, where Alan Turing helped break wartime codes.",
tl:"Ang AI safety at ethics ay pag-aaral kung paano gagawing patas, ligtas at kapaki-pakinabang ang artificial intelligence. Kabilang sa mga alalahanin ang bias, privacy, maling impormasyon at epekto sa trabaho."});

ADDART({id:"quantumcomp",t:"Quantum Computing",c:"Technology",i:"🔮",w:"Quantum computing",r:["quantum","comp","chip","superconduct","crypto"],
p:[["A new kind of computer","Quantum computers use the strange rules of quantum physics to process information in new ways."],["Qubits","Instead of bits that are 0 or 1, they use qubits, which can be in a combination of both at once."],["Still experimental","Today’s quantum computers are small and error-prone, but they could one day solve problems beyond ordinary computers."]],
l:"A quantum computer is a machine that uses principles of quantum mechanics, such as superposition and entanglement, to perform calculations. Ordinary computers store information as bits, each either 0 or 1. Quantum computers use quantum bits, or qubits, which can exist in combinations of 0 and 1. For certain problems, such as simulating molecules or breaking some codes, a large, reliable quantum computer could be vastly faster than any ordinary computer. Building one is extremely difficult, and current machines are still experimental.",
s:[["Bits and qubits","A classical bit is like a light switch, either off or on. A qubit can be in a superposition, a blend of 0 and 1 at the same time, until it is measured, when it settles into one value. Many qubits together can represent an enormous number of possibilities at once, which a quantum algorithm can use to find answers."],["Entanglement","Qubits can be entangled, meaning their states are linked so that measuring one immediately tells you about the other, no matter how far apart they are. Einstein called this spooky action at a distance. Entanglement lets quantum computers process information in ways ordinary computers cannot efficiently copy."],["How they are built","Qubits can be made in several ways: tiny superconducting circuits cooled to near absolute zero, single charged atoms called ions held in electric fields, neutral atoms trapped by lasers, particles of light, or special defects in crystals. Each approach has strengths and weaknesses, and companies and universities around the world are pursuing them."],["The problem of errors","Qubits are fragile: heat, vibration and stray signals can destroy their delicate quantum states in a fraction of a second, a problem called decoherence. Quantum error correction combines many physical qubits into one more reliable logical qubit. Recent experiments have shown error correction improving as more qubits are added, an important milestone."]],
k:[["Basic unit","The qubit"],["Key effects","Superposition, entanglement, interference"],["Proposed by","Richard Feynman and others, early 1980s"],["Famous algorithms","Shor’s algorithm (factoring), Grover’s algorithm (search)"],["Main challenge","Errors and decoherence"]],
d:[["1981","Richard Feynman suggests quantum computers to simulate nature"],["1985","David Deutsch describes a universal quantum computer"],["1994","Peter Shor publishes his factoring algorithm"],["1998","First small quantum computers using a few qubits"],["2019","Google claims quantum advantage on a specialized task"],["2024","Experiments show error correction improving with more qubits"]],
s2:[["Feynman’s idea","In 1981 the physicist Richard Feynman pointed out that simulating quantum systems, such as molecules, on ordinary computers becomes impossibly hard as they grow larger, because the number of possibilities explodes. He suggested building computers that work by quantum rules themselves to simulate nature."],["Shor’s algorithm","In 1994 Peter Shor showed that a large quantum computer could factor huge numbers into primes exponentially faster than known methods on ordinary computers. Much internet security relies on factoring being hard, so this could break widely used encryption. Governments have begun switching to new post-quantum encryption that quantum computers are not expected to crack; the United States published standards for it in 2024."],["Quantum advantage","In 2019 Google reported that its 53-qubit Sycamore processor performed a specialized calculation in about 200 seconds that it estimated would take the best supercomputer thousands of years, a claim others disputed. Teams in China and elsewhere have reported similar demonstrations. These tasks have no practical use yet but show quantum effects at work."],["What they could do","Researchers hope that future quantum computers could simulate molecules and materials to help design new medicines, batteries, fertilizers and solar cells, solve certain optimization problems and advance physics research. For most everyday tasks, such as browsing the web or word processing, ordinary computers will remain better."],["Quantum computers around the world","Companies such as IBM, Google, Quantinuum, IonQ and others, along with research programs in China, Europe, Japan, Canada, Australia and India, are building quantum computers. Some can be used over the internet. Machines have grown from a few qubits to hundreds or more, though most are still noisy."],["The quantum year","The United Nations declared 2025 the International Year of Quantum Science and Technology, marking 100 years since the development of quantum mechanics by Werner Heisenberg, Erwin Schrödinger and others. It celebrated how quantum physics underlies technologies from lasers and computer chips to MRI scanners and, in the future, quantum computers."]],
sp:["Colder than space","Many quantum computers use superconducting qubits that must be cooled to about one hundredth of a degree above absolute zero, colder than outer space. They sit inside dilution refrigerators, which look like golden chandeliers made of layered plates and coiled tubes, hanging from the ceiling. Only the bottom stage, holding a small chip, reaches the coldest temperature."],
g:[["Qubit","A quantum bit that can be in a combination of 0 and 1"],["Superposition","Being in a combination of states at the same time"],["Entanglement","A link between quantum particles so that their states are connected"],["Decoherence","The loss of quantum behavior caused by disturbances"]],
f:["Qubits can be in a combination of 0 and 1 at once.","Shor’s algorithm could break much of today’s encryption on a large quantum computer.","2025 was the International Year of Quantum Science and Technology."],
fun:"Many quantum computers run colder than outer space.",
tl:"Ang quantum computer ay gumagamit ng mga tuntunin ng quantum physics upang magproseso ng impormasyon. Gumagamit ito ng qubit na maaaring 0 at 1 nang sabay. Eksperimental pa ito ngunit maaaring lumutas ng mga problemang hindi kaya ng karaniwang kompyuter."});

;
/* data/living-1.js */
// Animal Kingdom articles, living part 1: giant pandas, cheetahs, camels and hippos.
ADDART({id:"panda",t:"Giant Pandas",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐼",w:"Giant panda",r:["bear","bamboo","chinacountry","eco"],
p:[["A bamboo-eating bear","The giant panda is a black-and-white bear that lives in the mountain forests of central China and eats almost nothing but bamboo."],["Back from the brink","Conservation work has helped panda numbers grow, and in 2016 the IUCN changed its status from Endangered to Vulnerable."],["A symbol of conservation","The panda is one of the world’s best-known animals and the logo of the conservation group WWF."]],
l:"The giant panda is a bear native to the mountains of central China, in the provinces of Sichuan, Shaanxi and Gansu. Famous for its black-and-white coat, it feeds almost entirely on bamboo, eating for up to 14 hours a day. Habitat loss once pushed it close to extinction, but protected reserves, bamboo corridors and breeding programs have helped the wild population rise to around 1,900 adults. China lends pandas to zoos around the world as a sign of friendship, a practice known as panda diplomacy.",
s:[["Built for bamboo","Pandas belong to the order of meat-eating mammals and have the short gut of a carnivore, yet bamboo makes up about 99 percent of their diet. Because bamboo gives little energy and is hard to digest, an adult may eat 12 to 38 kilograms a day. Pandas save energy by moving slowly and resting a lot."],["The panda’s thumb","Pandas grip bamboo with a special enlarged wrist bone that works like a thumb, letting them hold stems while they strip off leaves with their teeth. Their strong jaws and broad, flat back teeth crush tough stalks."],["Life in the mountains","Pandas live alone in cool, damp bamboo forests between about 1,200 and 3,400 meters high. They do not hibernate, but move to lower slopes in winter. They communicate with scent marks and calls such as bleats, honks and barks."],["Tiny cubs","A newborn panda weighs only about 100 to 200 grams, about 1/900 of its mother’s weight, and is pink, blind and nearly hairless. Mothers often give birth to twins in captivity but usually raise only one in the wild. Cubs stay with their mothers for about two years."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Ailuropoda melanoleuca"],["Range","Mountains of central China"],["Diet","About 99% bamboo"],["Wild population","About 1,900 adults (2014 survey)"],["IUCN status","Vulnerable"]],
d:[["1869","French missionary Armand David describes the giant panda for Western science"],["1961","WWF adopts the panda as its logo"],["1963","China opens its first panda reserve, Wolong"],["2016","Giant panda moved from Endangered to Vulnerable"],["2021","China creates Giant Panda National Park"]],
s2:[["Why black and white?","Scientists think the panda’s coloring helps it hide: the white parts blend with snow and the black parts with shadows in the forest. The dark eye patches and ears may help pandas recognize each other and signal to rivals."],["Threats","Farming, logging and roads broke panda forests into small, isolated patches, making it hard for pandas to find mates and fresh bamboo. Bamboo also flowers and dies off all at once every few decades, which once caused pandas to starve when they could not move to new areas."],["Saving pandas","China has created more than 60 panda reserves and in 2021 combined many into the Giant Panda National Park, larger than the island of Puerto Rico. Corridors of planted forest link isolated groups. Breeding centers, such as Chengdu’s, have raised hundreds of pandas, and some have been released into the wild."],["Panda diplomacy","China has long given or lent pandas to other countries as goodwill gifts. In 1972, after US President Richard Nixon visited China, two pandas named Ling-Ling and Hsing-Hsing were sent to the National Zoo in Washington. Today pandas are loaned to zoos in many countries, and cubs born abroad usually return to China."],["Pandas’ relatives","Genetic studies show that the giant panda is a true bear, related to brown bears and polar bears. The red panda, a small, reddish animal of the Himalayas with a ringed tail, shares the name and a similar false thumb but is not closely related; it belongs to its own family."],["An umbrella species","Protecting pandas also protects their whole forest home, including golden snub-nosed monkeys, takins, red pandas and many rare birds and plants. Animals like this, whose protection helps many others, are called umbrella species."]],
sp:["Eating all day","A wild panda spends up to 14 hours a day eating and most of the rest sleeping. Because it digests only about a fifth of what it eats, it produces a great deal of droppings, sometimes more than 100 a day. Scientists collect these droppings to study pandas’ diet, health and DNA without disturbing them."],
g:[["Carnivore","An animal that belongs to the meat-eating order, though some, like pandas, eat plants"],["Reserve","A protected area for wildlife"],["Umbrella species","A species whose protection also protects many others"],["Corridor","A strip of habitat linking separate areas"]],
f:["Giant pandas eat bamboo for up to 14 hours a day.","A newborn panda weighs about as much as a small apple.","The red panda is not closely related to the giant panda."],
fun:"A newborn giant panda is about 900 times smaller than its mother.",
tl:"Ang giant panda ay osong itim at puti na nakatira sa kabundukan ng gitnang Tsina at kumakain halos puro kawayan. Dahil sa pangangalaga, dumami ang bilang nito at hindi na ito itinuturing na endangered."});

ADDART({id:"cheetah",t:"Cheetahs",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐆",w:"Cheetah",r:["lion","tiger","kenyacountry","eco"],
p:[["The fastest land animal","The cheetah can sprint at up to about 100 kilometers per hour, faster than any other land animal."],["Built for speed","Its light body, long legs, flexible spine and long tail make it a perfect sprinter."],["In danger","Only about 7,000 cheetahs remain in the wild, mostly in eastern and southern Africa."]],
l:"The cheetah is a large wild cat of the African savannas and grasslands, famous as the fastest land animal. In short bursts it can reach speeds of about 100 kilometers per hour, accelerating faster than most sports cars. Cheetahs hunt by day, sprinting after gazelles and other antelopes. Once found across much of Africa and southwestern Asia, they have disappeared from most of their former range, and about 7,000 remain in the wild. A tiny population of Asiatic cheetahs survives in Iran.",
s:[["Speed machine","A cheetah’s spine bends like a spring, stretching its stride to about 7 meters. Large nostrils, lungs and heart take in extra oxygen. Its claws do not fully retract, gripping the ground like running spikes, and its long tail works like a rudder for sharp turns. A chase usually lasts less than a minute before the cat overheats and tires."],["Hunting","Cheetahs rely on sharp eyesight to spot prey, often from a termite mound or tree. They creep close, then sprint, trip the prey with a paw and seize its throat. Because they are lighter than lions and hyenas, they often lose their kills to these stronger predators and must eat quickly."],["Not like other big cats","Cheetahs cannot roar; instead they chirp, purr, hiss and growl. Their small, rounded heads and black tear marks running from their eyes to their mouths help reduce glare from the Sun. Unlike lions and leopards, they hunt mostly in daylight to avoid competition."],["Family life","Females live alone and raise their cubs, usually three to five, by themselves. Cubs have a fluffy gray mantle of fur along their backs that may help disguise them as honey badgers, fierce animals that predators avoid. Brothers often stay together for life in small groups called coalitions."]],
k:[["Top speed","About 100 km/h in short bursts"],["Scientific name","Acinonyx jubatus"],["Wild population","About 7,000"],["Range","Mainly eastern and southern Africa; a few in Iran"],["IUCN status","Vulnerable"]],
d:[["c. 3000 BCE","Cheetahs are tamed for hunting in ancient Egypt and Asia"],["1500s","Mughal emperor Akbar is said to keep about 1,000 hunting cheetahs"],["1952","Cheetahs are declared extinct in India"],["2001","Iran launches a program to save the Asiatic cheetah"],["2022","Cheetahs from Namibia are released in Kuno National Park, India"]],
s2:[["Cheetahs and people","For thousands of years, rulers in Egypt, Persia and India kept tamed cheetahs to hunt antelopes, bringing them to the field hooded like falcons. The cheetah’s name comes from a Hindi word, chita, meaning spotted. In the 1500s the Mughal emperor Akbar was said to have kept about a thousand."],["Threats","Cheetahs need huge areas to hunt, but farms and towns have broken up their habitat. They are killed by farmers protecting livestock, struck by cars, and cubs are captured illegally for the pet trade. Because cheetahs have low genetic diversity, they may also be more vulnerable to disease."],["Helping farmers and cheetahs","In Namibia, home to one of the largest wild populations, conservation groups give farmers trained guard dogs, such as Anatolian shepherds, to protect their herds. Fewer livestock are lost, so fewer cheetahs are shot. Most of Namibia’s cheetahs live on farmland rather than in parks."],["Asiatic cheetahs","The Asiatic cheetah once roamed from the Arabian Peninsula to India. Today fewer than 30 are thought to survive, all in the deserts of central Iran, making it one of the rarest big cats on Earth."],["Return to India","Cheetahs died out in India by the mid-1900s. In 2022 and 2023, cheetahs from Namibia and South Africa were moved to Kuno National Park in central India in the first intercontinental move of a large carnivore. Several cubs have been born, though some adults and cubs died, and the project is closely watched."],["Fast but fragile","Cheetahs are built for speed rather than strength. Their light bones and small teeth leave them poorly armed, and fewer than half of their chases succeed. Cub survival in the wild is low, especially in places with many lions and hyenas."]],
sp:["Faster than a car","A cheetah can go from standing still to about 95 kilometers per hour in roughly three seconds, as fast as many high-performance cars. During a sprint its feet touch the ground only briefly, and for parts of each stride all four feet are in the air. Scientists have studied cheetah movement to design faster, more agile running robots."],
g:[["Savanna","Tropical grassland with scattered trees"],["Coalition","A small group of male cheetahs, often brothers, that live together"],["Genetic diversity","Variety in the genes of a population"],["Predator","An animal that hunts other animals"]],
f:["The cheetah is the fastest land animal.","Cheetahs cannot roar, but they can purr and chirp.","Fewer than 30 Asiatic cheetahs may survive, all in Iran."],
fun:"Cheetah cubs’ fluffy fur may help them look like fierce honey badgers.",
tl:"Ang cheetah ang pinakamabilis na hayop sa lupa, kayang tumakbo nang mga 100 kilometro bawat oras. Nakatira ito sa mga damuhan ng Africa, at mga 7,000 na lamang ang natitira sa ilang."});

ADDART({id:"camel",t:"Camels",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐫",w:"Camel",r:["desert","sahara","saudicountry","mali"],
p:[["Ships of the desert","Camels are large mammals adapted to deserts, able to travel long distances with little water."],["One hump or two","Dromedaries have one hump and live mainly in Africa and Arabia; Bactrian camels have two and live in Central Asia."],["Partners of people","For thousands of years camels have carried goods and people and provided milk, meat and wool."]],
l:"Camels are large, long-legged mammals famous for their humps and their ability to survive in hot and cold deserts. The dromedary, or Arabian camel, has one hump and is found in North Africa, the Middle East and South Asia, as well as feral herds in Australia. The Bactrian camel has two humps and lives in Central Asia; a separate wild Bactrian species survives in small numbers in China and Mongolia. Domesticated thousands of years ago, camels made possible the great caravan routes across the Sahara and Arabia.",
s:[["The humps","A camel’s hump does not store water, as many people believe, but fat, up to about 35 kilograms. Storing fat in one place helps the rest of the body lose heat, and the fat can be broken down for energy and water when food is scarce. A hungry camel’s hump shrinks and flops over."],["Saving water","Camels can go many days without drinking in hot weather and much longer in cool weather. They lose very little water in urine and droppings, and their body temperature can rise by several degrees during the day before they start to sweat. When they do drink, they can gulp more than 100 liters in a few minutes."],["Desert equipment","Camels have long eyelashes and slit nostrils they can close against blowing sand, and thick pads on their knees and chests for kneeling on hot ground. Their broad, padded feet spread out to walk on sand without sinking, and tough mouths let them eat thorny desert plants."],["Two kinds","Dromedaries make up about 90 percent of the world’s camels. Bactrian camels have thick, shaggy coats to survive freezing winters in the Gobi Desert and the steppes. The wild Bactrian camel, a separate species, is critically endangered, with only about 1,000 left."]],
k:[["Species","Dromedary, Bactrian, wild Bactrian"],["Hump contains","Fat, not water"],["Water in one drink","More than 100 liters"],["Domesticated","About 3,000 to 4,000 years ago"],["Relatives","Llamas, alpacas, vicuñas, guanacos"]],
d:[["c. 2000–1000 BCE","Camels are domesticated in Arabia and Central Asia"],["c. 300 CE","Camel caravans begin crossing the Sahara regularly"],["1324","Mansa Musa’s camel caravan travels to Mecca"],["1840","Camels are first imported into Australia"],["2002","Wild Bactrian camel listed as critically endangered"]],
s2:[["Caravans and trade","Camels transformed trade in Africa and Asia. Caravans of hundreds or thousands of camels carried salt, gold, cloth and spices across the Sahara, linking West Africa with the Mediterranean, and along the Silk Road between China and the Middle East. A strong camel can carry about 200 kilograms."],["Milk, meat and wool","In many desert regions, camels are still valued for milk, which is rich in vitamin C, and for meat, leather and wool. Camel milk is sold in shops in the Gulf countries and is being studied as a food for dry regions where cattle struggle."],["Camel races and beauty contests","Camel racing is a popular sport in the Arabian Peninsula, where robot jockeys now ride the camels instead of children. Camel beauty contests judge features such as the shape of the lips, hump and neck, with prizes worth millions of dollars."],["Camels in Australia","In the 1800s camels were brought to Australia with handlers from Afghanistan, India and Pakistan to help explore and supply the dry interior. When trucks and railways replaced them, many were released. Today Australia has hundreds of thousands of wild dromedaries, the largest feral herd in the world."],["Family of camels","Camels belong to the camelid family, which first evolved in North America about 45 million years ago. Some crossed to Asia, becoming camels, while others moved to South America, becoming llamas, alpacas, vicuñas and guanacos. Camelids died out in North America about 10,000 years ago."],["Spitting and grumbling","When annoyed, camels may bring up partly digested food from their stomachs and spray it, a habit that gave them a reputation for spitting. They also groan, bellow and grumble. Despite this, they are often patient and calm working animals."]],
sp:["Arctic camels","Fossils found in the Canadian Arctic show that giant camels lived there about 3.5 million years ago, when the region was warmer and forested but still had long, freezing winters. Some scientists think that features such as humps of fat and wide feet first helped camels survive cold, snowy winters, and only later helped them in hot deserts."],
g:[["Dromedary","The one-humped camel"],["Bactrian camel","The two-humped camel of Central Asia"],["Caravan","A group of travelers and animals crossing a desert together"],["Feral","Once domesticated, now living wild"]],
f:["A camel’s hump stores fat, not water.","A thirsty camel can drink over 100 liters in minutes.","Australia has the largest herd of wild camels in the world."],
fun:"A camel’s hump stores fat, not water.",
tl:"Ang kamelyo ay malaking hayop na nabubuhay sa disyerto at kayang maglakbay nang malayo nang kaunti ang tubig. Taba ang laman ng umbok nito, hindi tubig. Matagal na itong katuwang ng tao sa pagdadala ng kalakal."});

ADDART({id:"hippo",t:"Hippopotamuses",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦛",w:"Hippopotamus",r:["whale","elephant","nile","eco"],
p:[["River horse","The name hippopotamus means river horse in Greek, but hippos are more closely related to whales."],["Huge and heavy","Adult hippos can weigh more than 1,500 kilograms, making them among the largest land animals."],["Dangerous","Hippos are aggressive and responsible for many deaths of people in Africa each year."]],
l:"The common hippopotamus is a huge, semi-aquatic mammal of sub-Saharan Africa. Hippos spend the day resting in rivers and lakes to keep cool, and come out at night to graze on grass, walking several kilometers. They can weigh more than 1,500 kilograms, with males sometimes much heavier. Despite their round shape, they can run fast on land and are very territorial, making them one of Africa’s most dangerous animals. A much smaller relative, the pygmy hippopotamus, lives in the forests of West Africa.",
s:[["Life in the water","Hippos cannot really swim; they walk or push off the bottom of rivers and lakes. They can hold their breath for about five minutes. Their eyes, ears and nostrils sit on top of the head, so they can see, hear and breathe while almost completely underwater, and they close their ears and nostrils when they dive."],["Natural sunscreen","Hippo skin is thin and dries out quickly in the sun. Glands in the skin release an oily red liquid, once called blood sweat, which scientists found contains red and orange pigments that block sunlight and slow the growth of bacteria."],["Grazers at night","At night hippos leave the water and follow paths to grassy feeding areas, eating about 35 kilograms of grass. Their droppings in rivers bring nutrients from land into the water, feeding fish and other animals, though too much can harm water quality in drought."],["Jaws and tusks","A hippo can open its mouth almost 150 degrees, showing tusk-like teeth up to 50 centimeters long. Males use them in fierce fights over territory. Yawning is a threat display, warning rivals and intruders to keep away."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Hippopotamus amphibius"],["Weight","Usually 1,300–1,800 kg; large males over 3,000 kg"],["Closest living relatives","Whales and dolphins"],["Range","Sub-Saharan Africa"],["IUCN status","Vulnerable"]],
d:[["c. 3500 BCE","Hippos appear in ancient Egyptian art"],["1850","Obaysch becomes the first hippo seen in Europe since Roman times, at London Zoo"],["1990s","Genetic studies show hippos are close relatives of whales"],["2006","Common hippo listed as Vulnerable"],["2023","Colombia announces plans to control its runaway hippo population"]],
s2:[["Cousins of whales","Genetic and fossil evidence shows that the closest living relatives of hippos are whales and dolphins. They share a common ancestor that lived more than 50 million years ago. Like whales, hippos give birth and nurse their young underwater."],["Dangerous neighbors","Hippos are aggressive, especially mothers with calves and males defending their stretch of river. They may overturn boats and attack people who come too close, and they are blamed for hundreds of human deaths a year in Africa, though reliable numbers are hard to find."],["Threats","Hippos are hunted illegally for meat and for their ivory teeth, and they lose habitat when rivers are dammed, drained or farmed. Droughts can crowd them into shrinking pools. Their numbers have fallen in many countries, though they remain common in parts of East and southern Africa."],["The pygmy hippo","The pygmy hippopotamus, which lives in the swampy forests of West Africa, especially Liberia, is about a tenth the weight of its larger cousin. It is shy, solitary and endangered, with only a few thousand left in the wild. A pygmy hippo calf named Moo Deng, born in a zoo in Thailand in 2024, became famous worldwide online."],["Colombia’s hippos","In the 1980s the drug lord Pablo Escobar brought four hippos to his private zoo in Colombia. After his death they escaped into nearby rivers and multiplied, with more than 150 today. With no natural predators, they threaten local wildlife and people, and Colombia has begun sterilizing and relocating them."],["Hippos in history","Ancient Egyptians feared and honored hippos. Taweret, a goddess of childbirth, had the head of a hippo. Small blue faience hippo figures were placed in tombs. Hippos once lived along the whole Nile and even in Europe during warm periods of the Ice Age."]],
sp:["Moo Deng","In July 2024, a pygmy hippo calf named Moo Deng, meaning bouncy pork in Thai, was born at Khao Kheow Open Zoo in Thailand. Videos of her playful and sometimes grumpy behavior spread around the world online, drawing huge crowds to the zoo. Her fame helped draw attention to the endangered pygmy hippopotamus in the wild."],
g:[["Semi-aquatic","Living partly in water and partly on land"],["Territorial","Defending an area against others"],["Pygmy","A smaller form of an animal"],["Graze","To feed on growing grass"]],
f:["Hippos are more closely related to whales than to horses.","A hippo can hold its breath for about five minutes.","Hippo skin makes its own red sunscreen."],
fun:"Hippos make their own reddish sunscreen.",
tl:"Ang hippopotamus ay malaking hayop sa Africa na naninirahan sa mga ilog at lawa. Mas malapit itong kamag-anak ng balyena kaysa kabayo, at isa ito sa pinakamapanganib na hayop sa Africa."});

;
/* data/living-2.js */
// Animal Kingdom articles, living part 2: rhinoceroses, crows, seahorses and crustaceans.
ADDART({id:"rhino",t:"Rhinoceroses",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦏",w:"Rhinoceros",r:["elephant","indonesiacountry","southafricacountry","eco"],
p:[["Horned giants","Rhinoceroses are large, thick-skinned plant-eaters with one or two horns on their snouts."],["Africa and Asia","There are five living species: two in Africa and three in Asia."],["Hunted for horns","Poaching for their horns has pushed several species close to extinction."]],
l:"Rhinoceroses, or rhinos, are among the largest land mammals, with thick skin and one or two horns made of keratin, the same material as fingernails. There are five living species: the white and black rhinos of Africa, and the greater one-horned, Javan and Sumatran rhinos of Asia. Once widespread, rhinos have been hunted for their horns, which are wrongly believed in some places to have medicinal value. Thanks to protection, some populations have recovered, but the Javan and Sumatran rhinos are critically endangered.",
s:[["Five species","The white rhino is the largest, weighing up to about 3,500 kilograms, with a wide mouth for grazing grass. The black rhino, smaller and with a pointed lip for browsing shrubs, is more aggressive. The greater one-horned rhino of India and Nepal has skin that looks like armor plates. The Javan and Sumatran rhinos are the rarest."],["Horns","A rhino’s horn is not bone but a mass of tightly packed keratin fibers that keeps growing throughout its life. Rhinos use their horns to defend themselves, dig for water, break branches and fight rivals. The horn has no proven medicinal power."],["Senses and skin","Rhinos have poor eyesight but a keen sense of smell and hearing. Their skin, up to about 5 centimeters thick in places, is sensitive to sunburn and insect bites, so they wallow in mud, which acts as sunscreen and insect repellent."],["Social life","Most rhinos live alone, though white rhinos sometimes form small groups. They mark territories with dung piles and urine. Females give birth to a single calf after a pregnancy of about 15 to 16 months, and the calf stays with its mother for two to three years."]],
k:[["Species","White, black, greater one-horned, Javan, Sumatran"],["Largest","White rhino, up to about 3,500 kg"],["Horn made of","Keratin"],["Rarest","Javan rhino, about 50 left"],["Main threat","Poaching for horns"]],
d:[["1970s","Rhino numbers crash due to poaching"],["1977","International trade in rhino horn is banned"],["2008–2015","Poaching surges in South Africa"],["2011","Western black rhino declared extinct"],["2018","Sudan, the last male northern white rhino, dies"],["2024","First pregnancy using a lab-made rhino embryo is reported"]],
s2:[["Poaching","Rhino horn is sold illegally at very high prices, mainly in parts of Asia, for traditional remedies or as a status symbol. Poaching surged after 2008, especially in South Africa, which has most of the world’s rhinos. Rangers, sniffer dogs, tracking collars and stricter laws have helped reduce poaching in some areas."],["The southern white rhino","By about 1900, fewer than 100 southern white rhinos survived, in what is now South Africa. Careful protection in reserves such as Hluhluwe-iMfolozi brought them back to many thousands, one of the great conservation successes. But poaching has since reduced their numbers again."],["The last northern white rhinos","The northern white rhino is functionally extinct: only two females, Najin and Fatu, survive in Kenya. Sudan, the last male, died in 2018. Scientists have made embryos using eggs from the females and stored sperm, hoping to carry a pregnancy using a southern white rhino surrogate mother."],["Rhinos of Asia","The greater one-horned rhino has recovered from fewer than 200 in the early 1900s to more than 4,000, thanks to protection in India’s Kaziranga National Park and Nepal’s Chitwan. The Javan rhino survives only in Ujung Kulon National Park in Indonesia, and the hairy Sumatran rhino, the smallest, numbers fewer than 50."],["Dehorning","In some reserves, wildlife workers sedate rhinos and saw off their horns, which do not hurt and grow back, to make them less valuable to poachers. Studies in South Africa have found that dehorning can greatly reduce poaching, though it must be repeated every year or two."],["Ancient rhinos","Rhinos have a long history. The woolly rhinoceros roamed Europe and Asia during the Ice Age and appears in cave paintings, such as those at Chauvet in France. Paraceratherium, a hornless rhino relative that lived in Asia about 30 million years ago, was one of the largest land mammals ever, about 5 meters tall at the shoulder."]],
sp:["Dürer’s rhinoceros","In 1515 an Indian rhinoceros arrived in Lisbon, the first seen in Europe since Roman times. The German artist Albrecht Dürer never saw it, but made a woodcut from a sketch and a description. His rhino has armor-like plates and an extra small horn on its back. Although inaccurate, the image was copied in books for nearly 300 years."],
g:[["Keratin","A tough protein found in hair, nails and horns"],["Poaching","Illegal hunting of animals"],["Functionally extinct","Having too few individuals left to survive without help"],["Wallow","To roll or lie in mud or water"]],
f:["Rhino horn is made of keratin, like fingernails.","The southern white rhino recovered from fewer than 100 animals.","Only two northern white rhinos are left, both female."],
fun:"A rhino’s horn is made of the same material as your fingernails.",
tl:"Ang rhinoceros ay malaking hayop na may isa o dalawang sungay na gawa sa keratin. May limang uri nito sa Africa at Asya, at marami ang nanganganib dahil sa iligal na pangangaso para sa sungay."});

ADDART({id:"crow",t:"Crows and Ravens",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐦",w:"Corvidae",r:["birds","parrot","eagle","owl"],
p:[["Clever birds","Crows, ravens, jays and magpies belong to the crow family, among the most intelligent animals on Earth."],["Tool users","Some crows make and use tools to get food, an ability once thought to be almost uniquely human."],["Found worldwide","Members of the crow family live on every continent except Antarctica."]],
l:"Crows and ravens belong to the corvid family, which also includes jays, magpies, rooks and nutcrackers. Corvids are found almost worldwide and are known for their intelligence: they solve puzzles, make tools, remember human faces and plan for the future. The common raven, with a wingspan of more than a meter, is the largest songbird. Corvids often live close to people in cities and farmland, and they appear in the myths and stories of many cultures.",
s:[["Brainy birds","Relative to body size, corvids have brains as large as those of chimpanzees. Their brains pack many neurons into a small space. In experiments, crows and ravens solve problems as well as young children or great apes, for example understanding that dropping stones into a tube of water will raise the water level and bring food within reach."],["Toolmakers","New Caledonian crows, from an island in the Pacific, shape twigs and leaves into hooks and probes to pull insects from holes in wood. They can even combine short pieces into longer tools. Young crows learn by watching adults, and different groups make different styles of tools."],["Remembering faces","Studies have shown that crows remember the faces of people who threatened them and scold them for years afterward. They also seem to pass this information to other crows, including young ones that never saw the original threat."],["Planning ahead","Corvids such as jays hide food in thousands of places and remember where they put it. Some jays rehide food if they notice another bird watching them, suggesting they understand what others might do. Ravens in experiments have saved tools to use later for a reward."]],
k:[["Family","Corvidae"],["Largest songbird","Common raven, wingspan over 1 m"],["Number of species","About 130"],["Famous toolmaker","New Caledonian crow"],["Found","Every continent except Antarctica"]],
d:[["c. 700 BCE","Aesop’s fable The Crow and the Pitcher is told in ancient Greece"],["1600s","Legend says ravens must stay at the Tower of London"],["2002","A New Caledonian crow named Betty bends wire into a hook in a lab"],["2010","Study shows crows remember dangerous human faces"],["2017","Ravens shown to plan for future events"]],
s2:[["Aesop’s fable","In Aesop’s ancient fable The Crow and the Pitcher, a thirsty crow drops pebbles into a jug until the water rises high enough to drink. Modern experiments have shown that rooks, crows and jays really can solve this kind of problem, choosing heavy stones over light ones and solid objects over hollow ones."],["Talking and playing","Some corvids, such as ravens and magpies, can imitate human speech and other sounds. Ravens have been seen sliding down snowy roofs on their backs, apparently for fun, and playing games with sticks and other animals, behavior usually linked with intelligence."],["Crows in cities","Crows thrive in towns and cities, eating scraps and nesting in parks. In Japan, carrion crows have been seen placing walnuts on roads for cars to crack, then waiting for traffic lights to change before picking them up. Their adaptability helps them succeed alongside people."],["Ravens of the Tower","A legend says that if the ravens ever leave the Tower of London, the kingdom will fall. At least six ravens are kept there, looked after by a special guard called the Ravenmaster. The legend probably dates from the 1800s, though it is often said to be older."],["Myths and stories","Ravens and crows appear in stories worldwide. In Norse myth, the god Odin had two ravens, Thought and Memory, that flew over the world bringing him news. Peoples of the Pacific Northwest of North America tell of Raven as a trickster who brought light to the world. In Japan, the three-legged crow Yatagarasu is a guide."],["Magpies and jays","The Eurasian magpie is one of the few animals known to recognize itself in a mirror, a sign of self-awareness. Blue jays, Steller’s jays and the colorful green jay of the Americas are also corvids. The Clark’s nutcracker can remember the locations of thousands of hidden pine seeds for months."]],
sp:["Betty the crow","In 2002, scientists at the University of Oxford gave a New Caledonian crow named Betty a straight piece of wire and a small bucket of food at the bottom of a tube. Betty bent the wire into a hook and used it to lift the bucket out. The experiment amazed scientists and helped show how inventive crows can be."],
g:[["Corvid","A member of the crow family"],["Songbird","A perching bird with a well-developed voice box"],["Cache","To hide food for later"],["Self-awareness","Recognizing oneself as an individual"]],
f:["The common raven is the largest songbird.","New Caledonian crows make hooked tools.","Crows can remember human faces for years."],
fun:"Some crows in Japan use passing cars to crack walnuts, then wait for the traffic lights to collect them safely.",
tl:"Ang uwak at mga kamag-anak nito ay kabilang sa pinakamatatalinong hayop. Kaya nilang gumawa at gumamit ng kasangkapan, tandaan ang mukha ng tao at magplano para sa hinaharap."});

ADDART({id:"seahorse",t:"Seahorses",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐴",w:"Seahorse",r:["reef","shark","octopus","eco"],
p:[["Fish with a horse’s head","Seahorses are small fish with heads shaped like a horse’s, curling tails and armor-like plates instead of scales."],["Fathers give birth","Male seahorses carry the eggs in a pouch and give birth to the babies."],["Slow swimmers","They are among the slowest fish, moving by fluttering a tiny fin on their backs."]],
l:"Seahorses are small marine fish found in shallow tropical and temperate waters around the world, among seagrass, coral reefs and mangroves. There are more than 40 species, ranging from less than 2 centimeters to about 35 centimeters long. They swim upright, anchor themselves with curling tails and suck up tiny shrimp through their tube-like snouts. Uniquely, it is the male seahorse that becomes pregnant and gives birth. Many species are threatened by habitat loss and overfishing.",
s:[["Strange fish","Seahorses are true fish, related to pipefish and sea dragons. Instead of scales, they have bony plates under their skin. They breathe with gills and have a swim bladder to control buoyancy. Their eyes move independently, like a chameleon’s, letting them look for food and danger at the same time."],["Slow but sneaky","A seahorse swims by fanning a small fin on its back up to about 50 times a second, steering with fins on its head. They are poor swimmers and cling to seagrass or coral with their tails in currents. To hunt, they creep up on tiny animals and snap them up with a quick upward flick of the head."],["Male pregnancy","After a courtship dance, which may last days, the female places her eggs into a pouch on the male’s belly. He fertilizes them and carries them for about two to four weeks, supplying oxygen and nutrients. He then gives birth to dozens to more than a thousand tiny babies, which must survive on their own."],["Masters of disguise","Many seahorses can change color to blend with their surroundings, and some grow skin filaments that look like seaweed. The pygmy seahorse, smaller than a fingernail, lives on sea fans and matches their color and bumps so well that it was only discovered when scientists collected a sea fan."]],
k:[["Species","More than 40"],["Size","Under 2 cm to about 35 cm"],["Who gives birth","The male"],["Habitat","Seagrass beds, coral reefs, mangroves"],["Relatives","Pipefish and sea dragons"]],
d:[["c. 350 BCE","Aristotle writes about pipefish carrying young"],["1758","Linnaeus describes the first seahorse species in his system"],["1969","The pygmy seahorse is discovered on a sea fan"],["2004","International trade in seahorses comes under CITES controls"],["2016","The first seahorse genome is sequenced"]],
s2:[["Threats","Millions of seahorses are caught each year, many as accidental catch in shrimp trawling nets, and many are dried for traditional medicine or sold as souvenirs and aquarium pets. Seagrass meadows and coral reefs, their homes, are being damaged by pollution, coastal development and warming seas."],["Protecting seahorses","Since 2004, international trade in all seahorse species has been regulated under CITES, the treaty on trade in endangered species. Marine protected areas and community projects, such as in the Philippines and Vietnam, help protect seahorse habitats and train fishers in sustainable practices."],["Pairs for life?","Many seahorse species are monogamous during a breeding season, and some pairs greet each other every morning with a dance, changing color and swimming side by side. This may help keep their breeding cycles in step. However, scientists have found that not all seahorses stay with one partner."],["Sea dragons","Leafy and weedy sea dragons, relatives of seahorses found off southern Australia, are covered in leaf-like flaps of skin that make them look like drifting seaweed. Like seahorses, the males carry the eggs, but on the underside of their tails rather than in a pouch."],["The seahorse in the brain","The hippocampus, a part of the brain important for memory, is named after the seahorse, whose scientific genus name is Hippocampus, because of its curved shape. The name comes from Greek words meaning horse and sea monster."],["Babies on their own","A newborn seahorse is only a few millimeters long and receives no care from its parents. Most are eaten by other animals or swept away by currents; scientists estimate that fewer than one in a thousand survives to adulthood. Producing many young helps the species survive."]],
sp:["Why do males give birth?","Scientists think male pregnancy may let seahorses have more babies: while the male carries one brood, the female can already be producing the next batch of eggs. The male’s pouch works much like a womb, controlling salt levels, supplying oxygen and even providing some nutrients to the developing young."],
g:[["Brood pouch","A pocket in which a male seahorse carries eggs"],["Monogamous","Having one partner"],["Camouflage","Colors or shapes that help an animal blend in"],["CITES","An international treaty controlling trade in threatened species"]],
f:["Male seahorses give birth.","Seahorses have no scales, but bony plates under their skin.","The brain’s hippocampus is named after the seahorse."],
fun:"Seahorses are fish, even though they swim upright and have no tail fin.",
tl:"Ang kabayong-dagat ay maliit na isda na may ulong hugis kabayo. Kakaiba ito dahil ang lalaki ang nagbubuntis at nanganganak. Nanganganib ang maraming uri nito dahil sa pagkasira ng tirahan at labis na panghuhuli."});

ADDART({id:"crustacean",t:"Crabs, Shrimp and Other Crustaceans",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦀",w:"Crustacean",r:["insect","spider","reef","fish"],
p:[["Armored animals","Crustaceans are animals with hard outer skeletons and jointed legs, including crabs, lobsters, shrimp and barnacles."],["Mostly in water","Most live in the sea or fresh water, but woodlice and land crabs live on land."],["Hugely important","Tiny crustaceans such as krill and copepods are food for whales, fish and seabirds."]],
l:"Crustaceans are a large group of arthropods, animals with jointed legs and a hard outer skeleton, that includes crabs, lobsters, crayfish, shrimp, krill, barnacles and woodlice. There are more than 60,000 known species, from tiny water fleas to the Japanese spider crab, whose legs can span nearly 4 meters. Most live in oceans and fresh water, breathing through gills. Crustaceans are a vital part of food webs and an important source of food for people around the world.",
s:[["Body plan","Crustaceans have a hard exoskeleton made of chitin, often strengthened with calcium. Their bodies are divided into segments with many pairs of jointed limbs used for walking, swimming, feeding and breathing. Most have two pairs of antennae, which they use to touch and smell, and many have eyes on movable stalks."],["Growing by molting","Because their hard skeleton cannot stretch, crustaceans grow by molting: they shed the old shell, swell up by taking in water, and grow a new, larger shell, which takes time to harden. While soft, they are very vulnerable and often hide."],["Crabs","Crabs have broad, flat bodies with a short tail tucked underneath, and front legs with claws for feeding and defense. Many walk sideways. Hermit crabs protect their soft bodies by living in empty snail shells, moving to bigger shells as they grow. The coconut crab, the largest land invertebrate, can crack coconuts with its claws."],["Krill and copepods","Krill are small shrimp-like crustaceans that swarm in vast numbers in cold oceans. Antarctic krill may be the most abundant wild animal by weight on Earth and are the main food of blue whales, penguins and seals. Copepods, even smaller, are among the most numerous animals in the oceans."]],
k:[["Known species","More than 60,000"],["Largest by leg span","Japanese spider crab, nearly 4 m"],["Largest land invertebrate","Coconut crab"],["Skeleton made of","Chitin"],["Group","Arthropods"]],
d:[["c. 500 million years ago","Early crustaceans appear in the Cambrian seas"],["1758","Linnaeus classifies crabs and lobsters"],["1846–1854","Darwin studies barnacles for about eight years"],["1925","The Discovery Investigations begin studying Antarctic krill and whales"],["2021","UK law recognizes crabs and lobsters as sentient beings"]],
s2:[["Barnacles","Barnacles start life as free-swimming larvae, then glue themselves head-first to rocks, ships or whales and build hard shells around themselves. They kick food into their mouths with feathery legs. Charles Darwin spent about eight years studying barnacles before publishing On the Origin of Species."],["Mantis shrimp","Mantis shrimp, which live in tropical seas, have one of the fastest strikes in the animal kingdom. Their club-like limbs hit with the speed of a small bullet, cracking snail shells and even aquarium glass. They also have extraordinarily complex eyes with up to 16 types of color-detecting cells, compared with our three."],["Land crabs","On Christmas Island in the Indian Ocean, tens of millions of red crabs migrate from the forest to the sea each year to release their eggs. Roads are closed and special bridges and tunnels built to help them cross safely. The migration is timed by the rains and the phases of the Moon."],["Food for people","Shrimp, prawns, crabs, lobsters and crayfish are popular foods worldwide, and shrimp farming is a major industry in Asia and Latin America. Poorly managed shrimp farms have destroyed mangrove forests in some countries, and fishing for crustaceans must be managed to avoid overfishing."],["Do crustaceans feel pain?","Experiments suggest that crabs and lobsters may experience pain: they avoid places where they received electric shocks and tend injured body parts. In 2021 the United Kingdom recognized decapod crustaceans, such as crabs and lobsters, as sentient beings in animal welfare law."],["Woodlice","Woodlice, also called pill bugs or roly-polies, are crustaceans that live on land in damp places under logs and stones. Some can roll into a ball for protection. They help break down dead leaves and wood, returning nutrients to the soil."]],
sp:["The Japanese spider crab","The Japanese spider crab, which lives in deep waters off Japan, has the largest leg span of any arthropod, nearly 4 meters from claw to claw, though its body is only about 40 centimeters across. It may live for up to 100 years. Despite its fearsome appearance, it is a gentle scavenger that eats dead animals and plants on the seafloor."],
g:[["Crustacean","An arthropod with a hard shell, such as a crab or shrimp"],["Exoskeleton","A hard skeleton on the outside of the body"],["Molting","Shedding an old shell to grow"],["Krill","Small shrimp-like crustaceans that swarm in the ocean"]],
f:["Crabs, shrimp and barnacles are all crustaceans.","Antarctic krill are among the most abundant animals on Earth.","Mantis shrimp have up to 16 types of color receptors."],
fun:"Woodlice, the roly-polies found under logs, are crustaceans like crabs.",
tl:"Ang mga crustacean, gaya ng alimango, hipon, ulang at barnacle, ay mga hayop na may matigas na balat sa labas at may kasukasuang mga paa. Mahalaga sila sa kadena ng pagkain sa dagat."});

;
/* data/living-3.js */
// Animal Kingdom articles, living part 3: snails and shellfish, dragonflies, termites and earthworms.
ADDART({id:"mollusc",t:"Snails, Clams and Other Molluscs",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐌",w:"Mollusca",r:["octopus","crustacean","reef","worms"],
p:[["Soft-bodied animals","Molluscs are soft-bodied animals, many protected by shells, including snails, slugs, clams, oysters and squid."],["A huge group","With about 85,000 known species, molluscs are one of the largest groups of animals."],["From gardens to the deep sea","They live on land, in fresh water and throughout the oceans."]],
l:"Molluscs are a large and varied group of invertebrates with soft bodies, most of which are protected by a hard shell made of calcium carbonate. The group includes gastropods such as snails and slugs, bivalves such as clams, mussels, oysters and scallops, and cephalopods such as octopuses, squid and cuttlefish. With about 85,000 living species, molluscs range from tiny snails less than a millimeter long to the colossal squid, which can weigh about 500 kilograms. They are important in food webs and as food for people.",
s:[["Body plan","Most molluscs have a muscular foot for moving or digging, a soft body containing the organs, and a fold of tissue called the mantle, which makes the shell. Many have a radula, a tongue-like ribbon covered in tiny teeth, used to scrape up food. Shells grow from the edge as the animal grows, often in beautiful spirals."],["Snails and slugs","Gastropods, meaning stomach-foot, are the largest group of molluscs. Snails carry a coiled shell and can pull inside for protection, while slugs have lost their shells or have only tiny internal ones. Land snails and slugs glide on a layer of slime, which protects them and helps them stick to surfaces, even upside down."],["Clams and oysters","Bivalves have two hinged shells. Most filter food from water, cleaning it as they feed: a single oyster can filter dozens of liters a day. Oysters make pearls when an irritant gets inside the shell and they coat it in layers of nacre, or mother-of-pearl. The giant clam of the Indo-Pacific can weigh over 200 kilograms."],["Cephalopods","Squid, octopuses, cuttlefish and nautiluses are cephalopods, meaning head-foot. They have arms or tentacles, large brains and excellent eyes, and most can change color in an instant. The nautilus still has a coiled external shell, but squid and cuttlefish have small internal shells, and octopuses have almost none."]],
k:[["Known species","About 85,000"],["Main groups","Gastropods, bivalves, cephalopods"],["Shell made of","Calcium carbonate"],["Largest","Colossal squid, about 500 kg"],["Oldest known animal","Ming, an ocean quahog clam, 507 years"]],
d:[["c. 540 million years ago","Early molluscs appear in the oceans"],["c. 1500 BCE","Phoenicians make purple dye from sea snails"],["1758","Linnaeus classifies molluscs"],["2006","Ming the clam is found off Iceland"],["2007","The heaviest colossal squid is caught near Antarctica"]],
s2:[["Purple dye","The Phoenicians of the eastern Mediterranean made a precious purple dye from murex sea snails. Thousands of snails were needed for a single gram of dye, so purple cloth became a symbol of royalty and power. Roman emperors wore Tyrian purple, named after the Phoenician city of Tyre."],["Ming the clam","In 2006 scientists dredged up an ocean quahog clam off Iceland and later counted the growth bands in its shell. It turned out to be 507 years old, having started life around 1499, making it the oldest individual animal whose age has been precisely measured. It was nicknamed Ming, after the Chinese dynasty ruling when it was born."],["Snails and disease","Some freshwater snails carry tiny parasitic worms that cause schistosomiasis, also called snail fever, a disease affecting more than 200 million people, mainly in Africa. Controlling snails and improving water and sanitation help prevent it."],["Pearls","Natural pearls are very rare, so most pearls today are cultured: pearl farmers place a small bead inside an oyster or mussel, which coats it in nacre over months or years. Kokichi Mikimoto in Japan pioneered cultured pearls in the 1890s. The South Sea pearls of Australia, Indonesia and the Philippines are among the largest."],["Molluscs in danger","Many land snails, especially on islands such as Hawaii and Tahiti, have become extinct because of habitat loss and introduced predators. Ocean acidification, caused by the ocean absorbing carbon dioxide, makes it harder for shellfish such as oysters and some snails to build their shells."],["Snail speed","A garden snail moves at about 1 millimeter per second, or around 50 meters per hour at top speed. Its slime is so useful that scientists study it for medical glues and skin care products. Some snails can sleep for months during dry weather, sealing their shells with a layer of dried mucus."]],
sp:["The giant clam","Giant clams live on coral reefs in the Indian and Pacific Oceans and can grow over a meter long. Tiny algae living in their colorful mantles make food from sunlight and share it with the clam. Despite stories, giant clams do not trap divers. They are protected in many countries because they were heavily harvested for food and shells."],
g:[["Mollusc","A soft-bodied invertebrate, often with a shell"],["Mantle","The fold of tissue that forms a mollusc’s shell"],["Radula","A ribbon of tiny teeth used for feeding"],["Nacre","Mother-of-pearl, the shiny material in shells and pearls"]],
f:["Snails, clams and squid are all molluscs.","A clam named Ming lived for 507 years.","Oysters make pearls by coating irritants in nacre."],
fun:"A clam found off Iceland was 507 years old, born around the time Columbus sailed.",
tl:"Ang mga mollusk, gaya ng suso, tulya, talaba at pusit, ay mga hayop na malambot ang katawan at kadalasang may balat-kabibe. Nakatira sila sa lupa, tubig-tabang at dagat."});

ADDART({id:"dragonfly",t:"Dragonflies",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🪰",w:"Dragonfly",r:["insect","butterfly","mosquito","wetlands"],
p:[["Ancient fliers","Dragonflies and their relatives have existed for more than 300 million years, since before the dinosaurs."],["Expert hunters","Adult dragonflies catch insects in midair and succeed in most of their hunts."],["Life in water","Young dragonflies, called nymphs, live underwater for months or years before becoming adults."]],
l:"Dragonflies are fast-flying insects with long bodies, two pairs of transparent wings and huge eyes. Together with the slimmer damselflies, they form the order Odonata, with about 6,000 species found on every continent except Antarctica. They begin life underwater as fierce nymphs, then climb out and transform into winged adults that hunt other insects, including mosquitoes, in flight. Dragonflies are among the most skilled fliers in the animal world and good indicators of healthy wetlands.",
s:[["Superb fliers","Dragonflies can move each of their four wings independently, letting them hover, fly backward, turn sharply and reach speeds of over 30 kilometers per hour. Engineers study their flight to design small flying robots and drones."],["Eyes that see almost everything","A dragonfly’s eyes cover most of its head and contain up to about 30,000 tiny lenses each, giving almost all-around vision. They can see colors and ultraviolet light and track moving prey precisely, predicting where it will go."],["Deadly hunters","Dragonflies catch prey with their legs, forming a basket to scoop insects out of the air. Studies have found they succeed in more than 90 percent of hunts, one of the highest rates of any predator. A single dragonfly can eat dozens to hundreds of mosquitoes a day."],["Underwater nymphs","Dragonfly eggs hatch in ponds, lakes and streams into nymphs. Nymphs breathe through gills inside their bodies and catch tadpoles, small fish and insect larvae with a hinged lower lip that shoots out like a spring. After months or years, a nymph climbs a plant stem, splits its skin and emerges as an adult."]],
k:[["Order","Odonata (dragonflies and damselflies)"],["Species","About 6,000"],["Speed","Over 30 km/h"],["Hunting success","Over 90% in some studies"],["Ancient relatives","Griffinflies, wingspan about 70 cm"]],
d:[["c. 320 million years ago","Early relatives of dragonflies take to the air"],["c. 300 million years ago","Giant griffinflies like Meganeuropsis live"],["1758","Linnaeus names dragonflies Libellula"],["1990s","Scientists track the migration of globe skimmers across the Indian Ocean"],["2016","Study confirms globe skimmers travel up to 18,000 km"]],
s2:[["Giants of the past","About 300 million years ago, relatives of dragonflies called griffinflies, such as Meganeuropsis, had wingspans of about 70 centimeters, the largest insects ever known. Higher oxygen levels in the air at that time may have allowed insects to grow so large."],["Dragonfly or damselfly?","Dragonflies are sturdier and usually rest with their wings spread flat. Damselflies are slender and usually fold their wings along their bodies when resting. Dragonflies’ eyes almost touch on top of the head, while damselflies’ eyes are separated."],["Long-distance travelers","The globe skimmer, also called the wandering glider, makes one of the longest insect migrations known, crossing the Indian Ocean between India and East Africa over several generations, a round trip of up to about 18,000 kilometers. It rides high winds of the monsoon."],["Signs of clean water","Because dragonfly nymphs need clean, unpolluted water, the presence of many dragonfly species is a sign of a healthy wetland. Their decline can warn of pollution or habitat loss. Building garden ponds helps dragonflies and many other animals."],["Dragonflies and people","In Japan, dragonflies, called tombo, are symbols of courage, strength and summer, and an old name for Japan, Akitsushima, means island of dragonflies. Many cultures admire them, though in parts of Europe old folk names, such as devil’s darning needle, reflect fear."],["Colors","Dragonflies come in brilliant blues, reds, greens and golds. Some colors come from pigments and some from tiny structures that reflect light. Many males are brightly colored to attract mates and warn rivals, while females are often duller."]],
sp:["A 300-million-year success","Dragonflies were among the first animals to fly, long before birds, bats or even dinosaurs existed. Their basic body plan has changed little in hundreds of millions of years, a sign of how well it works. They survived the great extinction that wiped out the dinosaurs and are still thriving today."],
g:[["Nymph","The young, water-living stage of a dragonfly"],["Odonata","The insect order of dragonflies and damselflies"],["Compound eye","An eye made of many tiny lenses"],["Migration","A seasonal journey from one region to another"]],
f:["Dragonflies have existed for over 300 million years.","They catch prey in more than 90 percent of their hunts.","Dragonfly nymphs live underwater."],
fun:"Some dragonflies migrate across the Indian Ocean, a round trip of up to 18,000 kilometers.",
tl:"Ang tutubi ay mabilis lumipad na insekto na may malalaking mata. Mahusay itong mangaso ng ibang insekto, gaya ng lamok, at nagsisimula ang buhay nito sa ilalim ng tubig."});

ADDART({id:"termite",t:"Termites",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🏰",w:"Termite",r:["ants","insect","bee","soil"],
p:[["Insect builders","Termites are social insects that live in large colonies and build some of the most impressive structures in the animal world."],["Wood eaters","They feed on wood, dead plants and leaf litter, helping to recycle nutrients."],["Not ants","Though sometimes called white ants, termites are more closely related to cockroaches."]],
l:"Termites are social insects that live in colonies ranging from hundreds to millions of members, led by a king and queen. There are about 3,000 known species, most in tropical regions. Termites feed on cellulose, the tough material in wood and plants, with the help of microbes in their guts. In doing so, they break down dead plant matter and enrich soils, making them vital to many ecosystems. Some species, however, damage wooden buildings and furniture.",
s:[["The colony","A termite colony has castes. The king and queen reproduce; in some species the queen grows to many times the size of the workers and lays thousands of eggs a day. Workers gather food, build and care for the young. Soldiers, with large heads and powerful jaws, defend the colony; in some species they spray sticky chemicals."],["Eating wood","Few animals can digest cellulose, but termites rely on bacteria and other microbes in their guts to break it down. Some termites even farm fungus inside their mounds, feeding it chewed plant material and then eating the fungus."],["Mound builders","In the African savanna and Australian outback, some termites build mounds of soil, saliva and droppings that can tower over 8 meters. Inside, tunnels and chambers help control temperature and humidity. The magnetic termites of northern Australia build flat, wedge-shaped mounds aligned north to south."],["Termites and cockroaches","Genetic studies show that termites are a branch of the cockroach family tree that became social. Their resemblance to ants is due to their similar lifestyle; ants are actually closer relatives of bees and wasps."]],
k:[["Species","About 3,000"],["Colony size","Hundreds to millions"],["Closest relatives","Cockroaches"],["Tallest mounds","Over 8 m"],["Food","Cellulose from wood and plants"]],
d:[["c. 130 million years ago","Termites appear in the age of dinosaurs"],["1700s","Naturalists describe African termite mounds"],["2007","Genetic study confirms termites are social cockroaches"],["2018","Termite mounds in Brazil found to be about 4,000 years old"],["2015","Study shows termite mounds help dry grasslands resist drought"]],
s2:[["Natural air conditioning","Some termite mounds have networks of tunnels that let air circulate, keeping the nest at a steady temperature and humidity. Architects have studied them; the Eastgate Centre in Harare, Zimbabwe, opened in 1996, used ideas inspired by termite mounds to cool the building with far less energy."],["A city seen from space","In northeastern Brazil, termites have built about 200 million regularly spaced mounds covering an area about as large as Great Britain. Some mounds are around 4,000 years old. Together they contain enough soil to make thousands of Great Pyramids of Giza."],["Ecosystem engineers","Termites aerate soil, help rain soak into the ground and recycle nutrients from dead wood and leaves. Their mounds create rich patches where plants grow well, attracting other animals. Studies suggest that termite mounds help savannas survive droughts."],["Food for others","Termites are an important food for many animals, including aardvarks, pangolins, anteaters, chimpanzees and birds. Chimpanzees use sticks as tools to fish termites out of mounds. In parts of Africa and Asia, people eat termites too, as a rich source of protein."],["Pests and methane","A few termite species damage houses, bridges and furniture, causing large repair costs. Termites also release methane, a greenhouse gas, as their gut microbes digest wood, though they produce only a small share of global emissions."],["Long-lived queens","Termite queens can live for decades, far longer than most insects. In some species, a queen can lay tens of thousands of eggs a day for years. Scientists study termites to learn how they live so long and stay healthy."]],
sp:["Mounds older than the pyramids","In 2018 scientists reported that the mounds in northeastern Brazil, each about 2.5 meters tall and 9 meters wide, cover around 230,000 square kilometers. Dating of the soil showed that some were built about 4,000 years ago, around the time the pyramids of Egypt were built, making them among the oldest known structures built by insects."],
g:[["Colony","A group of insects living and working together"],["Caste","A group within a colony with a particular role"],["Cellulose","The tough material that makes up plant cell walls"],["Ecosystem engineer","An animal that changes its environment in ways that help others"]],
f:["Termites are closely related to cockroaches.","Some termite mounds are over 8 meters tall.","Termite mounds inspired a building design in Zimbabwe."],
fun:"Termites in Brazil built about 200 million mounds over an area as large as Great Britain.",
tl:"Ang anay ay mga insektong panlipunan na nakatira sa malalaking kolonya at gumagawa ng matataas na punso. Kumakain sila ng kahoy at patay na halaman, at mas malapit silang kamag-anak ng ipis kaysa langgam."});

ADDART({id:"worms",t:"Earthworms and Other Worms",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🪱",w:"Earthworm",r:["soil","eco","insect","darwin"],
p:[["Soil builders","Earthworms burrow through soil, mixing it and turning dead leaves into rich earth."],["Many kinds of worm","Worms include earthworms, leeches, flatworms, roundworms and colorful sea worms."],["Darwin’s favorite","Charles Darwin studied earthworms for decades and wrote a book about them."]],
l:"Worms are soft-bodied animals with long, tube-shaped bodies and no legs. The best-known are earthworms, segmented worms that live in soil around the world, but the word worm covers several unrelated groups, including flatworms, roundworms and marine bristle worms. Earthworms are vital for healthy soil: by burrowing and eating dead plant matter, they mix and aerate the soil, help water drain and produce nutrient-rich castings. Farmers and gardeners value them highly.",
s:[["How earthworms live","An earthworm’s body is made of many ring-like segments, each with tiny bristles that grip the soil. It moves by squeezing muscles that make segments longer and thinner, then shorter and fatter. Earthworms have no lungs and breathe through their moist skin, which is why they come to the surface in heavy rain."],["Soil engineers","Earthworms swallow soil as they burrow, digesting bits of dead leaves and microbes and leaving behind castings rich in nutrients. Their tunnels let air and water reach plant roots. A healthy field can contain more than a million earthworms per hectare."],["Worms of many kinds","Flatworms, such as planarians, can regrow a whole body from a small piece. Roundworms, or nematodes, are among the most numerous animals on Earth, living in soil, water and inside other animals. Leeches, relatives of earthworms, include species that suck blood and others that hunt small animals."],["Sea worms","The oceans are home to many colorful worms. Feather duster and Christmas tree worms on coral reefs spread fan-like tentacles to catch food. Giant tube worms near deep-sea hot springs grow over 2 meters long and get their food from bacteria living inside them."]],
k:[["Earthworm species","About 7,000"],["Longest earthworm","Giant Gippsland earthworm, up to about 3 m"],["Breathe through","Their skin"],["Main groups of worms","Segmented worms, flatworms, roundworms"],["Darwin’s book","The Formation of Vegetable Mould through the Action of Worms (1881)"]],
d:[["c. 550 million years ago","Worm-like animals leave burrows in ancient seafloors"],["1881","Darwin publishes his book on earthworms"],["1977","Giant tube worms are discovered at deep-sea vents"],["1998","The roundworm C. elegans becomes the first animal to have its genome sequenced"],["2002","Nobel Prize for research using C. elegans"]],
s2:[["Darwin and the worms","Charles Darwin studied earthworms for about 40 years. He measured how they slowly bury stones and ancient ruins by bringing soil to the surface, and even tested whether they could hear by playing a piano and a bassoon to them. His last book, published in 1881, showed how important worms are for shaping soil and landscapes."],["A worm that changed science","The tiny roundworm Caenorhabditis elegans, about a millimeter long, has helped scientists understand how genes control development, aging and cell death. In 1998 it became the first animal to have its complete genome sequenced. Research using it has led to several Nobel Prizes."],["Medicinal leeches","For centuries, doctors used leeches to drain blood, often harmfully. Today medicinal leeches are used carefully in some hospitals after surgery to reattach fingers or ears, because they help blood flow and release substances that stop clotting."],["Parasitic worms","Some worms live inside other animals as parasites, including tapeworms, hookworms and roundworms. Parasitic worm infections affect more than a billion people worldwide, especially children in places without clean water and sanitation. Medicines given in schools and better sanitation help control them."],["Giant earthworms","The giant Gippsland earthworm of southeastern Australia can grow up to about 3 meters long. It lives deep in moist soil and makes gurgling sounds as it moves through its burrows. It is rare and protected."],["Composting with worms","Many people use worms to turn food scraps and garden waste into compost, a method called vermicomposting. Red wiggler worms are often used because they eat quickly and thrive in compost bins, producing rich fertilizer for gardens."]],
sp:["Regrowing a body","Planarian flatworms are famous for their power of regeneration. If one is cut into pieces, each piece can grow into a complete new worm, including a new head and brain. Scientists study them to learn how cells rebuild lost body parts, hoping one day to apply such knowledge to medicine."],
g:[["Segmented worm","A worm with a body made of ring-like sections"],["Castings","Nutrient-rich waste left by earthworms"],["Nematode","A roundworm"],["Regeneration","Regrowing lost body parts"]],
f:["Earthworms breathe through their skin.","Darwin wrote a book about earthworms in 1881.","A planarian flatworm can regrow from a small piece."],
fun:"Darwin played a piano and a bassoon to earthworms to test whether they could hear.",
tl:"Ang bulate sa lupa ay mahalaga sa malusog na lupa dahil hinahalo nito ang lupa at ginagawang pataba ang mga patay na dahon. Maraming uri ng bulate, kabilang ang linta at mga bulate sa dagat."});

;
/* data/living-4.js */
// Animal Kingdom (plants) articles, living part 4: bamboo, cacti, carnivorous plants, and mosses and ferns.
ADDART({id:"bamboo",t:"Bamboo",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🎋",w:"Bamboo",r:["panda","trees","photo","flower"],
p:[["A giant grass","Bamboo is not a tree but a giant grass, the tallest member of the grass family."],["Record grower","Some bamboos can grow nearly a meter in a single day, making them among the fastest-growing plants."],["Thousands of uses","People use bamboo for building, furniture, food, paper, musical instruments and much more."]],
l:"Bamboo is a group of more than 1,400 species of fast-growing grasses with woody, hollow stems called culms. Found naturally in Asia, Africa, the Americas and northern Australia, bamboo grows from cool mountain slopes to tropical forests. Some species reach over 30 meters tall. Strong, light and renewable, bamboo has been used by people for thousands of years for houses, scaffolding, tools, food and crafts, and it is the main food of the giant panda.",
s:[["Growing fast","Bamboo grows from underground stems called rhizomes. New shoots push up already as wide as they will ever be and stretch upward quickly; certain species have been measured growing about 90 centimeters in a day. A stem reaches its full height in a few months, then hardens over the next few years."],["Strong and light","Bamboo culms are hollow, divided into sections by solid nodes. This design makes them light but very strong for their weight. Some bamboo has a tensile strength comparable to steel, and in many parts of Asia it is used for scaffolding on tall buildings."],["Flowering all at once","Many bamboos flower only once every few decades, sometimes more than 100 years, and all plants of the same species flower at the same time, even in different countries, then die. The sudden supply of seeds can cause rat populations to explode, leading to famines in places such as northeastern India."],["Running and clumping","Running bamboos send rhizomes far underground and can spread quickly, sometimes becoming invasive. Clumping bamboos grow in tight groups. Gardeners plant clumping types or use barriers to keep running types from taking over."]],
k:[["Family","Grasses (Poaceae)"],["Species","More than 1,400"],["Fastest growth","About 90 cm in a day"],["Tallest","Over 30 m"],["Main users","People and giant pandas"]],
d:[["c. 500 BCE","Texts are written on bamboo strips in China (earliest surviving examples)"],["c. 105 CE","Paper is developed in China, later sometimes made from bamboo"],["1880","Thomas Edison uses carbonized bamboo in early light bulbs"],["1997","International Network for Bamboo and Rattan is founded"],["2009","First World Bamboo Day is held on 18 September"]],
s2:[["Bamboo in daily life","Bamboo is used for houses, bridges, floors, furniture, baskets, mats, chopsticks, fishing rods and musical instruments such as flutes and the angklung. Young bamboo shoots are eaten in many Asian dishes. Fibers from bamboo are made into paper and cloth."],["Writing on bamboo","Before paper became common, scribes in ancient China wrote on strips of bamboo tied together with cords to form books. Many important early texts survive on bamboo slips discovered by archaeologists in tombs."],["Light bulbs","When Thomas Edison was searching for a long-lasting filament for his electric light bulbs, his team tested thousands of materials. Carbonized bamboo from Japan worked well, and was used in his bulbs for several years in the 1880s."],["Green material?","Because it grows quickly and regrows from its roots after cutting, bamboo is promoted as a sustainable material that can replace some wood and plastics. Bamboo forests also absorb carbon dioxide and protect soil from erosion. However, processing and transporting bamboo products can use chemicals and energy."],["Bamboo forests","Famous bamboo forests include Arashiyama in Kyoto, Japan, where tall stems sway and creak in the wind, and the bamboo forests of Sichuan in China, home to giant pandas. Many animals besides pandas depend on bamboo, including red pandas, mountain gorillas and bamboo lemurs."],["The angklung","The angklung, a musical instrument from Indonesia made of bamboo tubes in a frame, produces a single note when shaken. A group of players, each with one or two angklungs, can play whole melodies together. UNESCO added it to its list of the world’s intangible cultural heritage in 2010."]],
sp:["Faster than you can watch","Some species of bamboo grow so fast that, on a warm, wet day, you could almost see them move. Growth of nearly a meter in 24 hours works out to about 4 centimeters an hour. Bamboo achieves this not by making many new cells quickly but by rapidly stretching cells that formed while the shoot was still underground."],
g:[["Culm","The woody, hollow stem of bamboo"],["Rhizome","An underground stem that sends up new shoots"],["Node","A solid joint dividing a bamboo stem into sections"],["Invasive","Spreading quickly into places where it is not wanted"]],
f:["Bamboo is a grass, not a tree.","Some bamboo grows nearly a meter in a day.","Many bamboos flower all at once only every few decades."],
fun:"Thomas Edison used bamboo from Japan to make the filaments of early light bulbs.",
tl:"Ang kawayan ay hindi puno kundi higanteng damo at isa sa pinakamabilis tumubong halaman. Ginagamit ito sa paggawa ng bahay, kasangkapan, pagkain at instrumentong pangmusika."});

ADDART({id:"cactus",t:"Cacti and Desert Plants",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🌵",w:"Cactus",r:["desert","flower","mexicocountry","photo"],
p:[["Surviving drought","Cacti and other desert plants have special ways to survive with very little water."],["Spines, not leaves","Most cacti have spines instead of leaves, which protect them and reduce water loss."],["Native to the Americas","Almost all of the roughly 1,750 cactus species are native to the Americas."]],
l:"Cacti are a family of flowering plants adapted to dry environments, with about 1,750 species. Nearly all are native to the Americas, from Canada to Patagonia, though some have spread to other continents. Cacti store water in thick, fleshy stems, have spines instead of leaves and often have waxy skins that slow evaporation. They range from tiny button cacti to the giant saguaro, which can grow over 12 meters tall. Other desert plants, such as succulents and acacias, have their own clever ways to survive drought.",
s:[["Storing water","Cacti store water in spongy tissue inside their stems. Many have pleated or ribbed stems that can swell like an accordion after rain and shrink during drought. A large saguaro can hold several tonnes of water."],["Spines","A cactus’s spines are modified leaves. They protect the plant from thirsty animals, provide shade, trap a layer of still air that reduces water loss, and in some species collect dew. Some cacti are covered with soft white hairs that reflect sunlight."],["Saving water","Cacti open their breathing pores, called stomata, at night, when it is cooler, to take in carbon dioxide, storing it until daytime for photosynthesis. This special process, called CAM photosynthesis, greatly reduces water loss. Their wide, shallow roots quickly soak up rain."],["Flowers and fruit","Many cacti produce large, colorful flowers, often blooming for only a day or a night. They are pollinated by bees, birds, moths and bats. Cactus fruits, such as prickly pears and dragon fruit, are eaten by animals and people."]],
k:[["Family","Cactaceae"],["Species","About 1,750"],["Native to","The Americas"],["Tallest","Mexican giant cardon and saguaro, over 12 m"],["Special photosynthesis","CAM"]],
d:[["Thousands of years ago","People in Mexico begin using prickly pear cactus for food"],["1325","Legend says the Aztecs found Tenochtitlan where an eagle sat on a cactus"],["1500s","Spanish explorers carry prickly pears to Europe"],["1920s","Cactus moths are used to control invasive prickly pears in Australia"],["1994","Saguaro National Park is established in Arizona"]],
s2:[["The saguaro","The saguaro cactus of the Sonoran Desert in the southwestern United States and northwestern Mexico grows very slowly, taking about 10 years to reach a few centimeters and 50 to 70 years to grow its first arm. It can live for 150 years or more. Woodpeckers carve nest holes in it, later used by owls and other birds."],["Mexico’s cactus","The prickly pear, or nopal, is important in Mexican food and culture. Its pads and fruits are eaten, and it appears on Mexico’s flag, which shows an eagle perched on a cactus eating a snake, recalling the legend of the founding of the Aztec capital, Tenochtitlan."],["Other desert survivors","Plants in other deserts have evolved similar features independently. In Africa, euphorbias look remarkably like cacti, with spines and fleshy stems. Living stones, or lithops, look like pebbles to avoid being eaten. Welwitschia, in the Namib Desert, has just two long leaves and can live for over 1,000 years."],["Invasive cacti","Prickly pears brought to Australia in the 1800s spread over millions of hectares of farmland. In the 1920s scientists introduced the cactus moth from South America, whose caterpillars eat prickly pear, and it cleared vast areas, one of the most successful cases of biological control."],["Cacti in danger","Many cacti are threatened by collection for the plant trade, farming and building. A 2015 study found that about 31 percent of cactus species are threatened with extinction, making them one of the most threatened groups of living things assessed."],["Dragon fruit","Dragon fruit, or pitaya, comes from climbing cacti native to Central America. Its bright pink or yellow skin covers white or red flesh dotted with tiny black seeds. It is now grown widely in Vietnam, the Philippines, Thailand, China and other countries."]],
sp:["Night bloomers","Some cacti, such as the night-blooming cereus and queen of the night, open their large, fragrant white flowers for just one night, often only once a year. They are pollinated by moths and bats. In some places, people gather to watch them bloom, and botanical gardens hold special night openings."],
g:[["Succulent","A plant with thick, fleshy parts that store water"],["Spine","A sharp, modified leaf on a cactus"],["Stomata","Tiny pores in plants that let gases in and out"],["CAM photosynthesis","A way of photosynthesis that saves water by opening pores at night"]],
f:["A cactus’s spines are modified leaves.","A saguaro takes 50 to 70 years to grow its first arm.","About 31 percent of cactus species are threatened."],
fun:"A cactus appears on the national flag of Mexico.",
tl:"Ang cactus ay halamang nabubuhay sa tuyong lugar at nag-iimbak ng tubig sa makapal na tangkay. May tinik ito sa halip na dahon. Halos lahat ng uri nito ay katutubo sa Amerika."});

ADDART({id:"carnivplant",t:"Carnivorous Plants",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🪴",w:"Carnivorous plant",r:["flower","insect","photo","wetlands"],
p:[["Plants that eat animals","Carnivorous plants trap and digest insects and other small animals."],["Clever traps","They use snapping leaves, slippery pitchers, sticky hairs and underwater bladders."],["Living in poor soils","Most grow in bogs and other places where the soil lacks nutrients such as nitrogen."]],
l:"Carnivorous plants are plants that attract, trap and digest animals, mostly insects, to obtain nutrients. There are more than 800 species, including the Venus flytrap, pitcher plants, sundews and bladderworts. They still make their own food by photosynthesis, but catch prey to get nutrients such as nitrogen and phosphorus that are scarce in the wet, acidic soils where they usually grow. Charles Darwin was fascinated by them and wrote a book about them in 1875.",
s:[["Snap traps","The Venus flytrap, which grows wild only in a small area of North and South Carolina in the United States, has hinged leaves lined with trigger hairs. If an insect touches two hairs within about 20 seconds, the trap snaps shut in a fraction of a second. It then seals and digests the prey over several days."],["Pitfall traps","Pitcher plants have leaves shaped like jugs, filled with digestive fluid. Insects are lured by nectar and color to the slippery rim, fall in and cannot climb out. Asian Nepenthes pitchers, found from Southeast Asia to Australia, can be large enough to catch frogs and occasionally small mammals."],["Sticky traps","Sundews and butterworts cover their leaves with glistening drops of sticky glue that look like nectar or dew. Insects get stuck, and the leaves or tentacles slowly curl around them. Sundews are found on every continent except Antarctica."],["Suction traps","Bladderworts, which float in water or grow in wet soil, have tiny bladders that are kept under low pressure. When a small water animal brushes a trigger hair, the bladder’s door opens and sucks it in within a fraction of a millisecond, one of the fastest movements in the plant world."]],
k:[["Species","More than 800"],["Famous examples","Venus flytrap, pitcher plants, sundews, bladderworts"],["Why carnivorous","To get nitrogen and other nutrients"],["Habitat","Bogs, swamps and poor soils"],["Darwin’s book","Insectivorous Plants (1875)"]],
d:[["1760","The Venus flytrap is described by Arthur Dobbs"],["1875","Darwin publishes Insectivorous Plants"],["2007","A rat-trapping pitcher plant is discovered in the Philippines"],["2009","Nepenthes attenboroughii is named after David Attenborough"],["2016","Study shows Venus flytraps count touches"]],
s2:[["Darwin’s favorite plants","Charles Darwin experimented with sundews, feeding them meat, egg and even his own hair, to show that they digested animal matter. He called the Venus flytrap one of the most wonderful plants in the world. His 1875 book Insectivorous Plants helped prove that plants could be carnivorous."],["Counting plants","In 2016 scientists found that the Venus flytrap effectively counts. One touch does nothing, two close the trap, and after five or more the trap begins producing digestive enzymes. This helps the plant avoid wasting energy on raindrops or tiny insects that could escape."],["Pitcher plants of the Philippines","Many species of Nepenthes grow in the Philippines, Borneo and Sumatra. Nepenthes attenboroughii, discovered on Mount Victoria in Palawan and named after the naturalist David Attenborough in 2009, has pitchers large enough to trap rats. New species are still being found on remote mountains."],["Partners not prey","Some pitcher plants have unusual partnerships. In Borneo, one species provides a toilet for tree shrews, which feed on nectar and leave droppings in the pitcher, supplying nitrogen. Another hosts bats that roost inside it. Some insects and spiders live safely inside pitchers, eating trapped prey."],["Threats","Many carnivorous plants are rare and threatened by draining of wetlands, development, fire suppression and illegal collection. The wild Venus flytrap is protected, and digging it up in North Carolina is a felony."],["Growing them at home","Carnivorous plants are popular with gardeners. Most need rainwater or distilled water, lots of light, and poor, acidic soil such as peat moss. They should not be fed hamburger or fertilizer, which can kill them; they catch insects themselves."]],
sp:["Rat-eating plants","In 2007 scientists exploring Mount Victoria on the island of Palawan in the Philippines found a pitcher plant with pitchers up to about 30 centimeters tall. Named Nepenthes attenboroughii, it is one of the largest carnivorous plants and has been reported to trap rats, though it mostly catches insects."],
g:[["Carnivorous plant","A plant that traps and digests animals"],["Pitcher","A jug-shaped leaf that traps insects"],["Enzyme","A substance that speeds up chemical reactions, such as digestion"],["Bog","Wet, spongy ground with acidic soil"]],
f:["The Venus flytrap grows wild only in North and South Carolina.","Bladderworts have one of the fastest movements of any plant.","Some pitcher plants can trap rats."],
fun:"A Venus flytrap only shuts if an insect touches its trigger hairs twice within about 20 seconds.",
tl:"Ang mga halamang carnivorous, gaya ng Venus flytrap at pitcher plant, ay nanghuhuli at tumutunaw ng insekto upang makakuha ng sustansiya. Ilang uri nito ang matatagpuan sa Palawan."});

ADDART({id:"ferns",t:"Mosses and Ferns",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🌿",w:"Fern",r:["trees","photo","flower","fungi"],
p:[["Plants without flowers","Mosses and ferns are plants that reproduce by spores instead of seeds and never make flowers."],["Ancient plants","Their ancestors were among the first plants to grow on land, hundreds of millions of years ago."],["Coal from ferns","Giant ferns and their relatives formed the forests whose remains became coal."]],
l:"Mosses and ferns are non-flowering plants that reproduce by spores rather than seeds. Mosses are small, soft plants without true roots or veins that grow in dense carpets in damp places. Ferns are larger, with true roots, stems and veins, and feathery leaves called fronds. Both groups descend from some of the earliest land plants. Today there are about 12,000 species of moss and about 10,500 species of fern and their relatives, found from rainforests to the Arctic.",
s:[["Mosses","Mosses have no true roots, but tiny threads called rhizoids that anchor them. Without veins to carry water, they absorb it directly through their leaves, which is why they grow in damp, shady places and stay small. Many can dry out completely and revive when it rains. Peat moss, or sphagnum, forms vast bogs."],["Ferns","Ferns have veins that carry water and food, letting them grow larger. New fronds unroll from tight coils called fiddleheads. Ferns range from tiny floating water ferns to tree ferns over 10 meters tall, which grow in tropical and subtropical forests, from Australia and New Zealand to the Philippines."],["Spores","Instead of seeds, mosses and ferns release spores, tiny cells carried by wind. Ferns make spores in brown clusters on the undersides of their fronds. A spore grows into a small, separate plant that produces eggs and sperm; the sperm must swim through a film of water to reach an egg, so these plants need moisture to reproduce."],["Pioneers","Mosses are often among the first plants to grow on bare rock or soil, after lichens. They trap dust and water, slowly building soil in which other plants can grow. Mosses help prevent erosion and hold huge amounts of water, releasing it slowly to streams."]],
k:[["Moss species","About 12,000"],["Fern species","About 10,500"],["Reproduce by","Spores"],["Tallest ferns","Tree ferns, over 10 m"],["First land plants","Over 470 million years ago"]],
d:[["c. 470 million years ago","The first plants colonize land"],["c. 360 million years ago","Fern-like plants form vast forests"],["c. 300 million years ago","Coal swamp forests flourish"],["1840s–1890s","Fern craze, called Pteridomania, sweeps Victorian Britain"],["2018","Fern genomes are sequenced for the first time"]],
s2:[["Forests that became coal","About 300 million years ago, in the Carboniferous period, swampy forests of giant club mosses, horsetails, tree ferns and early seed plants covered much of the land. When they died, they were buried and slowly turned into coal. The name Carboniferous means coal-bearing."],["Peat bogs","Sphagnum moss forms peat bogs, where dead moss builds up over thousands of years without fully rotting. Peatlands cover only about 3 percent of Earth’s land but store about twice as much carbon as all the world’s forests. Draining or burning them releases this carbon, adding to climate change."],["Bog bodies","Because peat bogs are acidic, cold and low in oxygen, they can preserve things for thousands of years. Bodies of people from the Iron Age, such as Tollund Man in Denmark, have been found in bogs with skin, hair and even their last meals preserved."],["Fern fever","In the mid-1800s, a craze for ferns, called Pteridomania, swept Britain. People collected ferns from the countryside, grew them in glass cases and decorated pottery, fabric and gravestones with fern patterns. Some wild ferns became rare because of overcollecting."],["Ferns we eat and use","Young fiddleheads of some ferns are eaten as vegetables in parts of Asia and North America, such as pako in the Philippines. Water ferns called Azolla live with bacteria that capture nitrogen and are used in rice paddies as natural fertilizer. Some ferns, however, are poisonous."],["Mosses everywhere","Mosses grow on every continent, including Antarctica, where they are among the few plants that survive. Some mosses can survive being frozen for centuries: in 2014 scientists revived moss that had been buried under Antarctic ice for about 1,500 years."]],
sp:["Reviving ancient moss","In 2014 British scientists took a core of moss from deep in an Antarctic moss bank, where it had been frozen for about 1,500 years. Placed in a warm, lit incubator, the moss began to grow again within weeks. The experiment showed that some plants can survive very long periods of freezing, which may help them recolonize land after ice ages."],
g:[["Spore","A tiny reproductive cell that can grow into a new plant"],["Frond","The leaf of a fern"],["Fiddlehead","The coiled young frond of a fern"],["Peat","Partly decayed plant matter, mostly moss, that builds up in bogs"]],
f:["Mosses and ferns reproduce by spores, not seeds.","Coal formed from ancient swamp forests of fern-like plants.","Moss frozen for about 1,500 years has been brought back to life."],
fun:"Peatlands store about twice as much carbon as all of the world’s forests.",
tl:"Ang lumot at pako ay mga halamang hindi namumulaklak at nagpaparami gamit ang spore. Kabilang ang kanilang mga ninuno sa mga unang halamang tumubo sa lupa, at nagmula sa sinaunang kagubatan ng pako ang uling."});

;
/* data/war-1.js */
// History articles, war part 1: the Western Front, the Treaty of Versailles and the rise of fascism.
ADDART({id:"westernfront",t:"The Western Front",c:"History",i:"🪖",w:"Western Front (World War I)",r:["wwi","francecountry","germanycountry","ukcountry"],
p:[["A line of trenches","The Western Front was a line of trenches stretching about 700 kilometers from the Belgian coast to Switzerland during World War I."],["Stalemate","For almost four years, neither side could break through, and millions of soldiers died for small gains."],["Industrial warfare","Machine guns, heavy artillery, poison gas, tanks and aircraft changed the nature of war."]],
l:"The Western Front was the main theater of fighting in World War I in western Europe, where the armies of Germany faced those of France, Britain, Belgium and, from 1917, the United States. After Germany’s rapid invasion of Belgium and France in 1914 was halted at the Battle of the Marne, both sides dug in. A line of trenches ran from the North Sea to the Swiss border, and for nearly four years huge battles such as Verdun and the Somme cost millions of lives for little ground. The front finally broke in 1918, leading to Germany’s defeat.",
s:[["The race to the sea","In August 1914 Germany invaded Belgium and northern France, hoping to defeat France quickly before turning on Russia. In September, French and British forces stopped the advance at the Marne, near Paris. Each side then tried to outflank the other northward in a race to the sea, until trenches stretched all the way to the coast."],["Life in the trenches","Soldiers lived in muddy, flooded ditches, protected by sandbags and barbed wire, with rats, lice and the constant danger of shellfire and snipers. Between the opposing trenches lay no man’s land, a stretch of mud and craters. Units rotated between the front line, support trenches and rest areas behind the lines."],["Verdun","In February 1916, Germany attacked the French fortress city of Verdun, hoping to wear down the French army. The battle lasted about ten months and caused around 700,000 casualties on both sides. The French rallying cry They shall not pass became famous, and Verdun became a symbol of endurance and slaughter in France."],["The Somme","On 1 July 1916, British and French forces attacked along the River Somme. On the first day alone, the British army suffered about 57,000 casualties, including about 19,000 dead, the worst day in its history. The battle lasted until November, with more than a million casualties in total, and the Allies advanced only about 10 kilometers."]],
k:[["Length","About 700 km, from the North Sea to Switzerland"],["Duration","1914–1918"],["Main opponents","Germany against France, Britain, Belgium and later the United States"],["Bloodiest battles","Verdun, the Somme, Passchendaele"],["Ended","Armistice, 11 November 1918"]],
d:[["August 1914","Germany invades Belgium and France"],["September 1914","Battle of the Marne halts the German advance"],["December 1914","Christmas Truce along parts of the front"],["February 1916","Battle of Verdun begins"],["July 1916","Battle of the Somme begins"],["11 November 1918","Armistice ends the fighting"]],
s2:[["The Christmas Truce","At Christmas 1914, along parts of the front, British and German soldiers stopped fighting on their own initiative. They sang carols, met in no man’s land, exchanged gifts and buried their dead; in a few places they even played football. Commanders on both sides disapproved, and such truces were not repeated on the same scale."],["New weapons","Machine guns and artillery made attacking across open ground deadly. In 1915 Germany used poison chlorine gas at Ypres, and soon both sides used gas, which caused terrible suffering. The British introduced tanks at the Somme in 1916, and aircraft were used first for spotting and later for fighting and bombing."],["Soldiers from around the world","Millions of soldiers from the British and French empires fought on the Western Front, including troops from India, Canada, Australia, New Zealand, South Africa, Senegal, Morocco and Vietnam. Chinese and Egyptian laborers dug trenches and built roads behind the lines."],["Passchendaele","In 1917, British and Commonwealth forces attacked near Ypres in Belgium in the Third Battle of Ypres, known as Passchendaele. Heavy rain turned the battlefield into a sea of mud in which men, horses and guns sank. After three months, the Allies had advanced only a few kilometers at the cost of about half a million casualties on both sides."],["The breakthrough","In spring 1918, Germany launched a series of huge offensives, gaining ground but exhausting its army. From August, Allied forces, now joined by large numbers of American troops and using tanks, aircraft and artillery together, pushed the Germans back in the Hundred Days Offensive. Germany asked for an armistice, which took effect at 11 a.m. on 11 November 1918."],["Remembering","The landscapes of the Western Front are scattered with war cemeteries and memorials, such as the Menin Gate at Ypres, where buglers have played the Last Post every evening since 1928, and the memorials at Thiepval and Verdun. Poppies, which bloomed in the churned fields, became a symbol of remembrance in many countries."]],
sp:["In Flanders Fields","In May 1915, after the death of a friend, the Canadian doctor John McCrae wrote the poem In Flanders Fields, describing poppies blowing between the crosses of soldiers’ graves. The poem became one of the most famous of the war and inspired the use of red poppies to remember those who died in war, a tradition still followed on Remembrance Day in many countries."],
g:[["Trench","A long, narrow ditch dug to protect soldiers"],["No man’s land","The ground between opposing trenches"],["Stalemate","A situation in which neither side can win"],["Armistice","An agreement to stop fighting"]],
f:["The Western Front stretched about 700 kilometers.","The first day of the Somme was the bloodiest day in British military history.","Soldiers held a Christmas Truce in 1914."],
fun:"Buglers have played the Last Post at the Menin Gate in Ypres almost every evening since 1928.",
tl:"Ang Western Front ay linya ng mga trinsera sa kanlurang Europa noong Unang Digmaang Pandaigdig. Sa loob ng halos apat na taon, milyon-milyong sundalo ang namatay sa mga labanang gaya ng Verdun at Somme."});

ADDART({id:"versailles",t:"The Treaty of Versailles",c:"History",i:"📜",w:"Treaty of Versailles",r:["wwi","ww2","germanycountry","fascism","un"],
p:[["Ending World War I","The Treaty of Versailles, signed on 28 June 1919, formally ended the war between Germany and the Allied Powers."],["Harsh terms","Germany lost territory, its colonies and most of its military, accepted blame for the war and was ordered to pay reparations."],["A troubled peace","Many historians see the treaty’s terms and their effects as one of the causes of World War II."]],
l:"The Treaty of Versailles was the most important of the peace treaties that ended World War I. Negotiated at the Paris Peace Conference and signed in the Hall of Mirrors at the Palace of Versailles on 28 June 1919, five years to the day after the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand, it set out the terms imposed on Germany. It also created the League of Nations. The treaty was bitterly resented in Germany, and its legacy remains much debated by historians.",
s:[["The peacemakers","The conference was dominated by the leaders of the victorious powers: President Woodrow Wilson of the United States, Prime Minister David Lloyd George of Britain, Prime Minister Georges Clemenceau of France and Prime Minister Vittorio Orlando of Italy. Germany was not allowed to negotiate and was presented with the terms."],["Territory and colonies","Germany returned Alsace-Lorraine to France, lost land to the new Poland, including a corridor to the sea that split East Prussia from the rest of Germany, and gave smaller areas to Belgium, Denmark and Czechoslovakia. All its colonies in Africa and the Pacific were handed to other powers as mandates."],["Military limits","The German army was limited to 100,000 men, and Germany was banned from having an air force, submarines, tanks or conscription. The Rhineland, the German region bordering France, was to be demilitarized and occupied by Allied troops for 15 years."],["War guilt and reparations","Article 231, often called the war guilt clause, held Germany and its allies responsible for the losses caused by the war. Germany was required to pay reparations; in 1921 the amount was set at 132 billion gold marks, though in practice only a fraction was ever paid."]],
k:[["Signed","28 June 1919"],["Place","Hall of Mirrors, Palace of Versailles, France"],["Main leaders","Wilson, Lloyd George, Clemenceau, Orlando"],["Created","The League of Nations"],["Reparations set","132 billion gold marks (1921)"]],
d:[["January 1919","Paris Peace Conference opens"],["28 June 1919","Treaty of Versailles is signed"],["January 1920","Treaty takes effect; League of Nations is founded"],["1921","Reparations are set at 132 billion gold marks"],["1923","France and Belgium occupy the Ruhr when Germany falls behind on payments"],["1936","Germany remilitarizes the Rhineland in breach of the treaty"]],
s2:[["Wilson’s Fourteen Points","In January 1918, President Wilson set out Fourteen Points for peace, including open diplomacy, free trade, arms reduction, the right of peoples to determine their own government and an association of nations. Many Germans expected a peace based on them and felt betrayed by the final terms. Self-determination was applied mainly in Europe, not to colonies."],["The League of Nations","The treaty created the League of Nations, the first worldwide organization meant to keep peace through cooperation. However, the United States Senate refused to ratify the treaty, so the United States never joined. The League settled some disputes but failed to stop aggression in the 1930s and was replaced by the United Nations in 1945."],["Voices from the colonies","Many people from colonized lands went to Paris hoping to be heard. The young Vietnamese activist who later became known as Ho Chi Minh petitioned for rights for his people, and Japan’s proposal to include a statement of racial equality was rejected. China was angered when German rights in Shandong were given to Japan, sparking the May Fourth Movement."],["A Carthaginian peace?","The British economist John Maynard Keynes, who attended the conference, wrote in 1919 that the treaty was too harsh and would damage Europe’s economy. Others argued it was not harsh enough to keep Germany weak, or that it was enforced too inconsistently. Historians still debate whether the treaty itself, or the failure to uphold it, contributed more to the next war."],["Resentment in Germany","Many Germans called the treaty a Diktat, a dictated peace, and blamed the politicians of the new Weimar Republic who signed it. Nationalists spread the false stab-in-the-back myth that Germany had not been defeated on the battlefield but betrayed at home. Adolf Hitler exploited this anger as he rose to power."],["Other treaties","Separate treaties were signed with Germany’s allies: Saint-Germain with Austria, Trianon with Hungary, Neuilly with Bulgaria and Sèvres with the Ottoman Empire, later replaced by the Treaty of Lausanne with Türkiye in 1923. Together they redrew the map of Europe and the Middle East, creating new states such as Poland, Czechoslovakia and Yugoslavia."]],
sp:["The Hall of Mirrors","The treaty was signed in the Hall of Mirrors at Versailles, the same room where, in 1871, the German Empire had been proclaimed after France’s defeat in the Franco-Prussian War. The French deliberately chose the location to reverse that humiliation. In 1940, Hitler in turn insisted that France sign its surrender in the same railway carriage used for the 1918 armistice."],
g:[["Reparations","Payments for damage caused by war"],["Mandate","A territory placed under another country’s administration by the League of Nations"],["Self-determination","The right of a people to choose their own government"],["Demilitarized","Without military forces or fortifications"]],
f:["The treaty was signed five years to the day after Franz Ferdinand’s assassination.","The United States never joined the League of Nations.","Germany’s army was limited to 100,000 men."],
fun:"Germany made its final payment on bonds connected with World War I reparations in 2010.",
tl:"Ang Kasunduan sa Versailles noong 1919 ang pormal na nagwakas sa Unang Digmaang Pandaigdig. Mabigat ang mga kondisyon nito sa Alemanya, at itinuturing ito ng maraming historyador na isa sa mga sanhi ng Ikalawang Digmaang Pandaigdig."});

ADDART({id:"fascism",t:"The Rise of Fascism",c:"History",i:"⚠️",w:"Fascism",r:["ww2","holocaust","versailles","depression","democ"],
p:[["A dangerous ideology","Fascism is a far-right political movement that glorifies the nation, rejects democracy and demands obedience to a single leader."],["Italy and Germany","Benito Mussolini took power in Italy in 1922, and Adolf Hitler’s Nazi Party in Germany in 1933."],["Road to war","Fascist expansion and aggression in the 1930s led to World War II and the Holocaust."]],
l:"Fascism is an authoritarian, ultranationalist political movement that arose in Europe after World War I. Fascist movements glorified the nation and often a supposed racial community, rejected democracy, liberalism and communism, used violence against opponents and demanded total loyalty to a leader. Benito Mussolini founded the first fascist movement in Italy and came to power in 1922. In Germany, Adolf Hitler’s National Socialist, or Nazi, Party took power in 1933 and established a dictatorship whose aggression led to World War II and the genocide of the Holocaust.",
s:[["Why fascism grew","After World War I, many Europeans faced economic hardship, unemployment, fear of communist revolution and anger at the peace treaties. Some felt democratic governments were weak and divided. Fascist movements promised national greatness, order and strong leadership, and blamed scapegoats, such as minorities and political opponents, for the nation’s problems."],["Mussolini’s Italy","Benito Mussolini, a former socialist journalist, founded the Fascist movement in 1919. Its blackshirted squads attacked socialists and trade unionists. In October 1922, after the March on Rome, the king appointed Mussolini prime minister. By 1926 he had banned other parties and ruled as a dictator, calling himself Il Duce, the leader."],["Hitler and the Nazis","Adolf Hitler, an Austrian-born veteran of World War I, became leader of the Nazi Party, which combined extreme nationalism with violent antisemitism. After the Great Depression hit Germany, the party became the largest in parliament. Hitler was appointed chancellor in January 1933 and within months had banned other parties and taken dictatorial power."],["Life under fascist rule","Fascist states controlled the press, schools and youth groups, used propaganda and mass rallies, and imprisoned or killed opponents. Secret police, such as the Gestapo in Germany, spied on citizens. In Germany, Jews were stripped of their rights by the Nuremberg Laws of 1935 and faced growing persecution."]],
k:[["Began","Italy, 1919"],["Key leaders","Benito Mussolini (Italy), Adolf Hitler (Germany)"],["Mussolini in power","1922–1943"],["Nazis in power","1933–1945"],["Main features","Ultranationalism, dictatorship, suppression of opposition, militarism"]],
d:[["1919","Mussolini founds the Fascist movement"],["October 1922","March on Rome; Mussolini becomes prime minister"],["30 January 1933","Hitler becomes chancellor of Germany"],["1935","Nuremberg Laws strip German Jews of citizenship; Italy invades Ethiopia"],["1936–1939","Spanish Civil War; Franco’s forces win with fascist support"],["1938","Germany annexes Austria; Kristallnacht attacks on Jews"]],
s2:[["Aggression in the 1930s","Fascist states pursued expansion by force. Italy invaded Ethiopia in 1935, using poison gas. Germany rearmed, remilitarized the Rhineland in 1936, annexed Austria in March 1938 and took the Sudetenland from Czechoslovakia after the Munich Agreement. Japan, ruled by militarists, invaded Manchuria in 1931 and China in 1937."],["Appeasement","Britain and France, eager to avoid another war, often tried to satisfy Hitler’s demands, a policy called appeasement. At Munich in September 1938, they agreed to let Germany take the Sudetenland. British Prime Minister Neville Chamberlain claimed it meant peace for our time, but in March 1939 Germany seized the rest of Czechoslovakia."],["The Spanish Civil War","From 1936 to 1939, Spain was torn by civil war between the elected Republican government and nationalist rebels led by General Francisco Franco. Germany and Italy sent troops and aircraft to help Franco, while the Soviet Union aided the Republic and volunteers came from many countries. In 1937, German and Italian planes bombed the town of Guernica, inspiring Picasso’s famous painting."],["Resistance","Many people opposed fascism at great risk, including socialists, communists, liberals, religious believers and students. In Germany, the White Rose student group distributed anti-Nazi leaflets and its members, including Sophie and Hans Scholl, were executed in 1943. During World War II, resistance movements fought occupation across Europe."],["The end of fascist rule","Fascist Italy and Nazi Germany were defeated in World War II. Mussolini was overthrown in 1943 and killed by Italian partisans in April 1945. Hitler took his own life in Berlin on 30 April 1945, days before Germany surrendered. At the Nuremberg trials, surviving Nazi leaders were tried for war crimes and crimes against humanity."],["Learning from history","Historians study how fascism rose to understand how democracies can fail: through economic crisis, political violence, propaganda, the targeting of minorities and the weakening of courts, the press and elections. Remembering the victims and the consequences of fascism is an important part of education in many countries."]],
sp:["The word fascism","The word fascism comes from the Italian fascio, meaning a bundle, and from the ancient Roman fasces, a bundle of rods tied around an axe carried before Roman officials as a symbol of authority. Mussolini adopted the fasces as the symbol of his movement to suggest strength through unity and to link his rule to the glory of ancient Rome."],
g:[["Fascism","A far-right movement that rejects democracy and demands obedience to a leader"],["Dictatorship","Rule by one person or group with unlimited power"],["Appeasement","Giving in to an aggressor’s demands to avoid conflict"],["Propaganda","Information used to spread a particular political message"]],
f:["Mussolini came to power in Italy in 1922.","Hitler became chancellor of Germany on 30 January 1933.","The word fascism comes from the Roman fasces."],
fun:"The word fascism comes from the fasces, a bundle of rods carried by officials in ancient Rome.",
tl:"Ang pasismo ay kilusang pampulitika na sumasamba sa bansa, tumatanggi sa demokrasya at humihingi ng ganap na pagsunod sa isang lider. Umakyat sa kapangyarihan si Mussolini sa Italya at si Hitler sa Alemanya, at humantong ito sa Ikalawang Digmaang Pandaigdig."});

;
/* data/war-2.js */
// History articles, war part 2: Stalingrad, D-Day and the Pacific War.
ADDART({id:"stalingrad",t:"The Battle of Stalingrad",c:"History",i:"❄️",w:"Battle of Stalingrad",r:["ww2","russiacountry","germanycountry","russrev"],
p:[["A turning point","The Battle of Stalingrad, from 1942 to 1943, was a turning point of World War II in Europe."],["Street by street","German and Soviet soldiers fought house by house through the ruined city on the Volga River."],["Huge losses","It was one of the bloodiest battles in history, with well over a million soldiers killed, wounded or captured."]],
l:"The Battle of Stalingrad was fought between Nazi Germany and its allies and the Soviet Union for control of the city of Stalingrad, now Volgograd, in southern Russia, from August 1942 to February 1943. The German Sixth Army fought its way into the city, but in November 1942 the Soviet Red Army surrounded it. After months of fighting in freezing conditions, the surviving German troops surrendered on 2 February 1943. The battle marked the end of Germany’s advance in the east and the beginning of its retreat.",
s:[["Why Stalingrad?","In June 1941, Germany invaded the Soviet Union. In 1942 Hitler sent his armies south toward the oil fields of the Caucasus. Stalingrad, an industrial city on the Volga River named after Soviet leader Joseph Stalin, protected their flank and controlled river traffic. Its name made it a symbol for both leaders."],["The attack","On 23 August 1942, German bombers struck Stalingrad in massive raids that killed many civilians and turned much of the city to rubble. The German Sixth Army, under General Friedrich Paulus, pushed into the city. Soviet forces under General Vasily Chuikov held on to a narrow strip along the river, ordered not to retreat."],["Rattenkrieg","Fighting raged through ruined factories, apartment blocks and sewers, often hand to hand. German soldiers called it Rattenkrieg, war of the rats. Snipers on both sides were feared. Soviet troops stayed close to German lines so that German aircraft and artillery could not attack without hitting their own men."],["Operation Uranus","On 19 November 1942, the Red Army launched Operation Uranus, attacking weaker Romanian forces on both sides of the city. Within days the Soviet pincers met, trapping about 300,000 German and allied soldiers. Hitler forbade a breakout, and an attempt to relieve them failed. Supplies dropped by air were far too few."]],
k:[["Dates","23 August 1942 – 2 February 1943"],["Place","Stalingrad (now Volgograd), Soviet Union"],["Commanders","Paulus (Germany); Chuikov, Zhukov (Soviet Union)"],["Casualties","Estimates range from about 1.2 to 2 million in total"],["Result","Soviet victory; German Sixth Army destroyed"]],
d:[["22 June 1941","Germany invades the Soviet Union"],["23 August 1942","Massive German air raids on Stalingrad"],["19 November 1942","Soviet counterattack, Operation Uranus, begins"],["23 November 1942","German Sixth Army is surrounded"],["31 January 1943","Paulus surrenders"],["2 February 1943","Last German troops in the city surrender"]],
s2:[["Surrender","In freezing temperatures, starving and short of ammunition, the trapped army was gradually crushed. On 31 January 1943, Paulus, just promoted to field marshal by Hitler, who expected him to fight to the death, surrendered. The last pocket gave up on 2 February. About 91,000 German prisoners were taken; only about 5,000 ever returned home."],["Civilians","Many civilians remained trapped in the city throughout the battle, living in cellars and ruins. Tens of thousands were killed. Children ran messages and gathered food in dangerous conditions. When the battle ended, the city was almost completely destroyed."],["Why it mattered","Stalingrad was Germany’s greatest defeat so far and broke the myth that its army could not be beaten. It boosted the morale of the Soviet Union and the Allies. In July 1943, Germany’s last major offensive in the east failed at Kursk, and from then on the Red Army advanced steadily toward Berlin."],["The Eastern Front","The war between Germany and the Soviet Union was the largest and deadliest theater of World War II. The Soviet Union lost an estimated 24 to 27 million people, soldiers and civilians, more than any other country. Most German military deaths in the war occurred on the Eastern Front."],["Pavlov’s House","A four-story apartment building in central Stalingrad was defended for about two months by a small group of Soviet soldiers, led for part of the time by Sergeant Yakov Pavlov. Surrounded by mines and barbed wire, they held off repeated attacks. Pavlov’s House became a famous symbol of Soviet resistance."],["Remembering Stalingrad","The city was renamed Volgograd in 1961. On the hill of Mamayev Kurgan, where some of the fiercest fighting took place, stands The Motherland Calls, a statue about 85 meters tall, among the tallest statues in the world. Streets and stations in cities such as Paris and London are named after Stalingrad."]],
sp:["The Motherland Calls","The statue The Motherland Calls, completed in 1967, shows a woman striding forward with a raised sword, calling her people to defend their country. At about 85 meters tall including the sword, it was the tallest statue in the world when built. Around it, a memorial complex holds the graves of thousands of soldiers who died in the battle."],
g:[["Encirclement","Surrounding an enemy force so it cannot escape or be supplied"],["Eastern Front","The war between Germany and its allies and the Soviet Union in World War II"],["Field marshal","The highest rank in many armies"],["Morale","The confidence and spirit of a group"]],
f:["Stalingrad is now called Volgograd.","About 91,000 German soldiers were captured at Stalingrad.","The Soviet Union lost more people in World War II than any other country."],
fun:"Only about 5,000 of the 91,000 German prisoners taken at Stalingrad ever returned home.",
tl:"Ang Labanan sa Stalingrad (1942–1943) ay naging pagbabago sa takbo ng Ikalawang Digmaang Pandaigdig sa Europa. Pinalibutan ng hukbong Sobyet ang hukbong Aleman, at isa ito sa pinakamadugong labanan sa kasaysayan."});

ADDART({id:"dday",t:"D-Day and the Normandy Landings",c:"History",i:"⚓",w:"Normandy landings",r:["ww2","francecountry","ukcountry","usacountry","canadacountry"],
p:[["The largest seaborne invasion","On 6 June 1944, Allied forces landed on the beaches of Normandy in northern France."],["Five beaches","American, British and Canadian troops came ashore on beaches code-named Utah, Omaha, Gold, Juno and Sword."],["Liberating Europe","The landings opened a second front in western Europe and led to the liberation of France."]],
l:"D-Day, 6 June 1944, was the day Allied forces launched the invasion of German-occupied France in World War II, known as Operation Overlord. In the largest seaborne invasion in history, about 156,000 soldiers from the United States, Britain, Canada and other Allied nations landed on five beaches in Normandy or dropped by parachute and glider behind them, supported by thousands of ships and aircraft. Despite heavy losses, especially at Omaha Beach, the Allies established a foothold, and Paris was liberated in August 1944.",
s:[["Planning","For years the Soviet Union had urged Britain and the United States to open a second front against Germany in the west. The invasion was planned in great secrecy under the overall command of US General Dwight D. Eisenhower. The Allies gathered millions of soldiers and huge amounts of equipment in southern England."],["Deception","To confuse the Germans about where the landings would come, the Allies ran Operation Fortitude, creating a fake army in southeastern England with inflatable tanks, dummy landing craft and false radio messages, pointing to the Pas-de-Calais, the shortest crossing. Double agents fed false information to German intelligence."],["The landings","After midnight, paratroopers and gliders landed inland to seize bridges and roads. At dawn, after bombing and naval bombardment, troops waded ashore. At Omaha Beach, American soldiers faced steep bluffs and strong German defenses and suffered heavy casualties. On the other beaches, troops advanced inland more quickly."],["The weather gamble","The landings depended on calm seas, a late-rising moon and low tide at dawn. Storms forced Eisenhower to postpone the invasion by a day. Meteorologists predicted a brief break in the weather on 6 June, and Eisenhower gave the order to go. Many German commanders, believing the weather too bad, were away from their posts."]],
k:[["Date","6 June 1944"],["Code name","Operation Overlord (landings: Operation Neptune)"],["Beaches","Utah, Omaha, Gold, Juno, Sword"],["Troops landed on D-Day","About 156,000"],["Supreme commander","General Dwight D. Eisenhower"]],
d:[["November 1943","Allied leaders at Tehran agree on the invasion"],["5 June 1944","Eisenhower decides to launch the invasion"],["6 June 1944","D-Day landings in Normandy"],["June–August 1944","Battle of Normandy"],["25 August 1944","Paris is liberated"],["8 May 1945","Germany surrenders"]],
s2:[["Casualties","Allied casualties on D-Day itself are estimated at over 10,000, including more than 4,400 confirmed dead. German losses are uncertain. During the whole Battle of Normandy, which lasted until late August, hundreds of thousands of soldiers on both sides were killed, wounded or captured, and about 20,000 French civilians died."],["Many nations","Most of the soldiers were American, British and Canadian, but Free French, Polish, Norwegian, Dutch, Belgian, Czechoslovak, Australian, New Zealand and other forces also took part. The French Resistance sabotaged railways and telephone lines to slow German reinforcements."],["Mulberry harbors","To unload supplies before they could capture a port, the Allies towed huge prefabricated concrete harbors, called Mulberries, across the Channel and assembled them off the beaches. A pipeline under the sea, PLUTO, later carried fuel. Within weeks, hundreds of thousands of vehicles and soldiers had come ashore."],["The breakout","German forces fought hard in the hedgerow country of Normandy, where small fields bordered by thick hedges made attacking difficult. In late July, the Allies broke out, and in August they trapped many German troops near Falaise. Paris rose up and was liberated on 25 August 1944 by French and American forces."],["The Eastern and Western Fronts","D-Day was one part of the Allied strategy. At the same time, in June 1944, the Soviet Union launched Operation Bagration, a massive offensive that destroyed a large German army group in Belarus. Squeezed from both east and west, Germany surrendered less than a year later."],["Remembering D-Day","The Normandy beaches are lined with memorials, museums and cemeteries, including the American Cemetery at Colleville-sur-Mer above Omaha Beach and Commonwealth and German cemeteries nearby. Each year on 6 June, veterans, leaders and visitors gather for ceremonies; the 80th anniversary in 2024 was attended by some of the last surviving veterans."]],
sp:["What does the D mean?","The D in D-Day simply stands for day. Military planners used D-Day for the date of any planned operation, and H-Hour for its starting time, so that plans could be made before the exact date was fixed. Days before or after were written as D-1 or D+1. Because of the Normandy landings, D-Day is now almost always used to mean 6 June 1944."],
g:[["Invasion","An armed attack into another country’s territory"],["Second front","A new area of fighting that divides an enemy’s forces"],["Paratrooper","A soldier trained to drop by parachute"],["Liberation","Freeing a place from occupation"]],
f:["About 156,000 Allied troops landed on D-Day.","The five beaches were code-named Utah, Omaha, Gold, Juno and Sword.","Paris was liberated on 25 August 1944."],
fun:"The Allies used inflatable tanks to trick the Germans about where the invasion would land.",
tl:"Noong 6 Hunyo 1944, D-Day, lumapag ang mga puwersang Alyado sa mga dalampasigan ng Normandy sa Pransiya. Ito ang pinakamalaking paglusob mula sa dagat sa kasaysayan at humantong sa pagpapalaya sa Pransiya."});

ADDART({id:"pacificwar",t:"The Pacific War",c:"History",i:"🌊",w:"Pacific War",r:["ww2","japocc","atomicbomb","japancountry","pacific"],
p:[["War in Asia and the Pacific","The Pacific War was the part of World War II fought in East Asia, Southeast Asia and the Pacific Ocean."],["Japan’s expansion","Japan invaded China in 1937 and, after attacking Pearl Harbor in 1941, conquered much of Southeast Asia."],["Island battles","The Allies fought back across the Pacific, island by island, until Japan surrendered in 1945."]],
l:"The Pacific War was fought between Japan and the Allies, chiefly the United States, China, Britain and its empire, Australia, New Zealand, the Netherlands, the Philippines and, at the very end, the Soviet Union. It grew out of Japan’s war in China, which began in full in 1937. After Japan attacked the US naval base at Pearl Harbor on 7 December 1941, it swept across Southeast Asia and the western Pacific. The tide turned at Midway in 1942, and after years of brutal fighting and the atomic bombings, Japan surrendered in August 1945.",
s:[["War in China","Japan seized Manchuria in 1931 and launched a full-scale invasion of China in July 1937. Japanese forces captured Shanghai and the capital, Nanjing, where they massacred tens of thousands to hundreds of thousands of civilians and prisoners. Chinese Nationalist and Communist forces kept fighting, and the war tied down huge numbers of Japanese troops."],["Pearl Harbor and expansion","On 7 December 1941, Japanese aircraft attacked the US Pacific Fleet at Pearl Harbor in Hawaii, killing about 2,400 Americans and bringing the United States into the war. Within hours Japan attacked the Philippines, Malaya and Hong Kong. By mid-1942 it had conquered Malaya, Singapore, the Dutch East Indies, the Philippines, Burma and many Pacific islands."],["Midway","In June 1942, near Midway Atoll, US code breakers who had learned Japan’s plans helped the US Navy ambush a Japanese fleet. American aircraft sank four Japanese aircraft carriers, a blow from which the Japanese navy never fully recovered. Midway is often called the turning point of the Pacific War."],["Island hopping","From 1942 the Allies advanced on two main paths: under General Douglas MacArthur through New Guinea toward the Philippines, and under Admiral Chester Nimitz across the central Pacific. They captured some islands and bypassed others, leaving Japanese garrisons cut off. Battles such as Guadalcanal, Tarawa, Saipan, Iwo Jima and Okinawa were fought with great ferocity."]],
k:[["Period","1937 (in China) / 1941 (Pearl Harbor) – 1945"],["Main powers","Japan against the United States, China, Britain, Australia and others"],["Turning point","Battle of Midway, June 1942"],["Largest naval battle","Leyte Gulf, October 1944"],["Ended","Japan’s surrender, 2 September 1945"]],
d:[["7 July 1937","Marco Polo Bridge incident begins full-scale war in China"],["7 December 1941","Japan attacks Pearl Harbor"],["15 February 1942","Singapore falls to Japan"],["4–7 June 1942","Battle of Midway"],["23–26 October 1944","Battle of Leyte Gulf"],["2 September 1945","Japan formally surrenders"]],
s2:[["Occupation and suffering","Japanese occupation brought great suffering to Asia. Forced labor, massacres and famine killed millions of people. Hundreds of thousands of laborers from Asia and Allied prisoners of war were forced to build the Burma Railway, where many died. Many women and girls from Korea, China, the Philippines and elsewhere were forced into sexual slavery by the Japanese military."],["Resistance movements","Across occupied Asia, people resisted. In the Philippines, guerrilla fighters supported by the United States harassed Japanese forces. Communist and nationalist movements in China, Vietnam, Malaya and Burma fought the occupation, and some later fought for independence from European colonial rule."],["Leyte Gulf","In October 1944, as MacArthur returned to the Philippines at Leyte, the Japanese navy made a desperate attempt to destroy the landing fleet. The Battle of Leyte Gulf, among the largest naval battles in history, ended in a decisive Allied victory. Japan first used kamikaze suicide aircraft in this battle."],["Iwo Jima and Okinawa","In 1945, US forces captured Iwo Jima, where a famous photograph showed marines raising the flag on Mount Suribachi, and then Okinawa, the largest battle of the Pacific War, lasting nearly three months. More than 100,000 Okinawan civilians, perhaps a quarter of the population, died. The heavy losses shaped US planning for an invasion of Japan’s main islands."],["The end of the war","In August 1945, the United States dropped atomic bombs on Hiroshima and Nagasaki, and the Soviet Union declared war on Japan and invaded Manchuria. On 15 August, Emperor Hirohito announced Japan’s surrender by radio. The formal surrender was signed aboard the battleship USS Missouri in Tokyo Bay on 2 September 1945."],["After the war","Japan was occupied by Allied forces led by the United States until 1952 and adopted a democratic, pacifist constitution. Leaders were tried for war crimes in Tokyo. Korea was freed from Japanese rule but divided. Across Southeast Asia, the war weakened European colonial powers, speeding movements for independence in Indonesia, Vietnam, Burma, Malaya and elsewhere."]],
sp:["Code breakers","Before Midway, US Navy cryptanalysts in Hawaii had partly broken Japan’s naval code. They knew an attack was coming on a target called AF but not where. To check, they sent a fake message saying Midway was short of fresh water; soon Japanese messages reported that AF was short of water. Knowing the target, the US Navy prepared its ambush."],
g:[["Island hopping","Capturing key islands while bypassing others"],["Kamikaze","Japanese suicide attacks by aircraft"],["Guerrilla","A fighter in a small, irregular force"],["Cryptanalyst","A person who breaks codes"]],
f:["The Battle of Midway was a turning point of the Pacific War.","Japan signed its surrender aboard the USS Missouri.","The Battle of Okinawa lasted nearly three months."],
fun:"US code breakers tricked Japan into revealing that its target was Midway with a fake message about a water shortage.",
tl:"Ang Digmaan sa Pasipiko ay bahagi ng Ikalawang Digmaang Pandaigdig sa Silangang Asya, Timog-Silangang Asya at Pasipiko. Sinakop ng Hapon ang maraming lupain, ngunit lumaban ang mga Alyado hanggang sa pagsuko ng Hapon noong 1945."});

;
/* data/war-3.js */
// History articles, war part 3: Hiroshima and Nagasaki, the Berlin Wall and the Cuban Missile Crisis.
ADDART({id:"atomicbomb",t:"Hiroshima and Nagasaki",c:"History",i:"🕊️",w:"Atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki",r:["ww2","pacificwar","nuclear","japancountry","coldwar"],
p:[["The only nuclear attacks","In August 1945, the United States dropped atomic bombs on the Japanese cities of Hiroshima and Nagasaki."],["Terrible destruction","The bombs killed an estimated 150,000 to 246,000 people by the end of 1945, most of them civilians."],["A new age","Japan surrendered days later, and the bombings began an age of nuclear weapons that still shapes the world."]],
l:"On 6 August 1945, the United States dropped an atomic bomb on Hiroshima, Japan, and on 9 August a second on Nagasaki. They remain the only uses of nuclear weapons in war. The explosions and the fires and radiation that followed killed tens of thousands instantly and many more in the following months; by the end of 1945, estimates of the dead range from about 150,000 to 246,000. Japan announced its surrender on 15 August. The bombings remain the subject of moral and historical debate.",
s:[["The Manhattan Project","Fearing that Nazi Germany might build an atomic bomb, the United States, with Britain and Canada, launched the secret Manhattan Project in 1942. Scientists led by J. Robert Oppenheimer at Los Alamos in New Mexico designed the weapons. On 16 July 1945, the first atomic bomb was tested in the desert at Trinity."],["Hiroshima","At 8:15 in the morning on 6 August 1945, the US bomber Enola Gay dropped a uranium bomb called Little Boy on Hiroshima, a city of about 350,000 people. It exploded with the force of about 15,000 tonnes of TNT, destroying most of the city in a fireball and blast. Tens of thousands died instantly."],["Nagasaki","On 9 August, a plutonium bomb called Fat Man was dropped on Nagasaki, a port city, after clouds covered the primary target, Kokura. Hills limited the damage to part of the city, but tens of thousands were killed. The same day, the Soviet Union invaded Japanese-held Manchuria."],["Surrender","On 15 August 1945, Emperor Hirohito announced Japan’s surrender in a radio broadcast, the first time most Japanese had heard his voice. He referred to a new and most cruel bomb. The formal surrender was signed on 2 September, ending World War II."]],
k:[["Hiroshima","6 August 1945, bomb named Little Boy (uranium)"],["Nagasaki","9 August 1945, bomb named Fat Man (plutonium)"],["Deaths by end of 1945","Estimates range from about 150,000 to 246,000"],["Japan’s surrender announced","15 August 1945"],["Survivors","Called hibakusha"]],
d:[["1939","Einstein signs a letter warning President Roosevelt about atomic bombs"],["1942","The Manhattan Project begins"],["16 July 1945","First atomic test at Trinity, New Mexico"],["6 August 1945","Hiroshima is bombed"],["9 August 1945","Nagasaki is bombed"],["2024","Nihon Hidankyo, an organization of survivors, wins the Nobel Peace Prize"]],
s2:[["The survivors","Survivors, called hibakusha in Japanese, suffered burns and injuries, and many later developed radiation sickness, leukemia and other cancers. They also faced discrimination. For decades, hibakusha have shared their stories to warn against nuclear weapons. In 2024 their organization, Nihon Hidankyo, won the Nobel Peace Prize."],["Debate","Historians and the public continue to debate the bombings. Supporters argue that they ended the war quickly and avoided a costly invasion of Japan that could have killed even more people, both Allied and Japanese. Critics argue that Japan was already close to surrender, that the Soviet declaration of war was decisive, or that deliberately killing civilians on such a scale was morally wrong."],["Sadako and the cranes","Sadako Sasaki was two years old when the bomb fell on Hiroshima. At 12 she developed leukemia. Following a Japanese tradition that folding 1,000 paper cranes grants a wish, she folded cranes in the hospital before she died in 1955. Her story made the paper crane a worldwide symbol of peace."],["Rebuilding","Both cities were rebuilt and are now thriving. In Hiroshima, the ruined dome of an exhibition hall, close to where the bomb exploded, was preserved as the Atomic Bomb Dome, now a UNESCO World Heritage Site. The Peace Memorial Park and Museum attract visitors from around the world."],["The nuclear age","Within a few years, the Soviet Union, Britain, France and China had built nuclear weapons, and the far more powerful hydrogen bomb was developed. During the Cold War, the United States and the Soviet Union built tens of thousands of warheads. Treaties since the 1960s have limited testing and spread, but nine countries are believed to have nuclear weapons today."],["Peace Day","Every 6 August, Hiroshima holds a peace ceremony at 8:15 a.m., the moment the bomb exploded, and the mayor reads a peace declaration calling for the abolition of nuclear weapons. Nagasaki holds a similar ceremony on 9 August. Lanterns are floated on Hiroshima’s rivers in memory of the dead."]],
sp:["The Atomic Bomb Dome","The Hiroshima Prefectural Industrial Promotion Hall, built in 1915, stood about 160 meters from the point below the explosion. Because the blast came from almost directly above, part of its walls and the steel frame of its dome survived while everything around was flattened. Preserved as it was, the Genbaku Dome became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1996, a stark reminder of the bombing."],
g:[["Atomic bomb","A weapon whose explosive power comes from splitting atoms"],["Hibakusha","Survivors of the atomic bombings"],["Radiation sickness","Illness caused by a high dose of radiation"],["Manhattan Project","The secret wartime program that built the first atomic bombs"]],
f:["Hiroshima and Nagasaki are the only cities attacked with nuclear weapons.","Survivors of the bombings are called hibakusha.","The paper crane is a symbol of peace because of Sadako Sasaki."],
fun:"The Atomic Bomb Dome in Hiroshima survived because the explosion happened almost directly above it.",
tl:"Noong Agosto 1945, naghulog ang Estados Unidos ng bombang atomika sa Hiroshima at Nagasaki sa Hapon, na pumatay ng mahigit 100,000 katao. Ilang araw pagkatapos ay sumuko ang Hapon, at nagsimula ang panahon ng mga sandatang nukleyar."});

ADDART({id:"berlinwall",t:"The Berlin Wall",c:"History",i:"🧱",w:"Berlin Wall",r:["coldwar","germanycountry","ussrfall","eu"],
p:[["A divided city","From 1961 to 1989, the Berlin Wall divided the German city of Berlin into East and West."],["Symbol of the Cold War","The wall became the most famous symbol of the division between communist and democratic Europe."],["Its fall","On 9 November 1989, the wall was opened, and Germany was reunited less than a year later."]],
l:"The Berlin Wall was a guarded concrete barrier that divided Berlin from 1961 to 1989. Built by communist East Germany, officially the German Democratic Republic, it encircled West Berlin, cutting it off from East Berlin and the surrounding East German countryside. East Germany called it a protection against fascism, but its real purpose was to stop people fleeing to the West. At least 140 people died at the wall. Its sudden opening on 9 November 1989 became a symbol of the end of the Cold War.",
s:[["A divided Germany","After World War II, Germany and its capital, Berlin, were divided into four zones controlled by the United States, Britain, France and the Soviet Union. In 1949 the Western zones became West Germany and the Soviet zone became communist East Germany. Berlin, deep inside East Germany, was also split, with West Berlin linked to the West."],["Why it was built","In the 1950s, millions of East Germans, many of them young and skilled, fled to the West, often simply by crossing in Berlin. By 1961 about 3 million had left. To stop this, on the night of 12–13 August 1961, East German soldiers and workers sealed the border with barbed wire, soon replaced by concrete walls."],["The wall","Over time, the barrier became a system of two walls, with a death strip between them, watchtowers, floodlights, dogs, trenches and guards ordered to shoot people trying to escape. It ran about 155 kilometers around West Berlin, including about 43 kilometers through the city itself."],["Escapes","About 5,000 people escaped over, under or through the wall, using tunnels, homemade balloons, cars with hidden compartments and even a zip line. At least 140 people were killed trying to cross or in incidents at the wall. Peter Fechter, an 18-year-old shot in 1962 and left to bleed to death in view of Western onlookers, became a symbol of its brutality."]],
k:[["Built","13 August 1961"],["Opened","9 November 1989"],["Length around West Berlin","About 155 km"],["Deaths at the wall","At least 140"],["German reunification","3 October 1990"]],
d:[["1949","Germany is divided into West and East Germany"],["13 August 1961","Construction of the Berlin Wall begins"],["26 June 1963","Kennedy declares Ich bin ein Berliner in West Berlin"],["12 June 1987","Reagan urges Gorbachev to tear down this wall"],["9 November 1989","The wall is opened"],["3 October 1990","Germany is reunified"]],
s2:[["Famous speeches","In 1963 US President John F. Kennedy visited West Berlin and told a huge crowd Ich bin ein Berliner, I am a Berliner, expressing solidarity with the city. In 1987 President Ronald Reagan, speaking at the Brandenburg Gate, called on Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev: Mr. Gorbachev, tear down this wall!"],["Checkpoint Charlie","Checkpoint Charlie was the best-known crossing point between East and West Berlin, used by Allied military personnel and foreigners. In October 1961, American and Soviet tanks faced each other there for 16 hours in a tense standoff. A replica guardhouse now stands at the site, and a museum tells the story of escapes."],["Revolutions of 1989","In 1989, communist governments across Eastern Europe faced rising protests. Hungary opened its border with Austria, and thousands of East Germans fled through it. Huge peaceful demonstrations, such as those in Leipzig, demanded freedom. Soviet leader Gorbachev made clear he would not send troops to prop up East Germany’s government."],["The night the wall opened","On 9 November 1989, an East German official, Günter Schabowski, announced at a press conference that citizens could travel freely and, when asked when, said it took effect immediately. Thousands rushed to the border crossings, and overwhelmed guards opened the gates. Berliners climbed on the wall, celebrated and began chipping pieces off it."],["Reunification","After East Germany’s first free elections in March 1990, the two Germanys negotiated reunification with the four former occupying powers. On 3 October 1990, East Germany joined West Germany, forming one country with Berlin as its capital. The day is celebrated as German Unity Day."],["Remembering the wall","Today only a few stretches of the wall remain. The Berlin Wall Memorial on Bernauer Strasse preserves a section of the death strip. The East Side Gallery, a 1.3-kilometer stretch covered in murals by artists from around the world, is one of Berlin’s most visited sites. Pieces of the wall stand in cities worldwide."]],
sp:["A mistaken announcement","The opening of the Berlin Wall happened partly by accident. Günter Schabowski had been handed a note about new, looser travel rules shortly before a live press conference and had not been told they were meant to start the next day. When a journalist asked when they took effect, he looked at his papers and said, as far as I know, immediately. Within hours, crowds had forced the border open."],
g:[["Cold War","The rivalry between the United States and the Soviet Union and their allies, 1947–1991"],["Death strip","The guarded zone between the two walls"],["Checkpoint","A place where travelers are stopped and inspected"],["Reunification","Joining together again after being divided"]],
f:["The Berlin Wall stood from 1961 to 1989.","About 5,000 people escaped across it.","Germany was reunified on 3 October 1990."],
fun:"The Berlin Wall was opened partly because an official misread when new travel rules would begin.",
tl:"Ang Berlin Wall ay pader na naghati sa lungsod ng Berlin mula 1961 hanggang 1989 upang pigilan ang mga taga-Silangang Alemanya na tumakas sa Kanluran. Binuksan ito noong 9 Nobyembre 1989, at muling nagkaisa ang Alemanya noong 1990."});

ADDART({id:"cubancrisis",t:"The Cuban Missile Crisis",c:"History",i:"🚀",w:"Cuban Missile Crisis",r:["coldwar","usacountry","russiacountry","nuclear","atomicbomb"],
p:[["Thirteen days","In October 1962, the United States and the Soviet Union came closer to nuclear war than at any other time."],["Missiles in Cuba","The crisis began when the United States discovered Soviet nuclear missiles being installed in Cuba, just 145 kilometers from Florida."],["A peaceful end","After tense negotiations, the Soviet Union agreed to remove the missiles, and the United States secretly agreed to remove its missiles from Türkiye."]],
l:"The Cuban Missile Crisis was a 13-day confrontation in October 1962 between the United States and the Soviet Union over Soviet nuclear missiles placed in Cuba. President John F. Kennedy announced a naval quarantine of the island, and for days the world feared a nuclear war. The crisis ended when Soviet leader Nikita Khrushchev agreed to withdraw the missiles in exchange for a US pledge not to invade Cuba and a secret agreement to remove US missiles from Türkiye. It led both sides to take steps to reduce the risk of nuclear war.",
s:[["Background","In 1959 Fidel Castro’s revolution took power in Cuba and soon allied with the Soviet Union. In April 1961, a US-backed invasion by Cuban exiles at the Bay of Pigs failed. Meanwhile, the United States had placed nuclear missiles in Italy and Türkiye, within range of the Soviet Union. Khrushchev decided to place Soviet missiles secretly in Cuba."],["Discovery","On 14 October 1962, a US U-2 spy plane photographed launch sites for Soviet medium-range missiles under construction in Cuba. On 16 October, President Kennedy was shown the photographs. He gathered his closest advisers, a group called ExComm, to decide how to respond."],["The quarantine","Some advisers urged air strikes or an invasion. Kennedy chose a naval quarantine, a blockade to stop more weapons reaching Cuba, while demanding the removal of the missiles. On 22 October, he announced the discovery and the quarantine in a televised address. US forces were put on high alert."],["The most dangerous days","On 24 October, Soviet ships approaching the quarantine line stopped or turned back. On 27 October, known as Black Saturday, a US U-2 was shot down over Cuba, killing its pilot, and US warships dropped practice depth charges to force a Soviet submarine to surface, unaware that it carried a nuclear torpedo. Both sides came very close to war."]],
k:[["Dates","16–28 October 1962"],["Leaders","John F. Kennedy (USA), Nikita Khrushchev (USSR), Fidel Castro (Cuba)"],["Distance from Cuba to Florida","About 145 km"],["Outcome","Soviet missiles removed; US pledged not to invade Cuba"],["Secret deal","US missiles removed from Türkiye"]],
d:[["April 1961","Bay of Pigs invasion fails"],["14 October 1962","U-2 photographs missile sites in Cuba"],["22 October 1962","Kennedy announces the quarantine"],["27 October 1962","Black Saturday: a U-2 is shot down"],["28 October 1962","Khrushchev agrees to remove the missiles"],["1963","Moscow–Washington hotline is set up; Limited Test Ban Treaty signed"]],
s2:[["The deal","On 26 and 27 October, Khrushchev sent letters offering to remove the missiles if the United States promised not to invade Cuba, and later also asked for the removal of US missiles from Türkiye. Kennedy publicly accepted the first offer, while his brother Robert Kennedy privately assured the Soviet ambassador that the missiles in Türkiye would be removed later. On 28 October, Khrushchev announced the withdrawal."],["Vasily Arkhipov","On 27 October, the Soviet submarine B-59, harassed by US ships and out of contact with Moscow, had a nuclear torpedo on board. Its captain reportedly wanted to launch it, but the decision needed the agreement of three officers. One of them, Vasily Arkhipov, refused. Many historians believe his decision may have prevented a nuclear war."],["Cuba’s view","Cuba’s leaders, who had been preparing for a US invasion, were angry that the Soviet Union negotiated the withdrawal without consulting them. Fidel Castro had even urged Khrushchev to use nuclear weapons if the United States invaded. The crisis left Cuba under a long US trade embargo that continues in part today."],["Lessons learned","Shaken by how close they had come, the two superpowers set up a direct communication link, the Moscow–Washington hotline, in 1963, so that leaders could talk quickly in a crisis. That year they also signed the Limited Test Ban Treaty, banning nuclear tests in the atmosphere, in space and underwater."],["Close calls","The crisis showed how mistakes and misunderstandings could lead to disaster. Historians have since learned of several close calls during the crisis, including a U-2 that strayed into Soviet airspace and the submarine incident. These lessons have shaped later efforts to manage nuclear risks."],["Thirteen Days","The crisis has been the subject of many books and films. Robert Kennedy’s memoir Thirteen Days, published in 1969, described the discussions inside the White House. Recordings of ExComm meetings, released decades later, have given historians a detailed view of how decisions were made."]],
sp:["A hotline for peace","During the crisis, messages between Kennedy and Khrushchev took hours to deliver, sometimes passing through embassies and telegraph offices. To prevent such delays, the two countries set up a direct link in 1963. Contrary to popular belief, it was not a red telephone at first but a teletype machine sending written messages, which were less likely to be misunderstood."],
g:[["Quarantine","Here, a naval blockade to stop weapons reaching Cuba"],["Superpower","An extremely powerful country, such as the United States or Soviet Union in the Cold War"],["Embargo","An official ban on trade with a country"],["Hotline","A direct communication link for emergencies"]],
f:["The Cuban Missile Crisis lasted 13 days in October 1962.","A Soviet officer, Vasily Arkhipov, refused to approve launching a nuclear torpedo.","The Moscow–Washington hotline was set up in 1963."],
fun:"The famous Moscow–Washington hotline was first a teletype machine, not a red telephone.",
tl:"Ang Cuban Missile Crisis noong Oktubre 1962 ang pinakamalapit na pagkakataon ng digmaang nukleyar sa pagitan ng Estados Unidos at Unyong Sobyet. Natapos ito nang alisin ng Sobyet ang mga misil sa Cuba."});

;
/* data/body2.js */
// The Human Body articles, part 2: cancer, diabetes, the microbiome and ageing.
ADDART({id:"cancer",t:"Cancer",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🎗️",w:"Cancer",r:["cell","dna","immune","radioact"],
p:[["Cells out of control","Cancer is a group of diseases in which body cells grow and divide uncontrollably and can spread to other parts of the body."],["A leading cause of death","Cancer is one of the leading causes of death worldwide, but many cancers can be prevented or treated."],["Better treatments","Surgery, radiotherapy, chemotherapy, immunotherapy and targeted drugs help millions of people survive cancer."]],
l:"Cancer is the name for more than 100 diseases in which abnormal cells divide without control and can invade nearby tissues and spread to other parts of the body. It begins when changes, called mutations, build up in a cell’s DNA. Cancer can affect almost any part of the body; common types include lung, breast, bowel, prostate and skin cancer. Around 20 million new cases are diagnosed each year worldwide. Avoiding tobacco, staying active, vaccination and screening can prevent many cancers, and treatments have greatly improved survival.",
s:[["How cancer starts","Normally, cells grow, divide and die in an orderly way, controlled by genes. Mutations in these genes, caused by aging, inherited faults or damage from things such as tobacco smoke, ultraviolet light or some viruses, can let a cell divide uncontrollably. Over time, it may form a lump called a tumor."],["Benign and malignant","Not all tumors are cancer. Benign tumors grow slowly and do not spread. Malignant tumors are cancerous: they can invade nearby tissue and break away, traveling through the blood or lymph system to form new tumors elsewhere, a process called metastasis."],["Risk factors","Tobacco is the single largest preventable cause of cancer. Other risks include alcohol, obesity, lack of exercise, too much sun and certain infections, such as human papillomavirus, HPV, and hepatitis B. The risk of most cancers rises with age. Some people inherit genes, such as BRCA1 and BRCA2, that raise their risk."],["Detection","Screening tests can find some cancers early, when they are easier to treat, such as mammograms for breast cancer, Pap or HPV tests for cervical cancer and tests for bowel cancer. Warning signs to check with a doctor include a new lump, unexplained weight loss, a changing mole or a persistent cough."]],
k:[["Types","More than 100"],["New cases worldwide","About 20 million a year (2022)"],["Largest preventable cause","Tobacco"],["Main treatments","Surgery, radiotherapy, chemotherapy, immunotherapy, targeted therapy"],["Preventable by vaccine","Cervical cancer (HPV) and some liver cancers (hepatitis B)"]],
d:[["c. 1600 BCE","An Egyptian papyrus describes breast tumors"],["c. 400 BCE","Hippocrates uses the word karkinos, crab"],["1896","X-rays are first used to treat cancer"],["1940s","The first chemotherapy drugs are developed"],["2006","First HPV vaccine is approved"],["2018","Nobel Prize for cancer immunotherapy"]],
s2:[["Treatments","Surgery removes tumors. Radiotherapy uses high-energy rays to kill cancer cells. Chemotherapy uses drugs that attack fast-dividing cells. Targeted therapies block specific molecules that cancer cells need to grow, and hormone therapies treat cancers such as some breast and prostate cancers. Patients often receive a combination."],["Immunotherapy","Immunotherapy helps the body’s own immune system fight cancer. Checkpoint inhibitor drugs release brakes that cancer uses to hide from immune cells; James Allison and Tasuku Honjo won the 2018 Nobel Prize for this work. CAR-T therapy reprograms a patient’s immune cells to attack certain blood cancers."],["Preventing cancer","Experts estimate that 30 to 50 percent of cancers could be prevented by avoiding tobacco, limiting alcohol, keeping a healthy weight, eating plenty of fruit and vegetables, being active, protecting skin from the sun and getting vaccinated against HPV and hepatitis B."],["Childhood cancer","Cancer in children is rare, and the most common types, such as leukemia and brain tumors, differ from adult cancers. In wealthy countries more than 80 percent of children with cancer survive, but in many low-income countries survival is much lower. The World Health Organization aims to raise global survival to at least 60 percent by 2030."],["Living with cancer","Thanks to better treatments and early detection, many more people survive cancer than in the past, and some cancers are now managed as long-term conditions. Support from family, friends, doctors, nurses and support groups helps patients and survivors cope with the physical and emotional effects."],["Why the name cancer?","The ancient Greek physician Hippocrates used the words karkinos and karkinoma, meaning crab, to describe tumors, perhaps because their swollen veins reminded him of a crab’s legs. The Roman doctor Celsus translated it into Latin as cancer. The study of cancer, oncology, comes from the Greek onkos, meaning mass or swelling."]],
sp:["Henrietta Lacks","In 1951, cells were taken from a tumor of Henrietta Lacks, a young African American woman with cervical cancer, without her knowledge. Her cells, called HeLa cells, were the first human cells to keep growing in a laboratory and have been used in research on polio vaccines, cancer, HIV and more. Her story has raised important questions about consent and fairness in medical research."],
g:[["Tumor","An abnormal lump of cells"],["Malignant","Cancerous; able to invade and spread"],["Metastasis","The spread of cancer to other parts of the body"],["Chemotherapy","Treatment with drugs that kill cancer cells"]],
f:["Tobacco is the largest preventable cause of cancer.","Vaccines can prevent cervical cancer.","Cancer immunotherapy won the 2018 Nobel Prize."],
fun:"The word cancer comes from the Greek and Latin words for crab.",
tl:"Ang kanser ay grupo ng mga sakit kung saan lumalaki at dumarami nang walang kontrol ang mga selula ng katawan. Maiiwasan ang marami nito sa pag-iwas sa sigarilyo, pag-eehersisyo at pagbabakuna, at may mas mahuhusay na lunas ngayon."});

ADDART({id:"diabetes",t:"Diabetes",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🩸",w:"Diabetes",r:["hormone","nutri","exercise","blood"],
p:[["Too much sugar in the blood","Diabetes is a long-term condition in which the body cannot control the level of sugar, or glucose, in the blood."],["Insulin","It happens when the body does not make enough insulin, a hormone, or cannot use it properly."],["Very common","About 590 million adults worldwide live with diabetes, according to the International Diabetes Federation."]],
l:"Diabetes is a chronic disease in which blood sugar levels are too high because the body does not produce enough insulin or does not respond to it properly. Insulin, a hormone made by the pancreas, lets cells take in glucose from the blood for energy. There are two main types: type 1, in which the immune system destroys the cells that make insulin, and type 2, much more common, linked to genetics, excess weight and lack of exercise. Without good control, diabetes can damage the heart, kidneys, eyes and nerves.",
s:[["How blood sugar works","When we eat, carbohydrates are broken down into glucose, which enters the blood. The pancreas releases insulin, which acts like a key, letting glucose into cells for energy and storing extra in the liver and muscles. Between meals, another hormone, glucagon, releases stored glucose so blood sugar stays steady."],["Type 1 diabetes","In type 1 diabetes, the immune system mistakenly destroys the insulin-making beta cells in the pancreas. It often starts in childhood or young adulthood. People with type 1 need insulin every day, by injection or pump, and must monitor their blood sugar carefully. Its exact cause is not known, and it cannot currently be prevented."],["Type 2 diabetes","In type 2 diabetes, the body’s cells become resistant to insulin, and over time the pancreas cannot keep up. It is linked to genes, age, excess body fat, especially around the waist, and lack of physical activity. It is becoming more common worldwide, including among younger people, and can often be prevented or delayed by healthy living."],["Symptoms","Common signs include being very thirsty, urinating often, feeling tired, losing weight without trying, blurred vision and slow-healing cuts. Type 2 may develop slowly with few symptoms, so many people do not know they have it. A simple blood test can diagnose diabetes."]],
k:[["Adults with diabetes","About 590 million worldwide (IDF, 2024)"],["Main types","Type 1 and type 2"],["Key hormone","Insulin, made by the pancreas"],["Insulin discovered","1921, in Toronto"],["World Diabetes Day","14 November"]],
d:[["c. 1550 BCE","An Egyptian papyrus describes frequent urination"],["1889","Scientists show the pancreas is linked to diabetes"],["1921","Banting and Best discover insulin"],["1922","First patient is treated with insulin"],["1982","First human insulin made by genetic engineering"],["2006","UN recognizes World Diabetes Day"]],
s2:[["The discovery of insulin","In 1921, at the University of Toronto, Frederick Banting and Charles Best, working in John Macleod’s laboratory, extracted insulin from the pancreas of dogs. With chemist James Collip’s help in purifying it, in January 1922 they treated 14-year-old Leonard Thompson, who was dying of diabetes, and he recovered. Banting and Macleod won the 1923 Nobel Prize."],["Managing diabetes","People with diabetes manage it through healthy eating, physical activity, medicines and regular checks. Many with type 2 take tablets such as metformin, and some need insulin. Glucose meters and continuous glucose monitors, sensors worn on the skin, help people track their levels. Insulin pumps can deliver insulin automatically."],["Complications","Over many years, high blood sugar can damage blood vessels and nerves, increasing the risk of heart attacks, strokes, kidney failure, blindness and foot problems that may lead to amputation. Good control of blood sugar, blood pressure and cholesterol greatly lowers these risks."],["A global challenge","The number of adults with diabetes has risen sharply since 1990, especially in low- and middle-income countries in Asia, the Middle East, Africa and the Pacific. Many people cannot afford insulin or medicines. The World Health Organization has made access to insulin and diabetes care a priority."],["Preventing type 2","Studies have shown that people at high risk can greatly reduce their chances of developing type 2 diabetes by losing a modest amount of weight, eating more vegetables, whole grains and fiber, cutting down on sugary drinks and being active for at least 150 minutes a week."],["Hypoglycemia","If blood sugar falls too low, called hypoglycemia, a person may feel shaky, sweaty, confused or dizzy. It can happen to people taking insulin or some medicines. It is treated quickly with sugar, such as fruit juice or glucose tablets. Friends and teachers of people with diabetes can learn the signs."]],
sp:["The miracle of insulin","Before 1922, a diagnosis of type 1 diabetes was almost always a death sentence, especially for children, who were kept on starvation diets that only delayed death. When insulin became available, children who had been near death recovered within days. Banting and his colleagues sold the patent for insulin to the University of Toronto for just one dollar each, so that it could be widely available."],
g:[["Glucose","A sugar that is the body’s main source of energy"],["Insulin","A hormone that lets cells take in glucose"],["Pancreas","An organ that makes insulin and digestive juices"],["Chronic","Lasting a long time"]],
f:["Insulin was discovered in Toronto in 1921.","Type 2 diabetes is the most common type.","World Diabetes Day is 14 November, Frederick Banting’s birthday."],
fun:"The discoverers of insulin sold its patent for one dollar each.",
tl:"Ang diabetes ay pangmatagalang sakit kung saan masyadong mataas ang asukal sa dugo dahil kulang o hindi gumagana nang maayos ang insulin. May dalawang pangunahing uri nito, at maiiwasan o maaantala ang type 2 sa malusog na pamumuhay."});

ADDART({id:"microbiome",t:"The Microbiome",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🦠",w:"Human microbiome",r:["microbe","digest","immune","antibiotics"],
p:[["Trillions of tiny partners","The human microbiome is the community of trillions of bacteria, fungi and viruses that live on and inside our bodies."],["Mostly in the gut","Most live in the large intestine, where they help digest food and make vitamins."],["Important for health","Scientists are discovering that the microbiome affects digestion, immunity and possibly even mood."]],
l:"The human microbiome is the vast community of microorganisms, mostly bacteria but also fungi, viruses and other microbes, that live on the skin, in the mouth, nose and gut, and elsewhere in the body. The number of bacterial cells is about the same as the number of human cells. Far from being harmful, most of these microbes are helpful: they digest fiber, produce vitamins, train the immune system and protect against disease-causing germs. Research on the microbiome has grown rapidly since the 2000s.",
s:[["How many microbes?","An adult body hosts roughly 38 trillion bacterial cells, similar to its roughly 30 trillion human cells. Together the microbes weigh only about 200 grams, because bacterial cells are tiny. They carry far more genes than our own cells, adding many abilities our bodies lack."],["The gut microbiome","Most microbes live in the large intestine, where hundreds of species break down fiber we cannot digest, producing short-chain fatty acids that nourish the gut lining. They also make vitamins such as vitamin K and some B vitamins. Each person’s mix of gut microbes is unique, like a fingerprint."],["Where we get them","Babies get their first microbes from their mothers during birth and breastfeeding and from their surroundings. The microbiome changes quickly in early childhood and becomes more stable by about age three. Diet, medicines, pets, siblings and where we live all shape it."],["Protecting us","Helpful microbes take up space and nutrients, making it harder for harmful germs to settle. They help train the immune system to tell friend from foe. Skin microbes help protect against infections, and microbes in the mouth and vagina help keep harmful organisms in check."]],
k:[["Bacterial cells in the body","About 38 trillion"],["Human cells","About 30 trillion"],["Total weight of microbes","About 200 grams"],["Main location","The large intestine"],["Big research project","Human Microbiome Project (2007–2016)"]],
d:[["1683","Antonie van Leeuwenhoek sees bacteria from his own mouth"],["1880s","Scientists begin identifying gut bacteria"],["2007","US launches the Human Microbiome Project"],["2012","First map of the healthy human microbiome is published"],["2013","Fecal transplants are shown to cure severe gut infections"],["2016","New estimate shows bacteria and human cells are about equal in number"]],
s2:[["Fiber feeds microbes","Gut microbes thrive on fiber from vegetables, fruits, beans and whole grains. Diets rich in a variety of plant foods tend to support a more diverse microbiome, which is linked with better health. Fermented foods, such as yogurt, kimchi and kefir, contain live microbes."],["Antibiotics and the microbiome","Antibiotics kill harmful bacteria but can also wipe out helpful ones, upsetting the balance of the microbiome. This can cause diarrhea and sometimes allow dangerous bacteria, such as Clostridioides difficile, to take over. The microbiome usually recovers, but not always completely."],["Fecal transplants","For people with severe, repeated C. difficile infections, doctors can transplant stool from a healthy donor, restoring a healthy microbiome. In trials since 2013, this has cured most patients. Scientists are developing capsules of selected bacteria as cleaner alternatives."],["Microbes and the brain","Researchers have found that gut microbes communicate with the brain through nerves, hormones and the immune system, sometimes called the gut-brain axis. Studies in mice suggest microbes can affect behavior and stress. Whether and how this matters for people’s mood and mental health is an active area of research."],["Changing microbiomes","People in industrialized countries tend to have less diverse gut microbiomes than people living traditional lifestyles, such as the Hadza hunter-gatherers of Tanzania. Scientists think processed diets, antibiotics and very clean environments may contribute, and that this may be linked to the rise of allergies and some autoimmune diseases."],["Probiotics","Probiotics are live microbes sold in foods and supplements that are meant to benefit health. Some probiotics help with specific problems, such as certain kinds of diarrhea, but for many other claims the evidence is weak. Scientists are still learning which microbes help which people."]],
sp:["Leeuwenhoek’s animalcules","In 1683 the Dutch cloth merchant and lens maker Antonie van Leeuwenhoek scraped plaque from his own teeth, mixed it with water and looked at it through one of his homemade microscopes. He saw tiny moving creatures he called animalcules: bacteria. He was the first person to describe the microbes that live in the human body."],
g:[["Microbiome","The community of microbes living in a particular place, such as the body"],["Probiotic","Live microbes taken to benefit health"],["Fiber","Plant material that humans cannot digest but gut microbes can"],["Gut-brain axis","The links between the digestive system and the brain"]],
f:["The body contains roughly as many bacterial cells as human cells.","Most of the body’s microbes live in the large intestine.","Each person’s gut microbiome is unique."],
fun:"Your body carries about 200 grams of microbes, roughly the weight of a mango.",
tl:"Ang microbiome ay ang trilyon-trilyong bakterya at iba pang mikrobyo na nakatira sa ating katawan, karamihan sa bituka. Tumutulong sila sa pagtunaw ng pagkain, paggawa ng bitamina at pagpapalakas ng immune system."});

ADDART({id:"aging",t:"Ageing and Lifespan",c:"Health and the Body",i:"⏳",w:"Ageing",r:["cell","dna","exercise","brain"],
p:[["Growing older","Ageing is the gradual change in the body and mind that happens over a lifetime."],["Living longer","Average life expectancy worldwide has more than doubled since 1900, to about 73 years."],["Healthy ageing","Staying active, eating well, staying social and avoiding tobacco help people stay healthy as they age."]],
l:"Ageing is the process of growing older, in which the body’s cells, tissues and organs gradually change and become less able to repair themselves. It affects everyone differently, depending on genes, lifestyle and environment. Thanks to better nutrition, clean water, vaccines and medicine, people today live far longer than in the past: global life expectancy has risen from about 32 years in 1900 to around 73 today. Scientists study ageing to help people live not only longer but healthier lives.",
s:[["What happens with age","With age, skin loses elasticity and wrinkles, hair turns gray, muscles and bones tend to weaken, and eyesight and hearing may decline. The heart, lungs and kidneys work less efficiently. These changes happen gradually and at different rates in different people."],["Why do we age?","Scientists think ageing results from the slow build-up of damage to cells and molecules over time. DNA collects mutations, the protective ends of chromosomes, called telomeres, shorten with each cell division, worn-out cells stop dividing, and cell repair systems become less effective."],["Life expectancy","Life expectancy is the average number of years a newborn can expect to live. It was low in the past mainly because many children died young. Today it is highest in places such as Japan, Hong Kong, Switzerland and South Korea, over 83 years, and lowest in some countries affected by poverty and conflict."],["The brain and ageing","Some memory and thinking skills, such as recalling names, may slow with age, while knowledge and vocabulary often keep growing. Diseases such as Alzheimer’s are not a normal part of ageing. Staying mentally, physically and socially active is linked to better brain health."]],
k:[["Global life expectancy","About 73 years (2023)"],["Life expectancy in 1900","About 32 years"],["Longest verified life","Jeanne Calment, 122 years (1875–1997)"],["Highest life expectancy","Over 83 years in Japan, Hong Kong, Switzerland and others"],["Key cell feature","Shortening telomeres"]],
d:[["1900","Global life expectancy is about 32 years"],["1961","Leonard Hayflick shows that human cells divide only a limited number of times"],["1997","Jeanne Calment dies at 122"],["2009","Nobel Prize for discoveries about telomeres"],["2021–2030","UN Decade of Healthy Ageing"]],
s2:[["Blue zones","Researchers have studied places where many people live to 100 or more, sometimes called blue zones, such as Okinawa in Japan, Sardinia in Italy and the Nicoya Peninsula in Costa Rica. Common features include plant-rich diets, daily physical activity, strong family and community ties and a sense of purpose, though the idea and some data have been debated."],["The Hayflick limit","In 1961 Leonard Hayflick discovered that normal human cells grown in a lab divide only about 40 to 60 times before stopping. This limit is linked to telomeres, which shorten each time a cell divides. Elizabeth Blackburn, Carol Greider and Jack Szostak won the 2009 Nobel Prize for discovering how telomeres and the enzyme telomerase protect chromosomes."],["The oldest people","The longest confirmed human lifespan belongs to Jeanne Calment of France, who lived to 122 years and 164 days, dying in 1997. People who live to 110 or more are called supercentenarians. The number of people over 100 is growing fast worldwide."],["An ageing world","Because people live longer and have fewer children, the world’s population is getting older. By 2030 one in six people will be 60 or over. Countries such as Japan, Italy and South Korea already have large older populations, which brings challenges for health care and pensions, and opportunities as older people continue to work and volunteer."],["Healthy habits","Regular exercise, a balanced diet, enough sleep, not smoking, limiting alcohol, keeping a healthy weight, staying connected with others and continuing to learn are all linked to healthier ageing. It is never too late to benefit: people who start exercising in later life still gain strength and balance."],["Animals and ageing","Lifespans vary enormously in nature. Some mayflies live as adults for only a day, while Greenland sharks may live for over 250 years, and a tiny jellyfish, Turritopsis dohrnii, can revert to an earlier life stage, making it potentially immortal. Scientists study long-lived animals to learn about ageing."]],
sp:["Jeanne Calment","Jeanne Calment was born in Arles, France, in 1875, before the Eiffel Tower was built, and said she had met the painter Vincent van Gogh as a girl. She took up fencing at 85, rode a bicycle until 100 and lived to 122 years and 164 days, dying in 1997. Her age was carefully verified with records, making her the oldest person ever confirmed."],
g:[["Life expectancy","The average number of years a person can expect to live"],["Telomere","A protective cap at the end of a chromosome"],["Supercentenarian","A person aged 110 or older"],["Alzheimer’s disease","A brain disease that causes memory loss and confusion"]],
f:["Global life expectancy has more than doubled since 1900.","Jeanne Calment lived to 122, the oldest person ever confirmed.","Greenland sharks may live for over 250 years."],
fun:"Jeanne Calment, who lived to 122, took up fencing at age 85.",
tl:"Ang pagtanda ay unti-unting pagbabago ng katawan at isip sa paglipas ng panahon. Mas mahaba ngayon ang buhay ng tao kaysa noon, at nakatutulong ang ehersisyo, wastong pagkain at pakikisalamuha upang manatiling malusog sa pagtanda."});

;
/* data/arts2.js */
// Arts and Culture articles, part 2: cave art, architecture, photography, and comics and manga.
ADDART({id:"caveart",t:"Cave Art and Prehistoric Art",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🦬",w:"Cave painting",r:["davinci","indonesiacountry","francecountry","caves"],
p:[["The oldest art","Prehistoric people painted and carved images of animals, people and signs on cave walls tens of thousands of years ago."],["Around the world","Cave and rock art has been found on every inhabited continent, from Indonesia and Australia to Europe and Africa."],["Mysterious meanings","No one knows for sure why it was made, but it may have been linked to hunting, beliefs or storytelling."]],
l:"Prehistoric art includes paintings, engravings and carvings made by people before the invention of writing. The most famous are cave paintings, such as those at Lascaux and Chauvet in France and Altamira in Spain, which show bison, horses, deer and other animals in vivid detail. Even older art has been found in Indonesia, where a painted scene on Sulawesi is at least 51,200 years old. Prehistoric people also carved figurines, decorated tools and made jewelry from shells and beads, showing that art is one of the oldest human activities.",
s:[["How it was made","Painters used natural pigments: red and yellow ocher from iron-rich earth, black from charcoal or manganese, and white from chalk or clay. They applied them with fingers, brushes made of fur or plant fibers, or by blowing paint through hollow bones to spray outlines of their hands. They worked by the light of fat-burning lamps and torches."],["Hand stencils","Some of the oldest and most widespread images are hand stencils, made by placing a hand on the rock and blowing paint around it. They appear in caves in Indonesia, Argentina’s Cueva de las Manos, Spain, France and Australia. Studies of their size suggest that many were made by women and children as well as men."],["Animals in motion","European cave painters often showed large animals, such as bison, horses, mammoths, aurochs, lions and rhinos, sometimes using the bumps of the cave wall to give them shape and drawing extra legs to suggest movement. People are shown less often and usually more simply."],["Carvings and figurines","Prehistoric people also carved small figures from ivory, bone and stone. The Lion Man of Hohlenstein-Stadel in Germany, carved from mammoth ivory about 40,000 years ago, has a human body and a lion’s head. Small female figures, such as the Venus of Willendorf, are found across Europe."]],
k:[["Oldest known painting","Sulawesi, Indonesia, at least 51,200 years old"],["Famous caves","Lascaux, Chauvet (France); Altamira (Spain)"],["Main pigments","Ocher, charcoal, manganese"],["Oldest figurative carving","Lion Man, about 40,000 years old"],["Found on","Every inhabited continent"]],
d:[["c. 51,200 years ago","Painted scene with a pig and human figures in Sulawesi"],["c. 40,000 years ago","Lion Man figurine is carved"],["c. 36,000 years ago","Chauvet Cave paintings"],["c. 17,000 years ago","Lascaux Cave paintings"],["1879","Altamira paintings are discovered in Spain"],["1940","Teenagers discover Lascaux"]],
s2:[["Lascaux","In September 1940, four teenagers in southwestern France followed their dog, according to one account, into a hole and discovered Lascaux Cave, covered with about 600 painted animals and many engravings, made about 17,000 years ago. Visitors’ breath and lights damaged the paintings, so the cave was closed in 1963. Visitors now see detailed replicas."],["Chauvet","Chauvet Cave, discovered in 1994, contains some of the oldest and finest paintings in Europe, about 36,000 years old, including lions, mammoths, rhinos and bears. Because the entrance collapsed long ago, the cave was sealed and its art preserved almost untouched. It is a UNESCO World Heritage Site."],["Altamira","The paintings at Altamira in northern Spain were discovered in 1879 by Maria, the young daughter of an amateur archaeologist, Marcelino Sanz de Sautuola, who looked up and saw bulls on the ceiling. Experts at first refused to believe prehistoric people could paint so well, and accused Sautuola of forgery. They later admitted they were wrong."],["Indonesia’s ancient art","In the caves of Sulawesi and Borneo, scientists have dated paintings using mineral crusts that formed on top of them. In 2024 researchers reported that a scene on Sulawesi showing human-like figures and a wild pig is at least 51,200 years old, the oldest known figurative art and the oldest known painted story."],["Rock art of Africa and Australia","Africa has hundreds of thousands of rock art sites, such as the paintings of the San people in southern Africa and the engravings of the Sahara, some showing a time when the desert was green with giraffes and cattle. Aboriginal Australians have one of the world’s longest continuous traditions of rock art, still connected to living cultures."],["Why did they make it?","Many ideas have been suggested: hunting magic to bring success, religious or spiritual rituals, records of important events, teaching, storytelling or simply the human love of creating images. Since caves were often dark and hard to reach, many researchers think the art had a special meaning, but the full answer may never be known."]],
sp:["The oldest story","The painting on Sulawesi dated in 2024 shows three human-like figures interacting with a wild pig. Scientists think it may be the oldest known depiction of a story, more than 51,000 years old. Some of the figures appear to have animal features, suggesting that even at this early date, people imagined beings that were part human and part animal."],
g:[["Prehistoric","From the time before written records"],["Ocher","A natural earth pigment, red or yellow"],["Stencil","An image made by spraying paint around an object"],["Figurative art","Art that represents real things, such as animals or people"]],
f:["The oldest known painting is at least 51,200 years old, in Indonesia.","Lascaux Cave was closed to visitors in 1963 to protect its paintings.","Altamira was discovered by a young girl in 1879."],
fun:"The Altamira cave paintings were discovered by a young girl who looked up at the ceiling.",
tl:"Ang sining sa kuweba ay mga larawang ipininta o inukit ng mga sinaunang tao sa dingding ng kuweba libu-libong taon na ang nakalipas. Ang pinakamatandang kilalang larawan ay nasa Sulawesi, Indonesia, mahigit 51,000 taon na ang edad."});

ADDART({id:"architecture",t:"Architecture",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🏛️",w:"Architecture",r:["gothic","pyramids","tajmahal","bridges","urban"],
p:[["The art of building","Architecture is the art and science of designing buildings and other structures."],["Styles through time","Each era and culture has created its own styles, from ancient temples to modern skyscrapers."],["Useful and beautiful","Good architecture combines strength, usefulness and beauty."]],
l:"Architecture is the art and science of designing and building structures, especially buildings that people live, work and worship in. Architects consider how a building will be used, how it will stand up, what materials to use and how it will look and feel. Architecture reflects the climate, technology, beliefs and values of each society, from the pyramids of Egypt, Greek temples and Chinese palaces to Gothic cathedrals, Islamic mosques and the glass skyscrapers of modern cities.",
s:[["Strength, usefulness, beauty","The Roman architect Vitruvius, writing in the 1st century BCE, said that good buildings need firmitas, utilitas and venustas: strength, usefulness and beauty. These three ideas still guide architects today. A building must stand safely, serve its purpose well and please the people who use and see it."],["Ancient architecture","Ancient Egyptians built pyramids and temples of stone. The Greeks developed the column orders, Doric, Ionic and Corinthian, seen in temples such as the Parthenon. The Romans used arches, vaults, domes and concrete to build aqueducts, amphitheaters and the Pantheon, whose dome remains the largest unreinforced concrete dome in the world."],["Architecture around the world","Chinese builders created wooden palaces and temples with curved tile roofs, such as Beijing’s Forbidden City. Islamic architecture is known for domes, arches, minarets and geometric decoration, as in the Alhambra in Spain. Hindu and Buddhist temples, such as Angkor Wat and Borobudur, represent sacred mountains."],["Medieval to modern","Gothic cathedrals rose with pointed arches and stained glass. The Renaissance revived classical forms. In the 1800s, iron and glass made new buildings possible, such as London’s Crystal Palace. In the 1900s, steel frames, reinforced concrete and elevators led to skyscrapers and modernist styles that rejected decoration."]],
k:[["Classical principles","Strength, usefulness, beauty (Vitruvius)"],["Greek column orders","Doric, Ionic, Corinthian"],["Tallest building","Burj Khalifa, Dubai, 828 m"],["Top award","Pritzker Architecture Prize, since 1979"],["Key modern materials","Steel, reinforced concrete, glass"]],
d:[["c. 2560 BCE","Great Pyramid of Giza is completed"],["447–432 BCE","The Parthenon is built in Athens"],["c. 125 CE","The Pantheon in Rome is rebuilt with its great dome"],["1420s–1436","Brunelleschi builds the dome of Florence Cathedral"],["1885","First skyscraper, the Home Insurance Building, is completed in Chicago"],["2010","Burj Khalifa opens in Dubai"]],
s2:[["Domes","Domes have long been used to create vast, impressive spaces. Rome’s Pantheon, Istanbul’s Hagia Sophia, built in the 530s, and Florence Cathedral, whose dome was engineered by Filippo Brunelleschi without a full wooden support frame, were wonders of their time. The Taj Mahal and St. Peter’s Basilica are other famous examples."],["Skyscrapers","The first skyscrapers were built in Chicago and New York in the late 1800s, made possible by steel frames and safe elevators. Since 2010 the tallest building has been the Burj Khalifa in Dubai, 828 meters tall. Many of the world’s tallest buildings are now in Asia and the Middle East."],["Modern architecture","In the early 1900s, architects such as Le Corbusier, Walter Gropius of the Bauhaus school and Ludwig Mies van der Rohe championed simple forms, open plans and new materials, summed up in the phrase form follows function, coined by Louis Sullivan. Frank Lloyd Wright designed buildings in harmony with nature, such as Fallingwater."],["Architecture and climate","Traditional buildings are often well suited to their climates: thick mud walls keep homes cool in deserts, steep roofs shed snow, and houses on stilts with open walls, such as the bahay kubo of the Philippines, stay cool and dry in tropical rain. Today architects design green buildings that use less energy and water."],["Famous architects","Notable architects include Sinan, who designed great mosques for the Ottoman Empire; Antoni Gaudí, whose Sagrada Família in Barcelona has been under construction since 1882; Zaha Hadid, the first woman to win the Pritzker Prize in 2004; Tadao Ando of Japan; and Balkrishna Doshi of India."],["Building for people","Architecture shapes how people live and feel. Good design can make homes healthier, schools better for learning and cities more pleasant to walk in. Architects also design for disasters, such as earthquake-resistant buildings in Japan and typhoon-resistant shelters in the Philippines and Bangladesh."]],
sp:["Form follows function","The American architect Louis Sullivan, who designed early skyscrapers in Chicago, wrote in 1896 that form ever follows function, meaning that a building’s shape should grow from its purpose. The phrase became a motto of modern architecture. Critics later argued that buildings also need beauty, character and a sense of place, not just efficiency."],
g:[["Architect","A person who designs buildings"],["Column","An upright pillar that supports a structure"],["Dome","A rounded roof shaped like half a sphere"],["Modernism","A style that favors simple forms and new materials"]],
f:["The Pantheon’s dome is still the largest unreinforced concrete dome in the world.","The Burj Khalifa is 828 meters tall.","Zaha Hadid was the first woman to win the Pritzker Prize."],
fun:"Gaudí’s Sagrada Família in Barcelona has been under construction since 1882.",
tl:"Ang arkitektura ay sining at agham ng pagdidisenyo ng mga gusali. Iba-iba ang istilo nito sa bawat panahon at kultura, mula sa mga piramide at templo hanggang sa mga modernong skyscraper."});

ADDART({id:"photography",t:"Photography",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"📷",w:"Photography",r:["camera","light","cinema","smartphone"],
p:[["Writing with light","Photography means writing with light: making images by capturing light on a sensitive surface."],["Invented in the 1820s and 1830s","The first lasting photographs were made in France, and photography spread around the world within years."],["Everyone a photographer","Today, with smartphones, people take well over a trillion photos every year."]],
l:"Photography is the art and science of creating images by recording light, either chemically on film or electronically with a digital sensor. Since the first permanent photograph was made in the 1820s, photography has transformed how people record history, share news, make art, do science and remember their lives. It moved from slow, expensive processes to snapshots with portable cameras and, in the 21st century, to digital cameras built into almost every phone.",
s:[["The first photographs","Around 1826, the French inventor Nicéphore Niépce made the oldest surviving photograph from nature, a view from his window, which needed an exposure of hours or even days. In 1839 Louis Daguerre announced the daguerreotype, which produced sharp images on silver-coated copper plates. That same year, William Henry Fox Talbot in Britain revealed a process using paper negatives, from which many prints could be made."],["Photography spreads","Photography quickly became popular for portraits, replacing painted miniatures. Exposure times shortened from minutes to seconds. In 1888 George Eastman’s Kodak camera, sold with the slogan You press the button, we do the rest, made photography easy for ordinary people. Roll film, color film and small cameras followed."],["How a camera works","A camera is a light-tight box with a lens that focuses light onto film or a sensor. The aperture, an adjustable opening, controls how much light enters, and the shutter controls how long. In digital cameras, millions of tiny light-sensitive cells, or pixels, record the image as data."],["Photography as art","At first, many debated whether photography could be art. Photographers such as Alfred Stieglitz, Ansel Adams and Henri Cartier-Bresson showed its creative power through landscapes, portraits and street scenes. Today photographs hang in major museums, and photography is studied as an art form."]],
k:[["Oldest surviving photograph","Niépce, about 1826–1827"],["Daguerreotype announced","1839"],["First Kodak camera","1888"],["First digital camera prototype","1975"],["Word origin","Greek for writing with light"]],
d:[["c. 1826","Niépce makes the oldest surviving photograph"],["1839","Daguerre announces the daguerreotype; Talbot reveals his paper process"],["1861","First color photograph, by James Clerk Maxwell"],["1888","The Kodak camera is introduced"],["1975","Steven Sasson builds the first digital camera at Kodak"],["2000","First camera phones go on sale"]],
s2:[["Recording history","Photographs have recorded history since the 1850s. Mathew Brady’s team photographed the American Civil War, and later photographers documented both world wars, the struggle for civil rights and the Moon landings. Photojournalists risk their lives in war zones to show the world what is happening."],["Photos that changed opinion","Some photographs have changed the world. Images of child laborers by Lewis Hine helped bring in child labor laws in the United States. Dorothea Lange’s Migrant Mother showed the hardships of the Great Depression. Pictures of Earth from space, such as Earthrise, helped inspire the environmental movement."],["The digital revolution","In 1975 Kodak engineer Steven Sasson built the first digital camera, which weighed about 4 kilograms and took 23 seconds to record a black-and-white image. Digital cameras became common in the 2000s, and camera phones, first sold around 2000, made photography part of everyday life. Ironically, Kodak struggled to adapt and filed for bankruptcy in 2012."],["Photography in science","Photography lets scientists see what the eye cannot: high-speed photos freeze a bullet or a hummingbird’s wings, microscopes photograph cells, and telescopes capture faint galaxies. In 1878 Eadweard Muybridge’s photographs of a galloping horse proved that all four hooves leave the ground at once."],["Truth and manipulation","Photographs have been altered almost since photography began, from removing people in old political photos to editing images today with software. Now artificial intelligence can create realistic images of events that never happened. Learning to question where an image comes from has become an important skill."],["Selfies","The word selfie was named word of the year by Oxford Dictionaries in 2013. People have made self-portraits since the early days of photography; in 1839 Robert Cornelius took what is often called the first photographic self-portrait. Today billions of selfies are shared on social media."]],
sp:["Muybridge’s galloping horse","In the 1870s, people argued about whether a galloping horse ever has all four hooves off the ground. In 1878, Eadweard Muybridge set up a row of 12 cameras along a racetrack in California, each triggered by a thread as the horse passed. His photographs showed that all four hooves do leave the ground, with the legs tucked underneath. His work led toward motion pictures."],
g:[["Exposure","The amount of light that reaches film or a sensor"],["Aperture","The adjustable opening that lets light into a camera"],["Shutter","The part of a camera that opens and closes to let in light"],["Negative","An image with reversed light and dark, used to make prints"]],
f:["Photography means writing with light in Greek.","The first digital camera took 23 seconds to record an image.","Muybridge proved that a galloping horse lifts all four hooves at once."],
fun:"The first digital camera, built in 1975, weighed about 4 kilograms.",
tl:"Ang potograpiya ay sining at agham ng paggawa ng larawan gamit ang liwanag. Naimbento ito noong 1820s at 1830s sa Pransiya, at ngayon ay halos lahat ng tao ay kumukuha ng larawan gamit ang telepono."});

ADDART({id:"comics",t:"Comics and Manga",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"💭",w:"Comics",r:["anim","novel","japancountry","fairy","manga"],
p:[["Stories in pictures","Comics tell stories through sequences of drawings, usually with speech bubbles and captions."],["Many traditions","Comic traditions include American comic books, Japanese manga, Franco-Belgian bandes dessinées and the komiks of the Philippines."],["Read by millions","Comics and manga are among the world’s most popular forms of reading and inspire films, cartoons and games."]],
l:"Comics are a medium that combines images and words, arranged in panels, to tell stories or convey ideas. They range from short newspaper strips and superhero comic books to long graphic novels and Japanese manga series running for decades. Comics have rich traditions around the world, including manga in Japan, manhwa in Korea, bandes dessinées in France and Belgium, fumetti in Italy and komiks in the Philippines. Today digital comics, read on phones, are reaching new audiences worldwide.",
s:[["The language of comics","Comics use panels, boxes that show moments in a story, arranged in order. Speech balloons show what characters say, thought bubbles what they think, and captions add narration. The spaces between panels, called gutters, let readers imagine what happens in between. Lines and symbols show motion, sound and emotion."],["Early comics","People have told stories in pictures for thousands of years, from Egyptian wall paintings to medieval tapestries. Modern comics grew in the 1800s with illustrated stories, such as those by the Swiss artist Rodolphe Töpffer. Newspaper comic strips became popular in the United States in the 1890s."],["Comic books and superheroes","American comic books took off in the 1930s. Superman, created by Jerry Siegel and Joe Shuster, appeared in 1938, followed by Batman, Wonder Woman, Spider-Man and many more. Superhero stories became hugely popular and later inspired some of the highest-earning films ever made."],["Manga","Manga, Japanese comics, are read by people of all ages and cover every subject, from sports and romance to history and science fiction. Osamu Tezuka, creator of Astro Boy in the 1950s, is called the god of manga. Manga is usually printed in black and white and read from right to left. Series such as One Piece have sold hundreds of millions of copies."]],
k:[["Key elements","Panels, speech balloons, captions, gutters"],["Superman’s debut","1938"],["God of manga","Osamu Tezuka"],["Best-selling manga","One Piece, over 500 million copies"],["Philippine tradition","Komiks"]],
d:[["1830s","Rodolphe Töpffer creates early comic stories"],["1895","The Yellow Kid newspaper strip appears in New York"],["1929","Tintin first appears in Belgium"],["1938","Superman debuts in Action Comics #1"],["1952","Osamu Tezuka’s Astro Boy begins"],["1992","Maus becomes the first graphic novel to win a Pulitzer Prize"]],
s2:[["Franco-Belgian comics","In France and Belgium, comics are called bandes dessinées and are considered the ninth art. Hergé’s Tintin, first published in 1929, and René Goscinny and Albert Uderzo’s Asterix, from 1959, are loved worldwide. The Angoulême International Comics Festival in France is one of the largest in the world."],["Komiks of the Philippines","The Philippines has a strong komiks tradition. Artists such as Francisco V. Coching and Mars Ravelo created popular characters, including Ravelo’s Darna, a heroine who first appeared in 1947 under the name Varga and as Darna from 1950, and who became the star of many films and television series. Komiks magazines were widely read from the 1940s to the 1990s."],["Graphic novels","Graphic novels are long, book-length comics, often with serious themes. Art Spiegelman’s Maus, which tells of his father’s survival of the Holocaust with Jews drawn as mice and Nazis as cats, won a special Pulitzer Prize in 1992. Marjane Satrapi’s Persepolis described growing up in Iran during the Islamic Revolution."],["Webtoons","In the 2000s, Korean artists developed webtoons, digital comics designed to be read by scrolling down on a phone. They spread worldwide through apps and have been adapted into popular television dramas and films. Digital platforms now let artists from any country publish their work."],["Comics and education","Comics are used in schools to encourage reluctant readers, teach history and science and explain complex topics simply. Research suggests that combining words and pictures can help people understand and remember information. Many libraries now have large graphic novel collections."],["From page to screen","Comics and manga have inspired countless films, animated series and games. Anime adaptations of manga, such as Dragon Ball, Naruto and Demon Slayer, are watched worldwide, and films based on superhero comics are among the highest-grossing ever. Many comic artists now design with adaptation in mind."]],
sp:["Reading right to left","Traditional Japanese books are read from right to left, so most manga printed in English and other languages keeps this order: readers start at what Westerners would call the back of the book and read the panels from right to left. Early translations flipped the pages into a mirror image, but fans preferred the original layout, which keeps the artist’s design."],
g:[["Panel","A single frame in a comic"],["Speech balloon","A shape containing a character’s words"],["Manga","Japanese comics"],["Graphic novel","A long, book-length comic"]],
f:["Superman first appeared in 1938.","Manga is usually read from right to left.","Maus was the first graphic novel to win a Pulitzer Prize."],
fun:"In France and Belgium, comics are called the ninth art.",
tl:"Ang komiks ay nagkukuwento gamit ang sunud-sunod na larawan, karaniwang may speech balloon. Kabilang dito ang American comic books, manga ng Hapon at komiks ng Pilipinas, gaya ng Darna ni Mars Ravelo."});

;
/* data/earth2.js */
// Planet Earth articles, part 2: ice ages and glaciers, caves, and wetlands and mangroves.
ADDART({id:"iceage",t:"Ice Ages and Glaciers",c:"Geography",i:"🏔️",w:"Ice age",r:["antarc","arctic","climchange","greenland","alps","glaciers"],
p:[["Rivers of ice","Glaciers are huge masses of ice that slowly flow downhill or spread outward under their own weight."],["Frozen worlds","During ice ages, vast ice sheets covered much of North America, Europe and Asia."],["Shaping the land","Glaciers carved valleys, lakes and fjords and left behind boulders and hills."]],
l:"A glacier is a large, long-lasting body of ice formed from compacted snow that slowly moves under its own weight. Glaciers today cover about 10 percent of Earth’s land, mostly in Antarctica and Greenland, and hold about 69 percent of the world’s fresh water. During ice ages, the most recent of which peaked about 20,000 years ago, ice sheets several kilometers thick covered large parts of North America, Europe and Asia. Today most glaciers are shrinking as the climate warms.",
s:[["How glaciers form","Where more snow falls in winter than melts in summer, snow builds up year after year. The weight of new snow squeezes the layers below into dense ice. When the ice becomes thick enough, usually tens of meters, it begins to flow slowly downhill or outward, like very thick syrup."],["Types of glaciers","Mountain or valley glaciers flow down valleys from high mountains, as in the Alps, Himalaya and Andes. Ice sheets are enormous glaciers covering whole landmasses; only two exist today, in Antarctica and Greenland. Where glaciers reach the sea, they break off icebergs in a process called calving."],["Carving the land","As glaciers move, they scrape the ground with rocks frozen into their base, carving U-shaped valleys, sharp peaks such as the Matterhorn, and fjords where the sea fills deep glacial valleys, as in Norway, Chile and New Zealand. They carry rocks long distances and leave them behind as moraines and giant boulders called erratics."],["Ice ages","Earth has had several major ice ages over its history. The current one began about 2.6 million years ago and includes colder periods, called glacials, and warmer ones, called interglacials. We are living in an interglacial that began about 11,700 years ago. In the last glacial maximum, sea levels were about 120 meters lower than today."]],
k:[["Land covered by glaciers","About 10%"],["Share of fresh water in ice","About 69%"],["Last glacial maximum","About 20,000 years ago"],["Sea level then","About 120 m lower"],["Ice sheets today","Antarctica and Greenland"]],
d:[["c. 2.6 million years ago","Current ice age begins"],["c. 20,000 years ago","Last glacial maximum"],["c. 11,700 years ago","Current warm interglacial begins"],["1837","Louis Agassiz proposes past ice ages"],["1920s","Milutin Milanković explains ice age cycles"],["2019","Okjökull in Iceland becomes the first glacier lost to warming to get a memorial"]],
s2:[["What causes ice ages?","Ice age cycles are triggered mainly by slow changes in Earth’s orbit and tilt, which alter how much sunlight reaches different parts of the planet over tens of thousands of years. The Serbian scientist Milutin Milanković worked this out in the early 1900s. Changes in greenhouse gases, ice cover and ocean currents amplify the effects."],["Life in the Ice Age","During the last glacial period, woolly mammoths, woolly rhinos, cave lions, giant deer and saber-toothed cats roamed. Early humans adapted to the cold with clothing, fire and shelters. Lower sea levels created land bridges, such as Beringia between Asia and North America, which people crossed into the Americas."],["Ice cores","Scientists drill deep into ice sheets to extract ice cores. Bubbles of ancient air trapped in the ice show the levels of carbon dioxide and temperature going back about 800,000 years in Antarctica. They show that carbon dioxide levels today are far higher than at any time in that period."],["Shrinking glaciers","Nearly all of the world’s mountain glaciers are shrinking because of global warming, and their melting adds to sea level rise. In 2019 Icelanders held a funeral for Okjökull, which had lost its status as a glacier, and placed a plaque addressed to the future. The United Nations declared 2025 the International Year of Glaciers’ Preservation."],["Water towers","Mountain glaciers act as water towers, storing water in winter and releasing it in summer. Rivers fed by Himalayan glaciers, such as the Indus, Ganges and Brahmaputra, supply water to hundreds of millions of people. As glaciers shrink, rivers may at first carry more meltwater, then less, threatening water supplies."],["Snowball Earth","Some scientists think that hundreds of millions of years ago Earth may have frozen over almost completely, from the poles to near the equator, a state called Snowball Earth. Volcanoes slowly built up carbon dioxide until the climate warmed and the ice melted. Soon afterward, complex life began to flourish."]],
sp:["A letter to the future","In August 2019, scientists and residents climbed a mountain in western Iceland to mark the loss of Okjökull, a glacier that had melted so much it no longer moved. They placed a plaque reading, in part: In the next 200 years all our glaciers are expected to follow the same path. This monument is to acknowledge that we know what is happening and what needs to be done. Only you know if we did it."],
g:[["Glacier","A large, slow-moving mass of ice"],["Ice sheet","A glacier covering a huge area of land"],["Fjord","A deep inlet of the sea carved by a glacier"],["Moraine","Rocks and soil deposited by a glacier"]],
f:["Glaciers hold about 69 percent of the world’s fresh water.","During the last ice age, sea levels were about 120 meters lower.","Iceland held a funeral for a glacier in 2019."],
fun:"Antarctic ice cores contain air bubbles trapped up to about 800,000 years ago.",
tl:"Ang glacier ay malaking masa ng yelo na dahan-dahang gumagalaw. Noong mga panahon ng yelo, natatakpan ng makakapal na yelo ang malaking bahagi ng Hilagang Amerika, Europa at Asya. Ngayon, lumiliit ang karamihan sa mga glacier dahil sa pag-init ng klima."});

ADDART({id:"caves",t:"Caves",c:"Geography",i:"🕳️",w:"Cave",r:["caveart","volc","bat","vietnamcountry"],
p:[["Hidden worlds","Caves are natural underground spaces large enough for a person to enter."],["Carved by water","Most large caves form when slightly acidic water slowly dissolves limestone rock over thousands of years."],["Home to life and history","Caves shelter unique animals and hold clues to Earth’s past and early human history."]],
l:"A cave is a natural hollow in the ground large enough for a person to enter. Most caves form in limestone, as rainwater, made slightly acidic by carbon dioxide, seeps through cracks and slowly dissolves the rock, creating passages, chambers and underground rivers over hundreds of thousands of years. Other caves form in lava, ice or sea cliffs. Caves contain beautiful formations such as stalactites and stalagmites, are home to specially adapted animals and have preserved fossils and ancient art.",
s:[["How limestone caves form","Rainwater absorbs carbon dioxide from the air and soil, forming a weak acid. As it trickles through cracks in limestone, it dissolves the rock, widening the cracks into tunnels and caverns. Landscapes shaped this way, with caves, sinkholes and underground streams, are called karst."],["Stalactites and stalagmites","When water dripping into a cave loses carbon dioxide, it leaves behind tiny amounts of the mineral calcite. Over thousands of years, these build up into stalactites hanging from the ceiling and stalagmites rising from the floor. When they meet, they form columns. They usually grow only a few centimeters every thousand years."],["Other kinds of caves","Lava tubes form when the surface of a lava flow cools and hardens while molten lava keeps flowing beneath, leaving a tunnel. Sea caves are carved by waves pounding cliffs. Glacier caves form inside ice. Some caves form when acidic water rises from below, as at Carlsbad Caverns in the United States."],["Cave life","Caves are home to animals adapted to darkness. Bats and some birds roost in caves and fly out to feed. Deep inside, true cave animals, such as blind cave fish, salamanders like the olm of Slovenia, and pale insects and spiders, have lost their eyes and color but have heightened senses of touch and smell."]],
k:[["Largest cave passage","Hang Sơn Đoòng, Vietnam"],["Longest cave system","Mammoth Cave, USA, over 680 km mapped"],["Deepest known cave","Veryovkina Cave, Georgia, about 2,200 m"],["Main rock","Limestone"],["Study of caves","Speleology"]],
d:[["c. 1 million years ago","Early humans use fire in Wonderwerk Cave, South Africa"],["1790s","Settlers begin exploring Mammoth Cave in Kentucky"],["1994","Chauvet Cave is discovered in France"],["2009","Hang Sơn Đoòng is explored by a British-Vietnamese team"],["2018","Twelve boys and their coach are rescued from Tham Luang cave, Thailand"],["2018","Veryovkina becomes the deepest known cave"]],
s2:[["Hang Sơn Đoòng","Hang Sơn Đoòng, in Phong Nha-Kẻ Bàng National Park in Vietnam, is the largest known cave passage in the world. Discovered by a local man, Hồ Khanh, around 1990 and explored in 2009, some chambers are more than 200 meters high, large enough to hold a skyscraper. Parts of the roof have collapsed, letting in sunlight so that a jungle grows inside."],["The Tham Luang rescue","In June 2018, twelve boys from a youth football team and their coach became trapped by floodwater in Tham Luang cave in northern Thailand. After they were found by British divers nine days later, an international team of divers and rescuers brought all 13 out safely in a daring operation. A Thai former navy diver, Saman Kunan, died during the effort."],["Caves and early humans","Caves have preserved evidence of early humans, including fossils, tools and art. In South Africa’s Rising Star cave, scientists found remains of Homo naledi. In Callao Cave in the Philippines, fossils of a small human species named Homo luzonensis, described in 2019, are at least 50,000 years old."],["Mammoth Cave","Mammoth Cave in Kentucky, in the United States, is the longest known cave system in the world, with more than 680 kilometers of mapped passages. Enslaved African Americans, such as Stephen Bishop in the 1830s and 1840s, explored and mapped much of it and guided early visitors."],["Caves and climate","Stalagmites grow in layers, like tree rings, and their chemistry records changes in rainfall and temperature over hundreds of thousands of years. Scientists use them to study ancient climates, such as monsoon patterns in Asia."],["Exploring caves safely","Caving, or spelunking, is exciting but dangerous: caves can flood quickly, be very cold and confusing to navigate. Cavers use helmets, lights, ropes and maps, travel in groups and tell others where they are going. Many caves are protected to preserve fragile formations and wildlife."]],
sp:["A jungle inside a cave","In Hang Sơn Đoòng, two places where the cave roof collapsed long ago, called dolines, let sunlight pour in. Beneath them, trees up to 30 meters tall grow on the cave floor, forming jungles with their own plants and animals. The cave also has clouds that form inside it and a river that runs through it."],
g:[["Karst","A landscape of caves and sinkholes formed by dissolving rock"],["Stalactite","A mineral formation hanging from a cave ceiling"],["Stalagmite","A mineral formation rising from a cave floor"],["Speleology","The scientific study of caves"]],
f:["Hang Sơn Đoòng in Vietnam is the largest known cave passage.","Mammoth Cave in the USA has over 680 km of mapped passages.","Stalactites hang down; stalagmites rise up."],
fun:"Hang Sơn Đoòng in Vietnam is so big that it has its own jungle and weather inside.",
tl:"Ang kuweba ay likas na butas sa lupa na sapat ang laki para pasukin ng tao. Karamihan ay nabubuo kapag tinutunaw ng tubig ang limestone. Matatagpuan sa Vietnam ang pinakamalaking kilalang daanan ng kuweba."});

ADDART({id:"wetlands",t:"Wetlands and Mangroves",c:"Geography",i:"🌾",w:"Wetland",r:["eco","climchange","frog","reef","borneo"],
p:[["Where land meets water","Wetlands are areas where water covers the soil or is near the surface for much of the year, such as marshes, swamps, bogs and mangroves."],["Rich in life","Wetlands support huge numbers of birds, fish, insects, amphibians and plants."],["Natural protectors","They filter water, prevent floods, protect coasts from storms and store large amounts of carbon."]],
l:"Wetlands are areas where water covers the soil, or is present at or near the surface, for all or part of the year. They include marshes, swamps, bogs, fens, floodplains, river deltas, lagoons and mangrove forests. Wetlands are among the most productive ecosystems on Earth, supporting a great variety of life. They also provide people with clean water, food, flood protection and protection from storms. Yet the world has lost a large share of its wetlands to drainage, farming and development.",
s:[["Kinds of wetlands","Marshes are dominated by grasses and reeds. Swamps have trees and shrubs. Bogs are spongy, acidic wetlands covered with peat moss. Mangroves are coastal forests of salt-tolerant trees that grow in tropical and subtropical tidal zones. Wetlands can be freshwater, salty or brackish, a mix of both."],["Mangrove forests","Mangroves grow along tropical coasts in Asia, Africa, the Americas and Oceania. Their tangled roots, some sticking up out of the mud to breathe, hold soil in place, slow waves and shelter young fish, crabs and shrimp. Indonesia has the largest area of mangroves of any country, followed by Brazil and Australia."],["Nature’s sponges and filters","Wetlands soak up rainwater and release it slowly, reducing floods and keeping rivers flowing in dry seasons. Their plants and soils trap sediments and absorb pollutants, cleaning the water. Coastal wetlands and mangroves act as buffers against storm surges and tsunamis."],["Wildlife","Wetlands are vital for migrating birds, which stop to rest and feed on long journeys. They are nurseries for many fish species, including those caught commercially. Frogs, turtles, crocodiles, otters, dragonflies and countless plants depend on them."]],
k:[["Types","Marshes, swamps, bogs, fens, mangroves, deltas"],["Wetland loss since 1970","About 35% (estimate)"],["Global treaty","Ramsar Convention (1971)"],["Largest tropical wetland","The Pantanal, South America"],["World Wetlands Day","2 February"]],
d:[["1971","Ramsar Convention on Wetlands is signed in Iran"],["1975","Ramsar Convention comes into force"],["1997","First World Wetlands Day is celebrated"],["2004","Indian Ocean tsunami shows the protective value of mangroves"],["2021","UN declares 2 February an official international day"]],
s2:[["The Ramsar Convention","In 1971, countries meeting in the Iranian city of Ramsar signed a treaty to protect wetlands, one of the first modern global environmental agreements. Today more than 170 countries have joined, and over 2,500 Wetlands of International Importance, called Ramsar Sites, have been designated, covering an area larger than Mexico."],["Famous wetlands","The Pantanal, mostly in Brazil, is the world’s largest tropical wetland and home to jaguars, capybaras and caimans. The Okavango Delta in Botswana floods each year in the middle of the Kalahari Desert, attracting elephants and other wildlife. The Sundarbans, shared by India and Bangladesh, is the largest mangrove forest and home to Bengal tigers."],["Disappearing wetlands","Wetlands have been drained for farming, cities, ports and aquaculture, such as shrimp ponds, or polluted. Studies suggest that about 35 percent of wetlands have been lost since 1970, faster than forests. Mangroves have also declined, though the rate of loss has slowed in recent years as countries protect and replant them."],["Blue carbon","Mangroves, salt marshes and seagrasses store large amounts of carbon in their soils, sometimes several times more per hectare than tropical forests. This is called blue carbon. When these ecosystems are destroyed, the stored carbon can be released, adding to climate change, so protecting them helps fight global warming."],["Protection from storms","After the 2004 Indian Ocean tsunami, studies found that some villages behind healthy mangroves suffered less damage. In the Philippines, Bangladesh, Vietnam and other countries, communities are replanting mangroves to protect coasts from typhoons and rising seas, and to restore fisheries."],["Peatlands","Peatlands, such as bogs, cover only about 3 percent of Earth’s land but store about a third of all soil carbon, twice as much as all the world’s forests. When drained or burned, as in parts of Indonesia during dry years, they release huge amounts of carbon dioxide and smoke."]],
sp:["The Sundarbans","The Sundarbans, meaning beautiful forest in Bengali, covers about 10,000 square kilometers of mangrove forest in the delta of the Ganges, Brahmaputra and Meghna rivers in Bangladesh and India. It is the largest mangrove forest in the world and one of the last strongholds of the Bengal tiger, which swims between islands. Both countries’ parts are UNESCO World Heritage Sites."],
g:[["Wetland","Land covered by or saturated with water for much of the year"],["Mangrove","A salt-tolerant tree or forest growing on tropical coasts"],["Brackish","Water that is a mix of fresh and salt water"],["Blue carbon","Carbon stored in coastal and marine ecosystems"]],
f:["The Ramsar Convention to protect wetlands was signed in 1971.","The Sundarbans is the world’s largest mangrove forest.","Wetlands help prevent floods and clean water."],
fun:"Bengal tigers in the Sundarbans mangrove forest are strong swimmers, crossing rivers between islands.",
tl:"Ang wetland ay mga lugar na natatakpan ng tubig ang lupa sa malaking bahagi ng taon, gaya ng latian at bakawan. Mahalaga ito sa mga hayop at halaman, at pinoprotektahan nito ang mga baybayin laban sa baha at bagyo."});

;
/* data/tech2-1.js */
// Technology articles, part 2.1: the wheel, the light bulb and 3D printing.
ADDART({id:"wheel",t:"The Wheel",c:"Technology",i:"⚙️",w:"Wheel",r:["cars","rail","mesop","bridges"],
p:[["An ancient invention","The wheel was invented about 5,500 years ago and changed transport, farming and industry."],["More than transport","Wheels are used in carts, pottery, mills, gears, clocks and countless machines."],["Wheel and axle","Joined to an axle, a wheel reduces friction and makes heavy loads easier to move."]],
l:"The wheel is a circular object that turns on an axle, one of the most important inventions in history. Potter’s wheels appeared in Mesopotamia around 4500 to 3500 BCE, and wheeled vehicles soon after, around 3500 BCE, in Mesopotamia, the Caucasus and central Europe. Wheels made it easier to carry heavy loads, led to chariots and carts and, through waterwheels, gears and pulleys, powered machines from mills to clocks. Today wheels turn in everything from bicycles and cars to turbines and hard drives.",
s:[["Why the wheel was hard to invent","A wheel on its own is not enough; it needs an axle, and the two must fit precisely: too tight and they jam, too loose and they wobble. Making smooth round wheels and axles required good tools and skill. That is one reason the wheel appeared thousands of years after farming began."],["Early wheels","The earliest vehicle wheels were solid disks made from planks of wood fastened together. One of the oldest surviving wheels, found in the Ljubljana Marshes in Slovenia, is about 5,150 years old. Pictures of wheeled wagons appear on clay tablets from the Sumerian city of Uruk and on a pot from Poland of similar age."],["Spokes and chariots","Around 2000 BCE, people in the steppes of Central Asia developed light wheels with spokes, much lighter than solid wheels. Spoked wheels made fast horse-drawn chariots possible, which spread across Asia, the Middle East, Egypt and Europe and changed warfare."],["Wheels at work","Wheels are not only for vehicles. Potter’s wheels let craftspeople shape round pots quickly. Waterwheels and windmills, using wheels with blades, ground grain and powered machines. Gears, toothed wheels that mesh together, transmit power and change speed in clocks, bicycles and engines."]],
k:[["Potter’s wheel","Mesopotamia, c. 4500–3500 BCE"],["First wheeled vehicles","c. 3500 BCE"],["Oldest surviving wheel","Ljubljana Marshes, Slovenia, about 5,150 years old"],["Spoked wheels","c. 2000 BCE"],["Key partner","The axle"]],
d:[["c. 4000 BCE","Potter’s wheels are used in Mesopotamia"],["c. 3500 BCE","Wheeled vehicles appear"],["c. 3150 BCE","The Ljubljana Marshes wheel is made"],["c. 2000 BCE","Spoked wheels and chariots develop"],["1845","Robert William Thomson patents the pneumatic tire"],["1888","John Boyd Dunlop develops a practical air-filled tire"]],
s2:[["The Americas","Peoples of ancient Mexico made small wheeled toys or figurines, but did not use wheels for transport. Without large animals to pull carts, and in many places with rugged land and dense forest, wheels were less useful than human porters, canoes and, in the Andes, llamas."],["Tires","Early wheels had rims of wood or iron. Air-filled, or pneumatic, rubber tires were patented by Robert William Thomson in 1845, and John Boyd Dunlop developed a practical version for bicycles in 1888. Pneumatic tires made rides smoother and helped bicycles and cars spread quickly."],["The wheel and the bicycle","The bicycle developed in the 1800s, from the wooden running machine of Karl Drais in 1817 to pedal-driven bicycles and the safety bicycle of the 1880s, with two equal wheels and a chain. It gave millions of people cheap personal transport and remains one of the most efficient ways to travel."],["Ball bearings","To reduce friction even more, engineers place ball or roller bearings between a wheel and its axle. Leonardo da Vinci sketched designs for bearings, and today they are used in everything from skateboards and fans to cars and jet engines."],["Giant wheels","Some wheels are enormous, such as Ferris wheels, first built for the World’s Columbian Exposition in Chicago in 1893, and today’s observation wheels like the London Eye and Ain Dubai. Huge wheels also turn in mining machines, ship propellers and wind turbines."],["Reinventing the wheel","The phrase reinventing the wheel means wasting time creating something that already exists. Yet engineers keep improving wheels, with lighter materials, airless tires, wheels for Mars rovers made of aluminum and titanium and wheels that can climb stairs."]],
sp:["The oldest wheel","In 2002, archaeologists in the Ljubljana Marshes in Slovenia found a wooden wheel and axle about 5,150 years old. The wheel, made of ash wood, is about 70 centimeters across, and the axle of oak. The axle turned together with the wheel, a design used on early two-wheeled carts. It is one of the oldest wooden wheels ever found."],
g:[["Axle","A rod on which a wheel turns"],["Spoke","A rod connecting the hub of a wheel to its rim"],["Friction","A force that resists movement between surfaces"],["Gear","A toothed wheel that meshes with another to transmit motion"]],
f:["The wheel was invented about 5,500 years ago.","One of the oldest surviving wheels is about 5,150 years old, from Slovenia.","Air-filled tires were patented in 1845."],
fun:"The potter’s wheel was probably invented before wheels were used on vehicles.",
tl:"Ang gulong ay isa sa pinakamahalagang imbensyon, naimbento mga 5,500 taon na ang nakalipas. Pinadali nito ang pagdadala ng mabibigat na bagay at ginagamit sa maraming makina."});

ADDART({id:"lightbulb",t:"The Electric Light Bulb",c:"Technology",i:"💡",w:"Incandescent light bulb",r:["currents","light","bamboo","laser"],
p:[["Light at the flick of a switch","Electric light bulbs transformed homes, streets, factories and cities after the late 1800s."],["Many inventors","Thomas Edison and Joseph Swan made practical bulbs in 1879 and 1880, building on decades of work by others."],["From glow to LED","Today efficient LED lights have largely replaced old incandescent bulbs."]],
l:"The electric light bulb is a device that produces light from electricity. The first practical incandescent bulbs, in which a thin filament glows when heated by electric current, were developed around 1879 by Joseph Swan in Britain and Thomas Edison in the United States. Electric lighting changed daily life, letting people work, study and travel safely after dark. Over the next century, fluorescent lamps and then light-emitting diodes, or LEDs, made lighting far more efficient.",
s:[["Before electric light","For thousands of years, people lit the dark with fires, oil lamps and candles. In the 1800s, many cities installed gas lamps, burning gas piped through the streets. These sources were dim, smoky, smelly and a fire hazard."],["Early experiments","In 1802 Humphry Davy made platinum strips glow with electricity and later demonstrated a brilliant arc lamp. Arc lamps lit some streets and lighthouses, but were too bright and harsh for homes. Inventors spent decades trying to make a long-lasting incandescent bulb small and cheap enough for everyday use."],["Swan and Edison","In Britain, Joseph Swan demonstrated a carbon filament lamp in 1879, and his house was among the first lit by electric light. In the United States, Thomas Edison and his team tested thousands of materials and in 1879 produced a carbon filament bulb that lasted many hours; they later used carbonized bamboo. Edison and Swan eventually joined forces in Britain in a company called Ediswan."],["Lighting systems","Edison’s greatest achievement was creating a complete system: generators, wires, switches, meters and bulbs. In 1882 his Pearl Street Station in New York began supplying electricity to customers. Electric lighting spread quickly, though many rural areas and poorer countries waited decades for electricity."]],
k:[["Practical bulbs","1879–1880, Swan and Edison"],["Filament materials","Carbon, then tungsten from about 1910"],["First central power station","Pearl Street, New York, 1882"],["LED efficiency","Uses about 75–90% less energy than incandescent bulbs"],["Nobel Prize for blue LED","2014"]],
d:[["1802","Humphry Davy makes metal glow with electricity"],["1879","Swan and Edison demonstrate carbon filament bulbs"],["1882","Pearl Street power station opens in New York"],["c. 1910","Tungsten filaments become standard"],["1962","First visible red LED is developed"],["2014","Nobel Prize for inventing the efficient blue LED"]],
s2:[["Tungsten filaments","Carbon filaments were fragile and dim. From about 1910, bulbs used filaments of tungsten, a metal with a very high melting point, drawn into fine wires. Filling bulbs with inert gas instead of a vacuum made them last longer. Tungsten bulbs were standard for most of the 1900s."],["Fluorescent lamps","Fluorescent tubes, common from the 1930s, pass electricity through mercury vapor, producing ultraviolet light that makes a coating inside the tube glow. They use much less energy than incandescent bulbs. Compact fluorescent bulbs became popular in homes in the 1990s and 2000s."],["The LED revolution","Light-emitting diodes produce light when electricity passes through a semiconductor. Red and green LEDs existed for decades, but white LED lighting needed an efficient blue LED, achieved in the early 1990s by Isamu Akasaki, Hiroshi Amano and Shuji Nakamura, who won the 2014 Nobel Prize in Physics. LEDs last many years and use far less energy."],["Banning inefficient bulbs","Because incandescent bulbs turn most of their electricity into heat rather than light, many countries, including the European Union, Australia, the Philippines and others, have phased them out in favor of efficient lighting, saving energy and reducing greenhouse gas emissions."],["Light pollution","Electric lighting has a downside: light pollution. Artificial light at night hides the stars, confuses migrating birds and newly hatched sea turtles, and may disturb human sleep. Shielded lights that point downward and warmer colors can reduce these effects."],["Light for everyone","Even today, hundreds of millions of people, mostly in sub-Saharan Africa and rural Asia, have no electricity at home. Cheap solar-powered LED lamps are replacing kerosene lamps in many homes, providing cleaner, safer light for studying and working."]],
sp:["The light that never goes out","In a fire station in Livermore, California, a hand-blown carbon filament bulb has been glowing almost continuously since 1901. Called the Centennial Light, it gives off only about 4 watts of dim light, but has been recognized as the longest-lasting light bulb. It even has its own webcam."],
g:[["Filament","A thin wire that glows when electric current flows through it"],["Incandescent","Giving off light because of heat"],["LED","Light-emitting diode, a semiconductor that gives off light"],["Light pollution","Excess artificial light at night"]],
f:["Swan and Edison both developed practical light bulbs around 1879.","LEDs use about 75 to 90 percent less energy than incandescent bulbs.","A light bulb in California has been glowing since 1901."],
fun:"A light bulb in a Californian fire station has been glowing since 1901.",
tl:"Ang bombilya ay gumagawa ng liwanag mula sa kuryente. Naging praktikal ito noong 1879 dahil kina Joseph Swan at Thomas Edison. Ngayon ay mas matipid na LED ang karaniwang ginagamit."});

ADDART({id:"printing3d",t:"3D Printing",c:"Technology",i:"🖨️",w:"3D printing",r:["print","robot","comp","ai"],
p:[["Building layer by layer","3D printing makes solid objects by adding material one thin layer at a time, following a digital design."],["From toys to rockets","It is used to make prototypes, tools, medical implants, houses and even rocket engine parts."],["Making at home","Affordable desktop 3D printers let schools, hobbyists and small businesses make their own objects."]],
l:"3D printing, also called additive manufacturing, is a way of making three-dimensional objects by building them up layer by layer from a digital model. Unlike traditional methods that cut material away or pour it into molds, 3D printing adds material only where it is needed. Invented in the 1980s, it is now used in engineering, medicine, aerospace, architecture, fashion and education. Printers can work with plastics, metals, ceramics, concrete, resins and even living cells.",
s:[["How it works","First, a designer creates a 3D model on a computer or scans an existing object. Software slices the model into hundreds or thousands of thin horizontal layers. The printer then builds the object one layer at a time, each bonded to the layer below, until the whole object is complete."],["Kinds of 3D printing","The most common desktop printers melt plastic filament and squeeze it through a hot nozzle, like a precise glue gun. Stereolithography uses a laser or light to harden liquid resin. Other printers fuse powders of plastic or metal with lasers, or spray binding agents onto powder."],["Invention","In 1984 Chuck Hull in the United States invented stereolithography, patenting the first 3D printing process and founding the company 3D Systems. Similar ideas were developed by Hideo Kodama in Japan and others. Early machines were expensive and used mainly by industry to make prototypes."],["Desktop printers","When key patents expired in the late 2000s, open-source projects such as RepRap, a printer designed to print many of its own parts, helped launch cheap desktop printers. Schools, libraries and makerspaces now use them to teach design, engineering and creativity."]],
k:[["Also called","Additive manufacturing"],["Invented","1984, stereolithography by Chuck Hull"],["Common materials","Plastics, resins, metals, concrete"],["Desktop boom","Late 2000s onward"],["Uses","Prototypes, medicine, aerospace, construction, education"]],
d:[["1981","Hideo Kodama describes a method of building layers with light"],["1984","Chuck Hull files a patent for stereolithography"],["2005","The RepRap project begins"],["2009","Key patents expire, cheap desktop printers spread"],["2014","A 3D printer is used on the International Space Station"],["2020s","3D-printed houses and rocket engines become reality"]],
s2:[["Medicine","Doctors use 3D printing to make custom implants, such as hip joints and skull plates, shaped exactly to fit a patient. Surgeons print models of a patient’s heart or tumor to plan operations. Dental crowns, hearing aids and prosthetic limbs are often printed. Scientists are experimenting with printing living tissues, called bioprinting."],["Space","In 2014 a 3D printer was used on the International Space Station to make tools, so astronauts could make spare parts instead of waiting for a delivery from Earth. Rocket companies print complex engine parts in metal, which are lighter and made of fewer pieces."],["Houses","Large printers can squeeze out layers of concrete to build the walls of houses in days. Printed homes have been built in countries including Mexico, the Netherlands, the United States, the United Arab Emirates and India, and they are being tested as a way to provide affordable housing."],["Prototypes and spare parts","Companies use 3D printing to make prototypes quickly and cheaply, testing designs before mass production. It is also used to make spare parts for old machines, cars and aircraft that are no longer produced, and custom tools on factory floors."],["Limits","3D printing is usually slower than mass production for large numbers of identical items, and printed parts can be weaker than molded or forged ones. Many materials and machines are still expensive. For now, it is best for complex, custom or small-batch objects."],["Concerns","3D printing raises some concerns, such as the printing of untraceable weapons, the copying of copyrighted designs and plastic waste from failed prints. Governments and companies are discussing rules, and researchers are developing recyclable and plant-based printing materials."]],
sp:["Printing in space","In November 2014, astronauts on the International Space Station used a 3D printer to make a small plastic part, the first object ever 3D-printed in space. Weeks later, engineers on the ground emailed the design for a ratchet wrench to the station, and it was printed there. Future missions to the Moon and Mars may print tools, parts and even buildings from local materials."],
g:[["Additive manufacturing","Making objects by adding material layer by layer"],["Filament","A thin strand of plastic used in many 3D printers"],["Prototype","An early model used to test a design"],["Bioprinting","Printing structures made of living cells"]],
f:["3D printing was invented in the 1980s.","Astronauts have 3D-printed tools in space.","Whole houses have been built with 3D printers."],
fun:"In 2014 a wrench was designed on Earth, emailed to space and 3D-printed on the International Space Station.",
tl:"Ang 3D printing ay paggawa ng bagay patong-patong mula sa disenyong digital. Ginagamit ito sa paggawa ng prototype, kagamitang medikal, bahay at maging bahagi ng rocket."});

;
/* data/tech2-2.js */
// Technology articles, part 2.2: canals, video games and cybersecurity.
ADDART({id:"canals",t:"Canals",c:"Technology",i:"🚢",w:"Canal",r:["ships","bridges","trade","egyptcountry"],
p:[["Man-made waterways","Canals are artificial waterways built for transport, irrigation or drainage."],["Shortcuts for ships","The Suez and Panama canals cut thousands of kilometers off sea voyages between oceans."],["Ancient and modern","People have dug canals for thousands of years, from ancient Mesopotamia to China’s Grand Canal."]],
l:"A canal is an artificial waterway constructed to carry boats, supply water for farming or drain land. Canals have been built since ancient times: the Grand Canal of China, begun in the 5th century BCE, is the longest in the world, and canals carried much of the freight of the early Industrial Revolution in Britain. Great ship canals, such as the Suez Canal, opened in 1869, and the Panama Canal, opened in 1914, transformed world trade by linking seas and oceans.",
s:[["Early canals","Ancient Mesopotamians dug canals to carry river water to their fields, making farming and cities possible. Egyptians built a canal linking the Nile to the Red Sea. In China, canals carried grain and goods between regions; the Grand Canal eventually stretched about 1,800 kilometers from Beijing to Hangzhou."],["Locks","Canals often cross hills and valleys. Locks raise and lower boats between different water levels: a boat enters a chamber, gates close, and water is let in or out until it matches the next level. The pound lock, with gates at both ends, was used in China by the 10th century and later spread to Europe."],["Canals and industry","In the 1700s and early 1800s, canals were the highways of the Industrial Revolution in Britain, carrying coal, iron and goods far more cheaply than roads. The Erie Canal in the United States, opened in 1825, linked the Great Lakes to New York City and helped it become a great port."],["The Suez Canal","The Suez Canal in Egypt connects the Mediterranean Sea with the Red Sea, letting ships travel between Europe and Asia without sailing around Africa. Built by a French company led by Ferdinand de Lesseps with the forced and paid labor of many Egyptian workers, it opened in 1869. Egypt nationalized it in 1956, leading to the Suez Crisis."]],
k:[["Longest canal","Grand Canal of China, about 1,800 km"],["Suez Canal opened","1869"],["Panama Canal opened","1914"],["Key device","The lock"],["Busiest ship canals","Suez, Panama, Kiel"]],
d:[["c. 486 BCE","Work begins on what becomes China’s Grand Canal"],["1825","The Erie Canal opens in the United States"],["1869","The Suez Canal opens"],["1914","The Panama Canal opens"],["1956","Egypt nationalizes the Suez Canal"],["2021","The ship Ever Given blocks the Suez Canal for six days"]],
s2:[["The Panama Canal","The Panama Canal crosses the narrow Isthmus of Panama, linking the Atlantic and Pacific Oceans. A French attempt led by de Lesseps failed in the 1880s, defeated by disease and difficult terrain. The United States completed the canal in 1914; tens of thousands of workers, many from the Caribbean, built it, and thousands died. Panama took full control in 1999."],["How the Panama Canal works","The Panama Canal uses locks to lift ships about 26 meters to Gatun Lake, an artificial lake, and lower them again on the other side. Each crossing uses millions of liters of fresh water from the lake. A wider set of locks opened in 2016 for bigger ships, and droughts in 2023 and 2024 forced limits on the number of crossings."],["The Ever Given","In March 2021, the giant container ship Ever Given, about 400 meters long, ran aground and blocked the Suez Canal for six days, holding up hundreds of ships and disrupting global trade. Diggers, tugboats and high tides finally freed it. The event showed how much world trade depends on a few narrow waterways."],["City canals","Some cities are famous for their canals, which serve as streets. Venice in Italy has about 150 canals crossed by around 400 bridges. Amsterdam’s ring of canals dates from the 1600s. Bangkok, once called the Venice of the East, was crisscrossed by canals called khlongs, some still used by boats today."],["Irrigation canals","Many canals carry water for farming. Ancient irrigation canals helped civilizations grow in Mesopotamia, Egypt, Peru and the American Southwest. Today, huge irrigation canals in countries such as Pakistan, India, Egypt and the United States water millions of hectares of crops."],["Canals today","Many old transport canals in Britain and Europe were abandoned when railways and roads took over, but are now restored for leisure boating, walking and wildlife. New canals and the widening of old ones continue to be debated, balancing the benefits for trade against costs to the environment and local communities."]],
sp:["Falkirk Wheel","Some canals lift boats with ingenious machines instead of locks. The Falkirk Wheel in Scotland, opened in 2002, is the world’s only rotating boat lift. Two huge arms carry water-filled gondolas holding boats, turning to raise them about 24 meters between two canals in a few minutes, using about as much energy as boiling a few kettles."],
g:[["Canal","An artificial waterway"],["Lock","A chamber with gates that raises or lowers boats between water levels"],["Isthmus","A narrow strip of land joining two larger areas"],["Irrigation","Supplying water to land for crops"]],
f:["China’s Grand Canal is the longest canal in the world.","The Suez Canal opened in 1869 and the Panama Canal in 1914.","The Falkirk Wheel is the world’s only rotating boat lift."],
fun:"In 2021 a single ship blocked the Suez Canal for six days.",
tl:"Ang kanal ay daluyang tubig na gawa ng tao para sa transportasyon, irigasyon o pagpapatuyo. Pinaikli ng Suez at Panama Canal ang paglalakbay ng mga barko sa pagitan ng mga karagatan."});

ADDART({id:"videogames",t:"Video Games",c:"Technology",i:"🎮",w:"Video game",r:["comp","chip","ai","anim"],
p:[["Play on a screen","Video games are interactive games played on computers, consoles, phones and arcade machines."],["A giant industry","Video games earn more money each year than films and recorded music combined."],["Billions of players","More than 3 billion people around the world play video games."]],
l:"A video game is an electronic game in which players interact with images on a screen using controllers, keyboards, touchscreens or motion sensors. Video games began as experiments in university and government laboratories in the 1950s and 1960s, became popular with arcade machines and home consoles in the 1970s and 1980s, and are now one of the world’s largest entertainment industries. They range from simple puzzles to vast online worlds, and are also used for education, training, art and competitive sport.",
s:[["Early games","In 1958 physicist William Higinbotham created Tennis for Two on an oscilloscope at a US laboratory. In 1962 students at MIT made Spacewar!, a space battle game for an early computer. In 1972 Atari released the arcade game Pong, a simple table tennis game that became a huge hit and launched the video game industry."],["The golden age of arcades","In the late 1970s and early 1980s, arcade games such as Space Invaders from Japan in 1978, Pac-Man in 1980 and Donkey Kong in 1981 drew crowds to arcades worldwide. Home consoles, such as the Atari 2600, brought games into living rooms."],["Consoles","After a market crash in 1983, Nintendo revived home gaming with the Nintendo Entertainment System and games such as Super Mario Bros. Rival companies such as Sega, Sony with the PlayStation and Microsoft with the Xbox followed. Handheld consoles, such as the Game Boy in 1989, let people play anywhere."],["Mobile and online games","Smartphones turned billions of people into gamers. Games can be downloaded instantly, and many are free to play. Online games let players around the world play together; some, such as Minecraft, are among the best-selling games of all time, with more than 300 million copies sold."]],
k:[["Players worldwide","More than 3 billion"],["First arcade hit","Pong (1972)"],["Best-selling game","Minecraft, over 300 million copies"],["Main platforms","Consoles, computers, phones"],["Esports","Professional competitive gaming"]],
d:[["1958","Tennis for Two is created"],["1962","Spacewar! is developed at MIT"],["1972","Pong and the first home console, the Magnavox Odyssey"],["1980","Pac-Man is released"],["1985","Super Mario Bros. is released in North America"],["2011","Minecraft is fully released"]],
s2:[["How games are made","Making a large game can involve hundreds of people over several years: designers, programmers, artists, animators, writers, musicians, sound engineers and testers. Game engines, software tools such as Unity and Unreal Engine, help developers build games. Small independent, or indie, studios also make many popular games."],["Esports","Esports are organized video game competitions, in which professional players and teams compete in games such as League of Legends, Dota 2 and Counter-Strike for prizes worth millions of dollars. Tournaments fill arenas and are watched online by millions. Esports were medal events at the 2022 Asian Games, held in Hangzhou, China, in 2023."],["Games and learning","Games can teach skills such as problem-solving, planning, teamwork and quick decision-making. Educational games teach math, languages and science, and simulations train pilots, surgeons and soldiers. Minecraft Education is used in classrooms worldwide to teach subjects from history to coding."],["Concerns","People worry about excessive gaming, violent content, spending money on in-game purchases and online harassment. The World Health Organization recognized gaming disorder as a condition in 2019, though it affects only a small share of players. Age ratings, parental controls and balanced habits help."],["Games as art","Many people consider video games an art form, combining storytelling, visual art, music and interaction. Museums such as the Smithsonian American Art Museum and the Museum of Modern Art in New York have held exhibitions of video games and added games to their collections."],["Technology pushed forward","Video games have driven advances in computer graphics, sound and chips. Graphics processing units, designed to render game graphics, later became the key hardware for training artificial intelligence. Game technology is also used in films, architecture and virtual reality."]],
sp:["Pong","In 1972, Atari placed its first Pong arcade machine in a bar in California. Within days, the owner called to complain that it had stopped working; when an engineer checked, he found the coin box so full of quarters that it had jammed. Pong’s simple game of bouncing a dot between two paddles started a worldwide industry."],
g:[["Console","A machine designed for playing video games"],["Arcade","A place with coin-operated game machines"],["Esports","Competitive professional video gaming"],["Game engine","Software used to build video games"]],
f:["More than 3 billion people play video games.","Pong, released in 1972, launched the video game industry.","Minecraft has sold more than 300 million copies."],
fun:"The first Pong machine stopped working because its coin box was too full.",
tl:"Ang video game ay larong elektroniko na nilalaro sa kompyuter, console o telepono. Mahigit 3 bilyong tao ang naglalaro nito, at isa itong napakalaking industriya at maging isport na tinatawag na esports."});

ADDART({id:"cybersecurity",t:"Cybersecurity",c:"Technology",i:"🔐",w:"Computer security",r:["comp","chip","crypto","ai","smartphone"],
p:[["Protecting the digital world","Cybersecurity is the protection of computers, networks, phones and data from attacks and theft."],["Many threats","Threats include viruses, scams, hackers, ransomware and data breaches."],["Everyone’s job","Strong passwords, software updates and caution online help keep people safe."]],
l:"Cybersecurity is the practice of protecting computer systems, networks, devices and data from unauthorized access, damage and theft. As more of life moves online, from banking and shopping to hospitals and power grids, cybersecurity has become vital for individuals, businesses and governments. Attackers range from individual criminals to organized gangs and state-backed groups. Defenders use technology, such as encryption and firewalls, as well as education, to stay one step ahead.",
s:[["Malware","Malware is malicious software designed to harm computers or steal information. Viruses spread by attaching to files; worms spread by themselves across networks; spyware secretly collects information; and trojans disguise themselves as useful programs. The Morris worm of 1988 was one of the first to spread widely across the early internet."],["Phishing and scams","Phishing tricks people into revealing passwords, bank details or personal information through fake emails, messages or websites that look real. Scammers may pretend to be a bank, a delivery company or a friend. Phishing is one of the most common ways attacks begin, because it targets people rather than machines."],["Ransomware","Ransomware locks or encrypts a victim’s files and demands payment to unlock them. Attacks have hit hospitals, schools, cities and companies. In 2017 the WannaCry ransomware spread to hundreds of thousands of computers in about 150 countries, disrupting hospitals in Britain and businesses worldwide."],["Encryption","Encryption scrambles data so that only someone with the right key can read it. It protects online banking, shopping, messages and passwords. Websites using HTTPS, shown by a padlock in the browser, encrypt the information sent between a user and the site."]],
k:[["Main threats","Malware, phishing, ransomware, data breaches"],["Key defenses","Encryption, updates, strong passwords, multi-factor authentication"],["Early internet worm","Morris worm, 1988"],["Major ransomware attack","WannaCry, 2017"],["Cybersecurity Awareness Month","October in many countries"]],
d:[["1971","Creeper, an early self-copying program, spreads on ARPANET"],["1988","The Morris worm infects thousands of computers"],["2000","The ILOVEYOU worm spreads worldwide by email"],["2010","Stuxnet, a cyberweapon targeting nuclear equipment, is discovered"],["2017","WannaCry ransomware attack"],["2024","A faulty security update causes worldwide computer outages"]],
s2:[["Staying safe online","Experts recommend using long, unique passwords or a password manager, turning on multi-factor authentication, which asks for a second proof of identity such as a code on your phone, keeping software updated, being cautious with links and attachments and backing up important files."],["Hackers","The word hacker originally meant a skilled programmer. Today it often refers to someone who breaks into computer systems. Ethical hackers, or white hats, are hired to find weaknesses before criminals do, and companies offer bug bounties, rewards for reporting security flaws."],["Attacks on infrastructure","Cyberattacks can target power grids, water systems, hospitals and transport. In 2010 Stuxnet, malicious code widely believed to have been created by state agencies, damaged centrifuges in Iran’s nuclear program. Protecting critical infrastructure is a major concern for governments."],["Data breaches","A data breach happens when attackers steal large amounts of personal information, such as names, passwords, card numbers or health records, from companies or governments. Some breaches have exposed the data of hundreds of millions or even billions of accounts. Laws in many countries require organizations to protect data and report breaches."],["AI and cybersecurity","Artificial intelligence is used on both sides: attackers use it to write convincing scam messages, create deepfake voices and find weaknesses, while defenders use it to detect unusual activity and respond to threats faster. Cybersecurity experts expect AI to change the field greatly."],["Careers in cybersecurity","There is a worldwide shortage of cybersecurity professionals. Jobs include security analysts, who watch for attacks, penetration testers, who try to break in to find weaknesses, cryptographers and digital forensics investigators. Many countries run competitions to find and train young talent."]],
sp:["The ILOVEYOU worm","In May 2000, millions of people received an email with the subject line ILOVEYOU and an attachment called LOVE-LETTER-FOR-YOU. Opening it released a worm that overwrote files and emailed itself to everyone in the victim’s address book. It spread around the world in hours, infecting tens of millions of computers and causing billions of dollars in damage. It was traced to a student in the Philippines; at the time the country had no law against it, and soon passed one."],
g:[["Malware","Software designed to harm or exploit computers"],["Phishing","Tricking people into revealing information through fake messages"],["Ransomware","Malware that locks files and demands payment"],["Multi-factor authentication","Logging in with more than one proof of identity"]],
f:["Phishing is one of the most common ways cyberattacks begin.","WannaCry hit computers in about 150 countries in 2017.","Encryption protects online banking and messages."],
fun:"The ILOVEYOU worm of 2000 led the Philippines to pass a law against computer crime.",
tl:"Ang cybersecurity ay pagprotekta sa mga kompyuter, network at datos laban sa pag-atake at pagnanakaw. Kabilang sa mga banta ang virus, phishing at ransomware, at nakatutulong ang matitibay na password at pag-update ng software."});

;
/* data/arts3.js */
// Arts and Culture articles, part 3: ukiyo-e, poetry, the orchestra, Norse mythology and puppetry.
ADDART({id:"ukiyoe",t:"Ukiyo-e: Japanese Woodblock Prints",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🗻",w:"Ukiyo-e",r:["japan","impress","vangogh","haikuart"],
p:[["Pictures of the floating world","Ukiyo-e means pictures of the floating world, a style of Japanese art popular from the 1600s to the 1800s."],["Printed from wood","Artists designed images that were carved into wooden blocks and printed in many colors."],["The Great Wave","Hokusai’s Under the Wave off Kanagawa is one of the most famous artworks in the world."]],
l:"Ukiyo-e is a genre of Japanese art that flourished during the Edo period, from the 17th to the 19th centuries. Its name means pictures of the floating world, referring to the pleasures of city life in Edo, now Tokyo: theater, entertainment and fashion. Most ukiyo-e were woodblock prints, made by teams of designers, carvers and printers and sold cheaply to ordinary townspeople. Artists such as Hokusai and Hiroshige created famous landscapes, and ukiyo-e later inspired European painters such as Monet and Van Gogh.",
s:[["The floating world","During the Edo period, Japan was peaceful and cities grew. Merchants and townspeople enjoyed kabuki theater, sumo, teahouses and festivals. Floating world described this lively, fleeting world of pleasure. Early ukiyo-e showed actors, beautiful women, wrestlers and scenes from popular stories."],["How prints were made","A publisher organized the work. An artist drew the design in ink on thin paper. A carver pasted it on a block of cherry wood and carved away everything except the lines. Separate blocks were carved for each color. Printers brushed ink onto the blocks and pressed paper onto them by hand, one color at a time, lining them up with precise marks."],["Hokusai","Katsushika Hokusai, who worked for about 70 years and called himself an old man mad about painting, created the series Thirty-six Views of Mount Fuji around 1830 to 1832. It includes Under the Wave off Kanagawa, often called The Great Wave, showing a huge wave curling over boats with Mount Fuji in the distance. He also made thousands of sketches, published as Hokusai Manga."],["Hiroshige","Utagawa Hiroshige was famous for landscapes showing travelers, weather and seasons. His series The Fifty-three Stations of the Tōkaidō, from the 1830s, shows stops along the great road between Edo and Kyoto, with rain, snow, mist and moonlight. His work was hugely popular in Japan and later abroad."]],
k:[["Meaning","Pictures of the floating world"],["Period","17th–19th centuries (Edo period)"],["Main technique","Color woodblock printing"],["Famous artists","Hokusai, Hiroshige, Utamaro, Sharaku"],["Most famous work","Under the Wave off Kanagawa (c. 1831)"]],
d:[["1603","The Edo period begins"],["1670s","Hishikawa Moronobu helps establish ukiyo-e prints"],["1765","Full-color brocade prints are developed"],["c. 1831","Hokusai’s Great Wave is published"],["1833–1834","Hiroshige’s Tōkaidō series appears"],["1860s–1880s","Ukiyo-e inspire Japonisme in Europe"]],
s2:[["The Great Wave around the world","The Great Wave has become one of the most recognized images in the world, reproduced on posters, clothes, stamps and even emoji. Thousands of copies were printed in Hokusai’s time; surviving original prints are held by museums such as the British Museum, the Metropolitan Museum of Art and the Tokyo National Museum."],["Inspiring Europe","After Japan opened to foreign trade in the 1850s, ukiyo-e prints reached Europe, sometimes used as wrapping paper for ceramics. Artists such as Claude Monet, Edgar Degas, Mary Cassatt and Vincent van Gogh collected them and borrowed their bold outlines, flat colors and unusual viewpoints. The craze for Japanese art was called Japonisme."],["Van Gogh’s copies","Vincent van Gogh owned hundreds of Japanese prints and painted copies of two Hiroshige prints, including Plum Park in Kameido. He wrote that all his work was in some way based on Japanese art, and his bright colors and strong outlines show its influence."],["Kabuki and beauties","Many ukiyo-e showed kabuki actors in dramatic poses, like posters of celebrities today. Kitagawa Utamaro was famous for portraits of beautiful women, and the mysterious Tōshūsai Sharaku produced striking actor portraits for less than a year in 1794 to 1795 before vanishing from the record."],["Decline and revival","In the late 1800s, photography and modern printing reduced demand for woodblock prints. In the 1900s, new movements revived the art: shin-hanga, new prints, continued traditional methods for collectors, while sōsaku-hanga, creative prints, had artists carve and print their own designs."],["Ukiyo-e and manga","Some see ukiyo-e as an ancestor of modern manga and anime, with their bold lines, expressive faces and popular subjects. The word manga itself was used for Hokusai’s sketchbooks, though it meant something like random sketches."]],
sp:["The Great Wave","Under the Wave off Kanagawa shows three boats of rowers bending beneath a towering wave whose foam curls like claws, while tiny Mount Fuji sits calmly in the distance. Hokusai made it when he was about 70. He used Prussian blue, a new imported pigment, which gave the print its striking color. It shows the power of nature and the fragility of human life."],
g:[["Ukiyo-e","Japanese art of the floating world, often woodblock prints"],["Woodblock print","A print made from a carved block of wood"],["Edo period","Japan’s era from 1603 to 1868, ruled by Tokugawa shoguns"],["Japonisme","European fascination with Japanese art in the 1800s"]],
f:["Ukiyo-e means pictures of the floating world.","Hokusai made The Great Wave when he was about 70.","Van Gogh painted copies of prints by Hiroshige."],
fun:"Some ukiyo-e prints first reached Europe as wrapping paper for porcelain.",
tl:"Ang ukiyo-e ay sining ng Hapon na nangangahulugang larawan ng lumulutang na mundo. Karaniwang inililimbag ito mula sa inukit na kahoy. Ang Great Wave ni Hokusai ay isa sa pinakatanyag na likhang-sining sa mundo."});

ADDART({id:"poetry",t:"Poetry",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"✒️",w:"Poetry",r:["haikuart","homer","shakes","balagtas","phlit"],
p:[["The art of words","Poetry uses the sound, rhythm and meaning of words to express feelings, ideas and stories."],["Older than writing","Poems were spoken and sung long before writing existed, passed down by memory."],["Many forms","Poems can rhyme or not, be long epics or short haiku, and follow strict patterns or none at all."]],
l:"Poetry is a form of literature that uses the beauty and rhythm of language, its sounds, images and patterns, to create meaning and emotion. Every culture has poetry, from ancient epics and religious hymns to songs, nursery rhymes and modern free verse. Poems may tell stories, express love or grief, celebrate nature, protest injustice or play with language. Poetry is often meant to be heard aloud, and many of the oldest poems were passed down orally for generations before being written.",
s:[["Sound and rhythm","Poets choose words for their sound as well as their meaning. Rhythm, or meter, is the pattern of stressed and unstressed syllables. Rhyme repeats sounds at the ends of lines. Alliteration repeats beginning sounds, as in Peter Piper picked a peck. These patterns make poems musical and memorable."],["Images and figures of speech","Poems paint pictures in the mind with imagery. Similes compare things using like or as, as in my love is like a red, red rose. Metaphors say one thing is another. Personification gives human qualities to objects or ideas. These techniques help readers see the world in new ways."],["Forms","Some poems follow strict forms. A sonnet has 14 lines with a set rhyme scheme. A haiku, from Japan, traditionally has three phrases of 5, 7 and 5 syllables. A ghazal, from Arabic and Persian poetry, has linked couplets. Free verse, common since the late 1800s, has no fixed rhyme or meter."],["Epic poetry","Epic poems are long stories of heroes and great deeds. The Epic of Gilgamesh from Mesopotamia is one of the oldest known works of literature. Homer’s Iliad and Odyssey, India’s Mahabharata and Ramayana, the Persian Shahnameh and West Africa’s Epic of Sundiata are among the world’s great epics."]],
k:[["Oldest major poem","Epic of Gilgamesh, Mesopotamia"],["Common forms","Sonnet, haiku, ghazal, ballad, free verse"],["Key techniques","Rhythm, rhyme, imagery, metaphor"],["World Poetry Day","21 March"],["Longest epic","Mahabharata, about 100,000 couplets"]],
d:[["c. 2100 BCE","Earliest versions of the Gilgamesh poems"],["c. 8th century BCE","Homer’s epics take shape"],["c. 1000 CE","Ferdowsi completes the Shahnameh"],["1300s","Dante completes the Divine Comedy"],["1838","Francisco Balagtas publishes Florante at Laura"],["1999","UNESCO declares 21 March World Poetry Day"]],
s2:[["Poets around the world","Great poets have written in every language: Li Bai and Du Fu in China; Rumi and Hafez in Persia; Kālidāsa and Kabir in India; Dante in Italy; Shakespeare in England; Pablo Neruda in Chile; Wisława Szymborska in Poland; and Rabindranath Tagore in Bengal. Many poems are read in translation worldwide."],["Poetry and song","Poetry and music have always been close. Ancient poems were often sung to music, and song lyrics are a kind of poetry. In 2016 the songwriter Bob Dylan won the Nobel Prize in Literature, sparking debate about whether songs count as literature."],["Philippine poetry","The Philippines has a rich poetic tradition, from oral forms such as the bugtong, riddles, and the epic Hinilawod to written works. Francisco Balagtas’s Florante at Laura, published in 1838, is a classic of Tagalog literature, and the poetic debate called balagtasan is named after him."],["Poetry in protest","Poems have often been used to protest injustice and inspire change. During the American civil rights movement, Langston Hughes and Maya Angelou wrote powerful poems about freedom and dignity. Poets in many countries have been jailed for their words, and their poems shared secretly."],["Spoken word and slam","Spoken word poetry is written to be performed aloud, often with strong rhythm and emotion. Poetry slams are competitions in which poets perform and audiences score them. Slams began in Chicago in the 1980s and spread worldwide, attracting many young poets."],["Why read poetry?","Poetry helps people express feelings that are hard to put into words, at moments such as weddings, funerals and celebrations. Reading and memorizing poems builds vocabulary and an ear for language, and writing poetry encourages creativity and careful observation."]],
sp:["The oldest poem","The Epic of Gilgamesh tells of Gilgamesh, a king of Uruk in Mesopotamia, who searches for immortality after the death of his friend Enkidu. Early versions were written on clay tablets in cuneiform around 4,000 years ago. The best-known version was found in the library of the Assyrian king Ashurbanipal at Nineveh. It includes a flood story similar to the later story of Noah."],
g:[["Meter","The rhythmic pattern of a line of poetry"],["Rhyme","Repetition of similar sounds at the ends of words"],["Metaphor","Describing something as if it were something else"],["Epic","A long poem about heroes and great deeds"]],
f:["The Epic of Gilgamesh is one of the oldest known poems.","A sonnet has 14 lines.","World Poetry Day is celebrated on 21 March."],
fun:"The Mahabharata, an Indian epic, is about ten times longer than the Iliad and the Odyssey combined.",
tl:"Ang tula ay anyo ng panitikan na gumagamit ng tunog, ritmo at kahulugan ng mga salita upang magpahayag ng damdamin at ideya. May tula sa bawat kultura, mula sa sinaunang epiko hanggang sa Florante at Laura ni Balagtas."});

ADDART({id:"orchestra",t:"The Orchestra",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🎻",w:"Orchestra",r:["mozart","musicw","opera","guitar"],
p:[["Many players together","An orchestra is a large group of musicians playing instruments together, led by a conductor."],["Four families","Orchestral instruments are grouped into strings, woodwinds, brass and percussion."],["Symphonies and more","Orchestras perform symphonies, concertos, opera, ballet and film music."]],
l:"An orchestra is a large ensemble of musicians playing string, woodwind, brass and percussion instruments, usually directed by a conductor. A full symphony orchestra may have 70 to 100 or more players. The modern orchestra developed in Europe in the 1600s and 1700s and grew larger in the 1800s, performing works by composers such as Haydn, Mozart, Beethoven, Tchaikovsky and Mahler. Today orchestras play classical music, opera, ballet and film and video game scores, and youth and community orchestras flourish worldwide.",
s:[["Strings","The string section, the largest, includes violins, violas, cellos and double basses. Players draw a bow across the strings or pluck them. Violins play the highest notes and often carry the melody; double basses provide the deep foundation. The harp is also part of the orchestra."],["Woodwinds","Woodwind instruments make sound when players blow across a hole or through a reed. They include flutes, piccolos, oboes, clarinets and bassoons. Though flutes are now usually made of metal, they still count as woodwinds."],["Brass and percussion","Brass instruments, such as trumpets, horns, trombones and tubas, are played by buzzing the lips into a cup-shaped mouthpiece. Percussion includes timpani, or kettledrums, cymbals, bass drums, triangles, xylophones and many others, adding rhythm, color and excitement."],["The conductor","The conductor stands in front of the orchestra, keeping the beat with a baton or hands, cueing entries and shaping the music’s speed, loudness and feeling. Before a concert, the conductor rehearses with the orchestra to decide how a piece will be played."]],
k:[["Size","Usually 70–100 or more players"],["Sections","Strings, woodwinds, brass, percussion"],["Largest section","Strings"],["Leader of the violins","Concertmaster"],["Tuning note","A, usually played by the oboe"]],
d:[["1607","Monteverdi’s opera L’Orfeo uses an early orchestra"],["1700s","Haydn and Mozart develop the classical symphony"],["1824","Beethoven’s Ninth Symphony premieres"],["1842","Vienna Philharmonic and New York Philharmonic are founded"],["1975","El Sistema youth orchestra program begins in Venezuela"],["1999","West–Eastern Divan Orchestra is founded"]],
s2:[["Seating","Orchestras are usually arranged in a semicircle facing the conductor, with strings at the front, woodwinds behind them, brass further back and percussion at the very back. This helps balance the sound, since brass and percussion are loud while strings are softer."],["Tuning up","Before a concert, the oboe plays the note A, and the other instruments tune to it. The oboe is used because its clear, steady sound is easy to hear. The concertmaster, the leading violinist, then leads the tuning of the strings."],["Great symphonies","A symphony is a large work for orchestra, usually in four movements. Famous examples include Mozart’s Symphony No. 40, Beethoven’s Fifth, with its famous opening of four notes, Beethoven’s Ninth, with its choral Ode to Joy, Dvořák’s New World Symphony and Tchaikovsky’s Pathétique."],["Orchestras around the world","Famous orchestras include the Vienna and Berlin Philharmonics, the London Symphony Orchestra, the New York Philharmonic and the Royal Concertgebouw Orchestra. Orchestras such as the NHK Symphony in Japan, the Shanghai Symphony and the Philippine Philharmonic Orchestra show how orchestral music has spread worldwide."],["El Sistema","In 1975 the Venezuelan musician José Antonio Abreu founded El Sistema, a program that gives free music education and orchestra places to children, many from poor neighborhoods. It has reached hundreds of thousands of young people and inspired similar programs in more than 60 countries. Its most famous graduate is the conductor Gustavo Dudamel."],["Orchestras and film","Many film and video game soundtracks are recorded by orchestras. Composers such as John Williams, who wrote the music for Star Wars, Jaws and Harry Potter films, Hans Zimmer and Joe Hisaishi, who scored many Studio Ghibli films, have brought orchestral music to huge audiences."]],
sp:["Beethoven’s Ninth","Beethoven’s Ninth Symphony, first performed in Vienna in 1824, was the first major symphony to include singers, who perform the poem Ode to Joy in its final movement. By then Beethoven was almost completely deaf; at the premiere, he had to be turned around to see the audience applauding. The Ode to Joy melody is now the anthem of the European Union."],
g:[["Conductor","The person who directs an orchestra"],["Symphony","A large musical work for orchestra"],["Concertmaster","The leading violinist of an orchestra"],["Ensemble","A group of musicians performing together"]],
f:["The oboe plays the note that the orchestra tunes to.","Strings are the largest section of the orchestra.","Beethoven was almost deaf when his Ninth Symphony premiered."],
fun:"The melody of Beethoven’s Ode to Joy is the anthem of the European Union.",
tl:"Ang orkestra ay malaking grupo ng mga musikero na tumutugtog nang sabay, pinamumunuan ng konduktor. Hinahati ang mga instrumento sa strings, woodwinds, brass at percussion."});

ADDART({id:"norsemyth",t:"Norse Mythology",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"⚡",w:"Norse mythology",r:["viking","gmyth","fairy","iceland"],
p:[["Gods of the North","Norse mythology is the collection of myths of the ancient Scandinavian peoples, including the Vikings."],["Odin, Thor and Loki","Its gods include Odin, the wise chief god, Thor, god of thunder, and Loki, the trickster."],["The end of the world","The myths tell of Ragnarök, a great battle in which the world is destroyed and reborn."]],
l:"Norse mythology is the body of myths and legends of the Norse, the peoples of Scandinavia in the Viking Age and earlier. It tells of gods such as Odin, Thor, Freyja and Loki, giants, elves and dwarves, and nine worlds connected by a giant ash tree, Yggdrasil. Much of what we know comes from medieval Icelandic texts, especially the Poetic Edda and the Prose Edda, written down after Scandinavia had become Christian. Norse myths have deeply influenced literature, films, comics and the names of the days of the week in English.",
s:[["The gods","The main gods, the Æsir, lived in Asgard. Odin, the all-father, gave up an eye to drink from a well of wisdom and learned the runes by hanging for nine nights on Yggdrasil. Thor, his son, protected gods and humans with his hammer Mjölnir. Frigg was Odin’s wife, and Freyja, of another group of gods called the Vanir, was goddess of love, beauty and war."],["Nine worlds","The Norse imagined nine worlds linked by Yggdrasil, the world tree. They included Asgard, home of the gods; Midgard, the world of humans, linked to Asgard by a rainbow bridge called Bifröst; Jötunheim, land of the giants; and Hel, the realm of the dead. Valhalla, Odin’s hall in Asgard, welcomed warriors who died bravely."],["Loki the trickster","Loki, a shape-shifter of giant birth, lived among the gods. He sometimes helped them with clever tricks and sometimes caused great harm. He arranged the death of Baldr, Odin’s beloved son, and was punished by being bound beneath a dripping serpent. At Ragnarök, he fights against the gods."],["Ragnarök","The myths foretell Ragnarök, the doom of the gods. After three long winters, monsters break free: the wolf Fenrir swallows Odin, and Thor kills the great serpent Jörmungandr but dies from its poison. Fire engulfs the world, which sinks into the sea. Then a new, green world rises, and a few gods and two humans survive to begin again."]],
k:[["Main sources","Poetic Edda, Prose Edda (13th century, Iceland)"],["Chief god","Odin"],["Thunder god","Thor, with the hammer Mjölnir"],["World tree","Yggdrasil"],["End of the world","Ragnarök"]],
d:[["c. 800–1050","The Viking Age"],["c. 1000","Iceland adopts Christianity"],["c. 1220","Snorri Sturluson writes the Prose Edda"],["c. 1270","The Codex Regius, with the Poetic Edda, is written"],["1876","Wagner’s Ring cycle, based partly on Norse myths, premieres"],["1962","Marvel Comics introduces its version of Thor"]],
s2:[["The Eddas","The Poetic Edda is a collection of anonymous poems about gods and heroes, preserved mainly in a manuscript called the Codex Regius from about 1270. The Prose Edda was written around 1220 by the Icelandic scholar and politician Snorri Sturluson as a guide to the myths for poets. Without them, much of Norse mythology would have been lost."],["Days of the week","Several English days of the week are named after Norse or related Germanic gods. Tuesday is named after Tyr, god of war and law; Wednesday after Woden, the English form of Odin; Thursday after Thor; and Friday probably after Frigg, Odin’s wife."],["Runes","The Norse wrote with runes, an alphabet of angular letters suited for carving on wood, stone and bone. In myth, Odin won the knowledge of runes through his sacrifice on Yggdrasil. Rune stones raised in memory of the dead still stand across Scandinavia."],["Heroes and sagas","Besides the gods, Norse legends tell of heroes. The Völsunga saga tells of Sigurd, who slays the dragon Fáfnir and wins a cursed treasure of gold. These stories inspired Richard Wagner’s operas and J. R. R. Tolkien, a professor of Old Norse and Old English, whose dwarves in The Hobbit take their names from the Poetic Edda."],["Norse myths today","Norse gods appear in many books, films, comics and games. Marvel’s Thor and Loki have become famous worldwide through films, though they differ greatly from the original myths. Writers such as Neil Gaiman have retold the myths for modern readers."],["Myths and belief","For the Norse, these stories were part of a living religion with rituals, sacrifices and festivals. After Christianity spread through Scandinavia between about 1000 and 1100, the old beliefs faded, though they survived in stories, place names and folklore. Today some people in Iceland and elsewhere practice a revived form of the old religion."]],
sp:["Odin’s ravens and Thor’s goats","Odin had two ravens, Huginn and Muninn, meaning thought and memory, who flew over the world each day and returned to tell him the news. Thor rode in a chariot pulled by two goats, Tanngrisnir and Tanngnjóstr. According to one myth, Thor could eat the goats for dinner and bring them back to life the next morning with his hammer, as long as their bones were kept whole."],
g:[["Æsir","The main family of Norse gods"],["Yggdrasil","The giant tree connecting the nine worlds"],["Ragnarök","The prophesied end and rebirth of the world"],["Rune","A letter of the ancient Germanic alphabet"]],
f:["Thursday is named after Thor.","Most Norse myths survive in texts from medieval Iceland.","Tolkien took the names of his dwarves from the Poetic Edda."],
fun:"Thursday is named after Thor, the Norse god of thunder.",
tl:"Ang mitolohiyang Norse ay mga alamat ng sinaunang Scandinavia, kabilang ang mga Viking. Kabilang dito ang mga diyos na sina Odin, Thor at Loki, at ang Ragnarök, ang wakas at muling pagsilang ng mundo."});

ADDART({id:"puppetry",t:"Puppetry",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🎎",w:"Puppetry",r:["theatre","anim","indonesiacountry","tv"],
p:[["Bringing objects to life","Puppetry is the art of bringing figures to life for performance, using hands, strings, rods or shadows."],["An ancient art","Puppet theater has been performed for thousands of years in Asia, Europe, Africa and the Americas."],["Many kinds","Puppets include hand puppets, marionettes, rod puppets, shadow puppets and giant parade puppets."]],
l:"Puppetry is a form of theater in which performers, called puppeteers, manipulate figures called puppets to tell stories. Puppets may be moved by hand, strings, rods or other mechanisms, or appear as shadows cast on a screen. Puppetry is one of the oldest forms of theater and is found in cultures around the world, from Indonesia’s wayang shadow plays and Japan’s bunraku to Europe’s Punch and Judy and the Muppets on television. It entertains children and adults and often carries religious, moral or political messages.",
s:[["Hand and glove puppets","Glove or hand puppets fit over the puppeteer’s hand, with fingers moving the head and arms. They are simple to make and are often used in street shows and classrooms. Punch and Judy, a comic glove-puppet show from Britain descended from the Italian character Pulcinella, has been performed since the 1600s."],["Marionettes","Marionettes are puppets moved from above by strings or wires attached to a control bar. Skilled puppeteers can make them walk, dance and gesture realistically. Marionette traditions are strong in Czechia, Italy, Myanmar and many other places. Salzburg’s Marionette Theater in Austria performs whole operas."],["Rod and shadow puppets","Rod puppets are moved by sticks attached to the head, hands or body. Shadow puppets are flat figures, often made of leather, held between a light and a screen so the audience sees their shadows. Shadow puppetry has ancient traditions in China, India, Indonesia, Malaysia, Türkiye and Greece."],["Puppets on screen","In the 20th century, puppets became stars of television and film. Jim Henson’s Muppets, including Kermit the Frog, appeared on Sesame Street from 1969 and The Muppet Show from 1976. Puppets are also used in films, stop-motion animation and theater productions such as The Lion King and War Horse."]],
k:[["Main types","Hand, marionette, rod, shadow, giant puppets"],["Famous traditions","Wayang kulit, bunraku, Punch and Judy, water puppetry"],["UNESCO heritage","Wayang (2003), bunraku (2008) and others"],["Famous puppeteer","Jim Henson"],["Sesame Street began","1969"]],
d:[["c. 400 BCE","Greek writers mention string puppets"],["c. 1000s","Water puppetry develops in Vietnam"],["1600s","Punch and Judy shows begin in England"],["1684","Bunraku theater develops in Osaka, Japan"],["1969","Sesame Street premieres"],["2003","UNESCO recognizes Indonesian wayang puppet theater"]],
s2:[["Wayang kulit","In Indonesia, especially Java and Bali, wayang kulit shadow puppet plays are performed with intricately carved leather puppets. A single master puppeteer, the dalang, voices all the characters and moves the puppets through all-night performances, accompanied by a gamelan orchestra. Stories often come from the Indian epics, the Ramayana and the Mahabharata."],["Bunraku","Bunraku is Japan’s traditional puppet theater, developed in Osaka in the 1600s. Large puppets, about half human size, are each operated by three puppeteers in full view of the audience: a master who moves the head and right hand, and two assistants dressed in black. A chanter tells the story, accompanied by a three-stringed shamisen."],["Vietnamese water puppetry","Water puppetry, mũa rối nước, began in the rice paddies of northern Vietnam about a thousand years ago. Puppeteers stand waist-deep in water behind a screen, moving wooden puppets on long poles hidden beneath the surface, so the puppets seem to glide and dance on the water."],["Giant puppets","Some puppets are enormous. Giant puppets are paraded in festivals in Spain, Belgium, France and Brazil. Since 2010, the British company Royal de Luxe and others have staged street shows with puppets several stories tall. Little Amal, a 3.5-meter puppet of a young refugee girl, walked thousands of kilometers across Europe in 2021 to draw attention to child refugees."],["Puppets for learning and healing","Teachers use puppets to help children learn languages and tell stories, and therapists use them to help children talk about feelings and difficult experiences. Puppets can say things that people might find hard to say themselves."],["Puppetry in the Philippines","Puppetry is part of Philippine theater too. Groups such as Teatrong Mulat ng Pilipinas, founded by Amelia Lapeña-Bonifacio in 1977, have used puppets to tell Filipino and Asian stories to children, drawing on traditions from across Asia."]],
sp:["Little Amal","Little Amal is a giant puppet, 3.5 meters tall, of a nine-year-old Syrian refugee girl, created by the Handspring Puppet Company of South Africa, makers of the horse puppets in War Horse. In 2021 she walked about 8,000 kilometers from the border of Syria and Türkiye across Europe to the United Kingdom, greeted by communities along the way, to raise awareness of refugee children. She has since traveled to other countries, including the United States."],
g:[["Puppeteer","A person who operates puppets"],["Marionette","A puppet moved by strings from above"],["Shadow puppet","A flat puppet seen as a shadow on a screen"],["Dalang","The master puppeteer of Indonesian wayang"]],
f:["Indonesian wayang is recognized by UNESCO.","Bunraku puppets are each operated by three puppeteers.","Vietnamese water puppets perform on a pool of water."],
fun:"In Vietnamese water puppetry, puppeteers stand waist-deep in water behind a screen.",
tl:"Ang puppetry ay sining ng pagbibigay-buhay sa mga papet para sa pagtatanghal. Matagal na itong ginagawa sa iba't ibang bansa, gaya ng wayang kulit ng Indonesia, bunraku ng Hapon at water puppetry ng Vietnam."});

;
/* data/earth3.js */
// Planet Earth articles, part 3: geysers, lakes, storms, grasslands and Kilimanjaro.
ADDART({id:"geysers",t:"Geysers and Hot Springs",c:"Geography",i:"♨️",w:"Geyser",r:["volc","iceland","taal","phpower"],
p:[["Fountains of hot water","A geyser is a hot spring that shoots water and steam into the air from time to time."],["Heated underground","Geysers form where water seeps down and is heated by hot rock deep below the surface."],["Rare wonders","There are only about 1,000 active geysers on Earth, and about half are in Yellowstone National Park."]],
l:"A geyser is a spring that erupts from time to time, throwing hot water and steam into the air. Geysers form only where three things come together: plenty of underground water, a strong source of heat from volcanic rock, and a special system of narrow cracks and chambers that lets pressure build up. Fewer than a thousand geysers exist worldwide, mostly in Yellowstone in the United States, Kamchatka in Russia, El Tatio in Chile, New Zealand and Iceland, which gave the word geyser to the world.",
s:[["How a geyser works","Rainwater and snowmelt trickle down through cracks into hot rock. Deep down, the weight of water above raises the boiling point, so the water becomes superheated, hotter than 100°C without boiling. When some of it finally turns to steam, it pushes water out of the vent, lowering the pressure. The rest flashes into steam all at once, blasting a column of water skyward. Then the chambers slowly refill and the cycle begins again."],["Hot springs, mud pots and fumaroles","Geysers are one kind of hydrothermal feature. Hot springs are pools of heated water that do not erupt. Mud pots are bubbling pools of hot, acidic mud. Fumaroles are vents that release steam and gases. Colorful bacteria and other microbes that love heat live in many hot springs, painting them bright orange, yellow and green."],["Yellowstone","Yellowstone National Park in the United States, the world’s first national park, founded in 1872, sits on top of a huge volcanic hotspot and holds about half of the world’s geysers. Old Faithful erupts roughly every 60 to 110 minutes, reaching heights of 30 to 55 meters. Steamboat Geyser, the tallest active geyser, can shoot water more than 90 meters high, but at unpredictable intervals."],["Iceland’s Geysir","The word geyser comes from Geysir, a famous spring in southwestern Iceland, from an Icelandic word meaning to gush. Geysir itself now erupts only rarely, but its neighbor Strokkur erupts every few minutes, sending water 15 to 20 meters or more into the air, to the delight of visitors."]],
k:[["Active geysers worldwide","About 1,000"],["Share in Yellowstone","About half"],["Tallest active geyser","Steamboat, Yellowstone"],["Name from","Geysir, Iceland"],["Largest ever recorded","Waimangu, New Zealand (1900–1904)"]],
d:[["1294","Geysir in Iceland becomes active after earthquakes"],["1870","Old Faithful is named by an expedition"],["1872","Yellowstone becomes the first national park"],["1900–1904","Waimangu Geyser erupts up to about 460 meters"],["1941","Russia’s Valley of Geysers in Kamchatka is discovered"],["2005","Cassini spacecraft sees geysers on Saturn’s moon Enceladus"]],
s2:[["Geyser fields of the world","Besides Yellowstone, major geyser fields include the Valley of Geysers on Russia’s Kamchatka Peninsula; El Tatio in the Andes of Chile, one of the highest geyser fields at over 4,000 meters; and the Taupō Volcanic Zone of New Zealand, where Pōhutu Geyser in Rotorua erupts many times a day."],["The biggest geyser ever","Waimangu Geyser in New Zealand, active from 1900 to 1904, was the most powerful geyser ever recorded. Its eruptions of black, muddy water and rocks reached up to about 460 meters high. It stopped after a landslide changed the local water table."],["Fragile features","Geysers are easily damaged. In New Zealand and Iceland, many geysers stopped erupting after people drilled wells for geothermal power nearby. Visitors throwing coins or rubbish into vents have also clogged geysers, which is why paths in geyser parks keep people at a safe distance."],["Heat from the Earth","The same underground heat that powers geysers can be used to make electricity and warm homes. Iceland gets most of its heating from geothermal energy. The Philippines is one of the world’s top producers of geothermal electricity, with power plants at places such as Tiwi, Makban and Leyte."],["Geysers in space","Geysers are not unique to Earth. Saturn’s moon Enceladus sprays plumes of water ice from cracks near its south pole, hinting at an ocean beneath its icy crust. In 1989 the Voyager 2 spacecraft saw dark plumes rising from Neptune’s moon Triton."],["Life in boiling water","The microbe Thermus aquaticus, discovered in a Yellowstone hot spring in the 1960s, survives in near-boiling water. An enzyme from it became essential to PCR, a technique used to copy DNA, which is used in medicine, forensic science and testing for diseases."]],
sp:["Old Faithful","Old Faithful was named in 1870 by members of the Washburn Expedition, who were impressed by how regularly it erupted. It is not the biggest geyser in Yellowstone, but it is one of the most predictable, so park rangers can forecast each eruption, usually within about ten minutes. Each eruption lasts from about one and a half to five minutes and can throw out tens of thousands of liters of boiling water."],
g:[["Geyser","A hot spring that erupts water and steam"],["Superheated","Heated above its normal boiling point without boiling"],["Fumarole","A vent releasing volcanic steam and gases"],["Geothermal","Heat from inside the Earth"]],
f:["About half of the world’s geysers are in Yellowstone.","The word geyser comes from Geysir in Iceland.","Saturn’s moon Enceladus has geysers of ice."],
fun:"An enzyme from a microbe found in a Yellowstone hot spring helps scientists copy DNA.",
tl:"Ang geyser ay bukal ng mainit na tubig na paminsan-minsang bumubuga ng tubig at singaw. Nabubuo ito kung saan pinapainit ng mainit na bato sa ilalim ng lupa ang tubig. Halos kalahati ng mga geyser sa mundo ay nasa Yellowstone."});

ADDART({id:"lakes",t:"Lakes",c:"Geography",i:"🏞️",w:"Lake",r:["taal","iceage","nile","water"],
p:[["Bodies of water on land","A lake is a large body of water surrounded by land, filling a hollow in the ground."],["Many origins","Lakes form in basins carved by glaciers, in volcanic craters, in cracks in the crust and behind dams."],["Fresh and salty","Most lakes hold fresh water, but some, such as the Dead Sea, are saltier than the ocean."]],
l:"A lake is a body of standing water that fills a basin on land, larger and deeper than a pond. Lakes hold a large share of the world’s liquid surface fresh water and provide drinking water, food, transport and homes for countless plants and animals. The Caspian Sea is the largest lake by area, Lake Baikal in Russia is the deepest and oldest, and Lake Superior in North America is the largest freshwater lake by area. Lakes are found on every continent, even beneath the ice of Antarctica.",
s:[["How lakes form","Many lakes in Canada, Finland and northern Europe filled hollows carved by Ice Age glaciers. Rift lakes, such as Baikal and Tanganyika, formed where the Earth’s crust is pulling apart. Crater lakes fill the mouths of old volcanoes. Oxbow lakes form when a river cuts off a loop of its course. People also create reservoirs by building dams."],["Fresh and salt lakes","Most lakes have rivers flowing in and out, keeping them fresh. Lakes with no outlet lose water only by evaporation, leaving salts behind, so they become salty. The Dead Sea, on the border of Jordan and Israel, is almost ten times saltier than the ocean, so people float easily in it. Its shore is the lowest place on land, about 430 meters below sea level."],["Life in lakes","Lakes support fish, insects, birds and plants. Lake Baikal has thousands of species, many found nowhere else, including the nerpa, the world’s only freshwater seal. Africa’s Lake Malawi and Lake Victoria have hundreds of species of colorful cichlid fish."],["Lakes and people","People have always settled beside lakes for water, fish and transport. Lake Titicaca, on the border of Peru and Bolivia at about 3,800 meters, is sacred in Andean culture. The Great Lakes of North America connect cities such as Chicago and Toronto by shipping routes to the Atlantic Ocean."]],
k:[["Largest by area","Caspian Sea, about 371,000 km²"],["Deepest","Lake Baikal, 1,642 m"],["Largest freshwater by area","Lake Superior"],["Lowest","Dead Sea, about 430 m below sea level"],["Largest in the Philippines","Laguna de Bay"]],
d:[["c. 25 million years ago","Lake Baikal begins to form"],["c. 12,000 years ago","Melting glaciers create many northern lakes"],["1960s","The Aral Sea begins to shrink"],["1986","A gas release from Lake Nyos, Cameroon, kills about 1,700 people"],["1996","Lake Vostok is confirmed beneath Antarctic ice"],["2005","Cassini begins to find lakes of methane on Titan"]],
s2:[["Lake Baikal","Lake Baikal in Siberia is the world’s deepest lake, at 1,642 meters, and its oldest, at about 25 to 30 million years. It holds around a fifth of the world’s unfrozen surface fresh water, more than all the North American Great Lakes combined. In winter, its surface freezes so thick that roads are built across the ice."],["The Caspian Sea","The Caspian Sea, bordered by Russia, Kazakhstan, Turkmenistan, Iran and Azerbaijan, is the world’s largest inland body of water. It is called a sea because of its size and slightly salty water, but it is surrounded by land. It is famous for sturgeon, whose eggs are sold as caviar, and for oil and gas."],["The shrinking Aral Sea","The Aral Sea in Central Asia was once the fourth-largest lake in the world. From the 1960s, the Soviet Union diverted its feeding rivers to irrigate cotton fields, and the lake shrank to a fraction of its size, leaving ships stranded in the desert. A dam built in 2005 has helped the northern part to partly recover."],["Lakes of the Philippines","Laguna de Bay, beside Metro Manila, is the largest lake in the Philippines, used for fishing and water supply. Lake Lanao in Mindanao is one of the oldest lakes in the world, according to some estimates, and home to unique fish. Taal Lake fills a volcanic caldera and contains Volcano Island, which itself has a crater lake."],["Deadly lakes","In 1986 Lake Nyos in Cameroon suddenly released a huge cloud of carbon dioxide that had built up in its deep water. The heavy gas flowed into valleys and suffocated about 1,700 people and thousands of animals. Pipes now release the gas slowly to prevent another disaster."],["Lakes beyond Earth","Saturn’s largest moon, Titan, is the only other place known to have stable liquid lakes on its surface, but they are filled with liquid methane and ethane, not water. Lakes once existed on Mars too: NASA’s Perseverance rover is exploring Jezero Crater, an ancient lake bed."]],
sp:["Lake Vostok","Lake Vostok lies beneath almost 4 kilometers of Antarctic ice, sealed off from the air for perhaps millions of years. It is one of the largest of hundreds of lakes hidden under the Antarctic ice sheet. Russian scientists drilled down to its surface in 2012. Researchers hope to learn whether microbes survive in this dark, cold world, which may resemble the hidden oceans on some moons."],
g:[["Basin","A hollow in the land that can hold water"],["Rift lake","A lake formed where the crust is pulling apart"],["Reservoir","An artificial lake made by a dam"],["Oxbow lake","A curved lake left by a river"]],
f:["Lake Baikal is the deepest lake in the world.","The Caspian Sea is the largest lake by area.","Titan has lakes of liquid methane."],
fun:"Canada has more lakes than any other country in the world.",
tl:"Ang lawa ay malaking anyong-tubig na napapaligiran ng lupa. Ang Caspian Sea ang pinakamalaking lawa, at ang Lake Baikal ang pinakamalalim. Ang Laguna de Bay ang pinakamalaking lawa sa Pilipinas."});

ADDART({id:"storms",t:"Hurricanes, Typhoons and Tornadoes",c:"Geography",i:"🌀",w:"Tropical cyclone",r:["phtyph","climate","climchange","tsunami"],
p:[["Powerful storms","Hurricanes, typhoons and cyclones are giant spinning storms that form over warm oceans."],["One storm, three names","They are called hurricanes in the Atlantic, typhoons in the western Pacific and cyclones in the Indian Ocean."],["Twisting winds","Tornadoes are smaller but violent spinning columns of air reaching down from thunderstorms."]],
l:"Storms are disturbances of the atmosphere bringing strong winds and often heavy rain, thunder and lightning. The most powerful are tropical cyclones: huge spinning storms hundreds of kilometers wide that form over warm tropical seas. Depending on where they occur, they are called hurricanes, typhoons or cyclones. Tornadoes are narrow, violently rotating columns of air that form under thunderstorms. Weather forecasters use satellites, radar and computer models to track storms and warn people in time to stay safe.",
s:[["How tropical cyclones form","Tropical cyclones form over oceans warmer than about 26.5°C. Warm, moist air rises, cools and forms clouds, releasing heat that fuels more rising air. Earth’s rotation makes the storm spin, counterclockwise in the Northern Hemisphere and clockwise in the Southern. At the center is a calm eye, surrounded by the eyewall, where winds are strongest."],["Dangers","Tropical cyclones bring destructive winds, flooding rain and storm surges, walls of seawater pushed onto the coast, which often cause the most deaths. Landslides and disease can follow. The 1970 Bhola cyclone in Bangladesh, one of the deadliest natural disasters, killed hundreds of thousands of people."],["Tornadoes","Tornadoes form when winds at different heights in a thunderstorm blow at different speeds and directions, creating rotation that is tilted upright by rising air. Most last only minutes but can have winds faster than 400 km/h. The United States has more tornadoes than any other country, about 1,200 a year, especially in the region called Tornado Alley."],["Thunderstorms and lightning","Thunderstorms grow in towering cumulonimbus clouds. Lightning is a giant spark of electricity, heating the air to around 30,000°C, five times hotter than the Sun’s surface; the air expands so fast it makes the boom of thunder. Lightning strikes Earth about 40 to 50 times every second."]],
k:[["Ocean temperature needed","About 26.5°C or warmer"],["Strongest category","Category 5: winds 252 km/h or more"],["Tornado scale","Enhanced Fujita, EF0–EF5"],["Most tornadoes","United States, about 1,200 a year"],["Deadliest cyclone","1970 Bhola cyclone, Bangladesh"]],
d:[["1900","Galveston hurricane kills about 8,000 people in Texas"],["1925","Tri-State Tornado in the US kills 695 people"],["1953","Atlantic hurricanes begin to receive official names"],["1970","Bhola cyclone strikes East Pakistan, now Bangladesh"],["2005","Hurricane Katrina floods New Orleans"],["2013","Typhoon Haiyan (Yolanda) devastates the central Philippines"]],
s2:[["Measuring storms","The Saffir–Simpson scale ranks hurricanes from Category 1 to 5 by wind speed; Category 5 storms have winds of at least 252 km/h. Tornadoes are rated on the Enhanced Fujita scale, from EF0 to EF5, based on the damage they cause. Typhoon warnings in the Philippines use a system of signal numbers issued by PAGASA."],["Naming storms","Giving storms names makes warnings clearer. The World Meteorological Organization keeps lists of names for each ocean region; names of very destructive storms are retired. The Philippines’ weather agency, PAGASA, also gives local names to storms entering its area of responsibility, which is why Typhoon Haiyan is known in the Philippines as Yolanda."],["Typhoon Haiyan","Typhoon Haiyan, called Yolanda in the Philippines, struck on 8 November 2013, one of the strongest tropical cyclones ever to make landfall. Its storm surge swept through Tacloban and other towns in the Visayas, killing more than 6,000 people and destroying over a million homes. It led to major improvements in Philippine storm warnings."],["Tornado records","The deadliest tornado recorded struck Bangladesh in 1989, killing about 1,300 people. In 1999, a tornado near Oklahoma City produced winds measured by radar at about 480 km/h, among the fastest ever recorded on Earth. Tornadoes also occur in Europe, Argentina, Australia and many other places."],["Storms and climate change","Scientists find that a warmer climate is making the strongest tropical cyclones more intense and wetter, and that rising seas make storm surges more dangerous. Whether the total number of storms will change is less certain. Research continues as better data is collected."],["Staying safe","Early warnings save many lives. In a cyclone, people should follow evacuation orders and move away from coasts and rivers. In a tornado, the safest place is a basement or a small room without windows on the lowest floor. During lightning, people should go indoors and avoid open fields and tall, lone trees."]],
sp:["Hurricane hunters","Some aircraft fly straight into hurricanes on purpose. Hurricane hunter crews from the US Air Force and NOAA fly through the violent eyewall into the calm eye, dropping instruments called dropsondes that measure wind, pressure and temperature as they fall. Their data helps forecasters predict a storm’s path and strength more accurately."],
g:[["Tropical cyclone","A large spinning storm formed over warm tropical seas"],["Eye","The calm center of a tropical cyclone"],["Storm surge","Seawater pushed onto land by a storm"],["Tornado","A violently rotating column of air from a thunderstorm"]],
f:["Hurricanes and typhoons are the same kind of storm.","The eye of a cyclone is calm.","Lightning is hotter than the Sun’s surface."],
fun:"Cyclones spin counterclockwise north of the equator and clockwise south of it.",
tl:"Ang bagyo ay malakas na umiikot na unos na nabubuo sa mainit na dagat. Tinatawag itong hurricane sa Atlantiko, typhoon sa kanlurang Pasipiko at cyclone sa Indian Ocean. Ang buhawi o tornado ay maliit ngunit napakalakas na umiikot na hangin."});

ADDART({id:"grasslands",t:"Grasslands and Savannas",c:"Geography",i:"🌾",w:"Grassland",r:["lion","elephant","desert","trees","rice"],
p:[["Seas of grass","Grasslands are wide open lands where grasses are the main plants and trees are few."],["Many names","They are called prairies, steppes, pampas, veld and savannas in different parts of the world."],["Great herds","Grasslands support huge herds of grazing animals, from wildebeest to bison."]],
l:"Grasslands are regions where grasses, rather than trees or shrubs, are the main plants. They cover a large share of Earth’s land, between deserts and forests, where there is too little rain for thick forests but too much for desert. Tropical grasslands with scattered trees are called savannas; temperate grasslands include the prairies of North America, the steppes of Eurasia, the pampas of South America and the veld of southern Africa. Grasslands support great herds of animals and grow much of the world’s food.",
s:[["Why grass, not trees?","Grasslands have dry seasons, frequent fires and grazing animals, all of which keep trees from taking over. Grasses survive because they grow from the base, not the tip, so they regrow quickly after being eaten or burned. Their deep, tangled roots hold soil together and store food underground."],["Savannas","Savannas are warm grasslands with scattered trees, found in Africa, South America, India and Australia. They have a wet season and a long dry season. Africa’s savannas, such as the Serengeti, are home to elephants, giraffes, zebras, lions, cheetahs and the great migrations of wildebeest."],["Temperate grasslands","Temperate grasslands have hot summers and cold winters. The Eurasian Steppe stretches thousands of kilometers from Hungary to Mongolia and China and was home to nomadic herders such as the Mongols. The North American prairies and South American pampas are now largely farmland."],["Breadbaskets of the world","Grassland soils are often deep and fertile because of centuries of decaying grass roots. Many have been plowed to grow wheat, maize and other crops, which are themselves grasses, and to graze cattle and sheep. Rice, wheat, maize, barley and sugarcane all belong to the grass family."]],
k:[["Main types","Savanna and temperate grassland"],["Regional names","Prairie, steppe, pampas, veld"],["Grass species","About 12,000"],["Famous migration","Serengeti wildebeest, about 1.5 million"],["Largest grassland","Eurasian Steppe"]],
d:[["c. 8 million years ago","Grasslands spread widely as climates dry"],["c. 4000 BCE","Horses are domesticated on the steppe"],["1206","Genghis Khan unites the steppe peoples"],["1889","Fewer than about 1,000 American bison remain"],["1930s","The Dust Bowl strikes the North American plains"],["1981","Serengeti National Park becomes a World Heritage Site"]],
s2:[["The great migration","Each year, about 1.5 million wildebeest, along with hundreds of thousands of zebras and gazelles, travel in a great loop through the Serengeti in Tanzania and the Maasai Mara in Kenya, following the rains and fresh grass. They face crocodiles at river crossings and lions, hyenas and cheetahs along the way. It is one of the largest animal migrations on Earth."],["The American bison","Tens of millions of bison once roamed the North American prairies and were central to the life of many Indigenous peoples. In the 1800s, they were slaughtered on a vast scale, leaving fewer than about 1,000 by 1889. Conservation efforts have brought them back, and hundreds of thousands now live in parks and on ranches."],["The Dust Bowl","In the 1930s, after farmers plowed up the prairie grasses of the southern Great Plains, a long drought turned the bare soil to dust. Huge dust storms swept across the land, burying farms and forcing many families to leave. The disaster led to new farming methods to protect the soil."],["Fire on the grassland","Fire is a natural part of many grasslands. Lightning starts fires that burn dry grass but leave roots unharmed, so new green shoots soon appear, attracting grazing animals. Many Indigenous peoples used careful burning to manage grasslands, a practice that is being revived today."],["Grasslands in the Philippines","Grasslands cover a significant part of the Philippines, though many formed after forests were cleared and are dominated by tough cogon grass. Natural grasslands are also found in places such as the mountain grasslands of Mount Pulag and the Bukidnon plateau in Mindanao, used for cattle ranching."],["Threats and conservation","Grasslands are among the least protected habitats in the world. Much of the original prairie and pampas has been turned into farms and cities. Overgrazing, invasive plants and climate change also threaten them. Protecting grasslands helps store carbon in the soil, conserves wildlife and prevents erosion."]],
sp:["Grass that fights back","Grasses have developed clever defenses against grazing animals. Many contain tiny bits of silica, the material in sand and glass, which wear down the teeth of animals that eat them. In turn, horses, cows and other grazers evolved tall, tough teeth that keep growing or wear slowly. This long contest between grasses and grazers has shaped both over millions of years."],
g:[["Savanna","A tropical grassland with scattered trees"],["Prairie","A temperate grassland of North America"],["Steppe","A dry, temperate grassland of Eurasia"],["Grazer","An animal that feeds mainly on grass"]],
f:["Rice, wheat and maize are all grasses.","Grasses grow from the base, so they regrow after grazing.","About 1.5 million wildebeest migrate through the Serengeti."],
fun:"Bamboo is a grass, and some kinds can grow almost a meter in a single day.",
tl:"Ang grassland ay malawak na lupain kung saan damo ang pangunahing halaman at kakaunti ang puno. Tinatawag itong savanna, prairie, steppe at pampas sa iba't ibang lugar. Nakatira rito ang malalaking kawan ng hayop."});

ADDART({id:"kilimanjaro",t:"Mount Kilimanjaro",c:"Geography",i:"🏔️",w:"Mount Kilimanjaro",r:["everest","volc","kenyacountry","iceage","climchange"],
p:[["Roof of Africa","Mount Kilimanjaro in Tanzania is the highest mountain in Africa, at 5,895 meters."],["A lone giant","It is the tallest freestanding mountain in the world, rising alone from the plains."],["Snow near the equator","Its summit has glaciers, even though it lies only about 330 kilometers from the equator."]],
l:"Mount Kilimanjaro, in northeastern Tanzania near the border with Kenya, is the highest mountain in Africa and the tallest freestanding mountain on Earth. It is a dormant volcano with three cones: Kibo, Mawenzi and Shira. Its highest point, Uhuru Peak on Kibo, reaches 5,895 meters. Though close to the equator, Kilimanjaro is crowned with glaciers, which are rapidly shrinking. It does not require technical climbing skills, so tens of thousands of people try to climb it each year.",
s:[["A volcanic mountain","Kilimanjaro formed over the past million years or more from volcanic eruptions linked to the East African Rift. Shira is the oldest cone and has collapsed; Mawenzi is jagged and eroded; Kibo, the youngest and highest, is dormant, with a crater at the top. Fumaroles still release volcanic gases in Kibo’s crater."],["Climbing through climates","A climb up Kilimanjaro passes through several zones: farmland and rainforest on the lower slopes, then moorland with giant groundsels and lobelias, then alpine desert, and finally the arctic summit with ice and snow. Climbers say it is like walking from the equator to the poles in a few days."],["People of the mountain","The Chagga people have farmed the fertile southern slopes for centuries, growing bananas and coffee and building irrigation channels. Many Tanzanians work as guides and porters, carrying food and equipment for climbers. Their skill and hard work make most climbs possible."],["First ascent","The first recorded ascent of Kibo’s summit was in 1889 by the German geographer Hans Meyer and the Austrian climber Ludwig Purtscheller, helped by local guides including Yohani Kinyala Lauwo, who lived to over 100. Today, routes such as Marangu, Machame and Lemosho lead climbers to the top in five to nine days."]],
k:[["Height","5,895 m (Uhuru Peak)"],["Country","Tanzania"],["Type","Dormant stratovolcano"],["Cones","Kibo, Mawenzi, Shira"],["First recorded ascent","1889"]],
d:[["1848","Missionary Johannes Rebmann reports snow on Kilimanjaro"],["1889","Hans Meyer and Ludwig Purtscheller reach the summit"],["1936","Hemingway publishes The Snows of Kilimanjaro"],["1961","The peak is renamed Uhuru, meaning freedom, at Tanganyika’s independence"],["1973","Kilimanjaro National Park is created"],["1987","The park becomes a UNESCO World Heritage Site"]],
s2:[["Disappearing ice","Kilimanjaro’s glaciers have shrunk by more than 80 percent since the early 1900s. Scientists think this is caused by a mix of drier air, less snowfall and warming temperatures, as the ice turns directly to vapor in the strong sunlight. Some studies predict that the ice may disappear entirely within the next few decades."],["Altitude sickness","At the summit, the air contains only about half as much oxygen per breath as at sea level. Climbers who go up too fast can suffer from altitude sickness, causing headaches, nausea and, in serious cases, dangerous swelling of the lungs or brain. Guides advise climbing slowly, pole pole in Swahili, and drinking plenty of water."],["Unusual plants","The middle slopes have strange plants found nowhere else, such as giant groundsels that can grow several meters tall, and lobelias that look like giant green bottles. These plants have adapted to cold nights and strong daytime sun by storing water and closing their leaves at night."],["Uhuru Peak","When Tanganyika, now part of Tanzania, became independent in 1961, a torch was placed on the summit and the highest point was named Uhuru Peak, meaning freedom in Swahili. The Uhuru Torch is still a national symbol."],["The Seven Summits","Kilimanjaro is one of the Seven Summits, the highest mountains on each of the seven continents, along with Everest, Aconcagua, Denali, Elbrus, Vinson and Puncak Jaya or Kosciuszko. Many mountaineers try to climb all seven, and Kilimanjaro is often the first."],["Water for the region","Rain and snow on Kilimanjaro’s forests feed streams and springs that supply water to farms, towns and wildlife on the plains around it. Protecting the mountain’s forests is important for millions of people who depend on this water."]],
sp:["A mountain alone","Unlike Everest, which is part of the Himalaya range, Kilimanjaro stands by itself, rising almost 5,000 meters above the surrounding plains. This makes it the tallest freestanding mountain on Earth. On clear days, it can be seen from Amboseli National Park in Kenya, where photographers capture elephants walking across the plains with the snowy peak behind them."],
g:[["Dormant volcano","A volcano not erupting now but which could erupt again"],["Stratovolcano","A tall, cone-shaped volcano built of layers of lava and ash"],["Glacier","A slow-moving mass of ice"],["Altitude sickness","Illness caused by low oxygen at high altitude"]],
f:["Kilimanjaro is the highest mountain in Africa.","Its summit is called Uhuru Peak, meaning freedom.","Kilimanjaro’s glaciers have shrunk by more than 80 percent."],
fun:"Climbing Kilimanjaro is like traveling from the equator to the Arctic in a few days.",
tl:"Ang Bundok Kilimanjaro sa Tanzania ang pinakamataas na bundok sa Africa, na may taas na 5,895 metro. Isa itong natutulog na bulkan, at may yelo sa tuktok nito kahit malapit ito sa ekwador."});

;
